// Runtime/GtRuntime.maxon — the emitted GREEN-THREAD scheduler runtime.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **GO's PARK MODEL, AND A STRAND IS WHAT KEEPS `async` SINGLE-THREADED.** This file holds two kinds of
// GT:
//
//   • a **GREEN THREAD** owns itself: everything `spawn` creates, and `main` (`__gt_run_main`).
//   • a **COROUTINE** is what `async f(…)` creates. ⚖ USER, 2026-08-27: *"async is not supposed to create a
//     new green thread. it allows a function to yield the current green thread while waiting for a blocking
//     operation."* A coroutine is owned by the green thread that called it (`GtOffOwner`).
//
// A green thread and its coroutines are one STRAND. Every wait parks the waiter onto its machine's
// scheduler context (`__gt_park`, Go's `gopark`) and whoever completes the wait readies it
// (`__gt_ready_locked`, Go's `ready`); no wait ever runs another green thread on the waiter's
// stack. What a run queue holds is the strand's OWNER, as a token; the machine that pops it becomes
// the strand's one executor (`GtOffStrandHolder`) and runs the owner and its coroutines from the
// strand queue (`__gt_run_strand`) until the strand has nothing runnable. So at most one machine
// runs any member of a strand at a time, and every hand-over between machines goes through the
// strand's holder word, which a compare-and-swap claims and an atomic decrement gives up.
//
// ⇒ **every refcount read-modify-write on a COUNTED box happens on the machine running that box's strand, one
// machine at a time**; a box a share walk marked shared steps atomically instead.
// `SchedRuntime.maxon`'s header states the invariant in full.
//
// `SchedRuntime.maxon` beside this file owns the PROCESSOR (P), the MACHINE (M), the TLS door that finds
// them and the scheduler loop every machine runs. The running GT is `M->currentGt`; the syscall stack is
// `M->systemStackSP`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **EVERY `.data` WORD THIS FILE LAYS OUT STATES ITS MULTI-M SHARING CLASS WHERE IT IS CREATED** — see
// `SchedRuntime.MultiMSharing` and `pushRuntimeGlobal`. The counters step through
// `MmRuntime.emitGlobalAccumulate` with `multiM: true` as a LITERAL: everything here is built by
// `installGtRuntime`, which runs only under `usesGt`, so a second OS thread can exist by construction.
//
// THE SPLIT:
//   HAND-ASSEMBLED raw bytes (X64GtRuntime / Arm64GtRuntime) — the context switch, the trampoline, the
//     relocating morestack and the syscall shim. rsp/rbp manipulation and `ret`-into-a-fresh-frame are
//     unrepresentable in Std.
//   BUILDER-BUILT `StdModule` (here) — everything else, so it gets register allocation, the frame layout and
//     the leak-safe backend for free.
//
// ⭐ WHY THE HAND CHUNKS NEED NO `.data`. `M->currentGt` is written only by `emitSchedBecomeM` and by the
// strand runner around each switch (`emitGtExecute`); the shim and the prologue guard read it through TLS.
// The context switch passes the `to` GT through in rdx / x1, so `__gt_trampoline` reads its own GT from there,
// and its completion switch targets `GtOffWaiter`, which `emitGtExecute` stores before every switch-in.
//
// ⚠ THE GPR CHANNEL. The trampoline moves an async call's arguments through the integer arg registers and
// its result through R8, so what crosses is an integer, a bool, or a managed value as its one box pointer.
// A `float` has no channel here and is refused at the call (E2015, `SemanticCheck.requireAsyncScalarParams`
// for a parameter and `Parser.requireAsyncResult` for the result) rather than left to read the wrong
// register file, which an argument check cannot catch because an int legally coerces to a float parameter.
//
// ⭐ RELOCATING STACKS. Each GT starts on a seed stack of `__gt_seed_bytes` (its record's kept one, or
// a fresh `__gt_stack_alloc`), a size that follows the stacks recent threads finished with
// (`__gt_seed_adapt`, Go's `startingStackSize`);
// every guarded function's prologue projects its frame against `gt.stackGuard` (X64GtRuntime's
// `emitGtStackGuard`) and, when it would overflow, reaches the hand-assembled `__gt_morestack`,
// which doubles the size until the requesting frame fits (up to `GtMaxStackBytes`) and moves the
// thread onto the larger stack (`__gt_stack_relocate`: copy the live region, walk the saved
// frame-pointer chain fixing every interior pointer by the relocation offset). A parked thread
// using under a quarter of its stack is moved onto one half the size (`__gt_stack_shrink`, Go's
// `shrinkstack`). So deep recursion inside an `async` body just works, and a thread that once
// recursed deep does not keep that stack for life. The GT record comes from the scheduler's record
// arena and its stack from the stack pool, both INVISIBLE to the heap leak gate's tracked live
// column (`SchedRuntime.MOffGtLive` is the gate for them). A finished GT's stack stays with its record when
// it is the seed's size and otherwise goes back to the pool on completion (`__gt_runner_done` →
// `__gt_stack_free`).

// --- GreenThread struct offsets ---
// Each offset is derived from the field before it, and `requireGtRecordLayout` refuses a layout whose fields
// leave a gap or overlap.
// The offsets the hand-assembled chunks (X64GtRuntime — the context switch, the trampoline and the
// syscall shim) read/write are EXPORTED so the byte code and this builder name one struct layout — the
// offsets are a contract the two sides share, not a fact each restates. `GtOffStackBase` is exported
// because the shim's GT-stack guard reads it (`stackBase == 0` ⇒ run the kernel call directly, no switch).

// One machine word — the width of most GT fields. NAMED for
// `GtTeardownBytes`' reason: one literal spelled at every field is one fact written many times.
export let PointerFieldBytes = 8

// ⭐⭐ **THE RECORD LOCK — FIRST IN THE RECORD AND OUTSIDE EVERY ZEROING.** It is a recursive runtime
// lock (`RuntimeLock`), free in the zeroed pages the arena carves a record or a cell from, and stays
// live across every reuse: `__gt_spawn` zeroes only the fields `gtFieldRoster` marks for it, and
// `__gt_cell_alloc` from `GtOffStackBase` up to `GtCellBytes`. On a
// green thread it is the strand lock over the strand queue (`GtOffCoroHead`). On a coroutine or a
// reply cell it is the promise lock, where await, awaitAny and the completer meet over
// `GtOffAwaiter` — Go's `hchan.lock`. The two roles never fall on one record.
let GtOffRecordLock = 0

let GtRecordLockBytes = RecursiveLockBytes

export let GtOffStackBase = GtOffRecordLock + GtRecordLockBytes  // low address of the GT's stack (0 for an M's scheduler context, a reply cell and a free record that kept none)

// EXPORTED because the hand-assembled grower reads the CURRENT stack size to compute the doubled size, and
// `__gt_stack_relocate` reads it for the old stack's top and writes the new size back.
export let GtOffStackSize = GtOffStackBase + PointerFieldBytes

// The green-thread stack GUARD: the low-water address a function's PROLOGUE compares its projected rsp against —
// `projectedRsp < stackGuard` means the frame would overflow, so the prologue's cold path reaches `__gt_morestack` to
// grow+relocate the stack first. `__gt_spawn` seeds it to `stackBase + gtStackGuardHeight`; `__gt_stack_relocate`
// moves it to `newBase + gtStackGuardHeight` after a grow or a shrink; an M's inline scheduler context keeps the
// slab zero, so its guard compare (`projectedRsp >= 0` unsigned) always passes — it runs on the OS thread's own
// stack and never grows. EXPORTED because the hand-assembled prologue guard reads it and `__gt_stack_relocate`
// writes it.
export let GtOffStackGuard = GtOffStackSize + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **A PREEMPTION REQUEST — Go's `stackPreempt`.** `__sysmon` stores it into the guard word of a thread that
// has held its processor too long (`SchedRuntime.__sched_preemptone`). Read unsigned it is above every stack
// pointer, so the prologue guard's one compare fails into its cold path and calls `__gt_guard_tripped`, which
// tells the request from an overflow by this exact value and yields (`__gt_preempt`). There is no request field
// beside it: the guard word IS the request. A guard that may not park withdraws it instead (`guardSiteMayPark`).
// EXPORTED for both lanes' guards and `__gt_guard_tripped`, and for the monitor.
export let GtStackGuardPreempt = -1314 as FullRangeInt

export let GtOffStatus = GtOffStackGuard + PointerFieldBytes  // `GtStatus` below

export let GtOffResult = GtOffStatus + PointerFieldBytes  // the R8 return value, captured by the trampoline when the GT completes

// The dual-register ERROR FLAG the trampoline captures from R10 when a THROWING async thunk returns:
// 0 = success, non-zero = threw (a scalar error's `ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`, or a boxed
// union's box pointer — whatever the thunk left in R10). Read ONLY by `__gt_try_await`, which returns it as
// the second register of its dual-register exit; `__gt_await` (a non-throwing await) never reads it, so a
// non-throwing thunk leaving garbage in R10 here is harmless.
export let GtOffThrew = GtOffResult + PointerFieldBytes

export let GtOffFuncPtr = GtOffThrew + PointerFieldBytes  // the async function's address

// The chain of whichever one list this GT is on: its owner's strand queue or — once reclaimed — a free
// list. (The global run queue has its own link, `GtOffRunqNext`.) EXPORTED because the free lists are
// `SchedRuntime`'s.
export let GtOffNext = GtOffFuncPtr + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **THE PER-CALLEE RELEASER — the address of this GT's `__gt_release_<callee>`, or 0.** A spawn
// whose callee has NO managed argument, result or error writes nothing here and both release sites
// skip rather than indirect-call through it; `Runtime/AsyncReleaser.maxon` is the one home of that
// decision and of the synthesized body. Written by the spawn lowering
// (`LowerMaxonToStd.emitAsyncSpawn`) beside the inline argument stores, read by
// `__gt_await`/`__gt_try_await` (result CONSUMED) and by `__gt_promise_drop` (result NOT consumed)
// — see `emitGtReleaserCall` for why those two differ and what each failure mode costs.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT DEPENDS ON EVERY RECORD A SPAWN TAKES HOLDING 0 HERE, AND THAT IS A CORRECTNESS TRAP
// RATHER THAN AN OPTIMIZATION.** Without it, a spawn of a releaser-free callee would INHERIT the
// previous tenant's releaser and release that tenant's arguments out of stale argument slots — a
// wild free rather than a leak. The zero is what makes "no releaser" the default state of every GT
// rather than a state the spawn must remember to restore. The spawn does not zero the word; a
// carved record arrives zero, and a recycled one is 0 because `__gt_reclaim`, the one caller of
// `__gt_free_put`, claims the word by swapping it to 0 first (`emitGtReleaserCall`). A second
// road onto a free list must clear it too.
export let GtOffReleaser = GtOffNext + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐ **THE ASYNC-TRACE ID (`spawn #1`, `await #1 [yield]`).** A small unique number assigned at spawn from
// `__gt_trace_counter` (`GtTraceCounterLabel`), so a trace line can name a green thread the way a reader can
// follow — the GT's ADDRESS would be unique but unreadable and different every run, and the trace is compared
// byte-for-byte against a spec. Read back at every trace site (`__gt_io_park` for the I/O pair, the awaits for `await #`), which
// is why it has to live on the struct rather than in the spawning frame: the awaiting frame is a different
// frame, and the parking one is a different function entirely.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS PRESENT WHETHER OR NOT TRACING IS ON, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** Only the trace CALLS are gated on
// the flag; the LAYOUT is not, because it is a contract shared with three hand-assembled Target-tier chunks
// (`__gt_context_switch`, `__gt_trampoline`, `__gt_morestack`) that read fixed offsets. A layout that changed
// shape with a debug flag would mean the byte code and the builder disagreed in exactly the configuration
// nobody routinely runs. Eight bytes per green thread is the price of one layout. The id is assigned only in a
// traced or `debugStream` build, the two that read it. `main`'s spawn zeroes it; in any other
// build every other thread's holds whatever the record's previous tenant left.
let GtOffTraceId = GtOffReleaser + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **THE TEARDOWN RENDEZVOUS WORD — the ONE place the two halves of a green thread's
// ownership meet, and the ONLY word in the struct that BOTH halves write.** Every green thread is owned by
// exactly two parties and each of them finishes at a moment the other cannot observe:
//
//   • the **CONSUMER** — the promise's owner. `__gt_await` / `__gt_try_await` take the result; a
//     `__gt_promise_drop` (and the `cancel` that routes through it) renounces it. Per-await linearity
//     (E3100) and `Parser.consumePromiseThread` make it EXACTLY ONE of those, exactly once.
//   • the **RUNNER** — whoever finishes with the thread's EXECUTION. That is the strand runner whose
//     `__gt_context_switch` came back from a `completed` thread, or — for a thread that never ran — the
//     party that takes it out of a queue (`__gt_strand_next` and `__gt_coro_sweep_dropped`), or the dropper
//     itself once it has deregistered a PARKED thread from every store. A green thread's runner arrives only
//     once its strand no longer needs the struct (`GtOffStrandRefs`).
//
// Each adds its own half-ticket exactly once with ONE `lock xadd`; **the party whose add returns the OTHER
// ticket is SECOND, and the second party performs the teardown** (`__gt_reclaim`: the releaser call and the
// struct's return to a free list). Neither side ever has to know what the other is doing, and — because the
// add is the hand-off — **every read of the struct that precedes a party's own add is safe**, which is what
// makes the runner's stack-length load and the awaiter's `result`/`threw` loads race-free without a lock.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT CANNOT LIVE IN `GtOffStatus`, AND THAT IS WHY IT IS A FIELD RATHER THAN A STATE.** The
// hand-assembled trampoline (`X64GtRuntime.buildGtTrampolineChunk`) stores `completed` over the status word
// UNCONDITIONALLY at completion, from bytes no builder writes — so a mark left in that word by a dropper is
// erased by the very event the dropper needs to rendezvous with. The teardown word is written by
// builder-emitted code only, and by nothing on the park/resume path, so it survives every transition the
// status word does not.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO TICKETS ARE DISTINCT POWERS OF TWO SO ONE WORD ANSWERS TWO QUESTIONS.** `xadd` hands back the
// PRIOR value, so `prior == theOtherTicket` is "I am second"; and a party that merely LOOKS (the sweeps,
// `__sched_find_runnable`, `__gt_strand_next`) reads `GtTeardownConsumerTicket` as *"the promise is gone and
// no runner has taken responsibility"* — which is `emitGtIsDropped`, the whole dropped-thread test, out of
// the same word. A counter would answer the first and not the second.
// ⚠ NOT exported. Every tombstone asker goes through `emitGtIsDropped`, which is in this file and pairs the
// load with the compare, so the OFFSET has no reader outside it — which is what E3092 refuses, and is the
// shape a single-homed layout constant should have.
let GtOffTeardown = GtOffTraceId + PointerFieldBytes

// One i64 word for the teardown rendezvous. NAMED rather than spelled `8` beside `GtOffTeardown` for the
// reason `StackWordBytes` was split out of `GtArgSlotBytes`: two facts sharing one literal means an edit to
// either silently moves the other.
let GtTeardownBytes = 8

// ⭐⭐ **THE GREEN THREAD THIS GT BELONGS TO — the head of its STRAND.** A coroutine's owner is the green thread
// that created it; a GREEN THREAD's owner is ITSELF, which is what makes the relation total rather than a
// special case with a null in it — `emitSchedBecomeM` stamps a machine's scheduler context with its own
// address, and `__gt_spawn` copies the SPAWNER's owner, so a coroutine spawned by a coroutine belongs to the
// same green thread.
//
// EXPORTED because `SchedRuntime.emitSchedBecomeM` stamps the scheduler context's self-ownership.
export let GtOffOwner = GtOffTeardown + GtTeardownBytes

// ⭐⭐ **THE GT PARKED WAITING FOR THIS ONE TO COMPLETE, OR 0.** Written under this GT's record lock
// (`GtOffRecordLock`) by the await and awaitAny registrations, taken under it by
// `__gt_runner_done`. An awaitAny registration stores its thread with `AwaitAnyTag` set, so the
// completer knows to ask `GtOffSelectDone` before readying it.
let GtOffAwaiter = GtOffOwner + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐ **ON A REPLY CELL ANSWERING WITH A VALUE HELD AT AN INTERFACE TYPE: THE VALUE'S WITNESS HALF.** A cell
// carries one value word (`GtOffResult`), and a fat pointer is two, so the service loop writes the witness
// here before `__gt_cell_complete` publishes (`ServiceLoop.storeReplyWitness`) and the awaiter takes it
// through `__gt_reply_witness`; the reply releaser drops through it (`AsyncReleaser.asyncSlotsAt`).
// Read only on a reply cell: a green thread's holds a stale word.
export let GtOffReplyWitness = GtOffAwaiter + PointerFieldBytes

export let GtOffRenounced = GtOffReplyWitness + PointerFieldBytes

// A reply cell is the record's first `GtCellBytes` and is carved exactly that long, so every field a cell
// is read or written through lies below it: one past it reads a neighbouring cell's bytes. The layout check
// sees only that the prefix tiles, never which fields a cell's readers touch.
export let GtCellBytes = GtOffRenounced + PointerFieldBytes

export let GtOffSp = GtCellBytes  // saved stack pointer at the context-switch point
export let GtOffFp = GtOffSp + PointerFieldBytes  // saved frame pointer (rbp) at the context-switch point

// ⭐⭐ **THE SCHEDULER CONTEXT TO SWITCH BACK TO WHEN THIS GT PARKS, YIELDS OR COMPLETES — and ALSO THE ANSWER
// TO "HAS THIS THREAD EVER RUN?".** `emitGtExecute` stores the executing machine's scheduler context here
// immediately before every switch-in; that is never 0 and nothing clears it. So `waiter == 0` means no machine
// has ever switched into it — the population whose stack and struct may be freed without asking its own code
// first, which is what `emitGtIsDropped` reads it for.
export let GtOffWaiter = GtOffFp + PointerFieldBytes

export let GtOffArgBuf = GtOffWaiter + PointerFieldBytes  // a self-pointer to this struct's inline argument region (`GtOffArgInline`)

// --- The overlapped-read fields ---
// The 32-byte Win32 OVERLAPPED the yielding ReadFile is issued against, EMBEDDED in the GT struct (four
// words). The poller's decode recovers the reading thread from the completion packet's
// lpOverlapped by CONTAINING_RECORD (`gt = ovlPtr - GtOffOverlapped`), so no per-read allocation and no separate
// waiter table is needed. EXPORTED because `NetpollRuntime`'s decode reads it to recover the thread and the read
// builtin passes `gt + GtOffOverlapped` to ReadFile. It MUST be zero-inited before each ReadFile (a dirty
// `hEvent` low bit suppresses the completion packet) — every transfer issued against it zeroes it
// first (`emitGtZeroOverlapped`).
export let GtOffOverlapped = GtOffArgBuf + PointerFieldBytes
let GtOverlappedBytes = 32

// ⭐ **THE NTSTATUS THE COMPLETION PACKET CARRIED, masked to its 32 bits.** Written by the poller's decode
// (`NetpollRuntime.emitNetpollDecodeCompletion`) beside `io_result` and read by `__gt_subp_yield_read` after
// its wait, which is how a parked read tells bytes from a clean end of stream from a failure. EXPORTED because
// `NetpollRuntime` writes it.
export let GtOffIoStatus = GtOffOverlapped + GtOverlappedBytes

// The bytes the completion packet reported transferred (`OVERLAPPED_ENTRY.dwNumberOfBytesTransferred`), read
// back by `__gt_subp_yield_read` once its wait ends. EXPORTED — the poller's decode writes it.
export let GtOffIoResult = GtOffIoStatus + PointerFieldBytes

// The GT's OWN Windows TIB stack bounds, saved so the syscall shim can repoint `gs:[0x08]`
// (NT_TIB.StackBase) / `gs:[0x10]` (NT_TIB.StackLimit) at the system stack for a kernel call and RESTORE
// them to this GT's stack afterwards — heavy kernel calls probe the TIB and fault when RSP is outside its
// bounds. `__gt_spawn` seeds them to the GT's own stack (`tibStackBase = stackBase + seed size`,
// `tibStackLimit = stackBase`); an M's inline scheduler context starts at 0/0 — the shim calls directly for it
// (`stackBase == 0`), and the first context switch away from it captures its OS thread's real bounds. EXPORTED
// because the shim (a hand-assembled Target-tier chunk) reads them.
export let GtOffTibStackBase = GtOffIoResult + PointerFieldBytes
export let GtOffTibStackLimit = GtOffTibStackBase + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **THE STAMPED SOURCE KEY OF THE READ THIS THREAD HAS IN FLIGHT** — `index | (gen << 32)` for the poll
// record `__np_op_open` gave the operation. The completion packet names the OPERATION through its
// `lpOverlapped` and carries no key of its own, so the decode recovers this thread and then reads the record
// to wake out of this word. Written BEFORE the read is issued, which is what makes it readable by a packet
// that arrives before the thread has parked. EXPORTED because `NetpollRuntime` writes and reads it.
//
// ⛔ **A ZERO HERE MEANS "NO READ IN FLIGHT", AND THE STAMP IS WHAT MAKES THAT EXACT.** `__gt_promise_drop`
// reads this word to tell a dropped READ from a dropped socket or child wait, so a live read must never
// stamp a zero — and it cannot: `__np_pd_adopt` BUMPS the record's generation, so every stamped key carries
// a generation of at least one in its high half whatever index it got, index zero included.
export let GtOffIoSource = GtOffTibStackLimit + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐ **THE PIPE HANDLE THE IN-FLIGHT READ WAS ISSUED ON.** Written before the read, read by
// `__gt_promise_drop` to cancel it — and READ ONLY WHERE `io_source` IS NON-ZERO, which is exactly while the
// kernel owns the operation. EXPORTED because `NetpollRuntime`'s aim writes it.
export let GtOffIoHandle = GtOffIoSource + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH DIRECTION THE IN-FLIGHT OVERLAPPED OPERATION IS, `NetpollModeRead` or `NetpollModeWrite`.**
// A completion packet names the OPERATION through its `lpOverlapped` and carries no direction of its own, so
// the decode reads it off the thread to know which of the record's two direction words to ready. Written
// before the operation is issued, beside `io_handle` and under the same guard: it is read only where
// `io_source` is non-zero. EXPORTED because `NetpollRuntime`'s decode reads it.
export let GtOffIoMode = GtOffIoHandle + PointerFieldBytes

// The TEB's `DeallocationStack` (`gs:[0x1478]`), saved, repointed and restored at every site that
// moves the two bounds above, so the three always name one stack.
export let GtOffTibDeallocation = GtOffIoMode + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐ **THE INLINE ARGUMENT REGION.** MaxAsyncArgs i64 slots inside the GT struct, so a spawn needs NO
// separate argument buffer — `__gt_spawn` sets `gt.argBuf` to a SELF-POINTER `gt + GtOffArgInline`,
// and the trampoline dereferences that self-pointer as it would a separate buffer.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THE REGION IS INLINE RATHER THAN A SEPARATELY ALLOCATED BUFFER.** A separate buffer is a SECOND
// allocation and a SECOND lifetime per spawn, and the second lifetime is the expensive half — the releaser
// reads these slots at the AWAIT or the DROP, long after the thread has finished, so a separate buffer would
// have to be freed by whichever of `__gt_await`, `__gt_try_await` and `__gt_promise_drop` got there, on
// every arm of each. Inline, the region's lifetime IS the struct's, and `__gt_reclaim` ends both.
//
// ⚠ **THE ARGUMENT SLOTS ARE NOT DEAD BY COMPLETION.** The trampoline reads each slot once at run-start, and
// `__gt_release_<callee>` reloads the managed ones to drop them at the await or the drop, after the GT has
// finished. Nothing between the spawn's stores and that read writes the region, so the values are still
// there; a change that reused the region as scratch would be a wild free, not merely a mis-loaded argument.
export let GtOffArgInline = GtOffTibDeallocation + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **THE STRAND QUEUE — the members of this green thread's strand that are runnable, an intrusive
// FIFO through `GtOffNext`.** Meaningful only on a green thread. A readied coroutine and a yielder
// join the back; the owner readied from a wait joins the FRONT, so a strand runs its owner first
// when the owner is ready. The owner's record lock (`GtOffRecordLock`) guards both ends. The head
// is stored with a release, because `__gt_strand_next` reads it with an acquire and no lock.
let GtOffCoroHead = GtOffArgInline + ArgBufBytes
let GtOffCoroTail = GtOffCoroHead + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE PARK NOTE — WHAT KIND OF WAIT THIS GT IS ENTERING, AND THE ONE ARGUMENT THAT WAIT NEEDS.** A thread
// about to suspend may NOT be registered on the store that will wake it until `__gt_context_switch` has
// finished saving its registers: between the registration and that save it is discoverable *while still
// executing on its own stack*, so a second M can fire its timer and resume it onto a stale SP — two Ms on one
// stack. So `__gt_park` writes what the thread wants here and switches; the strand runner reads the note on
// the far side of the switch (`__gt_run_strand`) and registers the park through `__gt_register_park`, or
// commits a mailbox park itself (`GtParkKind.mailbox`).
//
// ⚠ The kind is A NOTE, NOT A STATE: it is read only by the strand runner right after the switch it
// describes, so it is never cleared. `__gt_promise_drop` also reads the kind of a thread it found `waiting`,
// which is the kind the thread parked with.
//
// EXPORTED because `NetpollRuntime` reads it to tell a park on ONE source from a park on several: the two
// carry different things in `GtOffParkArg`, and only the kind says which.
export let GtOffParkKind = GtOffCoroTail + PointerFieldBytes

// EXPORTED because `NetpollRuntime` reads a netpoll park's `(fd, mode)` note out of it.
//
// A mailbox park's argument is also its handoff slot: the park commit empties it, a send stores the request
// it hands the parked receiver, and the receiver reads it when it resumes (`MailboxRuntime.buildMboxRecv`).
export let GtOffParkArg = GtOffParkKind + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH LIFE OF ITS POLL RECORD A NETPOLL PARK BELONGS TO** (`NetpollRuntime.PdOffGen`). A netpoll
// park is registered on the FAR side of the context switch (`__np_pd_commit`, out of `__gt_register_park`),
// by which time the descriptor may have been closed, reused and reopened onto that same record — and the
// only mark the commit could otherwise test, `PdWait`, reads identically across two lives of it. So the wait
// stamps the record's generation here before it switches, and the commit publishes only on a match.
// A park of any other kind never reads this and never writes it.
export let GtOffParkGen = GtOffParkArg + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHAT THIS GREEN THREAD HAS ALLOCATED AND FREED — the memory columns a POOL is measured with.** Every
// process-wide figure sums every row, so a bracket opened inside one worker would count every other worker's
// traffic too.
//
// ⭐ **THEY SUM BOTH ALLOCATOR LAYERS**, stepped at the slab doors once per request, so each is this thread's
// share of the process-wide total its count names (`MmRuntime.CombinedCount`), and a bracket on either
// measures the same quantity. Frees are a column of their own rather than a live count: a green thread does not
// own the boxes it allocated, so a per-thread live count would mean nothing.
//
// ⚠ **THEY ARE STEPPED WITHOUT A LOCK AND WITHOUT AN ATOMIC, and the ownership model is what makes
// that sound**: a strand runs on at most one M at a time, and hands over between machines only
// through its holder word (`GtOffStrandHolder`), so the one party that can step these words is the
// one thread they belong to — and it only ever adds.
//
// EXPORTED because the allocator steps them and their readers are built in `MmRuntime`'s counter family,
// which is where a caller looks for a memory column.
export let GtOffThreadAllocs = GtOffParkGen + PointerFieldBytes
export let GtOffThreadFrees = GtOffThreadAllocs + PointerFieldBytes
export let GtOffThreadAllocBytes = GtOffThreadFrees + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT MAKES `__Builtins.threadCpuTicks()` THIS GREEN THREAD's CLOCK RATHER THAN ITS MACHINE's.** The
// OS counter belongs to the OS thread, and a green thread that parks may resume on another machine, so a
// bracket across a park would subtract one thread's counter from another's. The clock a green thread reads is
// the counter of the OS thread it is on PLUS this offset, and every switch that can move it
// (`emitGtSwitchAway`) reads the clock on both sides and folds the difference in here, so the clock stands
// still while the thread is switched out and never runs backwards. Only a program that reads the clock
// (`GtKeepsCpuClockLabel`) pays for the two reads. Written only by the thread itself.
let GtOffCpuOffset = GtOffThreadAllocBytes + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **1 FOR `main`'s THREAD, 0 FOR EVERY OTHER GREEN THREAD.** Stamped by `__gt_spawn_main`;
// read by `__gt_runner_done`, because `main`'s completion is what ends the main machine's first
// loop (`SchedRuntime.SchedPhase`). It reads it on a green thread only, so a coroutine's spawn
// leaves the word.
let GtOffIsMainThread = GtOffCpuOffset + PointerFieldBytes
let GtMainThreadMark = 1

// ⭐⭐⭐ **1 WHILE SOME MACHINE OWNS THIS STRAND — its owner token is in a run queue, or a machine is
// running the strand — and 0 otherwise.** Meaningful only on a green thread. Every claim is a 0 → 1
// CAS (`emitGtStrandClaimLost`) made with no lock, by the two ready doors and by the parked-service
// ready (`emitGtReadyParkedService`), and its winner publishes the token. A spawned green thread is
// born held: `__gt_ready` stores 1 before the record is published. `__gt_strand_next` releases it
// with an atomic decrement when the strand queue is empty, then re-reads the queue and re-claims it
// if a member arrived.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THIS WORD IS WHAT KEEPS A STRAND ON ONE MACHINE.** The token is published only by the
// readier whose CAS wins, and this reads 0 only after the executing machine found the strand queue
// empty and gave the strand up. So a readier either loses and leaves the member on the strand queue
// for the machine that holds the strand — whose release re-reads the queue after it lets go
// (`buildGtReadyDoor`) — or wins, at a moment when no machine is running any member, and becomes
// the one publisher of the one token. No member of a strand can therefore be run by a second
// machine while one is running any of them, and the owner is never readied onto a run queue while
// one of its coroutines executes elsewhere.
let GtOffStrandHolder = GtOffIsMainThread + PointerFieldBytes
let StrandFree = 0
let StrandHeld = 1
let StrandRefsOwnerAndHold = 2

// ⭐⭐ **HOW MANY PARTIES STILL NEED THIS GREEN THREAD's STRUCT AS A STRAND HEAD**: the owner's own execution,
// each coroutine it created that has not yet finished, and the strand hold. A coroutine can outlive its
// owner's return — a dropped one runs out its body — and it still pushes onto this struct's strand queue, so
// the owner's runner arrival (`GtOffTeardown`) waits until this reaches 0. Stepped atomically: a claim is
// taken on whatever thread readies a member, a machine draining the poller included.
let GtOffStrandRefs = GtOffStrandHolder + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐ **ON AN awaitAny WAITER: 1 once a completer has readied it, 0 while it is still registered** —
// Go's `selectDone`, so K slots completing ready the waiter once. A completer claims it by CAS,
// because K completers hold K different record locks.
let GtOffSelectDone = GtOffStrandRefs + PointerFieldBytes
let SelectPending = 0
let SelectReadied = 1

// ⭐⭐ **THE GLOBAL RUN QUEUE's LINK.** The global queue holds owner tokens, and an owner can be in its own
// strand queue (through `GtOffNext`) while its token waits in the global queue, so the two lists cannot share
// one link.
let GtOffRunqNext = GtOffSelectDone + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE STACK MARKS (`GtStackMarkResized`, `GtStackMarkGrew`, `GtStackMarkParked`)** — what a spawn, a
// grow and a park can afford to record, one store and no clock read. The park door sets `parked` and turns
// `resized` into `GtOffStackResizedAt`; the finish reads `grew` and `parked` to choose the adaptive seed's sample,
// or to take none. Touched only by the thread itself and by the machine running its
// strand, one at a time, so it takes no atomic. EXPORTED because both morestack chunks store it.
export let GtOffStackMarks = GtOffRunqNext + PointerFieldBytes

// When this thread's stack size was last chosen, in `__gt_now_ns` nanoseconds: stamped by the park that found
// `GtStackMarkResized`, and by a shrink. `__gt_stack_shrink` halves only a stack whose size has stood for
// `GtStackResizeWindowNs`, which is what keeps a deep/shallow alternation from copying the stack at every park.
let GtOffStackResizedAt = GtOffStackMarks + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐ **THE DEEPEST `top - sp` A PARK DOOR HAS SEEN ON THIS THREAD** — the only depth the runtime observes on a
// thread that never grew, since the prologue guard reports only an overflow. Raised at every park
// (`emitGtNoteStackDepth`); read by the finish as the sample of a thread that parked and never needed to grow.
let GtOffStackHighWater = GtOffStackResizedAt + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **ON A STRAND's OWNER: THE MEMBER THE STRAND RUNS NEXT — OR 0** — a member a preemption
// stopped, or the owner a winning claim published. A coroutine gives up its green thread only where
// it waits (`async` is a coroutine, not a thread), so a preemption may take the member's processor
// but never its turn: `__gt_strand_next` answers this slot before the owner or any sibling in the
// queue, whatever was readied meanwhile. Written only by the strand's holder, with no lock: the
// strand runner's preempt arm, a claimer that wins a Free strand (`emitGtPublishClaimedOwner`) —
// Free means this slot is empty, and that claimer is its one writer until a machine takes the token
// — and `__gt_ready` for a green thread not yet published. Taken by `__gt_strand_next`; it is empty
// whenever a member runs, because the taking is what starts one.
let GtOffStrandResume = GtOffStackHighWater + PointerFieldBytes

export let GtPromiseHeld = 0
let GtPromiseGivenUp = 1

let GtRestraintArmed = 1

export let GtOffHold = GtOffStrandResume + PointerFieldBytes

export let GtHoldReleased = 0 as FullRangeInt
export let GtHoldRequested = 1 as FullRangeInt

let GtOffPinnedM = GtOffHold + PointerFieldBytes

let GtOffPinDepth = GtOffPinnedM + PointerFieldBytes

// This thread's last OS error while it is switched out, so a failed call's reason survives a
// reschedule onto another machine. On Linux and Windows `__gt_context_switch` moves the OS thread's
// own word in and out of it. On Darwin libc's `errno` is also written by the scheduler's own libc
// calls, so nothing is carried across a switch: each failure arm stashes `errno` here instead, and
// `mrt_host_last_error` reads it back on a green thread.
export let GtOffLastError = GtOffPinDepth + PointerFieldBytes

// A sender waiting on a full mailbox (`MailboxRuntime.MboxSendName`): its links in that mailbox's
// sender queue, under the mailbox lock and 0 once it is unlinked (`__mbox_close` threads its own
// chain through `GtOffSendNext` until it readies the sender); its absolute deadline,
// `GtDeadlineMaxNs` when it armed no timer; and the `MboxSendWake` a waker claims it with
// (`MailboxRuntime.emitMboxClaimSender`), which the sender reads with an acquire.
export let GtOffSendNext = GtOffLastError + PointerFieldBytes
export let GtOffSendPrev = GtOffSendNext + PointerFieldBytes
export let GtOffSendDeadline = GtOffSendPrev + PointerFieldBytes
export let GtOffSendOutcome = GtOffSendDeadline + PointerFieldBytes

export enum GtLastErrorDirection
	intoGt
	fromGt
end 'GtLastErrorDirection'

// The thread's message staging buffer (`MailboxRuntime.MboxStagingName`), or 0 until its first
// send. The spawn's zeroing leaves it: the buffer is arena memory that is never freed, so a reused
// record keeps the one it has, and a zeroed word would carve a second.
export let GtOffStaging = GtOffSendOutcome + PointerFieldBytes

// The GT struct's size. Must be a multiple of 8.
//
// A new field goes into `gtFieldRoster` with the rule that says who initialises it; the layout
// check iterates that roster and panics at install on a field it does not name.
// `SchedRuntime`'s M fields after the inline GT are each derived from the one before, so a growth
// walks them all along correctly.
//
// EXPORTED because `SchedRuntime`'s M struct embeds a whole GT inline as its scheduler context.
export let GtStructSize = GtOffStaging + PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐ **THE PARK KINDS — what the strand runner dispatches on after the switch.** Each names the STORE
// `__gt_register_park` puts the thread on; a yield and a preemption register nothing and are placed back on
// the thread's strand (`__gt_run_strand`).
//
// EXPORTED because `MailboxRuntime` and `NetpollRuntime` produce kinds whose arms are here — the
// files must spell one number.
export enum GtParkKind
	timer = 1

	// ⚠ **2 AND 3 ARE RESERVED, NOT FREE** — a park kind travels in a green thread's own record, so a number is
	// spent once allocated and a new kind takes the next one past the highest.

	// `__mbox_recv`: the argument is the mailbox. `__gt_run_strand` commits this park itself
	// (`MailboxRuntime.emitMboxParkCommit`), so `__gt_register_park` never sees it. The receiver is
	// published as `parkedGt` before its registers are saved, which is sound only because it holds the
	// mailbox lock across the switch and every waker must take that lock first.
	mailbox = 4

	// `__gt_await` / `__gt_try_await`: the argument is the awaited GT.
	awaitPromise = 5

	// `__gt_await_any`: the argument is the promise array's record.
	awaitAny = 6

	// `__gt_resched`: a yield, which the strand runner places rather than registers.
	yielded = 7

	// ⭐⭐ **`__np_pd_wait`: the argument is the `(fd, mode)` pair of the socket direction this thread waits on**
	// (`NetpollRuntime.emitNetpollParkArg`).
	netpoll = 8

	// `__gt_preempt`: a thread that honoured a preemption request, which the strand runner also places.
	preempt = 9

	// ⭐⭐ **`__np_pd_wait_any`: the argument is a GROUP RECORD naming several `(fd, mode)` pairs**
	// (`NetpollRuntime`'s group band). It is a kind of its own rather than a flag on the netpoll one because every
	// door that reads a parked thread's note — the commit, the drop, the readiness that wakes it — must read the
	// argument as a LIST, and a door that read one as the other would publish a thread into a record at the
	// address of its own group.
	netpollAny = 10

	// `__gt_quiesce`: the argument is unused; the thread registers in `GtQuiesceWaiterLabel`'s word, and
	// `__sched_checkdead` writes the answer into `GtOffParkArg` before readying it.
	quiesce = 11

	// `__mbox_send` on a full mailbox: the argument is the mailbox. Committed by `__gt_run_strand`
	// (`MailboxRuntime.emitMboxSendParkCommit`) under the same lock-across-the-switch rule as `mailbox`,
	// so `__gt_register_park` never sees it.
	mailboxSend = 12
end 'GtParkKind'

// ⭐ **THE POLLER OWNS TWO PARK KINDS, AND EVERY SITE THAT ASKS WHETHER A THREAD IS ON IT MEANS BOTH.** One
// source and several are the same wait to everything outside `NetpollRuntime`: the same commit, the same
// expiry, the same drop. A test naming only `GtParkKind.netpoll` would send a multi-source wait down the road
// for a kind registered on no store at all, and nothing would ever end it.
function emitGtBranchOnPollerPark(module StdModule, block IrBlock, kind ValueId, onPoller IrBlock, elsewhere IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let onOneSource = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.netpoll.rawValue, ids: ids)
	let onSeveral = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.netpollAny.rawValue, ids: ids)
	let parked = emitBin(module, block: block, a: onOneSource, b: onSeveral, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.condBranch(parked, thenBlockId: onPoller.id, elseBlockId: elsewhere.id))
end 'emitGtBranchOnPollerPark'

// The `arg` a park that needs none carries — a yield and a preemption — so the site says so rather than
// passing whatever happened to be in a register.
let GtParkArgUnused = 0

// ⭐ **THE LOW BIT OF `GtOffAwaiter` MARKS AN awaitAny WAITER.** GT structs are word-aligned, so the bit is free.
let AwaitAnyTag = 1

// ⭐ **THE TWO HALF-TICKETS.** Distinct so `xadd`'s prior value names WHO was already there, and powers of
// two so the sum is unambiguous: 0 = neither, 1 = the consumer alone, 2 = the runner alone, 3 = both (and
// the struct is gone). See `GtOffTeardown` for the protocol and `emitGtTeardownArrive` for the one emitter.
let GtTeardownConsumerTicket = 1
let GtTeardownRunnerTicket = 2

// --- GT status ---
// `ready` (a fresh record's zero, and what `__gt_ready_locked` stores), `completed` (set by the
// trampoline, read by the strand runner's completion gate) and `waiting` (stored only through
// `emitGtMarkWaiting`, cleared to `ready` by whoever readies the thread) are load-bearing.
//
// ⛔⛔ **`running` (1) EXISTS IN THE LAYOUT AND IS NEVER STORED, AND THAT IS A DECISION RATHER THAN A
// HOLE.** `ready` reads the same on a thread sitting in a ring and on one another M popped a
// microsecond ago, and the trampoline overwrites this word with `completed` from hand-assembled
// bytes, so no mark left here survives the event a dropper must rendezvous with. **The rendezvous
// therefore lives in a word of its own (`GtOffTeardown`) and the drop does not ask this one who is
// running** — it asks only `waiting?`, because a PARKED thread is the single shape whose teardown
// needs a store deregistered (`buildGtPromiseDrop` says why its unlocked read of this word is
// enough).
//
// `waiting` MUST be distinct from `completed` so the strand runner's `status == completed` gate NEVER hands a
// merely-parked GT to the runner's teardown (freeing a parked GT's stack is a use-after-free when it resumes).
export enum GtStatus
	ready = 0
	completed = 2
	waiting = 3
end 'GtStatus'

// ⭐ **THE FLOOR OF A GREEN THREAD'S STACK — Go's `stackMin`.** Every stack is a power of two at least this
// large, and the relocating morestack doubles it on demand (copy + frame-pointer-chain relocation), so the seed
// is a floor and not a ceiling. It is small on purpose: the guard fires within a couple of frames of a thread's
// entry, so `__gt_morestack` is exercised by ordinary programs rather than only by a contrived recursion. The
// syscall shim moves every kernel call off this stack onto the M's system stack, which is what lets it be tiny.
let GtMinStackBytes = 0x800 as FullRangeInt

// ⭐⭐ **THE SMALLEST SEED — Go's `fixedStack`: `GtMinStackBytes` plus the OS's own reservation, rounded up to a
// power of two.** A seed no taller than `gtStackGuardHeight` would grow on the first guarded frame of every
// spawn; this one gives the OS its reservation and Maxon its floor. It is what `__gt_spawn` commits until the
// first adaptive-seed window closes (`GtSeedBytesLabel`), and no shrink goes below it.
function gtSeedStackBytes(target Target) returns FullRangeInt
	let wanted = GtMinStackBytes + gtStackSystemBytes(target)
	return roundUpToPowerOfTwo(wanted as BytePos)
end 'gtSeedStackBytes'

// ⭐⭐ **THE LARGEST STACK A GREEN THREAD MAY GROW TO — Go's `maxstacksize`, a gigabyte on 64-bit.**
// `__gt_morestack` refuses a growth past it with `panic: stack overflow` BEFORE allocating: a
// program that recurses without bound is told so, rather than doubling until the address space is gone and
// failing somewhere unrelated. Go's limit is decimal (10⁹) because it reads better in its message; stacks are
// powers of two, so this binary gigabyte admits exactly the stacks Go's does, plus the one gigabyte-sized stack.
export let GtMaxStackBytes = 0x40000000

// ⭐⭐ **HOW LONG A STACK's SIZE MUST STAND BEFORE A PARK MAY HALVE IT.** Go shrinks once per garbage collection
// (`shrinkstack`); this runtime has no collector, so a second of wall time is the cycle. It bounds a thread's
// shrink copies to one a second whatever its parks do.
let GtStackResizeWindowNs = 1000000000

// ⭐⭐ **HOW MANY FINISHED STACKS ONE ADAPTIVE-SEED WINDOW AVERAGES.** Go recomputes
// `startingStackSize` once per garbage collection, over the stacks that cycle scanned; here a
// window closes at the finish that makes its processor's count a multiple of this, so the seed
// follows finished threads whether or not the program ever arms a timer, and the adapt runs once per
// window rather than once per finish. A power of two, so the test is a mask.
let GtSeedWindowShift = 6 as FullRangeInt
let GtSeedWindowSamples = 1 shl GtSeedWindowShift
let GtSeedWindowMask = GtSeedWindowSamples - 1

// ⭐ **THE LARGEST SEED THE ADAPTIVE START MAY CHOOSE.** Go caps `startingStackSize` only at
// `maxstacksize`, because its stacks come from a cache; every seed here is committed memory taken
// at the spawn, before the thread has used a byte, and the cap is also the stack pool's largest
// class (`requireStackPoolGeometry`). 64 KiB spares the average deep thread its first growths while
// ten thousand concurrent threads commit 640 MB at most.
let GtSeedMaxBytes = 0x10000 as FullRangeInt

// `GtOffStackMarks`' three bits. `resized`: the size was chosen since the thread last parked — set by the spawn and
// by every grow, cleared by the park that stamps the time. `grew`: `__gt_morestack` has run for this thread — set by
// every grow and never cleared. `parked`: the park door has seen this thread's depth — set by every park; a grow's
// store clears it, which is harmless because `grew` then selects the sample.
let GtStackMarkResized = 1 as FullRangeInt
let GtStackMarkGrew = 2 as FullRangeInt
let GtStackMarkParked = 4 as FullRangeInt
let GtStackMarksOfAnObservation = GtStackMarkGrew or GtStackMarkParked

// What a grow stores: `resized` and `grew`. EXPORTED for the morestack chunks.
export let GtStackMarksOfAGrow = GtStackMarkResized or GtStackMarkGrew

// ⭐⭐ **WHERE THE OS WRITES A HARDWARE FAULT'S FRAME WHEN A GREEN THREAD FAULTS.** A fault's frame is not a
// Maxon frame, so no guard ever projects it; each lane answers once here, and the two consequences read it:
// how much stack the guard reserves under every green thread (`gtStackSystemBytes`), and whether a machine
// installs a signal stack when it is born (`targetInstallsSignalStack`, read by
// `SchedRuntime.emitSchedWorkerBirth`).
enum FaultFrameHome
	// x64-Windows: the kernel pushes the exception record and the CONTEXT onto the FAULTING stack and runs the
	// vectored handler below them.
	faultingStack

	// A POSIX handler registered with `SA_ONSTACK`: the kernel switches to the thread's alternate signal stack
	// (`SignalStackInstallName`), so the faulting stack receives nothing.
	signalStack
end 'FaultFrameHome'

function gtFaultFrameHome(target Target) returns FaultFrameHome
	return match target.cpu 'cpu'
		x64 gives x64FaultFrameHome(target.os)
		arm64 gives FaultFrameHome.signalStack
		wasm32 panic("gtFaultFrameHome: wasm32-wasi has no green-thread stack — a WASI component takes a wasm trap, not a hardware fault. `targetProvidesFacility` denies `greenThreads` there, and reaching here means a compiler-inserted scheduler op got past that gate")
	end 'cpu'
end 'gtFaultFrameHome'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **DOES THIS PROGRAM INSTALL THE PREEMPTION SIGNAL? — THE ONE SPELLING, AND THREE MECHANISMS READ
// IT.** The handler's registration (`mrt_runtime_init` on each POSIX lane), the alternate stack its frame
// lands on, and whether a runtime-init chunk is laid out at all. It takes `usesGt` because only a program
// that runs green threads has a SENDER for the signal: a lane that stops threads still installs nothing in a
// program with no scheduler, and a predicate that omitted the bit would answer `true` where the chunk
// appender answers `false` — a `bl` to a symbol nothing laid out, which is a link failure rather than a
// diagnostic.
export function programInstallsPreemptSignal(target Target, usesGt bool) returns bool
	return usesGt and targetPreemptDelivery(target) == PreemptDelivery.signal
end 'programInstallsPreemptSignal'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES EVERY THREAD OF THIS PROGRAM OWE ITSELF AN ALTERNATE SIGNAL STACK?** Every POSIX lane registers
// its fault handler `SA_ONSTACK`, and a lane that stops threads by signal registers the preemption handler
// the same way beside it, so the fault handler's answer covers both. Neither frame fits in a green thread's
// few kilobytes.
export function targetInstallsSignalStack(target Target) returns bool
	return gtFaultFrameHome(target) == FaultFrameHome.signalStack
end 'targetInstallsSignalStack'

function x64FaultFrameHome(os Os) returns FaultFrameHome
	return match os 'os'
		windows gives FaultFrameHome.faultingStack
		linux gives FaultFrameHome.signalStack
		macos panic("x64FaultFrameHome: there is no x64-macOS backend — `buildBackend` dispatches macOS to the arm64 backend")
		wasi panic("x64FaultFrameHome: wasm32-wasi is not an x64 target")
	end 'os'
end 'x64FaultFrameHome'

// ⭐⭐ **THE STACK THE OS ITSELF WRITES BELOW A GREEN THREAD's STACK POINTER — Go's `stackSystem`.** Where a
// fault's frame lands on the faulting stack, the room for it is reserved under the guard word, as Go reserves
// it on Windows; everywhere else nothing is.
function gtStackSystemBytes(target Target) returns FullRangeInt
	return match gtFaultFrameHome(target) 'home'
		faultingStack gives GtWindowsExceptionDispatchBytes
		signalStack gives 0
	end 'home'
end 'gtStackSystemBytes'

// ⭐ **The room Windows needs to dispatch a fault on a green thread, sized for the widest host.** The kernel
// builds the exception record, the CONTEXT with its XSAVE area and the dispatcher's frames on the faulting
// stack, and the XSAVE area grows with the host's vector state. A fault can land with only the guard height
// left under the stack pointer, so the sum of those parts at their widest (AVX-512) plus the diagnostic chain
// that rides the margin (`X64Runtime.UnguardedDiagnosticChainBytes`) must fit in the margin plus this reserve,
// and `requireWindowsDispatchFitsItsReserve` refuses a build where it would not; too little room ends the
// process with the fault's own status (0xC0000005) instead of the runtime's report. Go reserves 4096 here,
// which does not hold this sum; 0x1200 holds it with little slack, so widening either part can fail the check.
let WindowsPageBytes = 0x1000 as FullRangeInt
let GtWindowsExceptionDispatchBytes = 0x1200 as FullRangeInt
let WindowsContextRecordBytes = 1232 as FullRangeInt
let WindowsContextExtensionBytes = 256 as FullRangeInt
let WindowsWidestXStateBytes = 2688 as FullRangeInt
let WindowsExceptionRecordBytes = 152 as FullRangeInt
let WindowsDispatcherFramesBytes = 512 as FullRangeInt
let WindowsWidestDispatchBytes = WindowsContextRecordBytes + WindowsContextExtensionBytes + WindowsWidestXStateBytes + WindowsExceptionRecordBytes + WindowsDispatcherFramesBytes

function requireWindowsDispatchFitsItsReserve(target Target)
	if gtFaultFrameHome(target) != FaultFrameHome.faultingStack 'noFrameOnTheStack'
		return
	end 'noFrameOnTheStack'

	let needed = WindowsWidestDispatchBytes + UnguardedDiagnosticChainBytes
	let room = gtStackGuardHeight(target)

	if needed > room 'dispatchOutgrowsGuardHeight'
		panic("requireWindowsDispatchFitsItsReserve: the widest exception dispatch is {WindowsWidestDispatchBytes} bytes and the diagnostic chain under it {UnguardedDiagnosticChainBytes}, but a fault can land with only the guard height ({room} bytes) left under the stack pointer — a fault on a nearly full stack would have no room for the kernel to build its frame and the process would end with the fault's own status. Raise GtWindowsExceptionDispatchBytes")
	end 'dispatchOutgrowsGuardHeight'
end 'requireWindowsDispatchFitsItsReserve'

// How an M's system stack is guarded, decided by where a fault's frame lands (`FaultFrameHome`).
enum SystemStackGuard
	// Windows writes the fault's frame onto the faulting stack, so `PAGE_GUARD` sits above headroom
	// that frame can still be written into.
	pageGuardWithHeadroom

	// The fault is delivered on the signal stack, so a no-access region alone suffices.
	noAccessPage
end 'SystemStackGuard'

function systemStackGuardOf(target Target) returns SystemStackGuard
	return match gtFaultFrameHome(target) 'home'
		faultingStack gives SystemStackGuard.pageGuardWithHeadroom
		signalStack gives SystemStackGuard.noAccessPage
	end 'home'
end 'systemStackGuardOf'

function gtSystemStackFaultingBytes(target Target) returns FullRangeInt
	return match systemStackGuardOf(target) 'guard'
		pageGuardWithHeadroom gives WindowsSystemStackGuardPageBytes
		noAccessPage gives gtSystemStackGuardRegionBytes(target)
	end 'guard'
end 'gtSystemStackFaultingBytes'

export function gtSystemStackGuardRegionBytes(target Target) returns FullRangeInt
	return match target.cpu 'cpu'
		x64 gives x64SystemStackGuardRegionBytes(target.os)
		arm64 gives arm64SystemStackGuardRegionBytes(target.os)
		wasm32 panic("gtSystemStackGuardRegionBytes: wasm32 has no per-machine system stack — `targetProvidesFacility` denies `greenThreads` there, and reaching here means a compiler-inserted scheduler op got past that gate")
	end 'cpu'
end 'gtSystemStackGuardRegionBytes'

function x64SystemStackGuardRegionBytes(os Os) returns FullRangeInt
	return match os 'os'
		windows gives WindowsSystemStackGuardRegionBytes
		linux gives X64LinuxSystemStackGuardRegionBytes
		macos panic("x64SystemStackGuardRegionBytes: there is no x64-macOS backend — `buildBackend` dispatches macOS to the arm64 backend")
		wasi panic("x64SystemStackGuardRegionBytes: wasm32-wasi is not an x64 target")
	end 'os'
end 'x64SystemStackGuardRegionBytes'

function arm64SystemStackGuardRegionBytes(os Os) returns FullRangeInt
	return match os 'os'
		macos gives Arm64MacosSystemStackGuardRegionBytes
		linux gives Arm64LinuxSystemStackGuardRegionBytes
		windows panic("arm64SystemStackGuardRegionBytes: there is no arm64-windows backend")
		wasi panic("arm64SystemStackGuardRegionBytes: wasm32-wasi is not an arm64 target")
	end 'os'
end 'arm64SystemStackGuardRegionBytes'

let WindowsSystemStackHeadroomBytes = (WindowsWidestDispatchBytes + UnguardedDiagnosticChainBytes + WindowsPageBytes - 1) and not (WindowsPageBytes - 1)
let WindowsSystemStackGuardPageBytes = 0x1000 as FullRangeInt
let WindowsSystemStackGuardRegionBytes = WindowsSystemStackHeadroomBytes + WindowsSystemStackGuardPageBytes
let X64LinuxSystemStackGuardRegionBytes = 0x1000 as FullRangeInt
let Arm64MacosSystemStackGuardRegionBytes = 0x4000 as FullRangeInt
let Arm64LinuxSystemStackGuardRegionBytes = 0x10000 as FullRangeInt

// ⭐⭐ **HOW FAR ABOVE A GREEN THREAD's STACK BASE ITS GUARD WORD SITS — Go's `stackGuard`.** Maxon's margin
// for the frames that free-ride between checkpoints, plus the OS's reservation below them. `__gt_spawn` and
// `__gt_stack_relocate` place the guard word here, the grower sizes a new stack so the frame that asked still
// clears it, and a shrink leaves room for it; they must read one height, so it is spelled once.
export function gtStackGuardHeight(target Target) returns FullRangeInt
	return gtStackGuardMargin(target) + gtStackSystemBytes(target)
end 'gtStackGuardHeight'

// The GUARD MARGIN: the cushion a function's prologue projects below its frame before comparing against the
// stack guard, and Maxon's part of the guard word's height (`gtStackGuardHeight`). It appears TWICE in the
// effective trigger (`projectedSp = sp - frameBytes - margin`, guard = `stackBase + margin + stackSystem`), so
// morestack fires while `sp - frameBytes` is still `2*margin` above the OS's reservation — the slack that covers
// the guarded function's pushes plus any UN-guarded runtime (`__`/`mrt_`) frames that free-ride on the caller's
// margin between guard checkpoints. 0x3A0 (928 B) is sized for exactly those frames, and it is Go's amd64
// `stackGuard` less `stackSystem` (`stackNosplit` 800 + `stackSmall` 128).
//
// ⭐ **THE TWO DIAGNOSTIC PATHS ARE NOT LEFT TO THAT SLACK.** A panic can be raised from any depth of
// un-guarded frames, so `mrt_panic` runs the guard itself, projecting one margin for the chain under it
// (`X64Runtime.emitPanicStackGuard`), and grows before it prints. A hardware fault cannot grow the stack its
// dispatcher is standing on, so its room is `gtStackSystemBytes` on Windows and a signal stack elsewhere
// (`FaultFrameHome`).
//
// ⭐⭐ **arm64 KEEPS THE SAME NUMBER, AND THAT IS A RE-DERIVATION RATHER THAN A COPY.** The margin's two
// terms answer differently on the two ISAs and they go OPPOSITE ways, so the sum is re-argued rather than assumed:
//   • **the guarded function's own un-projected stack.** On x64 `push rbp` and every callee-saved push sit
//     OUTSIDE the `sub rsp` the guard projects, so that term is real there. On arm64 the frame RESERVATION
//     already contains the x29/x30 record and every callee-saved slot (`Arm64PrologueEpilogue` opens the whole
//     frame with ONE `sub sp`), so the term is ZERO — this lane needs strictly LESS margin for it.
//   • **un-guarded `__`/`mrt_` frames free-riding between checkpoints.** The same set of runtime functions with
//     comparable frames on both ISAs, so this term is unchanged.
// ⇒ x64's number is an OVER-estimate here, and over-estimating is the safe direction: a larger margin fires the
// grower EARLIER, never later. The one thing arm64 adds is the guard's own saved-x30 cell, and that is added to
// the PROJECTION explicitly (`Arm64GtRuntime.Arm64GuardSaveBytes`) rather than folded into this number, so the
// two ISAs' guards stay readable against each other.
export function gtStackGuardMargin(target Target) returns FullRangeInt
	return match target.cpu 'cpu'
		x64 or
		arm64 gives GtStackGuardMarginBytes
		wasm32 panic("gtStackGuardMargin: wasm32 has no stack guard — a WASI component's call stack is not addressable, so nothing can project a frame against a guard word. `targetProvidesFacility` denies `greenThreads` there, and reaching here means a compiler-inserted scheduler op got past that gate")
	end 'cpu'
end 'gtStackGuardMargin'

let GtStackGuardMarginBytes = 0x3A0 as FullRangeInt

// ⭐⭐⭐ **HOW MUCH ROOM AN ASYNCHRONOUS STOP DEMANDS BELOW THE INTERRUPTED STACK POINTER.** A stop lands at an
// arbitrary instruction rather than at a prologue, so nothing has projected the trampoline's frame against
// the guard word — the safe-point predicate demands the room outright (`SchedRuntime.buildGtPreemptSafe`),
// and the backend PROVES the number covers the trampoline's register block plus everything its park reaches
// (`PreemptionDepth.requireAsyncPreemptionFitsItsMargin`) rather than trusting it.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS LARGER THAN `GtStackGuardMarginBytes` AND MUST BE.** The guard margin covers the frames that
// free-ride between checkpoints; this covers those PLUS a full register file — every general register, the
// flags and all sixteen XMMs — spilled before any of them runs.
//
// ⭐ **A THREAD THIS CLOSE TO ITS BASE IS SIMPLY NOT STOPPED**, which costs nothing: it is inside a guarded
// function, so its next prologue reads the request the cooperative way.
// ⭐⭐⭐ **HOW FAR ABOVE ITS STACK BASE A THREAD MUST BE BEFORE IT MAY BE STOPPED — the async twin of
// `gtStackGuardHeight`, and it has the SAME two terms for the same two reasons.** The trampoline's own cost
// is `gtAsyncPreemptMargin`; under it sits the OS's reservation, which nothing Maxon emits may write into —
// on x64-windows that is where the kernel builds a faulting thread's exception record and CONTEXT, and a
// green thread stopped below it would have no room left to take a fault. The cooperative guard word defends
// that reserve (`gtStackGuardHeight`); this floor is the only other thing that decides where a runtime frame
// may open, so it defends it too.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT BINDING TODAY AND IS STILL THE RIGHT NUMBER.** A preemptible PC is inside a guarded
// function, whose entry already proved `sp >= stackBase + gtStackGuardHeight + margin` — comfortably above
// this. The floor exists for the case that argument stops covering: it is read where the stop is decided,
// not where the guard is.
export function gtAsyncPreemptFloor(target Target) returns FullRangeInt
	return gtAsyncPreemptMargin(target) + gtStackSystemBytes(target)
end 'gtAsyncPreemptFloor'

// What the trampoline and its park actually spend below the interrupted stack pointer — the term
// `PreemptionDepth.requireAsyncPreemptionFitsItsMargin` proves against.
export function gtAsyncPreemptMargin(target Target) returns FullRangeInt
	return match target.cpu 'cpu'
		x64 or
		arm64 gives GtAsyncPreemptMarginBytes
		wasm32 panic("gtAsyncPreemptMargin: wasm32 has no addressable call stack to stop a thread on — `targetProvidesFacility` denies `greenThreads` there, and `targetStopsThreadsForPreemption` denies it twice over")
	end 'cpu'
end 'gtAsyncPreemptMargin'

// 0x400 (1024 B). MEASURED on x64-windows: the trampoline's own frame is 312 bytes and everything
// `__gt_async_preempt_park` reaches adds 272, so 584 of it is spent and the rest is headroom the certificate
// re-proves on every build.
let GtAsyncPreemptMarginBytes = 0x400 as FullRangeInt

// The SYSTEM (scratch) stack a green thread's kernel calls run on: a 64 KB region
// committed once PER M by `emitSchedBecomeM`, its TOP held in `M->systemStackSP`. The syscall shim switches
// RSP here for a Win32 / libSystem call so the kernel's deep stack usage (RPC, TIB probes) never touches the
// GT's own small stack — the infrastructure that makes the tiny GT stacks safe. EXPORTED because the shim
// subtracts it from the system-stack top to compute the TIB StackLimit it publishes for the call.
export let GtSystemStackSize = 0x10000

// The saved-register frame `__gt_context_switch` builds on a GT stack, and the ONE fact the Std
// tier and the Target tier share about a fresh green thread. `__gt_spawn` lays a frame of exactly
// this size with the trampoline's address in the word the first switch-in takes its return address
// from; every other word is zero (`emitGtSeedInitialFrame` clears the frame, because a pooled stack
// arrives holding another thread's words), which is correct because they become this GT's initial
// callee-saved registers and the user function establishes each before reading it.
//
// **x64 — 128 bytes.** The five callee-saved GPRs (rbx, r12-r15 — `calleeSavedOrder`) as pushed words, the ten
// callee-saved XMMs (xmm6-15 — `calleeSavedXmmOrder`) as `movsd` words below them, plus the RETURN-ADDRESS
// word, which sits at the TOP because the switch ends in `ret` and `ret` POPS.
//
// **arm64 — 160 bytes.** Ten `stp` PAIRS: x19..x28, d8..d15, and the x29/x30 frame record. There is no
// return-address WORD at all — AArch64's `ret` branches to x30 — so the trampoline's address rides the frame
// record, which the switch pushes LAST and therefore places at the LOWEST address. That is a STRUCTURAL
// difference in how a fresh frame is seeded rather than merely a different number, and
// `emitGtSeedInitialFrame` below is where the two shapes are written out.
//
// ⚠ It MUST byte-match the context switch's save/restore layout — a mismatch is a wild return on the first
// switch-in — so EACH backend CHECKS this against its OWN callee-saved lists at chunk-build time
// (`X64GtRuntime.assertGtFrameConsistent`, `Arm64GtRuntime.assertArm64GtFrameConsistent`). Add a callee-saved
// register to either file without moving its number and the next async compile panics rather than miscompiling.
export function gtInitialFrameBytes(target Target) returns FullRangeInt
	return match target.cpu 'cpu'
		x64 gives X64GtInitialFrameBytes
		arm64 gives Arm64GtInitialFrameBytes
		wasm32 panic("gtInitialFrameBytes: wasm32 has no context switch — a WASI component's call stack is not addressable, so there is no saved-register frame to lay. `targetProvidesFacility` denies `greenThreads` there, and reaching here means a compiler-inserted scheduler op got past that gate")
	end 'cpu'
end 'gtInitialFrameBytes'

let X64GtInitialFrameBytes = 128 as FullRangeInt  // 5 GPR words + 10 XMM words + the return-address word
let Arm64GtInitialFrameBytes = 160 as FullRangeInt  // 5 GPR pairs + 4 SIMD pairs + the x29/x30 frame record

// The argument buffer holds MaxAsyncArgs i64 slots. The trampoline loads ALL of them into the GPR
// argument registers unconditionally (a callee reads only the slots it declares; the rest are the
// slab's zeros in unread registers), so there is no arg COUNT to carry and the trampoline needs no
// count-guarded loads. MaxAsyncArgs equals the parser's parameter cap.
export let MaxAsyncArgs = 6

// One argument buffer slot per argument, i64-wide. The stride the lowering STORES at, the trampoline LOADS
// at, and `ArgBufBytes` sizes by — one fact, so the three cannot drift.
export let GtArgSlotBytes = 8
let ArgBufBytes = MaxAsyncArgs * GtArgSlotBytes

// One machine word ON THE STACK: what a `push r64` moves rsp by, what a `call`'s pushed return address
// occupies, and the stride of every saved-register word in the hand-laid frames this file's `__gt_spawn`
// lays and `__gt_context_switch`/`__gt_morestack` build (X64GtRuntime).
//
// ⚠ DISTINCT FROM `GtArgSlotBytes` THOUGH BOTH ARE 8. That one is the async ARGUMENT BUFFER's stride — a
// heap object's layout — and it had quietly become the spelling for "a stack word" as well, in four
// unrelated places across two files. Two facts sharing one name means an edit to either silently moves the
// other; naming them apart is what makes changing the argument buffer's width a local change.
export let StackWordBytes = 8

// --- Timer store ---
// The parked-GT deadline store: a MIN-HEAP of (deadline, gt, seq) entries, one per sleeping GT, ordered by
// `(deadline, seq)` ascending. `__gt_timer_add` appends and sifts up, and `__gt_timer_check` pops the root
// while it is due — so a due set fires in DEADLINE ORDER however it was armed — and every walk that moves the
// root publishes its deadline to `__gt_timer_when`, so the peek a schedule or a parking machine takes is one
// load rather than a scan or a lock.
//
// ⛔ **THE FIRING ORDER IS THE HEAP'S ANSWER, NOT ITS CALLER'S.** A parked machine waits on the EARLIEST
// deadline and so usually hands `__gt_timer_check` one due entry, but anything that coalesces a pass — a loaded
// box, a coarse OS timer, a thread that hogs its M — hands it several, and a walk in arming order would then
// wake the LONGEST sleeper first. `async-sleep.md`'s `coalesced-pass-fires-in-deadline-order` pins it.
//
// ⭐ **TIES FIRE IN ARMING ORDER, AND THAT IS WHAT `TimerOffSeq` BUYS.** A binary heap is not stable, so
// keying on the deadline alone would leave two GTs that armed the SAME deadline — two `sleep(50)`s inside
// one clock tick — firing in whatever order the sifts happened to leave them. `__gt_timer_seq` is a monotone
// counter stamped into each entry as it is armed, and the key is the PAIR `(deadline, seq)`, which is total:
// earlier deadline first, and among equal deadlines the one armed first.
//
// The heap region itself is `__slab_alloc`'d once in `__gt_init` (a zeroed block) rather than a `.data`
// reservation, because `.data` entries are single scalar slots.
let TimerHeapCapacity = 256
let TimerEntrySize = 32
let TimerOffDeadline = 0
let TimerOffGt = 8
let TimerOffSeq = 16
let TimerOffTag = 24
let TimerHeapBytes = TimerHeapCapacity * TimerEntrySize

// ⭐⭐ **WHAT THIS DEADLINE IS FOR — the half of an entry's IDENTITY that is not the green thread.** One
// thread may hold a `sleep` and a socket read deadline at the same instant, so a removal keyed on the thread
// alone takes whichever entry it meets first and leaves the other behind to fire at a record its owner has
// moved on from. The key is the PAIR `(gt, tag)`, which is unique: a thread holds at most one wait of each
// kind.
let TimerTagSleep = 0
let TimerTagReadDeadline = 1
let TimerTagWriteDeadline = 2 as FullRangeInt
export let TimerTagSend = 3

// A netpoll park's direction (`NetpollModeRead`/`NetpollModeWrite`) as the tag its deadline entry carries.
// EXPORTED for `NetpollRuntime`, which arms an entry at the park and removes it whenever the wait ends.
export function emitTimerTagOfNetpollMode(module StdModule, block IrBlock, mode ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: mode, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: TimerTagReadDeadline, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitTimerTagOfNetpollMode'

// What `__gt_timer_when` holds while the store is empty: a deadline no clock reading reaches, so *"is the
// earliest deadline past?"* is one compare that answers no. EXPORTED for the scheduler's owed-poller test
// (`SchedRuntime.emitSchedPollerOwed`), which asks whether any timer is pending.
export let TimerWhenEmpty = 0x7FFFFFFFFFFFFFFF as FullRangeInt

// --- Win32 wait / process constants ---
// `WAIT_OBJECT_0` (0) is what `WaitForSingleObject` returns for a SIGNALED handle (an exited child); the
// collect loop polls a handle with a zero timeout and tests for it.
// EXPORTED because the arm64-macOS lane EMULATES these waits over a pthread condition variable
// (`Arm64DarwinRuntime`'s `DarwinWaitObject`), so its chunk has to ANSWER the same two codes the Std tier
// tests for. A second spelling on that side would be one fact written twice, in a place where the two
// copies disagreeing means a poll that never sees a signalled handle.
export let WaitObject0 = 0

// `WAIT_TIMEOUT` — what a wait that ran out of time answers. Windows numbers it 258, and the value is not
// arbitrary on the emulating lane either: it must simply be DISTINCT from `WaitObject0`, which no caller
// could rely on if each side picked its own.
export let WaitTimeout = 258
let WaitPollTimeout = 0  // WaitForSingleObject timeout=0 — poll, never block

// INFINITE — the teardown's join of a worker M and a robbed worker's park at shutdown
// (`SchedRuntime.__sched_stop_workers`, `__sched_syscall_regain`). EXPORTED for those readers:
// `WaitForSingleObject`'s "wait for ever" is one Win32 constant.
export let WaitInfinite = 0xFFFFFFFF

// STARTUPINFOW is 0x68 bytes; its first field `cb` must equal that size. Every other field is 0 (slab-zeroed),
// so `dwFlags = 0` and the child inherits the parent console — `cmd /c exit N` runs and exits with its code.
let StartupInfoSize = 0x68
let StartupInfoCbOffset = 0

// PROCESS_INFORMATION is 0x18 bytes: hProcess@0, hThread@8, dwProcessId@16, dwThreadId@20. The record a spawn is
// handed is three words longer, for `SpawnFailedPreparationOffset`, `ProcInfoTreeObjectOffset` and
// `ProcInfoTreeAssignedOffset`, none of which `CreateProcessW` writes.
let ProcInfoSize = 0x30
export let ProcInfoHProcessOffset = 0
export let ProcInfoHThreadOffset = 8

// `PROCESS_INFORMATION.dwProcessId` — a DWORD after the two HANDLEs, which is why it is read at `u32` width
// and not `i64`: the `dwThreadId` beside it would otherwise ride in the high half of the answer.
export let ProcInfoDwProcessIdOffset = 16

// Where a spawn chunk that failed before launching stores its `SpawnPreparation`: the word after
// PROCESS_INFORMATION. A record the spawn fills is slab-zeroed, so a launch failure leaves `none` here.
export let SpawnFailedPreparationOffset = 0x18

// Where the spawn lowering parks the object that names the child's whole PROCESS TREE — the job object the
// Windows lane creates and assigns the child to. Zero on every lane that has none, which is what
// `emitSubpCloseTreeObject` and the `SubpSlotProcessTree` fill-in below both test.
export let ProcInfoTreeObjectOffset = 0x20

// 1 where the child was assigned to that job, 0 where the assignment failed.
export let ProcInfoTreeAssignedOffset = 0x28

// ⭐ **A LINUX CHILD THAT CANNOT LAUNCH REPORTS IN THE PARENT's OWN RECORD SHAPE.** The launch happens in a
// cloned process, so what failed crosses back down the spawn's status pipe: the child writes these bytes of its
// copy of the record in ONE write, and the parent reads them straight into its own — the `-errno` over
// `dwProcessId`'s word, which the parent takes out before it fills that word, and the `SpawnPreparation` over
// `SpawnFailedPreparationOffset`, where the launch failure already reads it. One write of fewer than
// `PIPE_BUF` bytes arrives whole, so the parent's one read sees all of it or none.
export let SpawnChildReportOffset = ProcInfoDwProcessIdOffset
export let SpawnChildReportBytes = SpawnFailedPreparationOffset + (MachineWordBytes as FullRangeInt) - SpawnChildReportOffset

// The last-error word carries the OS code in its low 32 bits and the `SpawnPreparation` above them, so the code
// a message renders is unchanged by the step it came from.
let SpawnPreparationShift = 32

// The 8-byte slab slot `GetExitCodeProcess` writes the child's DWORD exit code into; the runtime reads it back
// with a full i64 load, so the high dword must be clean. `GetExitCodeProcess` writes only the low DWORD, so the
// slot must arrive with its high half already zero — which a fresh `__slab_alloc` slot does. A slot REUSED
// across calls would need an explicit `store 0` in `reap` to re-establish that; the per-call slot does not.
let ExitCodeSlotBytes = 8
export let TrailingNulBytes = 1  // the one extra byte for the cmdline's NUL terminator (slab-zeroed → free)

// ⭐ **`runProcess` IS A THROWING BUILTIN — its USER-REACHABLE failure path THROWS, it does not
// ABORT.** A command the user typed is not a compiler invariant, so the failure hands the caller a
// `RunProcessError` through the dual-register `errorReturn` ABI, exactly as a throwing array accessor hands
// back an `ArrayError`: `try runProcess(cmd) otherwise <h>` catches it and the program RECOVERS.
//
//   * `RunProcessError.spawnFailed` — `__gt_process_run` when `CreateProcessW` FAILS (a command whose first token
//     is not a runnable executable). On failure CreateProcessW leaves the PROCESS_INFORMATION untouched, and
//     the call's is a freshly zeroed slot, so `hProcess == 0` detects it — and the runtime MUST NOT park on a
//     null handle, which nothing could ever make ready, leaving the thread parked for ever.
//
// ⭐⭐ **HOW MANY CHILDREN MAY BE PARKED AT ONCE IS NOT A QUESTION THIS ERROR ANSWERS.** A parked child is a
// SOURCE ON THE POLLER (`NetpollRuntime`'s child band), so the ceiling is the process's own descriptor limit
// and the host reports it by refusing to mint the source — `netpollFailed`, the same stop a socket the poller
// will not take gets, not a case of this error.
//
// `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon` declares `RunProcessError`, so an `otherwise (e)` binding is typed from it
// (`runtimeThrowsClause`) and `verifyRunProcessErrorOrdinals` holds the ordinal below to that declaration.
//
// ⚠ **THE NAME IS `"RunProcessError"`, DELIBERATELY *NOT* `"SubprocessError"` — DO NOT "FIX" IT BACK.**
// `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon` declares `public union SubprocessError implements Error`, a BOXED,
// String-carrying union with a DIFFERENT case set (`executableNotFound`/`spawnFailed`/`ioFailed`/`timeout`/
// `inputTooLarge`/`endOfStream`), which `Subprocess.run` throws. This error is a SCALAR with its
// OWN ordinal; sharing the union's name would be the project's signature ONE-NAME-TWICE bug AND a
// wild-free trap — the day an `otherwise (e)` binding resolved a builtin's throws-label
// `"SubprocessError"` to that boxed union it would bind the R10 flag (1/2) as a heap pointer and
// free `0x1`/`0x2`. A distinct name that names NO declared union makes that unrepresentable:
// `throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion("RunProcessError")` is false, so an `otherwise (e)` decode lands on the
// scalar ordinals below (correct), never a box.
let RunProcessErrorOrdinalInstaller = b"the green-thread runtime (RunProcessError)"
let RunProcessSpawnFailedCaseName = b"spawnFailed"

// The `RunProcessError` case ORDINAL — it rides R10 as `ordinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias`, the SAME scalar-error
// encoding a source `throw E.case` uses (`LowerMaxonToStd.lowerThrowError`), so the `otherwise (e)` decode
// (`e = flag - bias`, `parseOtherwiseBlock`) needs no third copy of the bias. `0` is reserved by the bias for
// "no error in flight".
let RunProcessSpawnFailedOrdinal = 0 as FullRangeInt

// The R10 error-flag VALUE the throw emits and a successful `runProcess` clears — the ordinal biased by the
// one `ErrorFlagOrdinalBias` (never a second literal, so the runtime encode cannot drift from the parser decode),
// and the shared `MaxonDialect.NoErrorFlag` alongside a real exit code — the success value the bias reserves,
// read from its one declaration rather than re-spelled `0` here.
let RunProcessSpawnFailedFlag = RunProcessSpawnFailedOrdinal + ErrorFlagOrdinalBias

function runProcessErrorThrows() returns ThrowsClause
	return ThrowsClause.throwing(String.from(RunProcessErrorTypeName))
end 'runProcessErrorThrows'

export function verifyRunProcessErrorOrdinals(project Project)
	match declaredBuiltinErrorEnum(project, typeName: RunProcessErrorTypeName, installer: RunProcessErrorOrdinalInstaller) 'declaration'
		notDeclared then return
		found(declaration) then claimRunProcessErrorOrdinals(project, declaration: declaration)
	end 'declaration'
end 'verifyRunProcessErrorOrdinals'

function claimRunProcessErrorOrdinals(project Project, declaration EnumLayout)
	var errors = BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck.overDeclared(project, declaration: declaration, installer: RunProcessErrorOrdinalInstaller)
	errors.claim(RunProcessSpawnFailedCaseName, ordinal: RunProcessSpawnFailedOrdinal)
	errors.requireTotal()
end 'claimRunProcessErrorOrdinals'

// --- Monotonic clock ---
// `ns = ticks * 1e9 / freq`, evaluated as the OVERFLOW-SAFE split identity
// `ns = (ticks/freq)*1e9 + ((ticks mod freq)*1e9)/freq` — see `buildGtNowNs`.
let NanosPerSecond = 1000000000

// EXPORTED for `NetpollRuntime`, which turns a deadline in milliseconds into the absolute nanoseconds the
// timer store keys on.
export let NanosPerMilli = 1000000

// Where `emitGtDeadlineAfterMs` saturates. A send whose deadline is this waits with no timer armed,
// which `ForeverMs` and every timeout too long to reach it share.
export let GtDeadlineMaxNs = i64.max - NanosPerMilli

let GtDeadlineMaxMs = GtDeadlineMaxNs / NanosPerMilli

// --- Runtime function names ---
export let GtContextSwitchName = b"__gt_context_switch"
export let GtTrampolineName = b"__gt_trampoline"

// The relocating stack-grower. A hand-assembled chunk on each lane, NOT a builder-built Std function — it switches
// stacks and walks the frame-pointer chain, which Std cannot express. EXPORTED so every caller and the chunk spell
// it once.
export let GtMorestackName = b"__gt_morestack"

// ⭐ **WHERE A FUNCTION-ENTRY GUARD's COLD PATH GOES**: it honours a preemption request (`GtStackGuardPreempt`)
// on the thread's own stack and returns to the guard, and otherwise jumps to `__gt_morestack`. A hand-assembled
// chunk on each lane, split from the grower because `mrt_panic`'s guard must reach the grower without it
// (`GtGuardSite`).
export let GtGuardTrippedName = b"__gt_guard_tripped"

// ⭐⭐ **WHERE A PROLOGUE STACK GUARD RUNS, WHICH DECIDES WHETHER ITS COLD PATH MAY PARK.**
export enum GtGuardSite
	// A guarded function's entry. The function is user-shaped code, which the runtime never enters while it holds
	// `__sched_lock`, so the guard may honour a preemption request.
	functionEntry

	// `mrt_panic`, which runtime code reaches too — code that may hold `__sched_lock`, where a park would never
	// end. Its guard withdraws a request before its compare and goes to the grower, never to a park.
	mrtPanic

	// A guarded runtime body (`IrFunction.pinsItsThread`), which runtime code may reach while it holds
	// a lock: its guard behaves as `mrtPanic`'s does, and the body is never stopped asynchronously.
	runtimeEntry
end 'GtGuardSite'

export function guardSiteMayPark(site GtGuardSite) returns bool
	return match site 'site'
		functionEntry gives true
		mrtPanic or
		runtimeEntry gives false
	end 'site'
end 'guardSiteMayPark'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_stack_relocate(gt, newBase, newSize)` — MOVE A STOPPED THREAD's STACK, IN EITHER DIRECTION.** The
// one routine a grow and a shrink share: it copies the live region `[gt.sp, top)` to the top of the new stack,
// rebases `gt.sp`, `gt.fp` and every saved frame-pointer link into the old stack, republishes the stack's bounds
// on the GT and frees the old pages. A hand-assembled chunk on both ISAs, for `__gt_morestack`'s reason; EXPORTED
// because `__gt_morestack` calls it by name and so does `__gt_stack_shrink` (Std).
export let GtStackRelocateName = b"__gt_stack_relocate"

export let GtStackAllocName = b"__gt_stack_alloc"
export let GtStackFreeName = b"__gt_stack_free"
export let GtSystemStackAllocName = b"__gt_system_stack_alloc"

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_stack_shrink(gt)` — Go's `shrinkstack`, at a park.** See `buildGtStackShrink`.
let GtStackShrinkName = b"__gt_stack_shrink"

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_preempt(gt)` — HONOUR A PREEMPTION REQUEST: Go's `newstack` preempt arm and `gopreempt_m`.** See
// `buildGtPreempt`. Called by every spawn (`emitGtHonourPreemptRequest`) and by `__gt_guard_tripped`'s preempt
// arm, a hand-assembled chunk on both ISAs, so EXPORTED
// for the root that keeps it alive (`DeadFunctionElimination.seedRoots`), for both arms and for the backends,
// which certify the depth of its call tree against the guard margin.
export let GtPreemptName = b"__gt_preempt"

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_seed_adapt()` — Go's `gcComputeStartingStackSize`, once a window.** See `buildGtSeedAdapt`.
let GtSeedAdaptName = b"__gt_seed_adapt"
let GtInitName = b"__gt_init"
export let GtSpawnName = b"__gt_spawn"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_spawn_green(funcPtr) -> gt` — CREATE A GREEN THREAD RATHER THAN A COROUTINE.** The
// SAME body as `__gt_spawn` (`buildGtSpawn` builds both from it) differing in the owner stamp: a coroutine
// copies its spawner's owner, a green thread is stamped with ITSELF and heads a strand of its own.
//
// ⭐ EXPORTED for `MailboxRuntime.buildSvcSpawn`, which calls it by name.
export let GtSpawnGreenName = b"__gt_spawn_green"

// `__gt_spawn_main(entry) -> gt` — `main`'s own green thread (`GtSpawnKind.mainThread`). Its one caller is
// `__gt_run_main`.
let GtSpawnMainName = b"__gt_spawn_main"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_run_main(entry) -> ExitCode` — RUN `main` AS A GREEN THREAD.** Go's `rt0_go` →
// `newproc(runtime.main)` → `mstart` → `schedule()`. The entry stub calls it where a program without green
// threads calls `main`, handing it the address of the main thread's entry
// (`BacktraceFormat.MainThreadEntryName`), which runs `__module_init`, `main` and the global cleanup on the new
// thread's stack and returns `main`'s ExitCode for the trampoline to capture.
//
// ⭐ `main` is an ordinary green thread: it parks, is readied and may run on any machine. The main machine
// runs the scheduler loop (`SchedRuntime.SchedMainLoopName`) until `main` has completed, then takes its
// ExitCode back to the entry stub.
//
// EXPORTED for its two cross-file readers: the entry stubs emit the call and `DeadFunctionElimination` roots
// the name for it.
export let GtRunMainName = b"__gt_run_main"

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_ready(gt)` — PUBLISH A SPAWNED GT, AND IT IS A SEPARATE CALL SO THAT NOTHING CAN OBSERVE THE
// GT UNTIL EVERY SLOT THE TRAMPOLINE LOADS IS WRITTEN.** Publishing inside `__gt_spawn` lets a worker M run
// the thread while the spawner is still storing its arguments, which reads as arguments silently lost.
export let GtReadyName = b"__gt_ready"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_ready_locked(gt) -> wakeDebt` — THE WAKE DOOR, Go's `ready`.** It moves `gt` from
// `waiting` to `ready` by CAS — a lost CAS is a second ready of one wait, which would queue the
// thread twice, so it is fatal (`RuntimeFatal.badGreenThreadStatusInReady`) — and, if no machine
// owns the strand, claims it and publishes the owner's token to this processor's ring (the global
// queue on an OS thread with none). Otherwise `gt` goes onto its strand queue under the owner's
// record lock — the FRONT for a strand's owner, which a strand runs first, the back for a
// coroutine. It answers `WakeDebtOwed` exactly when it published a token: a caller holding a lock
// then owes `__sched_wake_or_spawn` AFTER its release (`SchedRuntime.emitSchedWakeIfOwed`), because
// a wake inside a critical section has no StoreLoad fence and a nested leave of a recursive lock
// releases nothing. A mailbox-parked service comes here only when its strand is held, and is
// otherwise readied with no lock (`emitGtReadyParkedService`).
//
// EXPORTED for its callers in `MailboxRuntime` and for the root that keeps it alive for the hand-assembled
// chunks that name it (`DeadFunctionElimination.seedRoots`).
export let GtReadyLockedName = b"__gt_ready_locked"

// `__gt_ready_runnable(gt) -> wakeDebt` — the same enqueue for a thread never marked `waiting`: a
// fresh coroutine's publish, `main`'s start, and a park that finds its wait already over before it
// marked anything. It requires `ready`, and anything else is fatal.
let GtReadyRunnableName = b"__gt_ready_runnable"

// What the two ready doors answer. Named because every waker compares against them and a bare
// literal at each end would be one fact written twice.
let WakeDebtOwed = 1
export let WakeDebtNone = 0 as FullRangeInt

// ⭐⭐ **THE GLOBAL FIFO's TWO DOORS**, linked through `GtOffRunqNext`. The global queue holds owner tokens: a
// full ring's overflow, a publish by an OS thread with no processor, and a yielding strand. Both require the
// caller to hold `__sched_lock`.
export let GtEnqueueName = b"__gt_enqueue"

// EXPORTED because `SchedRuntime` pops the global run queue — `__sched_find_runnable`'s fairness and batch
// arms.
export let GtDequeueName = b"__gt_dequeue"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE STRAND QUEUE's DOORS — the per-green-thread counterparts of the global FIFO's.** They
// differ from their global twins only in where the ends live (the OWNER's struct) and which link
// they thread (`GtOffNext`). `__gt_coro_enqueue(gt)` appends `gt` to `gt.owner`'s strand queue and
// `__gt_coro_dequeue(owner)` pops its head; both require the CALLER to hold the owner's record lock
// (`GtOffRecordLock`).
//
// `__gt_strand_next(owner, parked)` is the strand runner's CONSUME door — the parked member's
// registration, pop and tombstone refusal — and it is where the strand is given up when its queue
// is empty. `__gt_coro_sweep_dropped(owner)` reclaims the tombstones at the queue's front.
let GtCoroEnqueueName = b"__gt_coro_enqueue"
let GtCoroDequeueName = b"__gt_coro_dequeue"
let GtStrandNextName = b"__gt_strand_next"
let GtCoroSweepDroppedName = b"__gt_coro_sweep_dropped"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_run_strand(m, owner)` — RUN A STRAND ON THIS MACHINE UNTIL IT HAS NOTHING RUNNABLE, OR HANDS
// ITSELF TO THE GLOBAL QUEUE.** Go's `execute` plus `park_m`/`goexit0`/`goyield_m`, looped over the strand
// queue: switch into the next member, and on the way back dispatch on what it did — completed (the runner's
// half of its teardown), parked (commit a mailbox park, or register any other on its store), or yielded or
// preempted (place it). Called by the scheduler loop (`SchedRuntime.buildSchedLoop`) on the machine that
// popped `owner`'s token, which owns the strand for the whole call.
export let GtRunStrandName = b"__gt_run_strand"

export let GtAwaitName = b"__gt_await"
export let GtTryAwaitName = b"__gt_try_await"

// ⭐ **THE UN-AWAITED-PROMISE DROP.** `__gt_promise_drop(gt)` reclaims + CANCELS a GT whose
// promise reached scope exit (or a re-arm) WITHOUT being awaited — the ownership DUAL of the `__gt_await`
// reclaim. The front-end enrols an `async` spawn's Promise as an owned value and emits this call on every path
// that neither `await`s nor re-arms it. Named in the `__gt_` band so `isGtRuntimeCallee` catches it and
// `TargetPrinter.isRuntimeFunction` keeps it out of the rendered Target IR.
export let GtPromiseDropName = b"__gt_promise_drop"

// `__gt_promise_field_release(box, offset)` — drop whatever thread a promise FIELD still holds, before a
// store puts another one there. The load is NULL-GUARDED because `__gt_promise_drop` is not: a field a
// previous consume emptied holds 0, which is the re-armed case and must drop nothing.
export let GtPromiseFieldReleaseName = b"__gt_promise_field_release"

// `__gt_promise_field_vacate(box, offset, read)` — the struct-field twin of `__managed_vacate`, sharing its
// occupant check (`ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitPromiseSlotVacateTail`) so a field consumed twice aborts exactly
// as an array slot does.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH LIVE IN THIS FAMILY FOR THEIR PREFIX.** `__gt_` enrols them on `usesGt`, which every program
// holding a promise already sets; under a `__managed_` name they would set `usesManagedMemory` and drag the
// whole array runtime into a program whose only array-ness is one promise field (`__drop_type_param` is the
// precedent).
export let GtPromiseFieldVacateName = b"__gt_promise_field_vacate"

// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE ENTRY POINTS OF THE TEARDOWN RENDEZVOUS — see `GtOffTeardown` for the protocol they
// implement between them.**
//
// **`__gt_runner_done(gt, heldStrand)` — THE RUNNER's half: this party is finished with the thread's
// EXECUTION.** It readies the thread awaiting `gt`, frees a stack that is not the seed's size and adds the
// runner ticket; if the consumer was already there it performs the teardown. Its callers are the strand
// runner when a switch comes back from a `completed` thread, the parties that take a renounced thread out of
// a queue rather than running it (`__gt_strand_next`, `__gt_coro_sweep_dropped`, `__sched_find_runnable`,
// `__sched_sweep_dropped`), and `__gt_cell_complete`. The two in `SchedRuntime` reach it through
// `emitGtTombstoneHandback` and `emitGtFifoSweepDropped`.
//
// ⛔⛔⛔ **IT IS NEVER CALLED WITH `__sched_lock` HELD.** `__sched_lock` is RECURSIVE: a nested leave
// decrements the depth and RELEASES NOTHING, silently. This call takes the lock itself to hand the
// awaiter over, and it reaches `__gt_reclaim` and through it the per-callee RELEASER, which can
// reach `__gt_promise_drop`, whose arms take and release the lock themselves. Under an outer lock a
// release there is a no-op, and the party it was released for blocks on its acquire for ever.
//
// **`__gt_owner_done(gt)` — THE CONSUMER's half: the promise is finished with.** It gives back the thread's
// slot in `MOffGtLive` and adds the consumer ticket; if the runner was already there it performs the
// teardown. Its callers are `__gt_await`/`__gt_try_await` (after the result has been taken and the
// arguments released), `__gt_promise_drop` (on every arm), `__gt_run_main` and a service's exit.
//
// **`__gt_reclaim(gt)` — the SECOND party's half, whichever of the two that turns out to be.** The releaser
// call and the struct free, in that order and once.
let GtRunnerDoneName = b"__gt_runner_done"

let GtNoHeldStrand = 0

// ⭐ EXPORTED: a SERVICE has no promise, so its green thread is its own consumer and calls this itself at the
// end of its loop (`MailboxRuntime.buildMboxServiceExit`, which carries why the ticket may not be added at the
// spawn instead).
export let GtOwnerDoneName = b"__gt_owner_done"
let GtReclaimName = b"__gt_reclaim"

// ⭐⭐ **THE NON-BLOCKING PEEK.** `__gt_is_complete(gt) -> 1 if that green thread has reached
// `completed`, 0 otherwise` — the floor of `__Builtins.gtIsComplete(promise.inner)`, and the ONE thing an
// N-slot dispatcher needs that `await` cannot give it: `await` waits for ONE named promise, so serving slot 0
// while slot 3 has already answered is head-of-line blocking.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS A QUESTION AND NOT A STEP.** It advances nothing and retires nothing — which is what lets
// `Testing/SpecWorkerPool`'s `findReadyDrain` (which SERVES on the answer) and `abortOnWedgedPool` (which
// ACCUSES on it) ask it about the same slot and get the same answer.
//
// ⚠ **AND IT IS TARGET-NEUTRAL, so it deliberately names no `HostFacility` in `calleeHostFacility`.** A
// null test, a load, a compare and a return; what restricts a peeking program elsewhere is its THUNK, and
// `specs/promise-peek.md:rejected-on-wasm` pins that attribution.
export let GtIsCompleteName = b"__gt_is_complete"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE REPLY CELL — A GT THAT NEVER RUNS.** `__gt_cell_alloc(releaser) -> cell` takes a cell, the
// record's first `GtCellBytes`, through `__gt_cell_get` and leaves it **UNPUBLISHED**: `stackBase = 0`, `funcPtr = 0`, `status = waiting`,
// on no queue and in no store. It is a wait token plus a result slot, and it is a `Promise` to everything
// above it — `specs/services.md` states a reply is an ordinary `Promise` — so `__gt_await`,
// `__gt_try_await`, `__gt_is_complete` and E3100's linearity treat it as any other promise.
//
// ⭐ **`funcPtr == 0` IS THE DISCRIMINATOR (`emitGtIsReplyCell`), AND IT IS A PREDICATE RATHER THAN A KIND
// WORD**: `__gt_spawn` stores the callee's address into that word before it publishes anything, so *"nothing
// will ever run this GT"* and *"this word is zero"* are one fact. A machine's scheduler context also carries
// `funcPtr == 0`, and no promise ever names one.
export let GtCellAllocName = b"__gt_cell_alloc"

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_cell_complete(cell, result, threw)` — THE REPLIER's HALF.** Called from the SERVICE's
// green thread (or from `__mbox_send`'s closed arm, on the sender's), it writes the two answer words and then
// publishes `completed` — **in the trampoline's own order**, because an awaiter tests `status` and only then
// loads `result`/`threw`.
//
// It then supplies the RUNNER's half of the teardown rendezvous (`__gt_runner_done`, which readies the
// awaiter and whose stack free `stackBase == 0` skips). **Nothing may read the cell after that call**: if the
// awaiter had already renounced its promise, the runner is the second arrival and the struct is on a free
// list.
export let GtCellCompleteName = b"__gt_cell_complete"

// ⭐⭐ **A REPLY HELD AT AN INTERFACE TYPE IS TAKEN IN TWO CALLS, AND THE CELL OUTLIVES THE FIRST.**
// `__gt_try_await_holding(cell)` is `__gt_try_await` — the wait, its trace and scheduler event, the value and
// the error word — without the consumer's arrival at the teardown rendezvous. `__gt_reply_witness(cell) ->
// witness` then reads `GtOffReplyWitness` and arrives. Nothing but the consumer's arrival can free a completed
// cell, so the read between the two calls is sound, and the wait happens once, where the trace reports it.
export let GtTryAwaitHoldingName = b"__gt_try_await_holding"
export let GtReplyWitnessName = b"__gt_reply_witness"

// Where an await's consumer arrives at the teardown rendezvous — see `GtTryAwaitHoldingName`.
enum GtAwaitArrival
	inTheAwait
	inReplyWitness
end 'GtAwaitArrival'

// `__gt_sleep` is the front-end target for `sleep(ms)`.
export let GtSleepName = b"__gt_sleep"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_park(kind, arg)` — Go's `gopark`.** Write the park note on the running GT and switch to the
// scheduler context that switched it in. The strand runner registers the park on the far side of the switch,
// which is what keeps a parked thread undiscoverable until its registers are saved (`GtOffParkKind`).
let GtParkName = b"__gt_park"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_await_any(record) -> index` — the front-end target for `__Builtins.awaitAny(promises)`.**
// `record` is the argument array's `__ManagedMemory` record (an `Array with Promise with …` IS its record);
// the answer is the INDEX of the first element found `completed`.
//
// ⭐ **IT RETIRES NOTHING, WHICH IS WHY IT IS A FUNCTION AND NOT A `select` STATEMENT.** No promise is
// consumed, no GT is reclaimed and `MOffGtLive` does not move — the caller then `await`s exactly one
// promise under the ordinary rules.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A WAIT REGISTERS ON EVERY SLOT, Go's `selectgo`.** With nothing complete the caller parks, its
// registration stores it as the tagged awaiter of every slot (`AwaitAnyTag`) and the first completer readies it
// once (`GtOffSelectDone`); on the way out it takes itself off every slot, so a slot is left with no awaiter
// for a later `await` to collide with.
export let GtAwaitAnyName = b"__gt_await_any"

// `__gt_any_completed(record) -> index` — the scan `__gt_await_any` and its registration both answer from.
// See `buildGtAnyCompleted` for why it answers `NoCompletedPromise` rather than 0 when nothing has finished.
let GtAnyCompletedName = b"__gt_any_completed"

let GtAnyLockName = b"__gt_any_lock"
let GtAnyUnlockName = b"__gt_any_unlock"

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_drain_at_exit(code) -> code` — RUN THE SCHEDULER OUT AND JOIN THE WORKER Ms, ON THE WAY TO
// `ExitProcess`.** The entry stub calls it between `__gt_run_main` and the leak gate, in the exit-code
// pass-through shape `__mm_leak_check` has.
//
// ⭐ **THE JOIN IS NOT OPTIONAL.** A worker M's `__gt_reclaim` runs releaser decrefs, which step the very
// counter `__mm_leak_check` is about to read; without the join those two race, and the failure is an
// intermittent exit 101 on a program with no leak. It waits on the workers' thread handles
// (`SchedRuntime.MOffOsThreadHandle`), walking `__sched_allm`, because a parked machine is on no processor.
//
// EXPORTED for its two cross-file readers: `Targets/BackendDispatch` emits the stub's call, and
// `DeadFunctionElimination` roots the name for it.
export let GtDrainAtExitName = b"__gt_drain_at_exit"

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_resched()` — THE COOPERATIVE YIELD BEHIND `Scheduler.yield()`** (`stdlib/Scheduler.maxon`, spec
// `specs/scheduler-yield.md`). A park of kind `GtParkKind.yielded`: the strand runner places the yielder behind
// whatever its strand has runnable, or — with nothing — puts the strand at the global queue's tail, Go's
// `Gosched` (`sched-runqueue.a-yield-goes-behind-the-global-queue`). Either way a spin-wait makes progress:
// the runner fires due timers and reaps exited children as it places a yielder.
//
// ⭐ **IT TAKES NO TIMER SLOT, WHICH IS THE WHOLE POINT VERSUS `sleep(0)`**: N green threads each yielding once
// occupy no store at all.
//
// EXPORTED because the front end targets it directly — `Parser.emitYield` lowers `__Builtins.yield()` to a
// call on this name, exactly as `emitSleep` targets `GtSleepName`.
export let GtReschedName = b"__gt_resched"

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_io_park()` — THE FILESYSTEM YIELD POINT.** Every `__mf_*`/`__md_*` runtime function that makes a
// kernel filesystem call opens with a call to this, so a green thread that touches the disk genuinely SUSPENDS
// there and other green threads run. It is what makes `async` over `File.exists` mean something, and what lets
// E3073's yield roster (`SemanticCheck.ioYieldingRuntimeCallees`) describe real behaviour rather than a model.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT IS A COOPERATIVE YIELD AROUND THE CALL, NOT OVERLAPPED I/O, AND THAT IS DELIBERATE.** Real overlap
// means submitting the file work to an IOCP completion port plus a pool of sync-fallback OS threads
// (`__io_submit_read`/`__io_submit_sync`), so the syscall itself runs on ANOTHER thread while the green
// thread is parked. There is no such pool here: the yield suspends the green thread at the I/O point, lets
// the scheduler run
// everything else that is ready, and performs the (synchronous) kernel call once the GT is resumed. So the
// CONCURRENCY the trace and E3073 promise is real — the green thread is off the CPU and others progress — but
// the kernel call is not overlapped with them. Stated plainly because the trace pair (`io_yield [file_exists]`
// … `io_resume [file_exists]`) must not be read as a claim that the syscall executed between those two lines.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE SUFFIX NAMES THE OPERATION, AND IT IS THE CALL SITE THAT SAYS WHICH — see `GtIoOperation`.** In a
// TRACED build `__gt_io_park` takes the suffix as a `(ptr, len)` pair and both its trace lines write whatever
// the site handed in, so a socket connect reports `[net_connect]` while a `File.exists` reports
// `[file_exists]` on the same green thread's timeline. The pair is what `specs/async-tcp.md`'s
// `trace-mixed-io` case asserts, and it is why the tag may not be a per-thread or per-family word: those two
// parks happen inside ONE green thread, one after the other.
//
// ⚠ **IN AN UNTRACED BUILD THE FUNCTION TAKES NO ARGUMENT AT ALL**, exactly as it did before the suffix
// existed — the arity follows `asyncTrace`, so a normal program's park sites emit one argument-less call and
// its image is byte-identical. That is the same compile-time gating the six trace SITES already take (see
// `GtTraceEmitName`), applied to a signature rather than to a call.
//
// ⭐ **WHY THE TIMER PARK AND NOT `__gt_resched`.** They are different promises. `Scheduler.yield()` is "let
// someone else have a turn IF anyone wants one", so it comes straight back when nothing else is runnable. An
// I/O yield says "this thread is now WAITING", which must hold whether or not another GT happens to be ready —
// so it parks on a deadline of `now` and comes back through the timer store, a genuine suspension every time.
let GtIoParkName = b"__gt_io_park"

// ⭐⭐ **THE ASYNC TRACE (`--async-trace`).** `__gt_trace_emit(tagRecord, value, suffixRecord)` prints one line
// — `<tag><value><suffix?>\n` — to stderr, and the six sites that call it (`spawn #`, `io_yield #`,
// `io_resume #`, `sleep_yield #`, `sleep_resume #`, `await #`) are what `specs/async-await.md`'s `trace-yield`
// and `specs/http-client.md`'s `async-trace-interleave` cases pin between them.
//
// ⭐ **THE TRACE CALLS ARE GATED AT COMPILE TIME, NOT BY A RUNTIME FLAG WORD.** The calls are simply not
// EMITTED unless the build asked for them, so there is no enabled-flag global and no per-spawn branch, and
// an untraced program carries none of it. (The struct FIELD is not gated — see `GtOffTraceId`.)
//
// ⚠ **A YIELD IS NOT TRACED HERE.** `Scheduler.yield()` / `__gt_resched` prints no line, because no spec pins
// a yield tag and a seventh spelling beside the six that ARE pinned would be an untested one. A yield storm
// is visible to `maxon monitor --filter=sched` instead (`emitSchedEventOf`).
let GtTraceEmitName = b"__gt_trace_emit"

// `(tagPtr, tagLen, value, suffixPtr, suffixLen)` — the tag and suffix travel as pointer+length pairs because
// they are raw `.rdata` bytes rather than String records (see `GtTraceTag`).
let GtTraceEmitArity = 5

// `__gt_io_park`'s arity IN A TRACED BUILD: the operation suffix, as the same `(ptr, len)` pair `.rdata` text
// travels in everywhere else here. An UNTRACED park takes zero — see `buildGtIoPark`.
let IoParkSuffixArity = 2 as ParamCount

// The tag TEXTS, spelled once. Each becomes an immortal `.rdata` String record whose label the emitting sites
// carry (`GtTraceTags`). They are the wire format the spec compares against, so a change here is a change to
// what `trace-yield` asserts.
let GtTraceSpawnText = b"spawn #"
let GtTraceAwaitText = b"await #"

// ⭐ **`try await` HAS ITS OWN TAG, BECAUSE IT IS ITS OWN ENTRY POINT.** `__gt_await` and `__gt_try_await` are
// two entry points (`buildGtAwait`'s `throwing` selects between them) printing two spellings, and both
// `specs/async-tcp.md`'s trace cases pin `try_await #` against `async-await.md`'s `await #`.
// One tag for both would make the trace unable to say which of the two a program took.
let GtTraceTryAwaitText = b"try_await #"

let GtTraceIoYieldText = b"io_yield #"
let GtTraceIoResumeText = b"io_resume #"

// ⭐⭐ **`sleep(ms)` HAS ITS OWN PAIR, AND IT IS NOT THE I/O PAIR.** A timer park and an I/O park take the same
// exit (`emitGtParkOnDeadline`), so spelling both `io_yield` would make a trace unable to say whether a green
// thread is waiting on the clock or on the disk. `specs/http-client.md`'s `async-trace-interleave` pins both
// spellings to the byte: `main`'s own `sleep_yield #0` among the `[net_*]` pairs of the task it spawned.
//
// ⚠ **NO SUFFIX, WHERE AN I/O PARK HAS ONE.** `[net_connect]` answers "which operation" — a question a
// sleep does not have, because a sleep IS the operation. So the sleep lines take `GtTraceSuffix.none`, as
// `spawn #` does.
let GtTraceSleepYieldText = b"sleep_yield #"
let GtTraceSleepResumeText = b"sleep_resume #"
let GtTraceYieldSuffixText = b" [yield]"
let GtTraceImmediateSuffixText = b" [immediate]"
let GtTraceNewlineText = b"
"

// The I/O-operation suffixes, beside the rest of the wire format for that block's reason. Which one a park
// prints is `GtIoOperation`'s question; these are the bytes it answers with, one per case.
let GtTraceFileExistsSuffixText = b" [file_exists]"
let GtTraceNetConnectSuffixText = b" [net_connect]"
let GtTraceNetListenSuffixText = b" [net_listen]"
let GtTraceNetAcceptSuffixText = b" [net_accept]"
let GtTraceNetSendSuffixText = b" [net_send]"
let GtTraceNetRecvSuffixText = b" [net_recv]"
let GtTraceNetCloseSuffixText = b" [net_close]"

// ⭐⭐ **THE I/O OPERATION A PARK SITE IS PARKED IN — the suffix `__gt_io_park` prints on both of its trace
// lines.** It is a property of the CALL SITE and of nothing else: `trace-mixed-io` runs a `File.exists` and a
// `TcpClient.connect` inside ONE green thread and asserts the two suffixes differ across that single
// timeline, so a per-thread, per-runtime-install or per-callee-family tag cannot express it.
//
// ⚠ **THE WHOLE FILESYSTEM BAND SHARES `fileExists`, AND THAT IS THE PINNED SPELLING RATHER THAN AN
// OVERSIGHT.** All fourteen `__mf_*`/`__md_*` park sites name this case, so a `File.write` still traces
// `[file_exists]` — every committed expectation that mentions a filesystem park (`async-await.md`'s
// `trace-yield`, `async-tcp.md`'s `trace-mixed-io`) spells it that way, and giving `__mf_write` a
// `[file_write]` of its own would be minting a spelling no spec has. Splitting the band is a spec change; the
// MECHANISM here is already per-site, so it is one word per site when a spec asks for it.
export enum GtIoOperation
	fileExists
	netConnect
	netListen
	netAccept
	netSend
	netRecv
	netClose
end 'GtIoOperation'

// The suffix text each operation prints. The ONE place an operation is spelled — `registerIoOperationTags`
// reads it for every case, so a new case cannot be added without one.
function ioOperationSuffixText(operation GtIoOperation) returns ByteArray
	return match operation 'operation'
		fileExists gives GtTraceFileExistsSuffixText
		netConnect gives GtTraceNetConnectSuffixText
		netListen gives GtTraceNetListenSuffixText
		netAccept gives GtTraceNetAcceptSuffixText
		netSend gives GtTraceNetSendSuffixText
		netRecv gives GtTraceNetRecvSuffixText
		netClose gives GtTraceNetCloseSuffixText
	end 'operation'
end 'ioOperationSuffixText'

// The `.rdata` label prefix every async-trace tag is interned under.
let GtTraceTagPrefix = "__at_tag_"

// ⭐ ONE TAG: its `.rdata` label and its BYTE LENGTH. The length travels with the label because the text is a
// compile-time constant — the trace writes `osWriteStderr(addr, len)` directly, with no String record in
// between.
//
// ⚠ **A RAW BLOB, NOT A `String` RECORD, AND THE RECORD WAS TRIED FIRST.** Registering each tag through
// `registerStringRecord` produced a record whose `buffer` field holds the blob's rdata OFFSET, not its address
// — a literal's record is only made whole by the buffer FIXUP the PE writer applies for it
// (`LowerMaxonToStd`'s literal path registers that fixup; this path cannot). MEASURED: the trace printed its
// digits and its newlines and NOTHING ELSE, because every tag write handed `osWriteStderr` a small integer as
// a pointer. A raw blob has no such indirection: `rdataAddr` IS the address of the bytes.
export type GtTraceTag
	export let label as ByteArray
	export let length as FullRangeInt

	export static function create(label ByteArray, length FullRangeInt) returns GtTraceTag
		return Self{label: label, length: length}
	end 'create'
end 'GtTraceTag'

union GtTraceSuffix
	none
	tagged(tag GtTraceTag)
end 'GtTraceSuffix'

export typealias GtTraceTagArray = Array with GtTraceTag

// The registered tags, resolved ONCE per build and handed to the sites that emit.
export type GtTraceTags
	export let spawn as GtTraceTag
	export let awaitTag as GtTraceTag
	export let tryAwaitTag as GtTraceTag
	export let ioYield as GtTraceTag
	export let ioResume as GtTraceTag
	export let sleepYield as GtTraceTag
	export let sleepResume as GtTraceTag
	export let yieldSuffix as GtTraceTag
	export let immediateSuffix as GtTraceTag

	// The I/O-operation suffixes, INDEXED BY `GtIoOperation.ordinal` — see `registerIoOperationTags` for why
	// an array rather than one field per case: the enum is then the only place an operation's existence and
	// its position are written down. EMPTY in an untraced build, which is what `ioTag` reads as "no suffix".
	export let ioOperations as GtTraceTagArray

	export let newline as GtTraceTag

	export static function register(globalData GlobalDataTable) returns GtTraceTags
		return Self{
			spawn: registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: GtTraceSpawnText),
			awaitTag: registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: GtTraceAwaitText),
			tryAwaitTag: registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: GtTraceTryAwaitText),
			ioYield: registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: GtTraceIoYieldText),
			ioResume: registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: GtTraceIoResumeText),
			sleepYield: registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: GtTraceSleepYieldText),
			sleepResume: registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: GtTraceSleepResumeText),
			yieldSuffix: registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: GtTraceYieldSuffixText),
			immediateSuffix: registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: GtTraceImmediateSuffixText),
			ioOperations: registerIoOperationTags(globalData),
			newline: registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: GtTraceNewlineText)
		}
	end 'register'

	// ⭐ **THE ONE ANSWER TO "WHAT SUFFIX DOES THIS PARK PRINT?"**
	export function ioTag(operation GtIoOperation) returns GtTraceTag
		return try self.ioOperations.get(operation.ordinal) otherwise panic("GtTraceTags.ioTag: no registered tag at ordinal {operation.ordinal} for '{operation.name}' — registerIoOperationTags walks GtIoOperation.allCases, so a gap here means the array was built somewhere else")
	end 'ioTag'
end 'GtTraceTags'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE FACT `__gt_io_park`'s SIGNATURE AND ITS CALL SITES BOTH READ.** The park takes a suffix
// pair exactly when there is a suffix to hand it, so the builder's ARITY and the sites' ARGUMENT COUNT
// are the same question asked twice — and asked of one place, they cannot disagree. Answered twice (a
// `asyncTrace` bool over here, a registered-tag test over there) a build could emit argument-less calls
// into a two-parameter park, which is not a wrong trace but a wrong FRAME.
export union GtTracing
	untraced
	traced(tags GtTraceTags)

	export function isTraced() returns bool
		return match self 'tracing'
			untraced gives false
			traced gives true
		end 'tracing'
	end 'isTraced'

	export function tags() returns GtTraceTags throws AbsentError
		match self 'tracing'
			untraced then throw AbsentError.absent
			traced(tags) then return tags
		end 'tracing'
	end 'tags'
end 'GtTracing'

// One tag's bytes, interned in `.rdata` and deduped by content like every other rdata constant.
function registerGtTraceTag(globalData GlobalDataTable, text ByteArray) returns GtTraceTag
	return GtTraceTag.create(globalData.registerRdataConstant(GtTraceTagPrefix, bytes: text, kind: RdataPayloadKind.traceTag).toByteArray(), length: text.count() as FullRangeInt)
end 'registerGtTraceTag'

// ⭐ The operation suffixes in `GtIoOperation` ORDINAL ORDER, because the walk IS `allCases` — the enum's
// declaration order is the array's index order by construction, so the two cannot drift the way a hand-written
// list of pushes beside a hand-written enum can. Adding an operation is one case plus one arm of
// `ioOperationSuffixText`; nothing here has to be remembered as well.
function registerIoOperationTags(globalData GlobalDataTable) returns GtTraceTagArray
	var tags = GtTraceTagArray.create()

	for operation in GtIoOperation.allCases 'eachOperation'
		tags.push(registerGtTraceTag(globalData, text: ioOperationSuffixText(operation)))
	end 'eachOperation'

	return tags
end 'registerIoOperationTags'

// ⭐ **EXPORTED BECAUSE THE FRONT END TARGETS IT DIRECTLY** — `__Builtins.currentTimeNanos()` and
// `currentTimeMs()` lower to a call to THIS function rather than to a monotonic clock of their own
// (`Parser.parseCallNamed`'s `__Builtins` table). That is the point rather than a convenience: the
// scheduler's timer deadlines are expressed in this counter, so a program that SLEEPS and a program that
// TIMES itself must read the same one — two monotonic clocks that could disagree about the epoch is exactly
// how `sleep(30)` comes back measuring 29 ms.
//
// ⚠ **A clock read therefore turns on `usesGt`** (`isGtRuntimeCallee` matches the `__gt_` prefix), so a
// program whose only clock question is monotonic installs the whole scheduler plus the heap floor under it.
// That is the price of the shared counter, and it is the right side of the trade — the alternative is two
// clocks. The WALL clock has no such relationship to the scheduler and correctly rides its own
// `usesWallClock` bit instead (see `ClockRuntime.maxon`'s header).
export let GtNowNsName = b"__gt_now_ns"
export let GtTimerAddName = b"__gt_timer_add"

// ⭐ EXPORTED because the scheduler loop fires the timer store itself when a deadline is due at the top of a
// schedule and after its park (`SchedRuntime.buildSchedLoop`). It answers the wake debt the fires owe and pays
// none of it: the machine that fired is about to look for work itself.
export let GtTimerCheckName = b"__gt_timer_check"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_register_park(gt) -> wake debt` — THE SECOND HALF OF EVERY PARK BUT A MAILBOX'S
// (`GtParkKind.mailbox`), AND WHERE SUCH A THREAD IS MADE DISCOVERABLE.** It reads the note
// `__gt_park` left on the GT (`GtOffParkKind`/`GtOffParkArg`) and puts the thread on the store that
// will wake it. Its one caller is `__gt_strand_next`, before it takes the strand's next member and
// after `__gt_context_switch` has returned — so the parking thread's registers are provably saved
// and any M may take it.
let GtRegisterParkName = b"__gt_register_park"

// `__gt_process_run` is the front-end target for `runProcess(cmd)`.
export let GtProcessRunName = b"__gt_process_run"

// `__gt_io_read` is the front-end target for the `spawnReadLine` probe builtin. Named in the `__gt_` band so
// `isGtRuntimeCallee` catches it and it stays out of the rendered Target IR.
export let IoReadName = b"__gt_io_read"

// --- `.data` globals (all i64, zero-initialized) ---
// The zero image IS the initial state: no current GT and an empty run queue — exactly as `__slab_state`'s
// zero means "the allocator has committed no region yet". `__gt_run_main` brings the rest up before `main`.
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `__gt_current_gt` `.data` WORD: THE RUNNING GT IS `M->currentGt`.** A single global
// cannot express *"the green thread THIS M is running"* once there is more than one M — two Ms would write
// the same word and each would read the other's answer — and it sits on the MACHINE rather than on the
// processor because the thread executing inside a kernel call belongs to the M blocked there and not to the
// processor a handoff is about to take away. Every reader
// — the eight in this file, the hand-assembled ones in `X64GtRuntime` and `Arm64GtRuntime`, and the
// DebugStream event — goes through `SchedRuntime.emitLoadCurrentGt` / its Target-tier twins. `SchedRuntime`'s
// `__sched_find_runnable` and `__sched_sweep_dropped` peek this word UNLOCKED, through `emitQueueLoadHead`,
// to decide whether taking the lock is worth it — Go's `sched.runq.empty()` guard at the two identical points
// (`proc.go:3456`, `proc.go:3490`). The peek decides only whether to LOOK; the locked dequeue behind it is
// what decides anything.
let GtRunQueueHeadLabel = b"__gt_run_queue_head"

// ⭐⭐ **EVERY GREEN THREAD EVER CARVED — Go's `allgs`, and a FLAT VECTOR rather than an intrusive list.**
// Its one reader is a debugger in another process, which takes the whole roster with a single foreign read of
// `len * 8` bytes; a chain through a link field would cost one read per node. The base is published with a
// release store and the length after it, so a reader that sees a length sees the slots below it.
export let GtAllGLabel = b"__gt_allg"
export let GtAllGLenLabel = b"__gt_allglen"
let GtAllGCapLabel = b"__gt_allgcap"
let GtAllGLockLabel = b"__gt_allg_lock"

// ⚠ **A COUNT, NOT A FLAG.** Two green threads of one process can each hold a pin at once — a DAP session
// and an MCP session in the same compiler — so the hand-off gate is armed while any of them stands and dark
// only at zero.
export let GtHoldEpochLabel = b"__gt_hold_epoch"
let GtRunQueueTailLabel = b"__gt_run_queue_tail"

// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `__gt_system_stack_top` `.data` WORD: THE SYSCALL STACK IS `M->systemStackSP`.** One
// global region rests on *"single-M runs one thing at a time"* and a per-P one on *"an M can only reach its
// own P"*; multi-M falsifies the first and P migration the second, and either way two Ms inside shimmed calls
// set RSP to the same top and write the same 64 KB, as silent interleaved corruption rather than a fault. The
// region belongs to the M that is EXECUTING. `SchedRuntime.MOffSystemStackSP` carries the argument in full;
// `GtSystemStackSize` below is the size of EACH M's region.

// ⭐ **A STACK COMES FROM A POOL OF POWER-OF-TWO SIZE CLASSES AND GOES BACK TO IT**
// (`__gt_stack_alloc`, `__gt_stack_free`). Stacks are VARIABLE-SIZED (a seed, doubled on demand,
// halved at a park), so a free must land in the class its allocation came from: both round the size
// the thread records up to the same class, and a size above the largest class is mapped and
// unmapped directly. `__gt_free_get` allocates a spawn's seed of `__gt_seed_bytes` unless the
// record it hands back kept one, `__gt_stack_relocate` frees the OLD stack when a grow or a shrink
// moves the thread, and `__gt_runner_done` frees a finished thread's stack unless it is the seed's
// size, which stays with the record as Go's `gfput` keeps it (`emitGtDropStackUnlessSized`). A
// stack kept on the global free list is freed at the next seed-window close
// (`SchedRuntime.emitGtFreeStacks`).
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE RECORD IS THE OPPOSITE: TYPE-STABLE, RECYCLED THROUGH FREE LISTS AND NEVER FREED.** It
// is carved from the scheduler's record arena, `__gt_reclaim` hands it to `__gt_free_put` and
// `__gt_spawn` takes one from `__gt_free_get` (`SchedRuntime.GtFreePutName` and `GtFreeGetName`
// carry why), and a reply cell travels the same road through `__gt_cell_put` and `__gt_cell_get`; a
// spawn zeroes the fields `gtFieldRoster` marks for its kind, and a cell is zeroed past its record
// lock.
//
// ⭐ **RECLAIM AT THE AWAITER, NOT AT COMPLETION.** A GT can complete long before anybody awaits it, and its
// struct still holds its result for a live, un-awaited promise. Reclaiming at completion would put that struct
// on a free list under the pending `await`, and the next spawn would zero it and overwrite its result. So the
// struct goes back only when both the runner and the consumer are finished (`GtOffTeardown`).

export let GtQuiesceName = b"__gt_quiesce"
export let GtQuiesceWaiterLabel = b"__gt_quiesce_waiter"

// ⭐⭐ **THE ADAPTIVE SEED — Go's `startingStackSize`.** The size `__gt_spawn` asks
// `__gt_free_get` for a new thread's stack: the smallest power of two in [`gtSeedStackBytes`,
// `GtSeedMaxBytes`] that holds the average stack the threads that finished in the last window
// needed (`__gt_seed_adapt`, `GtSeedWindowShift`). Starting at the average costs at most twice the
// space an oracle would, and a thread that needs more still grows. `MultiMSharing.lastWriterWins`:
// the adapt writes it under `__gt_seed_guard` and a spawn reads it without, and every value a spawn
// can read is a seed some window chose.
let GtSeedBytesLabel = b"__gt_seed_bytes"

// The summed per-P sample counters (`POffStackSampleSum`, `POffStackSampleCount`) as the last window close
// read them, touched only inside `__gt_seed_guard`'s claim (`__gt_seed_adapt`).
let GtSeedSumSeenLabel = b"__gt_seed_sum_seen"
let GtSeedCountSeenLabel = b"__gt_seed_count_seen"
let GtSeedGuardLabel = b"__gt_seed_guard"

// ⭐⭐ **WHETHER A PARK KEEPS THE GREEN THREAD's CPU CLOCK (`GtOffCpuOffset`).** A RUN-TIME word rather than a
// build-time fork of `__gt_park`, so the park's body is one function of nothing; the program's answer rides
// the word's SEED, `GtCpuClockKept` exactly where the program reads `__Builtins.threadCpuTicks()`. Nothing
// writes it after the image is loaded.
let GtKeepsCpuClockLabel = b"__gt_keeps_cpu_clock"
let GtCpuClockUnkept = 0 as FullRangeInt
let GtCpuClockKept = 1 as FullRangeInt

// The timer store: the live entry COUNT and the base address of the `__slab_alloc`'d heap region (both 0
// until `__gt_init` allocates the region and seeds the base). The count is read under the lock, by the store's
// own walks and by `__sched_checkdead` (`emitGtStoresPending`).
let GtTimerCountLabel = b"__gt_timer_count"
let GtTimerHeapBaseLabel = b"__gt_timer_heap_base"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE EARLIEST DEADLINE, PUBLISHED — Go's `timers.minWhenHeap`.** The heap root's deadline, or
// `TimerWhenEmpty`. Written under `__sched_lock` by every walk that can move the root (`__gt_timer_add`,
// `__gt_timer_check`, `emitGtTimerStoreRemove`), through the one door `emitGtPublishTimerWhen`.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT IS WHAT LETS A SCHEDULE ASK "IS A TIMER DUE?" WITHOUT THE LOCK.** The root itself cannot be read
// unlocked — a grow frees the region it sits in — but this word is a copy, so every due test reads it with
// acquire and takes the lock only when `now` has reached it (`emitGtBranchOnTimerDue`). A stale value later
// than the truth delays a fire to the next look, and the machine that armed the earlier deadline reads its own
// write at its next one; a stale value earlier than the truth costs one lock that `__gt_timer_check` finds
// nothing due under.
let GtTimerWhenLabel = b"__gt_timer_when"

// How many entries the timer store can hold RIGHT NOW. A runtime word rather than `TimerHeapCapacity` itself,
// because `__gt_timer_add` doubles the store when it fills — the constant is the seed, this is the truth.
let GtTimerCapacityLabel = b"__gt_timer_capacity"

// The ARM SEQUENCE stamped into every timer entry — the tiebreaker that makes the heap's order TOTAL. A
// monotone counter bumped once per `__gt_timer_add` under the scheduler lock, so two entries carrying the
// same deadline still compare, and they compare in arming order. Slab-zeroed, so nothing seeds it, and a
// program would have to arm 2^63 timers to wrap it.
let GtTimerSeqLabel = b"__gt_timer_seq"

// The async-trace id dispenser. A processor takes `GtTraceIdBatch` ids from it at a time into
// `POffTraceIdNext`/`POffTraceIdEnd`, as Go's `goidcache` does, so ids start at `#1` and are unique but run
// in spawn order only within one processor's batch (`emitGtMintTraceId`). Slab-zeroed, so nothing seeds it.
let GtTraceCounterLabel = b"__gt_trace_counter"

let GtTraceIdBatch = 16

// ⭐⭐ **THE THREE OUT-PARAM SCRATCH REGIONS ARE FRAME SLOTS, BECAUSE A `.data` WORD IS SHARED BY EVERY M
// BY CONSTRUCTION.** The trace's digit buffer and the two QueryPerformanceCounter/Frequency out-params are
// each written and read straight back by one function, which looks safe by an argument about RE-ENTRY:
// *"straight-line with no yield point inside it, so it cannot be re-entered mid-read"*. **That argument is
// about ONE thread of control, and two Ms do not need a yield point to be inside the same function at
// once.** As globals, two green threads reading `__gt_now_ns` on two Ms would hand each other's QPC samples
// back — a monotonic clock stepping BACKWARDS, which is what the timer store's deadlines are compared
// against — and two tracing Ms would interleave digits inside one line.
//
// ⭐ **A FRAME SLOT IS THE FIX RATHER THAN AN ATOMIC OR A PER-P WORD, AND IT IS STRICTLY SMALLER THAN BOTH.**
// The scratch is written and consumed inside ONE CALL, so what it wants is per-CALL storage, and that is what
// a frame is; an atomic cannot make a two-step write-then-read indivisible, and a per-P slot needs a P — which
// `__gt_now_ns` cannot assume, since `Runtime.currentTimeNanos()` compiles into a program that never calls
// `__gt_init` and so has no `__sched_procs` array to index. It also deletes three `.data` words and, for the
// trace, one `__slab_alloc` in `__gt_init`.
//
// ⚠ **`StdOp.stackRecordAddr` IS x64/arm64 ONLY (a wasm local has no address), WHICH COSTS NOTHING HERE**:
// `osReadClock` is already x64-windows-gated in both other backends, and the trace rides `--async-trace` on
// the same lane. The reserving function sets `func.stackSize` itself, exactly as `promoteStackRecords` does,
// and the register allocator's spiller then starts above the region (`SplitDriver.create`).
//
// 24 bytes covers the widest i64 (`-9223372036854775808`, 20 characters) with room to spare.
let GtTraceDigitsBytes = 24
let GtTraceDigitsSlot = 0 as SpillSlot

// `__gt_now_ns`'s two frame slots — the words `osReadClock` hands to QueryPerformanceCounter and
// QueryPerformanceFrequency and reads straight back.
let GtClockTicksSlot = 0 as SpillSlot
let GtClockFreqSlot = 1 as SpillSlot
let GtClockScratchBytes = 16

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE-SHOT READ'S ACROSS-THE-PARK SCRATCH — ONE REGION, TWO USES.** `__gt_io_read` needs a read
// buffer big enough for one line of child stdout (the probe reads `cmd /c echo hello`, ~7 bytes) AND an
// 8-byte slot for `ReadFile`'s transferred-byte out-param, and both must survive the park. They are ONE
// allocation because that is what makes them ONE free: the reading thread is the only party that ever frees
// it, on the normal road and on the renounced one alike.
//
// ⚠ **THE OUT-SLOT IS FIRST SO THE WHOLE REGION IS EXACTLY A SIZE CLASS.** Adding the slot to a round 4096
// would push the request into the next class up and waste most of the difference; taking it OUT of 4096
// leaves the request exact and costs the buffer eight bytes it does not need.
let IoReadScratchBytes = 4096
let IoReadBytesOutOffset = 0
let IoReadBytesOutBytes = 8
let IoReadBufferOffset = IoReadBytesOutBytes
let IoReadBufferBytes = IoReadScratchBytes - IoReadBytesOutBytes

// SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES: nLength=24 @0, lpSecurityDescriptor=0 @8, bInheritHandle=1 @16 — the inheritable SA the
// child WRITE end (CreateFileW) is opened with, so CreateProcessW(bInheritHandles=TRUE) passes it to the child.
let SecurityAttributesSize = 24
let SecurityAttributesNLengthOffset = 0
let SecurityAttributesLpSdOffset = 8
let SecurityAttributesBInheritOffset = 16
let SecurityAttributesInheritable = 1

// STARTUPINFOW fields the read builtin sets so the child's stdout is the pipe write end. `cb`=0x68 (0x70 for
// the STARTUPINFOEXW every x64-windows spawn passes, `emitSubpAllocStartupInfo`), `dwFlags`
// STARTF_USESTDHANDLES (0x100) @0x3C, hStdInput @0x50, hStdOutput @0x58, hStdError @0x60. Every other field 0.
let StartfUseStdHandles = 0x100
let StartupInfoDwFlagsOffset = 0x3C
export let StartupInfoHStdInputOffset = 0x50
export let StartupInfoHStdOutputOffset = 0x58
export let StartupInfoHStdErrorOffset = 0x60

// ReadFile / GetLastError classification: ERROR_IO_PENDING (997) → the read yields; ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE (109)
// → clean EOF (0 bytes). Any other value is a real fault, and `__gt_subp_yield_read` answers it with the
// count below so a caller can tell a stopped stream from a finished one.
//
// ⚠ **THE BROKEN-PIPE FOLD IS A WINDOWS QUESTION AND CANNOT MISFIRE ON A POSIX LANE.** There, an
// `osReadOverlapped` is `read(2)` through `mrt_host_read_file`, which answers TRUE for every `read` that
// returned `>= 0` — so an end of stream is a 0-byte SUCCESS and never reaches the error classification at
// all. What reaches it there is a `read` that returned negative, which is a real fault on any lane.
//
// ⚠ `export` because a POSIX lane's errno translator answers `ERROR_IO_PENDING` for `EAGAIN`: a non-blocking
// read that could not be served yet IS an operation still in flight, and translating it to anything else would
// send the caller down the classification road instead of the park.
export let ErrorIoPending = 997
let ErrorBrokenPipe = 109

// ⭐ **THE NTSTATUS VALUES THE READERS OF A COMPLETION PACKET TELL APART** (`GtOffIoStatus`). A packet carries
// an NTSTATUS rather than a Win32 error, so every fold is written in that vocabulary. The parked pipe read's
// two ends of stream are the child's write end gone (`STATUS_PIPE_BROKEN`) and the last byte already delivered
// (`STATUS_END_OF_FILE`); a socket transfer tells a request its cancel reached (`STATUS_CANCELLED`) from one the
// kernel finished first.
export let NtStatusSuccess = 0 as FullRangeInt
export let NtStatusCancelled = 0xC0000120 as FullRangeInt
let NtStatusPipeBroken = 0xC000014B
let NtStatusEndOfFile = 0xC0000011

// ⭐ **`__gt_subp_yield_read`'s ANSWER FOR A READ THAT FAILED**, against the 0 that means a clean end of
// stream. It rides the byte count rather than an out-parameter because every caller already branches on
// that count — and each must branch on it as `<= 0` rather than `== 0`, since a negative added to a buffer
// length is a length past what the buffer holds.
let SubpReadFailedCount = -1

// The wide pipe-name buffer: prefix `\\.\pipe\mx` (11 wchars) + a 16-wchar hex uniquifier + NUL, in a `__slab_alloc`
// buffer. `PipeNamePrefixWchars` counts the prefix wide chars; `PipeNameHexDigits` the uniquifier digits (a 64-bit
// value); `WideCharBytes` is 2 (UTF-16). The uniquifier packs GetCurrentProcessId in the high 32 bits and a
// per-process monotonic counter in the low 32, so two concurrent reads (different seq), two sequential reads (same
// recycled GT struct, different seq), and two separate PROCESSES (different pid) all get distinct names.
// The wide-char index into the pipe-name buffer a prefix store targets (0..the prefix length).
// The pipe-name uniquifier packs GetCurrentProcessId (cross-process) in the high 32 bits and a per-process
// monotonic counter (within-process) in the low 32 bits, so FILE_FLAG_FIRST_PIPE_INSTANCE never collides.
let PidShiftBits = 32
let PipeSeqMask = 0xFFFFFFFF

typealias PipeWcharIndex = int(0 to 63)

let PipeNamePrefixWchars = 11
let PipeNameHexDigits = 16
let WideCharBytes = 2
let PipeNameTrailingNulBytes = 2
let HexRadix = 16
let HexDigitZero = 48 as FullRangeInt  // '0'
let HexDigitLetterA = 97 as FullRangeInt
let HexAlphaThreshold = 10 as FullRangeInt
let HexDigitLetterBase = HexDigitLetterA - HexAlphaThreshold
let NibbleBits = 4
let TopNibbleShift = 60  // (PipeNameHexDigits - 1) * NibbleBits — the highest nibble of a 64-bit value
let NibbleMask = 0xF

// The global pipe-name uniquifier counter's `.data` slot. Stepped through `MmRuntime.emitGlobalAccumulate`,
// which is an ATOMIC add — every M can reach a pipe-opening green thread, and a lost update here is not
// a wrong count but a NAME COLLISION, which `FILE_FLAG_FIRST_PIPE_INSTANCE` turns into a failed open.
let IoPipeSeqLabel = b"__io_pipe_seq"

// ⛔⛔ **`__gt_process_run` ALLOCATES ITS OWN SCRATCH AND FREES IT; NOTHING IS REUSED ACROSS CALLS.** Sharing
// one buffer across calls is safe only under an argument about the code — that a call writes and consumes its
// scratch inside a window with no yield in it — which the probe next door cannot make at all (`__gt_io_read`
// YIELDS, so a concurrent read must not share scratch). Per-call allocation is the discipline that needs no
// argument, and with a free path it costs four slab requests against a `CreateProcessW`. It also needs no
// re-zeroing stores: `hProcess` (the spawn-failure sentinel), the exit-code slot's high DWORD and the
// cmdline's NUL terminator come zeroed by the allocator's contract, where a reused buffer arrives dirty and
// each is a place a reader could be handed the previous call's answer.

// The prefix every GT runtime entry point shares — the "does this program spawn a green thread?" test.
let GtRuntimeCallPrefix = b"__gt_"

// ⭐ **DOES THIS CALLEE REACH THE GT SCHEDULER?** The `__gt_`-only test that decides whether the program
// carries the scheduler runtime + its `.data` globals + the three hand-assembled chunks. Deliberately
// narrower than MmRuntime's `isRuntimeCallee`: a `__gt_*` call implies the whole scheduler (spawn enqueues,
// await parks, both context-switch), and it also implies the HEAP (the scheduler's tables come from
// `__slab_alloc`), which is why `scanRuntimeUsage` turns on `usesHeap` alongside `usesGt`.
export function isGtRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: GtRuntimeCallPrefix)
end 'isGtRuntimeCallee'

// The THROWING GT runtime callees. `__gt_process_run` returns via the dual-register `errorReturn` ABI
// and can throw `RunProcessError` (`spawnFailed`), so a `runProcess` call MUST become a `tryCall`
// (under a `try`, which `rewriteLastCallToTryCall` converts). The GT-side twin of
// `ManagedMemoryRuntime.isThrowingManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee`: the two are composed by `isThrowingRuntimeCallee`, read at the ONE
// call site in the parser (E3055 accept) and the ONE in SemanticCheck (E3057 reject) so those two cannot drift.
// ⚠ This names `__gt_process_run` as throwing; `buildGtProcessRun` stamps its `throws` clause. Those two facts
// must agree, and — unlike `ManagedMemoryRuntime.verifyThrowingAccessorsMatchPredicate` — there is no install-time guard
// tying them. That is deliberate and proportionate: there is exactly ONE throwing GT callee, both sides key on
// the SAME `GtProcessRunName` constant (not a copied literal), and subprocess is x64-only, so the wasm-signature
// drift the array guard protects against cannot arise here (E3074 rejects subprocess on wasm). If a SECOND
// throwing GT callee is ever added, add the guard rather than a second name in this `or`.
export function isThrowingGtRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return callee.equals(GtProcessRunName)
end 'isThrowingGtRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐ **THE ONE HOME OF "WHICH COMPILER-INTERNAL RUNTIME CALLEE THROWS."** A throwing builtin (an out-of-bounds
// array accessor, `runProcess`, or a possibly-zero divide) is invisible to the user-signature registry — no file
// declares it — so the two
// gates that decide `try`-ness for it (the parser's E3055 accept in `requireThrowingTryTarget`, SemanticCheck's
// E3057 reject in `validatePlainCall`) both read THIS composed predicate rather than each listing the families,
// which is what keeps the accept and the reject naming the same set. Grows one disjunct per throwing runtime family.
//
// ⚠ **THE DIVISION FAMILY IS THE ONE MEMBER WITH NO BUILT FUNCTION BEHIND IT** — `__checked_div`/`__checked_mod`
// are expanded in place at the Std tier rather than called (`CheckedDivisionRuntime`'s header says why). That
// changes nothing here: this predicate answers "must a `try` wrap it", which is a property of the OPERATION, and
// both gates that read it run on the MAXON tier where the callee is still a call.
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `Map` DISJUNCT AND THERE MUST NOT BE ONE.** No lowering path can mint a `__map_get` or
// `__map_insert` callee, and the corpus `Map.get`/`Map.insert` are DECLARED throwing functions — the
// user-signature registry answers for them, which is the route this predicate exists to stand in for.
// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `String` DISJUNCT.** `String.byteAt` lives in `stdlib/String.maxon`, so nothing mints a
// `__str_byte_at` callee and a disjunct for it could only answer `false`. The corpus body's
// `try managed.byteAt(index)` reaches `isThrowingManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee` — the FIRST disjunct here —
// which is where a buffer byte read belongs: `managedMemorySourceMethodName` names
// `__ManagedMemory.byteAt`, which IS the construct the author wrote.
export function isThrowingRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return isThrowingManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee(callee) or isThrowingGtRuntimeCallee(callee) or isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee(callee) or isThrowingManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee(callee) or isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(callee) or isManagedListenerRuntimeCallee(callee) or isThrowingCharacterRuntimeCallee(callee) or isThrowingListRuntimeCallee(callee) or isThrowingDivisionRuntimeCallee(callee) or isEnumLookupRuntimeCallee(callee)
end 'isThrowingRuntimeCallee'

// ⭐ **THE ONE HOME OF "WHICH ERROR TYPE A COMPILER-INTERNAL RUNTIME CALLEE THROWS"** — the composer beside
// the predicate above, and the WHAT to its THAT. `ProgramSignatures.throwsOf` asks it once, so a family that
// gains a discriminable error is added HERE and every caller's `otherwise (e)` binding is typed by it, rather
// than `throwsOf` growing one `match` per family.
//
// ⚠ **A FAMILY THAT ANSWERS `none` IS A SCOPED STATEMENT, NOT AN OVERSIGHT**, and the reason is `throwsOf`'s
// own: the GT and plain-`ArrayError` families throw too, but no committed case BINDS and discriminates their
// error, so a clause for them would be an untested claim about their ordinals. Add the arm with the case that
// needs it, as the file and memory families each did.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT PAIRS EACH FAMILY'S PREDICATE WITH ITS CLAUSE SPELLER AND RETURNS A `ThrowsClause` RATHER THAN AN
// OPTIONAL OVER ONE.** `ThrowsClause.none` already says *"this callee throws nothing"*, so an optional
// wrapper says one thing twice and charges for the second: one extra 16-byte union box per `try` in the
// program, per family asked. ⭐ **A DEDUP THAT IS CORRECT CAN STILL BE UNSCALABLE** —
// `docs/optimization-log.md` carries that signature.
export function runtimeThrowsClause(callee ByteArray) returns ThrowsClause
	if isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee(callee) 'fileFamily'
		return managedFileThrows()
	end 'fileFamily'

	// ⭐ **THE DIRECTORY FAMILY, ADDED WITH THE CASES THAT NEED IT.**
	// `specs/managed-directory.md`'s `open-search-not-found-variant` discriminates `notFound` from the
	// `openSearchFailed` catch-all, so this family's ordinals are a checked claim rather than an untested one.
	//
	// ⚠ It asks the THROWING predicate rather than `isManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee`, unlike the memory arm
	// below: `__md_close`/`__md_filename`/`__md_exists` are `__md_`-prefixed and do not throw, and typing a
	// non-throwing callee's clause as `throwing` is exactly what makes `try` required on a call that cannot
	// fail (E3055). The memory family can use the wide test because ALL of its members throw and its own
	// comment says so.
	if isThrowingManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee(callee) 'directoryFamily'
		return managedDirectoryThrows()
	end 'directoryFamily'

	// THE SOCKET FAMILY, ADDED WITH THE CASES THAT NEED IT - exactly as the note above prescribes.
	// `specs/managed-socket.md`'s `connect-bad-host` and `connect-refused` BIND the error and
	// discriminate `resolveFailed` from `connectFailed`, so this family's ordinals are a checked claim rather
	// than an untested one. Without this arm `throwsOf` answers `none`, `tryErrorEnumType` types the `(e)`
	// binding `integer`, and `match e` reports a MISSING FEATURE for a program whose feature is present.
	//
	// It asks the THROWING predicate rather than `isManagedSocketRuntimeCallee`, for the directory arm's
	// reason: `__ms_close` is `__ms_`-prefixed and does not throw, and typing a non-throwing callee's clause
	// as `throwing` is exactly what makes `try` required on a call that cannot fail (E3055).
	if isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(callee) 'socketFamily'
		return managedSocketThrows()
	end 'socketFamily'

	// THE LISTENER FAMILY. Every `__ml_` entry throws, so the band predicate answers the question the socket
	// arm needs a narrower one for. `specs/netpoll-socket.md`'s `a-second-bind-of-a-live-port-is-refused`
	// discriminates `bindFailed` through the stdlib wrapper, so this family's ordinals are a checked claim
	// rather than an untested one.
	if isManagedListenerRuntimeCallee(callee) 'listenerFamily'
		return managedListenerThrows()
	end 'listenerFamily'

	if isManagedMemoryMemberCallee(callee) 'memoryFamily'
		return managedMemoryThrows()
	end 'memoryFamily'

	// ⭐ **THE CURSOR FAMILY, WHICH THROWS THE BUFFER'S ENUM.** A cursor is a position inside a
	// `__ManagedMemory` and every failure it can report is an out-of-range position, so the errors it raises
	// are that surface's.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT MUST STAY ABOVE THE ARRAY ARM, for the memory family's reason one line up**: the cursor entries
	// are `__managed_`-prefixed and `isThrowingManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee` COMPOSES this predicate in, so asking the wide
	// test first would type every cursor failure as `ArrayError`.
	if isThrowingCursorRuntimeCallee(callee) 'cursorFamily'
		return managedMemoryThrows()
	end 'cursorFamily'

	// ⭐ **THE ARRAY FAMILY, ADDED WITH THE CASE THAT NEEDED IT** — precisely as the note above
	// prescribes. `specs/array-slots.md` discriminates `ArrayError.emptySlot` from
	// `ArrayError.indexOutOfBounds` on an `arr.get` failure, so this family's ordinals are now a checked claim
	// rather than an untested one.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT MUST STAY BELOW THE MEMORY ARM**: `isThrowingManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee` INCLUDES
	// `isManagedMemoryMemberCallee` (the buffer members are `__managed_`-prefixed — one record, one runtime), so
	// asking it first would type every `__ManagedMemory` member's error as `ArrayError` and reroute the six
	// cases `managed-memory-error-variants` pins.
	if isThrowingManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee(callee) 'arrayFamily'
		return arrayErrorThrows()
	end 'arrayFamily'

	// ⭐ **THE DIVISION FAMILY, CARRIED BY THE CASES THAT NEED IT** — exactly as the note above prescribes.
	// `specs/safety.md`'s `divide-by-zero-expression-form` and `float-divide-by-zero-expression-form` BIND
	// the error and discriminate `divisionByZero` in a `match`, so this family's one ordinal is a checked claim
	// rather than an untested one. Without this arm `throwsOf` answers `none`, `tryErrorEnumType` types the `(e)`
	// binding `integer`, and `match e` reports a MISSING FEATURE for a program whose feature is present.
	//
	// ⚠ It asks the THROWING predicate, which for this family is the WHOLE family: a checked divide exists only
	// because its divisor might be zero, so there is no non-throwing member the wide test could wrongly claim.
	if isThrowingDivisionRuntimeCallee(callee) 'divisionFamily'
		return divisionByZeroThrows()
	end 'divisionFamily'

	// ⚠ **THERE IS NO MAP ARM.** The corpus `Map.get`/`Map.insert` declare `throws MapError` in
	// `stdlib/Map.maxon` themselves, so their clause is read off the signature registry and never composed here.
	// ⭐ **THE LIST FAMILY THROWS `ArrayError`, NOT AN ERROR OF ITS OWN — and that is `stdlib/List.maxon`'s
	// OWN declaration rather than a shortcut.** `first`/`get`/`removeFirst` are each written
	// `throws ArrayError` there (`:44`, `:73`, `:112`), and the case they raise is `indexOutOfBounds`. Minting
	// a `ListError` would put a SECOND enum behind the one the canonical spec names, so a user function that
	// propagates `list.get(i)` and `arr.get(i)` through one `throws ArrayError` clause — which that file's own
	// `walkTo` does — would meet E3059 for a difference the language does not have.
	//
	// ⚠ It asks the THROWING predicate, not the wide `isListRuntimeCallee`: `create`/`count`/`append`/
	// `prepend`/`decref`/`from` and the three cursor primitives cannot fail, and typing a non-throwing callee
	// as `throwing` is exactly what makes `try` REQUIRED on a call that cannot (E3055).
	if isThrowingListRuntimeCallee(callee) 'listFamily'
		return arrayErrorThrows()
	end 'listFamily'

	// ⭐ **THE ENUM-LOOKUP FAMILY** — `__enum_from_raw_<Enum>` and `__enum_from_name_<Enum>`, throwing
	// `__EnumLookupError` (`noSuchRawValue` / `noSuchCaseName`, one per member: a lookup that misses says
	// WHICH lookup missed). The arm is here from the family's first day for the map family's stated reason:
	// without it `throwsOf` says `none`, the `(e)` binding types `integer`, and `match e` reports a missing
	// feature for a feature that is present. The wide predicate IS the throwing one here — every member of
	// this family throws, which is the whole reason either is a function at all.
	if isEnumLookupRuntimeCallee(callee) 'enumLookupFamily'
		return enumLookupThrows()
	end 'enumLookupFamily'

	if isThrowingGtRuntimeCallee(callee) 'processRun'
		return runProcessErrorThrows()
	end 'processRun'

	return ThrowsClause.none
end 'runtimeThrowsClause'

// The outcome of asking a runtime callee what the AUTHOR called it. `found` carries the source method name;
// `notFound` says this callee has no user-written spelling to quote — a `__managed_get` is reached by writing
// `get`, but through an accessor family whose diagnostic words itself differently, and a family added
// tomorrow has no map at all. A union rather than an empty `ByteArray`, so the absent state is structurally
// distinct instead of a sentinel.
export union RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod
	found(let method ByteArray)

	// ⚠⚠ **TWO SOURCE SPELLINGS, ONE CALLEE.** Where one runtime entry serves two source methods the
	// callee is a LOSSY KEY and no map over it can be injective. Quoting ONE of the two is a false
	// statement about a program that contains only the other — exactly "the right code naming a
	// construct the program does not contain". So the answer names BOTH, which is true whichever was
	// written, and the message says why the compiler cannot narrow it. No family produces it (see
	// `ManagedFileRuntime`'s source-method map).
	sharedByTwoSpellings(let spelling ByteArray, let alsoSpelled ByteArray)
	notFound
end 'RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE HOME OF "WHAT DID THE AUTHOR CALL THIS RUNTIME CALLEE?"** — the third composer beside
// `isThrowingRuntimeCallee` (THAT it throws) and `runtimeThrowsClause` (WHAT it throws), pairing each family's
// predicate with that family's own map. `SemanticCheck.throwingRuntimeCalleeMessage` asks it ONCE and builds
// ONE sentence from the answer, so the five families that name a method share a single spelling of it rather
// than an inline copy each.
//
// ⚠ **A FAMILY WITHOUT A MAP ANSWERS `notFound`, AND THAT IS THE POINT:** the message then names
// the callee itself rather than borrowing another family's noun for a construct the program does
// not contain.
//
// ⭐⭐ **AND THE THIRD LIST IS HELD TO THE SECOND AT INSTALL TIME — THE ONE HOME OF WHY.**
// A family writes its throwing callees down TWICE: once in `isThrowingXRuntimeCallee`, once as the arms
// of its source-method map. Nothing in the language ties them, and a name
// added to the predicate and not to the map compiles clean, passes every green suite, and then **panics the
// compiler in front of a user who merely forgot a `try` on that one callee**. A latent crash shipped in a
// binary, triggered by source nobody has written yet.
//
// ⇒ **Each family's install-time drift guard now ASKS ITS OWN MAP for every throwing callee it installed**
// (`installManagedFileRuntime`, `installManagedDirectoryRuntime`, `installManagedSocketRuntime`, and
// `ManagedMemoryRuntime.verifyThrowingAccessorsMatchPredicate` for the buffer members). A missing arm is the map's
// own `panic`, fired at INSTALL of any program that uses the family — which the spec suite does on every
// run — instead of at a user's missing `try`. The builder set is already held equal to the predicate in
// those same loops, so the chain is builders ≡ predicate ≡ map, checked in ONE traversal.
//
// ⚠ **NO SECOND LIST, AND THAT IS THE POINT OF THE SHAPE.** The names come from `module.functions` — what
// was actually installed — and the map is interrogated directly rather than described by a parallel column
// of names: a parallel column reintroduces, in the guard's own call shape, the drift the guard
// exists to catch (`Project.BuiltinErrorOrdinalCheck`'s header).
//
// ⚠ **FORWARD ONLY — "every throwing callee has a spelling" — and the backward arm is deliberately absent
// rather than forgotten**, for the same reason one step over. "Every name the map answers for is
// throwing" cannot be asked without enumerating the map's DOMAIN, which is exactly the name column
// above; and it would guard nothing, because a map is only ever reached through its family's
// throwing predicate, so an arm for a non-throwing callee is unreachable rather than wrong.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE FORWARD ARM COVERS THE NINE GENUINE `Array`-SURFACE ACCESSORS TOO.** E3057 words those
// nine AS A FAMILY, but that is a SENTENCE's policy, and this function answers a different question
// — *"what did the author write?"*, which has an answer for all nine. E3059 needs that NOUN
// (*"'array accessor' does not return a value"* is not a sentence); `notFound` would print a
// `__managed_` symbol at authors, one E2051 forbids them to type. The family wording lives where
// the policy belongs, in `SemanticCheck.throwingRuntimeCalleeMessage`, which asks
// `isThrowingArrayAccessorCallee` for its own sentence before reaching this map.
//
// ⭐ **THE ARMS ARE ORDER-INDEPENDENT.** `isThrowingManagedMemoryRuntimeCallee` COMPOSES
// `isManagedMemoryMemberCallee`, so a wide array test would swallow all seven buffer members; the
// array arm below asks `isThrowingArrayAccessorCallee`, the narrow factoring, so the two domains
// are disjoint BY CONSTRUCTION and swapping any two `if`s here changes no answer.
export function runtimeCalleeSourceMethod(callee ByteArray) returns RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod
	if isThrowingManagedDirectoryRuntimeCallee(callee) 'directoryFamily'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(managedDirectorySourceMethodName(callee))
	end 'directoryFamily'

	// The socket family - three callees, three distinct spellings the author writes, so it is the
	// several-names map shape rather than the `Character` family's one-name identity.
	if isThrowingManagedSocketRuntimeCallee(callee) 'socketFamily'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(managedSocketSourceMethodName(callee))
	end 'socketFamily'

	// The listener family - three callees, three distinct spellings the author writes.
	if isManagedListenerRuntimeCallee(callee) 'listenerFamily'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(managedListenerSourceMethodName(callee))
	end 'listenerFamily'

	// ⚠ The file family answers the union DIRECTLY where its three siblings answer a NAME the composer wraps,
	// and the asymmetry is a fact about the families rather than a style drift: `__mf_open_write` is reached
	// by two source spellings, so this is the one family whose callee is not injective onto one. A return
	// type as narrow as it is PROVABLY correct — the siblings cannot answer anything but `found`, and their
	// `ByteArray` says so at the signature.
	if isThrowingManagedFileRuntimeCallee(callee) 'fileFamily'
		return managedFileSourceMethod(callee)
	end 'fileFamily'

	if isManagedMemoryMemberCallee(callee) 'memoryFamily'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(managedMemorySourceMethodName(callee))
	end 'memoryFamily'

	// The cursor family — four names, four distinct spellings the author writes, so it is the several-names
	// map shape and not the `Character` family's one-name identity. Above the array arm for the reason
	// `runtimeThrowsClause`'s cursor arm gives.
	if isThrowingCursorRuntimeCallee(callee) 'cursorFamily'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(cursorSourceMethodName(callee))
	end 'cursorFamily'

	if isThrowingArrayAccessorCallee(callee) 'arrayFamily'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(arrayAccessorSourceMethodName(callee))
	end 'arrayFamily'

	// ⚠ The `Character` family answers from the CONSTANT rather than through a map function, and that is not
	// a shortcut taken because the family is small. Its predicate IS `callee.equals(CharAsciiValueName)` — one
	// name — so a map would be a one-arm dispatcher over a set of size one, i.e. the identity written as a
	// function, with a panic arm nothing can reach. The five maps above exist because their families are
	// several names each and a name can go missing from one; here the predicate and the answer are the same
	// fact, and pairing them at the one site that asks is what keeps them one fact. A SECOND throwing
	// `Character` callee makes this a map, at the same edit that makes `isThrowingCharacterRuntimeCallee` an
	// `or` — see the note there.
	if isThrowingCharacterRuntimeCallee(callee) 'characterFamily'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(CharacterAsciiValueMethod)
	end 'characterFamily'

	// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `String` ARM.** `String.byteAt` is an ordinary DECLARED function whose name an E3057
	// already has, and what its body calls IS `__ManagedMemory.byteAt`, which the `memoryFamily` arm above
	// names correctly.
	// ⚠ **THERE IS NO `Map` ARM EITHER.** No lowering mints a `__map_get`/`__map_insert` callee, and the
	// corpus `Map.get`/`Map.insert` are declared functions whose own name a diagnostic already has.
	// The `List` family answers through a map: three callees, three distinct spellings the author writes
	// (`first`, `get`, `removeFirst`), so this is the several-names shape the five maps above have and not the
	// `Character` family's one-name identity.
	if isThrowingListRuntimeCallee(callee) 'listFamily'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(listAccessorSourceMethodName(callee))
	end 'listFamily'

	// The enum-lookup family answers through a map: two members (`fromRawValue`, `fromName`) over any number
	// of declarations, so the spelling has to be composed from the callee rather than looked up in a fixed
	// table — which is what `enumLookupSourceMethodName` does, off the constants that built the symbol.
	if isEnumLookupRuntimeCallee(callee) 'enumLookupFamily'
		return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.found(enumLookupSourceMethodName(callee))
	end 'enumLookupFamily'

	// ⚠ **`notFound` IS NOW EXACTLY TWO FAMILIES, AND NEITHER NAMES A METHOD.** The subprocess family's
	// `runProcess` is a free FUNCTION and the division family is an OPERATOR — there is no method spelling to
	// quote for either, so this is the honest answer rather than a missing arm, and both callers word those two
	// from the construct instead (`throwingRuntimeCalleeMessage`'s subprocess and division arms). A SIXTH
	// family joining without a map lands here too, which is the point: it gets its own callee named by the
	// caller that can, or refused loudly by the caller that cannot, never another family's noun.
	return RuntimeCalleeSourceMethod.notFound
end 'runtimeCalleeSourceMethod'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE SPELLING OF "THIS FAMILY'S MAP ANSWERED, AND WITH SOMETHING QUOTABLE" — what the four
// install-time drift guards call.** The five families ask their own map for every throwing callee they
// installed; the ASKING is the existence check (a missing arm is that map's own panic), and this is what
// makes the answer USED rather than discarded — **E3064 refuses a discarded pure result**, which is the
// language declining to let a guard be written as a call whose value nobody reads.
//
// ⚠ The emptiness test is not a formality bolted on to satisfy E3064: an empty spelling would put `''` in
// front of an author in place of the method they wrote, which is the same class of wrong answer the map
// exists to prevent — just spelled with no characters. One home for the rule, so five families
// cannot word it five ways.
export function requireQuotableSourceSpelling(spelling ByteArray, callee ByteArray)
	if spelling.count() == 0 'unquotable'
		panic("requireQuotableSourceSpelling: the source-method map answers an EMPTY spelling for '{String.from(callee)}' — an E3057 would quote '' at an author in place of the method they actually wrote")
	end 'unquotable'
end 'requireQuotableSourceSpelling'

export function requireThrowsStampMatchesPredicate(installer String, predicate String, callee ByteArray, stamped bool, named bool)
	if stamped and not named 'stampedNotNamed'
		let drift = "{installer}: '{String.from(callee)}' stamps a `throws` clause but {predicate} does NOT name it — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted"
		panic("{drift}")
	end 'stampedNotNamed'

	if named and not stamped 'namedNotStamped'
		let drift = "{installer}: {predicate} names '{String.from(callee)}' as throwing but its builder stamps NO `throws` clause — the throwing-builder set and the predicate have drifted, and a caller's `try` would stop being required"
		panic("{drift}")
	end 'namedNotStamped'
end 'requireThrowsStampMatchesPredicate'

// The `sharedByTwoSpellings` half of the same check — BOTH names reach the message, so both must be
// quotable. Separate from the single-name form because a `match` arm binds two values and may not
// drop one: a check that read only the first would leave the second — the very spelling the case
// exists to carry — unverified.
export function requireQuotableSharedSpellings(spelling ByteArray, alsoSpelled ByteArray, callee ByteArray)
	requireQuotableSourceSpelling(spelling, callee: callee)
	requireQuotableSourceSpelling(alsoSpelled, callee: callee)
end 'requireQuotableSharedSpellings'

// The GT runtime's `.data` slots, appended to the user's globals before `layOut` orders them — ordinary
// i64 slots in every respect, exactly like `SlabRuntime.slabRuntimeGlobals`. Every one is read only by Std
// bodies, so each is laid out exactly where a surviving body names it (`DataReach.walked`).
//
// Empty on a target with no green-thread stack: the seed of `GtSeedBytesLabel` is that stack's size,
// which `gtSeedStackBytes` refuses to compute where the facility is denied, and no body there can name
// a slot anyway.
export function gtRuntimeGlobals(usage RuntimeUsage, target Target) returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	if not targetProvidesFacility(target, facility: HostFacility.greenThreads) 'noGreenThreadStack'
		return out
	end 'noGreenThreadStack'

	// The roster's three words are written under `__gt_allg_lock`; the base and the length are also
	// read without it.
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtAllGLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.publishedFromTheLock, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtAllGLenLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.publishedFromTheLock, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtAllGCapLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underOwnLock, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtAllGLockLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.atomicStep, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtHoldEpochLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.lastWriterWins, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtRunQueueHeadLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtRunQueueTailLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock, reach: DataReach.walked)

	// The timer store — count, heap base, capacity, sequence and earliest deadline — plus the trace counter
	// and the clock's out-param scratch words: what a mid-body yield parks on and wakes from.
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtTimerCountLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtTimerHeapBaseLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtTimerCapacityLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtTimerSeqLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underSchedLock, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtTimerWhenLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.publishedFromTheLock, reach: DataReach.walked, seed: TimerWhenEmpty)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtTraceCounterLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.atomicStep, reach: DataReach.walked)

	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtQuiesceWaiterLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.atomicStep, reach: DataReach.walked)

	// The adaptive seed starts at the floor, so the first spawn commits what every spawn committed before any
	// thread has finished.
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtSeedBytesLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.lastWriterWins, reach: DataReach.walked, seed: gtSeedStackBytes(target))
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtSeedSumSeenLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underOwnLock, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtSeedCountSeenLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.underOwnLock, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtSeedGuardLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.atomicStep, reach: DataReach.walked)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtKeepsCpuClockLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers, reach: DataReach.walked, seed: GtCpuClockKept if usage.usesThreadCpuTicks else GtCpuClockUnkept)
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: GtStackPoolLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.sealedBeforeWorkers, reach: DataReach.walked)
	return out
end 'gtRuntimeGlobals'

// The pipe substrate's one `.data` slot — the pipe-name uniquifier counter, 0 until the first pipe is
// created. It is read only by Std bodies, so it is laid out exactly where a surviving body names it.
export function ioRuntimeGlobals() returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: IoPipeSeqLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.atomicStep, reach: DataReach.walked)
	return out
end 'ioRuntimeGlobals'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **HOW A READ ON THIS TARGET FINISHES — the one fact about a pipe read that is not target-neutral.**
//
// Windows's overlapped `ReadFile` may answer `ERROR_IO_PENDING`: the read is IN FLIGHT and its completion will
// be delivered LATER, as a packet on the scheduler's poller. A descriptor read on a POSIX lane cannot be in
// flight: it either moves bytes or it does not, in the caller, before it returns.
//
// ⇒ under `inlineCompletion` no read is ever outstanding across a park, so there is nothing to cancel and
// `__gt_promise_drop`'s in-flight-read arm is unreachable.
//
// ⚠ **BOTH LANES PARK A READER ON THE POLLER, AND WHAT THEY PARK ON DIFFERS.** `__gt_subp_yield_read` forks on
// this shape: a completion port carries the READ, so the source is the OPERATION and its packet is what ends
// the wait; a POSIX read that cannot be served answers `EAGAIN`, which the errno translators map to
// `ERROR_IO_PENDING`, so the thread suspends on the DESCRIPTOR's poll record and re-issues the read when it is
// readable. Both arms are live; what differs is WHAT the thread is waiting on.
//
// ⚠ **THIS IS NOT `usesChildPipes`, AND CONFLATING THE TWO IS WHAT THE SPLIT FIXES.** `usesChildPipes` asks *"does this
// program read a pipe?"*; this asks *"does a read on this target complete somewhere else?"*, and only the
// second one is about the target.
export enum IoCompletionShape
	// Windows: the read is handed to a completion port and finishes as a packet on it.
	completionPort

	// POSIX and WASI: a read completes in its caller.
	inlineCompletion
end 'IoCompletionShape'

// ⚠ Keyed on the OS, like every other shape question in this compiler: whether a read can be in flight is a
// property of the platform's IO model, not of its instruction set.
export function ioCompletionShape(target Target) returns IoCompletionShape
	return match target.os 'os'
		windows gives IoCompletionShape.completionPort
		linux or
		macos or
		wasi gives IoCompletionShape.inlineCompletion
	end 'os'
end 'ioCompletionShape'

// What gives each GT field its value in a new tenancy. A spawn zeroes a field only when a reader
// can reach it before any writer of that tenancy; every other field holds the previous tenant's
// word until written, so a rule naming the wrong writer is a stale read. `requireGtRecordLayout`
// walks the roster, so a field left out of it panics at install.
enum GtFieldRule
	keptAcrossReuse
	setByFreeGet
	setBySpawn
	setBySpawnOnX64
	setBySpawnOfStrandOwners
	setBeforeRead
	zeroedBySpawn
	zeroedBySpawnOfStrandOwners
	zeroedBySpawnOfMain
	zeroedByReclaim
end 'GtFieldRule'

type GtField
	export let name as String
	export let offset as ByteOffset
	export let width as ByteOffset
	export let rule as GtFieldRule

	static function create(name String, offset ByteOffset, width ByteOffset, rule GtFieldRule) returns Self
		return Self{name: name, offset: offset, width: width, rule: rule}
	end 'create'
end 'GtField'

typealias GtFieldArray = Array with GtField

function gtFieldRoster() returns GtFieldArray
	var fields = GtFieldArray.create()

	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffRecordLock", offset: GtOffRecordLock, width: GtRecordLockBytes as ByteOffset, rule: GtFieldRule.keptAcrossReuse))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStackBase", offset: GtOffStackBase, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setByFreeGet))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStackSize", offset: GtOffStackSize, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setByFreeGet))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStackGuard", offset: GtOffStackGuard, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStatus", offset: GtOffStatus, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))

	// A dropped thread that never ran has its releaser read both
	// (`AsyncReleaser.emitResultOrErrorRelease`), so a stale result would be freed.
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffResult", offset: GtOffResult, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffThrew", offset: GtOffThrew, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))

	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffFuncPtr", offset: GtOffFuncPtr, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffNext", offset: GtOffNext, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffReleaser", offset: GtOffReleaser, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedByReclaim))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffTraceId", offset: GtOffTraceId, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawnOfMain))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffTeardown", offset: GtOffTeardown, width: GtTeardownBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffOwner", offset: GtOffOwner, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffAwaiter", offset: GtOffAwaiter, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffReplyWitness", offset: GtOffReplyWitness, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffRenounced", offset: GtOffRenounced, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffSp", offset: GtOffSp, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffFp", offset: GtOffFp, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffWaiter", offset: GtOffWaiter, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffArgBuf", offset: GtOffArgBuf, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffOverlapped", offset: GtOffOverlapped, width: GtOverlappedBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffIoStatus", offset: GtOffIoStatus, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffIoResult", offset: GtOffIoResult, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffTibStackBase", offset: GtOffTibStackBase, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBySpawnOnX64))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffTibStackLimit", offset: GtOffTibStackLimit, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBySpawnOnX64))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffIoSource", offset: GtOffIoSource, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffIoHandle", offset: GtOffIoHandle, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffIoMode", offset: GtOffIoMode, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffTibDeallocation", offset: GtOffTibDeallocation, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBySpawnOnX64))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffArgInline", offset: GtOffArgInline, width: ArgBufBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffCoroHead", offset: GtOffCoroHead, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawnOfStrandOwners))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffCoroTail", offset: GtOffCoroTail, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawnOfStrandOwners))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffParkKind", offset: GtOffParkKind, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffParkArg", offset: GtOffParkArg, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffParkGen", offset: GtOffParkGen, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffThreadAllocs", offset: GtOffThreadAllocs, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffThreadFrees", offset: GtOffThreadFrees, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffThreadAllocBytes", offset: GtOffThreadAllocBytes, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffCpuOffset", offset: GtOffCpuOffset, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffIsMainThread", offset: GtOffIsMainThread, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawnOfStrandOwners))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStrandHolder", offset: GtOffStrandHolder, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawnOfMain))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStrandRefs", offset: GtOffStrandRefs, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBySpawnOfStrandOwners))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffSelectDone", offset: GtOffSelectDone, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffRunqNext", offset: GtOffRunqNext, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStackMarks", offset: GtOffStackMarks, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStackResizedAt", offset: GtOffStackResizedAt, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStackHighWater", offset: GtOffStackHighWater, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStrandResume", offset: GtOffStrandResume, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffHold", offset: GtOffHold, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffPinnedM", offset: GtOffPinnedM, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffPinDepth", offset: GtOffPinDepth, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffLastError", offset: GtOffLastError, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.zeroedBySpawn))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffSendNext", offset: GtOffSendNext, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffSendPrev", offset: GtOffSendPrev, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffSendDeadline", offset: GtOffSendDeadline, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffSendOutcome", offset: GtOffSendOutcome, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.setBeforeRead))
	fields.push(GtField.create("GtOffStaging", offset: GtOffStaging, width: PointerFieldBytes, rule: GtFieldRule.keptAcrossReuse))

	return fields
end 'gtFieldRoster'

function requireGtRecordLayout()
	var cursor = 0 as ByteOffset
	var cellEdgeSeen = false

	for field in gtFieldRoster() 'eachField'
		if field.offset != cursor 'tiles'
			panic("requireGtRecordLayout: {field.name} is at offset {field.offset} but the field before it ends at {cursor} — the record's fields must tile it with no gap and no overlap")
		end 'tiles'

		for kind in GtSpawnKind.allCases 'eachKind'
			if gtFieldIsZeroedBySpawn(field.rule, kind: kind) and field.width mod PointerFieldBytes != 0 'wholeWords'
				panic("requireGtRecordLayout: {field.name} is zeroed a word at a time but is {field.width} bytes wide, which is not a multiple of {PointerFieldBytes} — the last {field.width mod PointerFieldBytes} bytes would keep the previous owner's contents")
			end 'wholeWords'
		end 'eachKind'

		if cursor == GtCellBytes 'cellEdge'
			cellEdgeSeen = true
		end 'cellEdge'

		cursor = cursor + field.width
	end 'eachField'

	if not cellEdgeSeen 'cellPrefixEnds'
		panic("requireGtRecordLayout: no field starts at GtCellBytes ({GtCellBytes}) — a cell is carved exactly that long, so a field straddling it reads beyond the cell")
	end 'cellPrefixEnds'

	if cursor != GtStructSize 'structEnds'
		panic("requireGtRecordLayout: the fields end at offset {cursor} but GtStructSize is {GtStructSize}")
	end 'structEnds'

	if GtOffStackGuard > i8.max 'guardReachesX64Disp8'
		panic("requireGtRecordLayout: the stack guard word is at offset {GtOffStackGuard}, past the {i8.max} a signed disp8 reaches, which the x64 prologue guard's compare is encoded with")
	end 'guardReachesX64Disp8'

	if not arm64ByteOffsetFitsScaledImm12(GtOffStackGuard, scale: Arm64SlotScale) 'guardReachesArm64Imm12'
		panic("requireGtRecordLayout: the stack guard word is at offset {GtOffStackGuard}, which has no scaled imm12 form for the arm64 prologue guard's ldr")
	end 'guardReachesArm64Imm12'
end 'requireGtRecordLayout'

function gtFieldIsZeroedBySpawn(rule GtFieldRule, kind GtSpawnKind) returns bool
	return match rule 'rule'
		zeroedBySpawn gives true
		zeroedBySpawnOfStrandOwners gives kind != GtSpawnKind.coroutine
		zeroedBySpawnOfMain gives kind == GtSpawnKind.mainThread
		keptAcrossReuse or
		setByFreeGet or
		setBySpawn or
		setBySpawnOnX64 or
		setBySpawnOfStrandOwners or
		setBeforeRead or
		zeroedByReclaim gives false
	end 'rule'
end 'gtFieldIsZeroedBySpawn'

function emitGtSpawnZeroing(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, kind GtSpawnKind, ids ValueMinter)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)

	for field in gtFieldRoster() 'eachField'
		if not gtFieldIsZeroedBySpawn(field.rule, kind: kind) 'keptAsItIs'
			continue
		end 'keptAsItIs'

		for word in 0 upto field.width / PointerFieldBytes 'eachWord'
			emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: field.offset + word * PointerFieldBytes, value: zero)
		end 'eachWord'
	end 'eachField'
end 'emitGtSpawnZeroing'

export function emitGtZeroOverlapped(module StdModule, block IrBlock, overlapped ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)

	for word in 0 upto GtOverlappedBytes / PointerFieldBytes 'eachWord'
		emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: overlapped, offset: word * PointerFieldBytes, value: zero)
	end 'eachWord'
end 'emitGtZeroOverlapped'

export function installGtRuntime(module StdModule, globalData GlobalDataTable, asyncTrace bool, target Target, streamState EnumLayout, releasesRegistry bool, releasesGlobals bool, requestStagingBytes ByteOffset)
	requireGtRecordLayout()

	// The first function index this install owns — the range `assertCompletedPublicationIsOrdered` checks at
	// the end. Taken from the module rather than assumed to be zero: the runtime installs run after the whole
	// user program is already lowered into this module.
	let firstFuncIndex = module.funcCount()

	// The tag records exist only in a traced build; an untraced one registers no rdata and emits no calls.
	var tracing = GtTracing.traced(GtTraceTags.register(globalData)) if asyncTrace else GtTracing.untraced

	// ⭐ Published on the MODULE before anything is built, because the nineteen `__gt_io_park` CALL SITES
	// live in three other files and cannot be handed the table: `emitIoPark` asks the module for the suffix its
	// operation prints, and `buildGtIoPark` asks the same field for the arity to receive it in. Threaded as an
	// argument instead, a park site added later that did not pass it along would emit a call with the WRONG
	// ARGUMENT COUNT rather than a compile error — the same reasoning `IrModule.debugStream` records.
	module.asyncTracing = tracing

	if let tags = try tracing.tags() 'traceEmitter'
		buildGtTraceEmit(module, tags: tags)
	end 'traceEmitter'

	// ⭐ The P/M half — the processor structure, the TLS door, the scheduler loop and the wake. FIRST in the
	// list because `__gt_init` calls `__sched_init_procs` before it does anything else.
	installSchedRuntime(module, target: target)
	buildGtInit(module)
	buildGtPinMachine(module)
	buildGtUnpinMachine(module)
	buildGtCurrentMachine(module)

	// ⭐ ALL THREE spawn doors, from one body. Whether a program can REACH one is `DeadFunctionElimination`'s
	// question, and answering it here would be a second reachability analysis that could disagree with it.
	buildGtSpawn(module, kind: GtSpawnKind.coroutine, tracing: tracing, target: target)
	buildGtSpawn(module, kind: GtSpawnKind.greenThread, tracing: tracing, target: target)
	buildGtSpawn(module, kind: GtSpawnKind.mainThread, tracing: tracing, target: target)
	buildGtReady(module)
	buildGtReadyDoor(module, gate: GtReadyGate.fromWaiting)
	buildGtReadyDoor(module, gate: GtReadyGate.alreadyRunnable)
	buildGtEnqueue(module)
	buildGtDequeue(module)

	// The strand queue's doors, beside the global FIFO's two they are modelled on.
	buildGtCoroEnqueue(module)
	buildGtCoroDequeue(module)
	buildGtStrandNext(module)
	buildGtCoroSweepDropped(module)
	buildGtRunStrand(module, target: target)
	buildGtStackShrink(module, target: target)
	buildGtStackAlloc(module)
	buildGtStackFree(module)
	buildGtSystemStackAlloc(module, target: target)
	buildGtSeedAdapt(module, target: target)
	buildGtPark(module)
	buildGtNowNs(module)
	buildGtTimerAdd(module)
	buildGtTimerCheck(module)
	buildGtAnyCompleted(module)
	buildGtAnyLock(module)
	buildGtAnyUnlock(module)
	buildGtAwaitAny(module)
	buildGtDrainAtExit(module, releasesRegistry: releasesRegistry, releasesGlobals: releasesGlobals)
	buildGtQuiesce(module)
	buildGtRunMain(module, target: target)
	buildGtAwait(module, name: GtAwaitName, throwing: false, arrival: GtAwaitArrival.inTheAwait, tracing: tracing)
	buildGtAwait(module, name: GtTryAwaitName, throwing: true, arrival: GtAwaitArrival.inTheAwait, tracing: tracing)
	buildGtAwait(module, name: GtTryAwaitHoldingName, throwing: true, arrival: GtAwaitArrival.inReplyWitness, tracing: tracing)
	buildGtReplyWitness(module)
	buildGtPromiseDrop(module)
	buildGtPromiseFieldRelease(module)
	buildGtPromiseFieldVacate(module)

	// The three halves of the teardown rendezvous, beside the drop and the two awaits that step it.
	// `__gt_reclaim` last, because it is what the other two call. See `GtOffTeardown` for the protocol.
	buildGtRunnerDone(module, target: target)
	buildGtOwnerDone(module)
	buildGtReclaim(module)

	// The non-blocking PEEK, beside the two awaits it exists as the alternative to. See `buildGtIsComplete` for
	// why it is unconditional where `__proc_pid` is bit-gated.
	buildGtIsComplete(module)

	// The reply cell's two doors, beside the rendezvous they step and the drop that renounces them.
	buildGtCellAlloc(module)
	buildGtCellComplete(module)
	buildGtSleep(module, tracing: tracing)

	// The park's registrar, beside the store doors it dispatches to.
	buildGtRegisterPark(module)
	buildGtResched(module)
	buildGtPreempt(module, target: target)
	buildGtIoPark(module)
	buildGtProcessRun(module, commandShape: subprocessCommandShape(target))

	// The shell wrapper `__gt_process_run` and the one-shot read probe take a command LINE through, on a lane
	// whose spawn primitive takes a vector. Beside its callers, for the reason its own header carries.
	installSubpShellCommandBuilder(module, target: target)

	// The pipe readers and the whole child-process API, for the spawn doors' reason: the only edges into them
	// are calls lowered from the program's own source, so `DeadFunctionElimination` keeps exactly the ones it
	// reaches, and an install gate here would be a second reachability analysis.
	buildSubpYieldRead(module, target: target)
	buildGtIoRead(module, commandShape: subprocessCommandShape(target))
	installSubpRuntime(module, target: target, streamState: streamState)

	// ⭐ **THE MAILBOX RIDES `usesGt` EXACTLY AS THE SCHEDULER DOES, AND FOR THE SAME REASON: there is no
	// program that wants one without the other.** `__svc_spawn` calls `__gt_spawn_green` and every mailbox
	// wake goes through `__gt_ready`; a program that never spawns a service loses every mailbox
	// function to `DeadFunctionElimination`, whose only edge into them is the `funcAddr`/`call` pair
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.lowerServiceSpawn` emits.
	installMailboxRuntime(module, requestStagingBytes: requestStagingBytes)

	assertCompletedPublicationIsOrdered(module, firstFuncIndex: firstFuncIndex)
	assertRunQueueObservationIsOrdered(module, firstFuncIndex: firstFuncIndex)
end 'installGtRuntime'

// ⚠ **THE RUN-QUEUE LOCK IS UNCONDITIONAL**: every green-thread program has a second cross-thread mutator (a
// worker M) as soon as `MAXON_MAX_PROCS` allows one. The queue primitives (`__gt_enqueue`, `__gt_dequeue`,
// `__gt_ready_locked`) take no lock of their own; the CALLER holds it, so a readiness walk can call them
// inside its own critical section.

// `__gt_init()` — scheduler bring-up, called once by `__gt_run_main` on the main machine's scheduler context
// before `main`'s green thread exists. Every later scheduler call can therefore take the P/M structure, the
// stores and the run-queue lock as given.
function buildGtInit(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtInitName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// The P/M half first: the TLS slot, every P, and this OS thread made M0 holding P[0], with its own
	// syscall stack and its own inline scheduler GT.
	let schedInitResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(schedInitResult, callee: SchedInitProcsName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))

	// The timer store's heap region: one `__slab_alloc` of TimerHeapBytes (slab-zeroed, so count starts at 0
	// entries), its base published to `__gt_timer_heap_base`. Done here rather than lazily in `__gt_timer_add`
	// so the add path stays a straight append with no allocation check.
	let heapSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(heapSizeConst, value: TimerHeapBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let heapBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(heapBase, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(heapSizeConst)))
	let heapBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(heapBaseAddr, label: GtTimerHeapBaseLabel))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(heapBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueId: heapBase, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// The store's CAPACITY is a runtime word, not the compile-time constant, because `__gt_timer_add` GROWS the
	// store when it fills (see its header). `TimerHeapCapacity` is only the SEED.
	let capSeedConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(capSeedConst, value: TimerHeapCapacity, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let capAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(capAddr, label: GtTimerCapacityLabel))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(capAddr, offset: 0, valueId: capSeedConst, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let poolRaw = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osAllocPages(poolRaw, sizeId: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: GtStackPoolHeaderBytes, ids: ids)))
	let pool = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(pool, callee: SlabAssertOsAllocName, args: argsOf(poolRaw)))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: GtStackPoolLabel, value: pool, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtInit'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE THREE KINDS OF GT THIS RUNTIME SPAWNS.** They are the two halves of `GtOffOwner`'s relation,
// named where the creation happens: an `async f(…)` frame is a COROUTINE of the green thread that called it,
// and a `spawn` and `main` are GREEN THREADS, which own themselves. `main`'s thread additionally carries the
// main-thread mark (`GtOffIsMainThread`), has no promise, so it takes no slot in `MOffGtLive`, and is not
// a spawn a trace names: it keeps trace id 0, which is what every async trace prints for `main`. Nothing else
// about a spawn differs, which is why this is an argument to ONE builder rather than three builders.
enum GtSpawnKind
	coroutine
	greenThread
	mainThread
end 'GtSpawnKind'

// Which entry point a kind is built under. Beside the kind rather than at the builder's `createFromStd` call,
// because the pairing of a kind with its symbol is a fact about the kind.
function gtSpawnNameOf(kind GtSpawnKind) returns ByteArray
	return match kind 'kind'
		coroutine gives GtSpawnName
		greenThread gives GtSpawnGreenName
		mainThread gives GtSpawnMainName
	end 'kind'
end 'gtSpawnNameOf'

// `__gt_spawn(funcPtr) -> gt` — create a ready green thread. Takes a GT struct with its seed stack
// from `__gt_free_get`, lays the initial context-switch frame so the first switch-in `ret`s into
// the trampoline, and points `gt.argBuf` at the struct's OWN inline arg region. Returns the GT
// pointer as the promise handle.
//
// ⭐ **THE ARG BUFFER IS NOT A PARAMETER.** It lives INLINE at `gt + GtOffArgInline` (the slots the lowering
// fills after this returns, safe because a spawned GT does not run until it is published); `gt.argBuf` is
// set to that self-pointer. See `GtOffArgInline` for why the region is inline.
//
// It does NOT run the GT — a spawned GT runs only once `__gt_ready` has published it.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **IT BUILDS `__gt_spawn`, `__gt_spawn_green` AND `__gt_spawn_main`, AND THEY DIFFER ONLY WHERE
// `GtSpawnKind` SAYS.** Everything else a spawn does — the struct, the guard, the TIB bounds,
// the initial context-switch frame — is identical for the three, so building them from three bodies would be
// one algorithm written three times with nothing making the copies agree.
function buildGtSpawn(module StdModule, kind GtSpawnKind, tracing GtTracing, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(gtSpawnNameOf(kind), returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var initBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: initBlock, count: 1)
	let funcPtr = 0 as ValueId

	let stackSizeVal = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: initBlock, label: GtSeedBytesLabel, ids: ids)
	let gt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.call(gt, callee: GtFreeGetName, args: argsOf(stackSizeVal)))

	emitGtSpawnZeroing(module, block: initBlock, gt: gt, kind: kind, ids: ids)

	// gt.argBuf = gt + GtOffArgInline — a SELF-POINTER into this struct's own inline arg region.
	// The trampoline reads `gt.argBuf` as a pointer and loads the slots from there, so pointing it at the inline
	// region makes the buffer part of the struct — one allocation, one lifetime, one free.
	let argInlineOffConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.const(argInlineOffConst, value: GtOffArgInline, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let argInlinePtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.binOp(argInlinePtr, lhs: gt, rhs: argInlineOffConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffArgBuf, valueId: argInlinePtr, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **gt.owner = currentGt.owner, AND A ZERO HERE IS NOT A BENIGN DEFAULT.** Every zero the
	// spawn's zeroing stores is the correct initial state (`GtOffStatus` is `ready`, `GtOffWaiter` is
	// *"never ran"*, the strand queue is *"empty"*); this word is outside it, so a freshly carved
	// record's owner is 0 and a recycled one's is its previous tenant's. An owner of 0 is a coroutine
	// owned by NOBODY, and the first ready of it would write a strand queue through a null pointer.
	//
	// ⭐ **A COROUTINE TAKES THE SPAWNER's OWNER, NOT THE SPAWNER**, so a coroutine spawned by a coroutine joins
	// the same strand; a green thread takes itself and heads a strand of its own.
	let owner = match kind 'ownerOf'
		coroutine gives emitLoadField(module, block: initBlock, record: emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: initBlock, ids: ids), offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids)
		greenThread or
		mainThread gives gt
	end 'ownerOf'

	emitStoreField(module, block: initBlock, record: gt, offset: GtOffOwner, value: owner)

	// The strand's reference for this thread's execution (`GtOffStrandRefs`): a coroutine adds one to
	// its owner's count. A green thread's own count starts at two, because `__gt_ready` hands it the
	// strand hold without the token publish that counts one (`emitGtStrandTokenPublish`); `main`'s
	// starts at one.
	let oneRef = emitConst(module, block: initBlock, value: OneMore, ids: ids)

	match kind 'strandRef'
		coroutine then _ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: initBlock, record: owner, offset: GtOffStrandRefs, delta: oneRef, multiM: true, ids: ids)
		greenThread then emitStoreField(module, block: initBlock, record: gt, offset: GtOffStrandRefs, value: emitConst(module, block: initBlock, value: StrandRefsOwnerAndHold, ids: ids))
		mainThread then emitStoreField(module, block: initBlock, record: gt, offset: GtOffStrandRefs, value: oneRef)
	end 'strandRef'

	match kind 'markMain'
		mainThread then emitStoreField(module, block: initBlock, record: gt, offset: GtOffIsMainThread, value: emitConst(module, block: initBlock, value: GtMainThreadMark, ids: ids))
		coroutine or
		greenThread then break
	end 'markMain'

	let stackBase = emitLoadField(module, block: initBlock, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackBase, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffFuncPtr, valueId: funcPtr, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// A seed above the floor is a size chosen now, and its first park must not halve it before the thread has had
	// a window to use it (`GtStackMarkResized`).
	emitStoreField(module, block: initBlock, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackMarks, value: emitConst(module, block: initBlock, value: GtStackMarkResized, ids: ids))

	let stackTop = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(initBlock, op: StdOp.binOp(stackTop, lhs: stackBase, rhs: stackSizeVal, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))

	emitGtRestoreStackGuard(module, block: initBlock, gt: gt, target: target, ids: ids)
	emitGtSeedInitialFrame(module, block: initBlock, gt: gt, stackBase: stackBase, stackTop: stackTop, target: target, ids: ids)

	var seeded = initBlock

	match kind 'countedAndNamed'
		coroutine or
		greenThread then seeded = emitGtSpawnCountAndTraceId(module, func: func, block: seeded, gt: gt, tracing: tracing, ids: ids)
		mainThread then break
	end 'countedAndNamed'

	// Runtime functions skip the stack guard, so without this a loop that only spawns never reads a pending
	// request, and at one processor nothing it spawned runs until it parks.
	match kind 'preemptionPoint'
		coroutine or
		greenThread then seeded = emitGtHonourPreemptRequest(module, func: func, block: seeded, ids: ids)
		mainThread then break
	end 'preemptionPoint'

	// ⚠ **THE GT IS NOT PUBLISHED HERE.** It is returned UNQUEUED, and the lowering calls `__gt_ready` once
	// every argument slot and the releaser are written — see `GtReadyName` for the wrong answer that ordering
	// exists to prevent.
	module.setTerminator(seeded, op: StdOp.ret(gt))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtSpawn'

function emitGtHonourPreemptRequest(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var honour = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnpreempt", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var resume = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnresume", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let gt = emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnPreemptRequest(module, block: block, gt: gt, requested: honour, notRequested: resume, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(honour, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtPreemptName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	module.setTerminator(honour, op: StdOp.branch(resume.id))

	return resume
end 'emitGtHonourPreemptRequest'

export function emitBranchOnPreemptRequest(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, requested IrBlock, notRequested IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let guard = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackGuard, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: block, value: guard, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtStackGuardPreempt, whenTrue: requested, whenFalse: notRequested, ids: ids)
end 'emitBranchOnPreemptRequest'

// `gt.stackGuard = gt.stackBase + gtStackGuardHeight` — the low-water mark a guarded prologue compares its
// projected stack pointer against, and what clears a preemption request. The spawn seeds it, a switch-in and
// `__gt_preempt` restore it; `__gt_stack_relocate` spells the same sum in its own hand-assembly.
function emitGtRestoreStackGuard(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, target Target, ids ValueMinter)
	let stackBase = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackBase, ids: ids)
	let guard = emitBin(module, block: block, a: stackBase, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: gtStackGuardHeight(target), ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackGuard, value: guard)
end 'emitGtRestoreStackGuard'

// 1 when a stack allocation's answer `stackBase` is a refusal, for `__gt_free_get`'s seed and a
// shrink's smaller stack alike. The test is the sign, for `__slab_assert_os_alloc`'s reason:
// `VirtualAlloc` refuses with NULL and a raw `mmap` with a negative errno.
export function emitGtStackRefused(module StdModule, block IrBlock, stackBase ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: stackBase, pred: StdCmpPred.le, against: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtStackRefused'

// A promise-bearing spawn's two numbers: one green thread onto the live count, which `__gt_owner_done` lowers
// by one per promise so a clean exit balances it to zero; and, in a build that reads it, the trace id (see
// `GtOffTraceId`). Returns the block to continue in, because the DebugStream event splits it.
function emitGtSpawnCountAndTraceId(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, tracing GtTracing, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	emitSchedStepGtLive(module, block: block, delta: OneMore, ids: ids)

	var current = block

	if tracing.isTraced() or module.debugStream 'assignTraceId'
		var minted = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawntraceid", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
		let traceId = ids.mint()
		minted.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(traceId, argType: StdType.i64))
		emitGtMintTraceId(module, func: func, cur: current, minted: minted, ids: ids)
		current = minted

		module.appendOp(current, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(gt, offset: GtOffTraceId, valueId: traceId, valueType: StdType.i64))

		if let tags = try tracing.tags() 'traceTheSpawn'
			emitGtTraceLine(module, block: current, tag: tags.spawn, gtId: traceId, suffix: GtTraceSuffix.none, ids: ids)
		end 'traceTheSpawn'
	end 'assignTraceId'

	return emitSchedEventOf(module, func: func, block: current, eventType: DsEvSchedSpawn, gt: gt, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtSpawnCountAndTraceId'

function emitGtMintTraceId(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, minted IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var refill = module.addBlock(func, label: b"traceidrefill", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var take = module.addBlock(func, label: b"traceidtake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let p = emitLoadCurrentP(module, block: cur, ids: ids)
	let next = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: p, offset: POffTraceIdNext, ids: ids)
	let batchEnd = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: p, offset: POffTraceIdEnd, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: cur, a: next, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: batchEnd, whenTrue: refill, whenFalse: take, ids: ids)
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(take.id, argIds: argsOf(next)))

	let batch = emitConst(module, block: refill, value: GtTraceIdBatch, ids: ids)
	let prior = emitGlobalAccumulate(module, block: refill, label: GtTraceCounterLabel, delta: batch, multiM: true, ids: ids)
	let first = emitBin(module, block: refill, a: prior, b: emitConst(module, block: refill, value: OneMore, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let past = emitBin(module, block: refill, a: prior, b: emitConst(module, block: refill, value: GtTraceIdBatch + OneMore, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: refill, record: p, offset: POffTraceIdEnd, value: past)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: refill, to: take, value: first)

	let id = ids.mint()
	take.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(id, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitStoreField(module, block: take, record: p, offset: POffTraceIdNext, value: emitBin(module, block: take, a: id, b: emitConst(module, block: take, value: OneMore, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: take, to: minted, value: id)
end 'emitGtMintTraceId'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **SEED A FRESH GREEN THREAD'S INITIAL CONTEXT-SWITCH FRAME — the ONE place the two ISAs disagree
// STRUCTURALLY rather than numerically, which is why it is a per-target ARM and not a per-target constant.**
//
// Both lanes lay `gtInitialFrameBytes(target)` bytes at the top of the fresh stack, leave every word the
// mapping's zeros (they become this green thread's initial callee-saved registers, each established by the
// user function before it is read), write `&__gt_trampoline` into the ONE word the first switch-in takes its
// return address from, and point `gt.sp` at the bottom of the block. What differs is WHICH word that is:
//
//   • **x64** ends its switch in `ret`, which POPS. So the trampoline's address is the frame's TOP word,
//     `[stackTop - 8]`, and the fifteen saved-register words sit below it.
//   • **arm64**'s `ret` branches to x30, which the switch's LAST `ldp` restores — and the switch pushes the
//     x29/x30 record last, so that pair sits at the LOWEST address. The trampoline's address therefore goes
//     to `[frameBase + 8]`, the record's second word, with `frameBase == gt.sp` itself. x29's slot beside it
//     stays zero, which is what stops the panic backtrace at a green thread's boundary.
//
// ⚠ **THE TIB BOUNDS ARE SEEDED ON x64 ONLY, AND THAT IS NOT AN OMISSION HERE.** `GtOffTibStackBase`/
// `GtOffTibStackLimit` exist because a Win32 kernel call PROBES `gs:[0x08]`/`gs:[0x10]` and faults when RSP
// is outside them, so the syscall shim and the context switch must keep those two words describing the stack
// that is executing. Darwin has no such contract: nothing reads the fields on that lane, and writing them
// would be two stores maintaining a fact with no reader — see `Arm64GtRuntime`'s header, which states the
// same split from the other side.
function emitGtSeedInitialFrame(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, stackBase ValueId, stackTop ValueId, target Target, ids ValueMinter)
	let frameBytes = emitConst(module, block: block, value: gtInitialFrameBytes(target), ids: ids)
	let frameBase = emitBin(module, block: block, a: stackTop, b: frameBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.memFill(frameBase, byteCountId: frameBytes, value: 0))
	let trampAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.funcAddr(trampAddr, funcName: GtTrampolineName))

	match target.cpu 'cpu'
		x64 then emitGtSeedX64ReturnWord(module, block: block, gt: gt, stackBase: stackBase, stackTop: stackTop, trampAddr: trampAddr, ids: ids)
		arm64 then emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: frameBase, offset: Arm64GtSeedLinkRegisterOffset, value: trampAddr)
		wasm32 then panic("emitGtSeedInitialFrame: wasm32 has no context switch to seed a frame for — `targetProvidesFacility` denies `greenThreads` there, and reaching here means a compiler-inserted `__gt_spawn` got past that gate")
	end 'cpu'

	// gt.sp = the bottom of the saved block; gt.fp is the slab zero on both lanes.
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffSp, value: frameBase)
end 'emitGtSeedInitialFrame'

// The trampoline address's offset within the arm64 seed frame: the SECOND word of the x29/x30 record the
// context switch pushes last, i.e. the x30 half. `StackWordBytes` rather than a bare 8 — it is a stack word,
// which is exactly what that constant names.
let Arm64GtSeedLinkRegisterOffset = StackWordBytes as ByteOffset

// x64's half of the seed: the trampoline address in the frame's TOP word, plus the NT_TIB bounds this GT's
// stack occupies. `tibStackBase = stackTop` (high address), `tibStackLimit = stackBase` (low);
// `__gt_stack_relocate` moves both when a grow or a shrink moves the stack.
function emitGtSeedX64ReturnWord(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, stackBase ValueId, stackTop ValueId, trampAddr ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffTibStackBase, value: stackTop)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffTibStackLimit, value: stackBase)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffTibDeallocation, value: stackBase)
	let retSlotAddr = emitBin(module, block: block, a: stackTop, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: StackWordBytes, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: retSlotAddr, offset: 0, value: trampAddr)
end 'emitGtSeedX64ReturnWord'

// `__gt_ready(gt)` — make a spawned GT discoverable. See `GtReadyName` for why this is not the tail
// of `__gt_spawn`. A coroutine is readied through `__gt_ready_runnable` and joins its owner's
// strand. A green thread heads a new strand whose token goes to this processor's `runnext` slot,
// and is born holding it: no other thread can reach the record yet, so its holder and resume words
// take plain stores, which the run queue put's release publishes.
function buildGtReady(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtReadyName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var greenArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readygreen", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var coroArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readycoro", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readywake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readydone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0

	let isGreen = emitGtIsGreenThread(module, block: entry, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isGreen, thenBlockId: greenArm.id, elseBlockId: coroArm.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: greenArm, record: gt, offset: GtOffStrandHolder, value: emitConst(module, block: greenArm, value: StrandHeld, ids: ids))
	emitStoreField(module, block: greenArm, record: gt, offset: GtOffStrandResume, value: gt)
	emitSchedRunqPut(module, block: greenArm, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(greenArm, op: StdOp.branch(wake.id))

	let debt = emitGtReady(module, block: coroArm, gt: gt, gate: GtReadyGate.alreadyRunnable, ids: ids)
	emitSchedWakeIfOwed(module, cur: coroArm, owed: debt, wake: wake, cont: doneBlock, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtReady'

export function emitGtReady(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, gate GtReadyGate, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let debt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(debt, callee: gtReadyNameOf(gate), args: argsOf(gt)))
	return debt
end 'emitGtReady'

export enum GtReadyGate
	fromWaiting
	alreadyRunnable
end 'GtReadyGate'

function gtReadyNameOf(gate GtReadyGate) returns ByteArray
	return match gate 'gate'
		fromWaiting gives GtReadyLockedName
		alreadyRunnable gives GtReadyRunnableName
	end 'gate'
end 'gtReadyNameOf'

// `__gt_ready_locked` and `__gt_ready_runnable`, which differ only in the status gate. See
// `GtReadyLockedName` and `GtReadyRunnableName`.
//
// ⭐ **THE OWNER GOES TO THE FRONT OF ITS STRAND QUEUE AND A COROUTINE TO THE BACK.** A readied owner is what
// its strand was waiting on; a coroutine joins behind whatever its strand already has runnable.
//
// ⭐⭐ **PUSH, THEN CLAIM — ONE HALF OF A DEKKER PAIR WITH `__gt_strand_next`'s RELEASE.** The member
// is on the strand queue before this side's CAS on the holder word; the releaser decrements that
// word and then re-reads the queue head with an acquire, re-claiming by CAS if it is not empty.
// Each side makes a locked read-modify-write on the holder before it reads the other's half, so
// either this claim finds the strand free or the releaser finds the member, and no strand is given
// up past a queued member. The claim being a CAS is what makes two claimers unable to both win. An
// owner whose first claim wins is not queued at all: it becomes the strand's resume member
// (`GtOffStrandResume`).
function buildGtReadyDoor(module StdModule, gate GtReadyGate)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(gtReadyNameOf(gate), returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var ownerArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readyowner", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var ownerWon = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readyownerwon", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var ownerQueue = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readyownerqueue", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var coroArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readycoro", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var claim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readyclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var alreadyHeld = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readyheld", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var publish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readypublish", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0

	var statusOk = emitGtReadyGate(module, func: func, cur: entry, gt: gt, gate: gate, ids: ids)
	let owner = emitLoadField(module, block: statusOk, record: gt, offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids)
	let isOwner = emitCmp(module, block: statusOk, a: owner, b: gt, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(statusOk, op: StdOp.condBranch(isOwner, thenBlockId: ownerArm.id, elseBlockId: coroArm.id))

	let ownerHeld = emitGtStrandClaimLost(module, block: ownerArm, owner: owner, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ownerArm, op: StdOp.condBranch(ownerHeld, thenBlockId: ownerQueue.id, elseBlockId: ownerWon.id))

	emitGtPublishClaimedOwner(module, block: ownerWon, owner: owner, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: ownerWon, value: WakeDebtOwed, ids: ids)

	emitGtRecordLockEnter(module, block: ownerQueue, record: owner, ids: ids)
	var ownerQueued = emitGtFifoPushFront(module, func: func, cur: ownerQueue, ends: GtQueueEnds.ownerFields(owner), g: gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(ownerQueued, op: StdOp.branch(claim.id))

	emitGtRecordLockEnter(module, block: coroArm, record: owner, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(coroArm, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtCoroEnqueueName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	module.setTerminator(coroArm, op: StdOp.branch(claim.id))

	let heldByAnother = emitGtStrandClaimLost(module, block: claim, owner: owner, ids: ids)
	emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: claim, record: owner, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(claim, op: StdOp.condBranch(heldByAnother, thenBlockId: alreadyHeld.id, elseBlockId: publish.id))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: alreadyHeld, value: WakeDebtNone, ids: ids)

	emitGtStrandTokenPublish(module, block: publish, owner: owner, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: publish, value: WakeDebtOwed, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtReadyDoor'

function emitGtReadyGate(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, gt ValueId, gate GtReadyGate, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	return match gate 'gate'
		fromWaiting gives emitGtCasWaitingToReady(module, func: func, cur: cur, gt: gt, ids: ids)
		alreadyRunnable gives emitGtRequireRunnable(module, func: func, cur: cur, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	end 'gate'
end 'emitGtReadyGate'

function emitGtRequireRunnable(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var notRunnable = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readynotrunnable", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var runnable = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readyrunnable", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let status = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: gt, offset: GtOffStatus, ids: ids)
	let isReady = emitGtStatusEquals(module, block: cur, status: status, want: GtStatus.ready, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(isReady, thenBlockId: runnable.id, elseBlockId: notRunnable.id))
	emitRuntimeFatal(module, func: func, block: notRunnable, fatal: RuntimeFatal.badGreenThreadStatusInReadyRunnable, ids: ids)

	return runnable
end 'emitGtRequireRunnable'

function emitGtCasStatus(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, from GtStatus, to GtStatus, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitCasField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffStatus as ByteOffset, expected: emitConst(module, block: block, value: from.rawValue, ids: ids), replacement: emitConst(module, block: block, value: to.rawValue, ids: ids), ids: ids)
end 'emitGtCasStatus'

function emitGtCasWaitingToReady(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var lost = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readybadstatus", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var won = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readystatusok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let result = emitGtCasStatus(module, block: cur, gt: gt, from: GtStatus.waiting, to: GtStatus.ready, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: cur, result: result, lost: lost, won: won, ids: ids)
	emitRuntimeFatal(module, func: func, block: lost, fatal: RuntimeFatal.badGreenThreadStatusInReady, ids: ids)

	return won
end 'emitGtCasWaitingToReady'

export function emitGtMarkWaiting(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffStatus, value: emitConst(module, block: block, value: GtStatus.waiting.rawValue, ids: ids))
end 'emitGtMarkWaiting'

function emitGtRecordLockEnter(module StdModule, block IrBlock, record ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitLockEnterAt(module, block: block, lock: emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: record, offset: GtOffRecordLock as ByteOffset, ids: ids), kind: RuntimeLockKind.recursive, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtRecordLockEnter'

function emitGtRecordLockLeave(module StdModule, block IrBlock, record ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitLockLeaveAt(module, block: block, lock: emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: record, offset: GtOffRecordLock as ByteOffset, ids: ids), kind: RuntimeLockKind.recursive, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtRecordLockLeave'

function emitGtStrandClaimLost(module StdModule, block IrBlock, owner ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let won = emitCasField(module, block: block, record: owner, offset: GtOffStrandHolder as ByteOffset, expected: emitConst(module, block: block, value: StrandFree, ids: ids), replacement: emitConst(module, block: block, value: StrandHeld, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	return emitCasLost(module, block: block, result: won, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtStrandClaimLost'

function emitGtStrandTokenPublish(module StdModule, block IrBlock, owner ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: block, record: owner, offset: GtOffStrandRefs, delta: emitConst(module, block: block, value: OneMore, ids: ids), multiM: true, ids: ids)
	emitSchedRunqPut(module, block: block, gt: owner, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtStrandTokenPublish'

function emitGtPublishClaimedOwner(module StdModule, block IrBlock, owner ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: owner, offset: GtOffStrandResume, value: owner)
	emitGtStrandTokenPublish(module, block: block, owner: owner, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtPublishClaimedOwner'

// `service` must be a green thread's own record, its strand's owner: a mailbox park happens only on
// a service's own green thread, so no owner is loaded. A coroutine here would claim a holder word
// nothing reads and publish itself as a token.
export function emitGtReadyParkedService(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, service ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var lockFree = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readyservicefree", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var locked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readyservicelocked", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var lockedWake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readyservicewake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let heldByAnother = emitGtStrandClaimLost(module, block: cur, owner: service, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(heldByAnother, thenBlockId: locked.id, elseBlockId: lockFree.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: lockFree, record: service, offset: GtOffStatus, value: emitConst(module, block: lockFree, value: GtStatus.ready.rawValue, ids: ids))
	emitGtPublishClaimedOwner(module, block: lockFree, owner: service, ids: ids)
	emitSchedWakeAnIdleM(module, block: lockFree, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(lockFree, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))

	let debt = emitGtReady(module, block: locked, gt: service, gate: GtReadyGate.fromWaiting, ids: ids)
	emitSchedWakeIfOwed(module, cur: locked, owed: debt, wake: lockedWake, cont: cont, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtReadyParkedService'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHERE ONE INTRUSIVE FIFO'S TWO ENDS LIVE AND WHICH LINK IT THREADS — the only things the global run
// queue and a strand queue differ in, and therefore the only things the emitters below take.**
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE TWO QUEUES ARE ONE ALGORITHM, SO THEY HAVE ONE BODY PER OPERATION.** The intrusive-list protocol
// is ONE fact — the `next = 0` seed, the empty/append split, the tail clear when the queue drains, the
// tombstone refusal — and a clause added to one copy and not its twin would read as correct at both sites.
export union GtQueueEnds
	// The GLOBAL run queue — its head and tail are two `.data` words, and it threads `GtOffRunqNext`.
	globalLabels

	// A green thread's strand queue — its head and tail are two fields of the struct `owner` addresses, and it
	// threads `GtOffNext`. `owner` must be minted in a block that dominates every block the emitter writes into.
	ownerFields(owner ValueId)
end 'GtQueueEnds'

// The link field a queue threads. The global queue has its own because an owner's token can wait there while
// the owner itself sits in its strand queue (`GtOffRunqNext`).
function emitQueueLinkOffset(ends GtQueueEnds) returns ByteOffset
	return match ends 'ends'
		globalLabels gives GtOffRunqNext as ByteOffset
		ownerFields gives GtOffNext as ByteOffset
	end 'ends'
end 'emitQueueLinkOffset'

// The four one-word accesses a FIFO's ends need, and the pop CALL that goes with them. The head load is
// exported because every emptiness PEEK (`SchedRuntime.emitSchedGlobalQueueIs`) reads through it, so a peek
// cannot read a word the FIFO does not.
export function emitQueueLoadHead(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return match ends 'ends'
		globalLabels gives emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: GtRunQueueHeadLabel, ids: ids)
		ownerFields(owner) gives emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: owner, offset: GtOffCoroHead, ids: ids)
	end 'ends'
end 'emitQueueLoadHead'

function emitQueueStoreHead(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, value ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	match ends 'ends'
		globalLabels then emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: GtRunQueueHeadLabel, value: value, ids: ids)
		ownerFields(owner) then module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeRelease(owner, offset: GtOffCoroHead, valueId: value, valueType: StdType.i64))
	end 'ends'
end 'emitQueueStoreHead'

function emitQueueLoadTail(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return match ends 'ends'
		globalLabels gives emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: GtRunQueueTailLabel, ids: ids)
		ownerFields(owner) gives emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: owner, offset: GtOffCoroTail, ids: ids)
	end 'ends'
end 'emitQueueLoadTail'

function emitQueueStoreTail(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, value ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	match ends 'ends'
		globalLabels then emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: GtRunQueueTailLabel, value: value, ids: ids)
		ownerFields(owner) then emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: owner, offset: GtOffCoroTail, value: value)
	end 'ends'
end 'emitQueueStoreTail'

// The runtime function that pops this queue's head, called with whatever it needs to NAME the queue
// — the global one is a `.data` pair and takes no argument, an owner's is the owner. Its result is
// discarded: the one caller (`emitGtFifoSweepDropped`) already holds the head it is about to free,
// and under the queue's lock (`emitQueueLockEnter`) the pop and the peek name the same GT.
function emitQueuePopCall(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, ids ValueMinter)
	match ends 'ends'
		globalLabels then module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtDequeueName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
		ownerFields(owner) then module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtCoroDequeueName, args: argsOf(owner)))
	end 'ends'
end 'emitQueuePopCall'

function emitQueueLockEnter(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, ids ValueMinter)
	match ends 'ends'
		globalLabels then emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: block, ids: ids)
		ownerFields(owner) then emitGtRecordLockEnter(module, block: block, record: owner, ids: ids)
	end 'ends'
end 'emitQueueLockEnter'

function emitQueueLockLeave(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, ids ValueMinter)
	match ends 'ends'
		globalLabels then emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: block, ids: ids)
		ownerFields(owner) then emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: block, record: owner, ids: ids)
	end 'ends'
end 'emitQueueLockLeave'

// ⭐⭐ **APPEND `g` AT THE TAIL — the ONE body `__gt_enqueue` and `__gt_coro_enqueue` are.** The caller owns
// the three blocks and the ends; both arms return, because an append has nothing to do afterwards.
function emitGtFifoAppend(module StdModule, entry IrBlock, appendBlock IrBlock, emptyBlock IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, g ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let link = emitQueueLinkOffset(ends)
	let zeroConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: g, offset: link, value: zeroConst)

	let tail = emitQueueLoadTail(module, block: entry, ends: ends, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: tail, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: emptyBlock, whenFalse: appendBlock, ids: ids)

	// Non-empty: tail.next = g; tail = g.
	emitStoreField(module, block: appendBlock, record: tail, offset: link, value: g)
	emitQueueStoreTail(module, block: appendBlock, ends: ends, value: g, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(appendBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	// Empty: head = tail = g.
	emitQueueStoreHead(module, block: emptyBlock, ends: ends, value: g, ids: ids)
	emitQueueStoreTail(module, block: emptyBlock, ends: ends, value: g, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(emptyBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
end 'emitGtFifoAppend'

// ⭐ **PUSH `g` AT THE HEAD** — the one queue operation only a strand queue needs, for its owner. It terminates
// `cur` and answers the block to continue in.
function emitGtFifoPushFront(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, g ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var setTail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pushfronttail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pushed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pushfrontdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let head = emitQueueLoadHead(module, block: cur, ends: ends, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: g, offset: emitQueueLinkOffset(ends), value: head)
	emitQueueStoreHead(module, block: cur, ends: ends, value: g, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: head, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: setTail, whenFalse: pushed, ids: ids)

	emitQueueStoreTail(module, block: setTail, ends: ends, value: g, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(setTail, op: StdOp.branch(pushed.id))
	return pushed
end 'emitGtFifoPushFront'

// ⭐⭐ **POP THE HEAD — the ONE body `__gt_dequeue` and `__gt_coro_dequeue` are.** `head` is minted in
// `entry`, which dominates every block below, so `doneBlock` returns it in both cases: 0 when the queue was
// empty, the popped GT otherwise. It sets the return terminator itself, which is why it hands nothing back.
function emitGtFifoPop(module StdModule, entry IrBlock, popBlock IrBlock, clearTail IrBlock, doneBlock IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, ids ValueMinter)
	let head = emitQueueLoadHead(module, block: entry, ends: ends, ids: ids)
	let zeroConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: head, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: doneBlock, whenFalse: popBlock, ids: ids)

	// head = head.next; if the new head is null the queue has drained → clear the tail too.
	let next = emitLoadField(module, block: popBlock, record: head, offset: emitQueueLinkOffset(ends), ids: ids)
	emitQueueStoreHead(module, block: popBlock, ends: ends, value: next, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: popBlock, a: next, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: zeroConst, whenTrue: clearTail, whenFalse: doneBlock, ids: ids)

	emitQueueStoreTail(module, block: clearTail, ends: ends, value: zeroConst, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(clearTail, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.ret(head))
end 'emitGtFifoPop'

// ⭐⭐ **RECLAIM THE TOMBSTONES AT THE FRONT OF ONE FIFO — the ONE body `__gt_coro_sweep_dropped` and
// `__sched_sweep_dropped`'s GLOBAL half are.** The caller terminates `cur` into this walk; the walk leaves
// through `cont`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **IT IS WHAT KEEPS A NEVER-SCHEDULED PROGRAM's MEMORY BOUNDED.** `__gt_promise_drop` hands back the half
// a dropped promise owns — its managed arguments and its slot in the live count — but the STACK and the
// STRUCT can only be freed by a party allowed to take the GT out of a queue. A program that spawns and drops in
// a loop and never awaits would otherwise pile them up.
//
// ⚠ **THE FRONT ONLY.** It stops at the first live entry, so the work is O(entries actually reclaimed); a
// tombstone sitting BEHIND a live one is left for the queue's popper (`emitGtTombstoneHandback`).
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE LOCK IS THIS WALK'S OWN, AND IT IS RELEASED ACROSS EVERY `__gt_runner_done`**, which must never run
// under `__sched_lock` (`GtRunnerDoneName`). Re-taking it at each iteration is not a weakening: the pop is the
// claim, made in the same critical section as the peek that identified the thread.
//
// ⚠ EXPORTED for `SchedRuntime.buildSchedSweepDropped`'s global half, which is its other caller.
export function emitGtFifoSweepDropped(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, cont IrBlock, ends GtQueueEnds, ids ValueMinter)
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"fifosweephdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var peek = module.addBlock(func, label: b"fifosweeppeek", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var free = module.addBlock(func, label: b"fifosweepfree", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var leaveEmpty = module.addBlock(func, label: b"fifosweepempty", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var leaveLive = module.addBlock(func, label: b"fifosweeplive", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	emitQueueLockEnter(module, block: loopHdr, ends: ends, ids: ids)
	let head = emitQueueLoadHead(module, block: loopHdr, ends: ends, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: loopHdr, value: head, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: leaveEmpty, whenFalse: peek, ids: ids)

	let headDropped = emitGtIsDropped(module, block: peek, gt: head, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(peek, op: StdOp.condBranch(headDropped, thenBlockId: free.id, elseBlockId: leaveLive.id))

	// The pop is the claim and it comes FIRST, so nothing can reach the GT once the lock is released.
	emitQueuePopCall(module, block: free, ends: ends, ids: ids)
	emitQueueLockLeave(module, block: free, ends: ends, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(free, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtRunnerDoneName, args: twoArgs(head, second: emitConst(module, block: free, value: GtNoHeldStrand, ids: ids))))
	module.setTerminator(free, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	// The two ways out both release. They are two blocks rather than one because they are reached from two
	// different tests and a merge block would need a block argument to carry nothing.
	emitQueueLockLeave(module, block: leaveEmpty, ends: ends, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(leaveEmpty, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
	emitQueueLockLeave(module, block: leaveLive, ends: ends, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(leaveLive, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitGtFifoSweepDropped'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **HAND A CANDIDATE BACK, OR RECLAIM IT AND LOOK AGAIN — the ONE body `__gt_strand_next`'s tail and
// `__sched_find_runnable`'s tail are.** `candidate` carries whatever the queue answered as its single block
// argument (`found`); 0 means it answered nothing. A GT renounced while it was still QUEUED is a TOMBSTONE
// (`emitGtIsDropped`), and handing one back would RUN a thread whose promise is gone — so the pop is followed
// by the test, a reclaim, and a branch to `retry`.
//
// ⚠ **`retry` IS THE ONLY THING THE TWO CALLERS DIFFER IN.** `__sched_find_runnable` retries at `localPop` so a
// reclaimed thread costs no second FAIRNESS TICK; a strand queue has no tiers to be fair between, so
// `__gt_strand_next` retries at the top of its own pop.
//
// ⚠ **THE RECLAIM RUNS WITH THE QUEUE's LOCK RELEASED AT BOTH CALLERS**, and the POP is what makes
// that safe: a GT taken off a queue is unreachable by anyone else.
export union GtHandOffAnswer
	giveTheProcessor(answer FullRangeInt)
	giveUpTheStrand(strandOwner ValueId)
end 'GtHandOffAnswer'

export function emitGtTombstoneHandback(module StdModule, func IrFunction, candidate IrBlock, found ValueId, retry IrBlock, requeue ByteArray, handOff GtHandOffAnswer, ids ValueMinter)
	var noWork = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tsnowork", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var checkDropped = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tscheckdropped", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var reclaim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tsreclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var hand = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshand", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: candidate, value: found, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: noWork, whenFalse: checkDropped, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: noWork, value: 0, ids: ids)

	let wasDropped = emitGtIsDropped(module, block: checkDropped, gt: found, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkDropped, op: StdOp.condBranch(wasDropped, thenBlockId: reclaim.id, elseBlockId: hand.id))
	emitGtHandOffGate(module, func: func, hand: hand, found: found, retry: retry, requeue: requeue, handOff: handOff, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(reclaim, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtRunnerDoneName, args: twoArgs(found, second: emitConst(module, block: reclaim, value: GtNoHeldStrand, ids: ids))))
	module.setTerminator(reclaim, op: StdOp.branch(retry.id))
end 'emitGtTombstoneHandback'

let GtRestrainedRefusePauseUs = 200

export let GtProcessorHandedOff = -1 as FullRangeInt

let GtAllGSeedSlots = 64

let GtAllGSlotBytes = 8

function emitGtStartLockedOwner(module StdModule, func IrFunction, startLocked IrBlock, owner ValueId, found ValueId, declined IrBlock, handOff GtHandOffAnswer, ids ValueMinter)
	match handOff 'shape'
		giveTheProcessor(answer) then emitGtGiveTheProcessorToTheLockedOwner(module, func: func, startLocked: startLocked, owner: owner, found: found, declined: declined, answer: answer, ids: ids)
		giveUpTheStrand then module.setTerminator(startLocked, op: StdOp.branch(declined.id))
	end 'shape'
end 'emitGtStartLockedOwner'

function emitGtGiveTheProcessorToTheLockedOwner(module StdModule, func IrFunction, startLocked IrBlock, owner ValueId, found ValueId, declined IrBlock, answer FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter)
	var gave = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshslmgave", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	emitSchedStartLockedMachine(module, func: func, cur: startLocked, owner: owner, strand: found, gave: gave, declined: declined, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: gave, value: answer, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtGiveTheProcessorToTheLockedOwner'

// A refusal at the hand-over moves nothing: the candidate goes back on the tail of the queue it came off and
// the machine looks again, so a restrained thread stays inside the scheduler's own structures, nothing is
// parked on a chain the runtime cannot see, and a release needs no wake.
//
// ⚠ `requeue` is the CALLER's own door, not one door. `__sched_find_runnable`'s candidate is an owner token of
// the global queue and `__gt_strand_next`'s is a member of its owner's strand queue; re-queueing either
// through the other's door loses the thread.
//
// The back-off fires what is due before retrying, because the retry target sits below `findRunnable`'s
// `checkTimers` and the short sleep is a `SwitchToThread` on this lane: without it a refused hand-over
// starves every sleeping thread in the process.
function emitGtHandOffGate(module StdModule, func IrFunction, hand IrBlock, found ValueId, retry IrBlock, requeue ByteArray, handOff GtHandOffAnswer, ids ValueMinter)
	var examine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshexamine", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var checkPin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshcheckpin", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var comparePin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshcomparepin", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var holdItBack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshholdback", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var putItBack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshputback", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var startLocked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshstartlocked", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var noOwner = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshnoowner", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var wakeOwner = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshwakeowner", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var backOff = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshbackoff", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var fireDue = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshfiredue", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var payWake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshpaywake", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var handOver = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshhandover", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let armed = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: hand, label: GtHoldEpochLabel, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: hand, value: armed, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: handOver, whenFalse: examine, ids: ids)

	let hold = emitLoadField(module, block: examine, record: found, offset: GtOffHold, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: examine, value: hold, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtHoldRequested, whenTrue: holdItBack, whenFalse: checkPin, ids: ids)

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: holdItBack, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(holdItBack, op: StdOp.branch(putItBack.id))

	let pinned = emitLoadField(module, block: checkPin, record: found, offset: GtOffPinnedM, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: checkPin, value: pinned, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: handOver, whenFalse: comparePin, ids: ids)

	let here = emitLoadCurrentM(module, block: comparePin, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: comparePin, a: pinned, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: here, whenTrue: handOver, whenFalse: startLocked, ids: ids)

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: startLocked, ids: ids)
	emitGtStartLockedOwner(module, func: func, startLocked: startLocked, owner: pinned, found: found, declined: putItBack, handOff: handOff, ids: ids)

	let owner = emitLoadField(module, block: putItBack, record: found, offset: GtOffPinnedM, ids: ids)

	match handOff 'requeueUnder'
		giveTheProcessor then module.appendOp(putItBack, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: requeue, args: argsOf(found)))
		giveUpTheStrand(strandOwner) then emitGtRequeueUnderStrandLock(module, block: putItBack, strandOwner: strandOwner, requeue: requeue, found: found, ids: ids)
	end 'requeueUnder'

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: putItBack, value: owner, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: noOwner, whenFalse: wakeOwner, ids: ids)

	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: noOwner, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(noOwner, op: StdOp.branch(backOff.id))

	view afterBackOff = emitGtHandOffWake(module, func: func, wakeOwner: wakeOwner, owner: owner, backOff: backOff, retry: retry, handOff: handOff, ids: ids)

	_ = emitNetpoll(module, block: backOff, verb: NetpollVerb.shortSleep, operands: argsOf(emitConst(module, block: backOff, value: GtRestrainedRefusePauseUs, ids: ids)), ids: ids)

	let checkedAt = emitGtBranchOnTimerDue(module, func: func, cur: backOff, due: fireDue, notDue: afterBackOff, notDueArgs: ValueIdArray.create(), ids: ids)
	let timerDebt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fireDue, op: StdOp.call(timerDebt, callee: GtTimerCheckName, args: argsOf(checkedAt)))
	emitSchedWakeIfOwed(module, cur: fireDue, owed: timerDebt, wake: payWake, cont: afterBackOff, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(handOver, op: StdOp.ret(found))
end 'emitGtHandOffGate'

function emitGtRequeueUnderStrandLock(module StdModule, block IrBlock, strandOwner ValueId, requeue ByteArray, found ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitGtRecordLockEnter(module, block: block, record: strandOwner, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: requeue, args: argsOf(found)))
	emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: block, record: strandOwner, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtRequeueUnderStrandLock'

function emitGtHandOffWake(module StdModule, func IrFunction, wakeOwner IrBlock, owner ValueId, backOff IrBlock, retry IrBlock, handOff GtHandOffAnswer, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	return match handOff 'shape'
		giveTheProcessor(answer) gives emitGtHandOffToOwnersMachine(module, func: func, wakeOwner: wakeOwner, owner: owner, backOff: backOff, retry: retry, answer: answer, ids: ids)
		giveUpTheStrand(strandOwner) gives emitGtHandOffGivingUpTheStrand(module, func: func, wakeOwner: wakeOwner, owner: owner, backOff: backOff, strandOwner: strandOwner, ids: ids)
	end 'shape'
end 'emitGtHandOffWake'

function emitGtHandOffToOwnersMachine(module StdModule, func IrFunction, wakeOwner IrBlock, owner ValueId, backOff IrBlock, retry IrBlock, answer FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var gave = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshgave", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	emitSchedHandProcessorToOwner(module, func: func, cur: wakeOwner, owner: owner, handedOff: gave, kept: backOff, ids: ids)
	_ = emitNetpoll(module, block: gave, verb: NetpollVerb.shortSleep, operands: argsOf(emitConst(module, block: gave, value: GtRestrainedRefusePauseUs, ids: ids)), ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: gave, value: answer, ids: ids)

	return retry
end 'emitGtHandOffToOwnersMachine'

function emitGtHandOffGivingUpTheStrand(module StdModule, func IrFunction, wakeOwner IrBlock, owner ValueId, backOff IrBlock, strandOwner ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var gave = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshgavestrand", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var giveUp = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tshgiveupstrand", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	emitSchedHandProcessorToOwner(module, func: func, cur: wakeOwner, owner: owner, handedOff: gave, kept: backOff, ids: ids)

	emitGtPublishStrandToken(module, block: gave, strandOwner: strandOwner, ids: ids)
	_ = emitNetpoll(module, block: gave, verb: NetpollVerb.shortSleep, operands: argsOf(emitConst(module, block: gave, value: GtRestrainedRefusePauseUs, ids: ids)), ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: gave, value: GtProcessorHandedOff, ids: ids)

	emitGtPublishStrandToken(module, block: giveUp, strandOwner: strandOwner, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: giveUp, value: 0, ids: ids)

	return giveUp
end 'emitGtHandOffGivingUpTheStrand'

function emitGtPublishStrandToken(module StdModule, block IrBlock, strandOwner ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtEnqueueName, args: argsOf(strandOwner)))
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	emitSchedWakeAnIdleM(module, block: block, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtPublishStrandToken'

export let GtPinMachineName = b"__gt_pin_m"

export let GtUnpinMachineName = b"__gt_unpin_m"

export let GtCurrentMachineName = b"__gt_current_m"

function buildGtCurrentMachine(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtCurrentMachineName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"curmoffset", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"curmmachine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var noMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"curmnone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let m = emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(module, cur: entry, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, bail: noMachine, bailValues: FullRangeIntArray.create(), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveMachine, op: StdOp.ret(m))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: noMachine, value: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtCurrentMachine'

function emitGtReleasePin(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, m ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let released = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffPinDepth, value: released)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffPinnedM, value: released)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffLockedGt, value: released)
	_ = emitGlobalAccumulate(module, block: block, label: GtHoldEpochLabel, delta: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0 - GtRestraintArmed, ids: ids), multiM: true, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtReleasePin'

// ⛔ The pin is recorded on the running thread's STRAND OWNER (`GtOffOwner`), never on `M->currentGt` itself.
// A driver asks for it from a coroutine, and `__sched_find_runnable` hands the gate an owner token, so a pin
// on the member would be invisible there and the session's thread would migrate anyway.
function buildGtPinMachine(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtPinMachineName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pinoffset", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pinmachine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveThread = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pinthread", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var refused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pinrefused", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let m = emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(module, cur: entry, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, bail: refused, bailValues: FullRangeIntArray.create(), ids: ids)
	let running = emitLoadField(module, block: haveMachine, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentGt, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: haveMachine, value: running, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: refused, whenFalse: haveThread, ids: ids)

	var armRestraint = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pinarm", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pinTaken = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pintaken", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let gt = emitLoadField(module, block: haveThread, record: running, offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids)
	let heldDepth = emitLoadField(module, block: haveThread, record: gt, offset: GtOffPinDepth, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: haveThread, record: gt, offset: GtOffPinDepth, value: emitBin(module, block: haveThread, a: heldDepth, b: emitConst(module, block: haveThread, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids))
	emitStoreField(module, block: haveThread, record: gt, offset: GtOffPinnedM, value: m)
	emitStoreField(module, block: haveThread, record: m, offset: MOffLockedGt, value: gt)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: haveThread, value: heldDepth, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: armRestraint, whenFalse: pinTaken, ids: ids)

	_ = emitGlobalAccumulate(module, block: armRestraint, label: GtHoldEpochLabel, delta: emitConst(module, block: armRestraint, value: GtRestraintArmed, ids: ids), multiM: true, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(armRestraint, op: StdOp.branch(pinTaken.id))

	emitConstReturn(module, block: pinTaken, value: 1, ids: ids)

	emitConstReturn(module, block: refused, value: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtPinMachine'

function buildGtUnpinMachine(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtUnpinMachineName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"unpinoffset", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"unpinmachine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveThread = module.addBlock(func, label: b"unpinthread", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveHold = module.addBlock(func, label: b"unpinhold", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var releaseIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"unpinrelease", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var keepOne = module.addBlock(func, label: b"unpinkeepone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"unpindone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let m = emitLoadCurrentMOrBail(module, cur: entry, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, bail: doneBlock, bailValues: FullRangeIntArray.create(), ids: ids)
	let running = emitLoadField(module, block: haveMachine, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentGt, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: haveMachine, value: running, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: doneBlock, whenFalse: haveThread, ids: ids)

	let gt = emitLoadField(module, block: haveThread, record: running, offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids)
	let heldDepth = emitLoadField(module, block: haveThread, record: gt, offset: GtOffPinDepth, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: haveThread, value: heldDepth, pred: StdCmpPred.le, against: 0, whenTrue: doneBlock, whenFalse: haveHold, ids: ids)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: haveHold, value: heldDepth, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 1, whenTrue: releaseIt, whenFalse: keepOne, ids: ids)

	emitGtReleasePin(module, block: releaseIt, gt: gt, m: m, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(releaseIt, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: keepOne, record: gt, offset: GtOffPinDepth, value: emitBin(module, block: keepOne, a: heldDepth, b: emitConst(module, block: keepOne, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(keepOne, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtUnpinMachine'

export function emitGtAllGAppend(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var grow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"allggrow", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var copyHead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"allgcopyhead", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var copyBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"allgcopybody", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var publish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"allgpublish", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var store = module.addBlock(func, label: b"allgstore", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"allgdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitLockWordEnter(module, block: cur, label: GtAllGLockLabel, kind: RuntimeLockKind.exclusive, ids: ids)
	let len = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: GtAllGLenLabel, ids: ids)
	let cap = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: GtAllGCapLabel, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: cur, a: len, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: cap, whenTrue: grow, whenFalse: store, ids: ids)

	let copied = ids.mint()
	copyHead.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(copied, argType: StdType.i64))

	let twice = emitBin(module, block: grow, a: cap, b: cap, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let wanted = emitBin(module, block: grow, a: twice, b: emitConst(module, block: grow, value: GtAllGSeedSlots, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let wantedBytes = emitBin(module, block: grow, a: wanted, b: emitConst(module, block: grow, value: GtAllGSlotBytes, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let fresh = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.osAllocPages(fresh, sizeId: wantedBytes))
	let stale = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: grow, label: GtAllGLabel, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: grow, value: fresh, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: done, whenFalse: copyHead, ids: ids)
	grow.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyHead.id, argIds: argsOf(emitConst(module, block: grow, value: 0, ids: ids))))

	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: copyHead, a: copied, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: len, whenTrue: publish, whenFalse: copyBody, ids: ids)

	let copyOffset = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: copied, b: emitConst(module, block: copyBody, value: GtAllGSlotBytes, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let staleSlot = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: stale, b: copyOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let word = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(copyBody, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(word, addrId: staleSlot, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let into = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: fresh, b: copyOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(copyBody, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(into, offset: 0, valueId: word, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let stepped = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: copied, b: emitConst(module, block: copyBody, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(copyBody, op: StdOp.branch(copyHead.id))
	copyBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyHead.id, argIds: argsOf(stepped)))

	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: publish, label: GtAllGCapLabel, value: wanted, ids: ids)
	emitStoreReleaseGlobalWord(module, block: publish, label: GtAllGLabel, value: fresh, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(publish, op: StdOp.branch(store.id))

	let base = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: store, label: GtAllGLabel, ids: ids)
	let roomLen = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: store, label: GtAllGLenLabel, ids: ids)
	let storeOffset = emitBin(module, block: store, a: roomLen, b: emitConst(module, block: store, value: GtAllGSlotBytes, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitBin(module, block: store, a: base, b: storeOffset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(store, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(slot, offset: 0, valueId: gt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let grown = emitBin(module, block: store, a: roomLen, b: emitConst(module, block: store, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreReleaseGlobalWord(module, block: store, label: GtAllGLenLabel, value: grown, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(store, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	emitLockWordLeave(module, block: done, label: GtAllGLockLabel, kind: RuntimeLockKind.exclusive, ids: ids)
	return done
end 'emitGtAllGAppend'

// `__gt_enqueue(g)` — append owner token `g` to the tail of the global FIFO run queue. The caller holds
// `__sched_lock`.
//
// ⭐ **IT AND `__gt_dequeue` ARE THE ONLY TWO FUNCTIONS THAT MOVE THE GLOBAL QUEUE, SO THEY ARE WHERE
// `__sched_runq_size` IS STEPPED** — no caller can move the queue without moving its count. The cumulative
// `__sched_global_pushes` is stepped here for the same reason and on this side only: it counts arrivals, so
// nothing a dequeue does may take it back down.
function buildGtEnqueue(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtEnqueueName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var appendBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"append", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var emptyBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"empty", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	emitSchedStepLockedCount(module, block: entry, label: SchedRunqSizeLabel, delta: OneMore, ids: ids)
	emitSchedStepLockedCount(module, block: entry, label: SchedGlobalPushesLabel, delta: OneMore, ids: ids)
	emitGtFifoAppend(module, entry: entry, appendBlock: appendBlock, emptyBlock: emptyBlock, ends: GtQueueEnds.globalLabels, g: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtEnqueue'

// `__gt_dequeue() -> g` — pop the head token off the global FIFO run queue, or 0 if empty. The caller holds
// `__sched_lock`. The count comes down on the arm that really popped, which `popBlock` is.
function buildGtDequeue(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtDequeueName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var popBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var clearTail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cleartail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSchedStepLockedCount(module, block: popBlock, label: SchedRunqSizeLabel, delta: OneFewer, ids: ids)
	emitGtFifoPop(module, entry: entry, popBlock: popBlock, clearTail: clearTail, doneBlock: doneBlock, ends: GtQueueEnds.globalLabels, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtDequeue'

// `__gt_coro_enqueue(gt)` — append `gt` to its owner's strand queue. `__gt_enqueue`'s body off the
// owner's struct. The caller holds the owner's record lock.
//
// ⚠ **THE OWNER IS READ OFF THE GT AND NOT FROM TLS**, because a waker may be an OS thread with no M — a
// machine draining the poller with no processor — and the GT it recovered knows which strand it belongs to.
function buildGtCoroEnqueue(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtCoroEnqueueName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var appendBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"append", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var emptyBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"empty", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let g = 0

	// `entry` dominates both arms, so the owner is read once here and the ends name it throughout.
	let owner = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: g, offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids)
	emitGtFifoAppend(module, entry: entry, appendBlock: appendBlock, emptyBlock: emptyBlock, ends: GtQueueEnds.ownerFields(owner), g: g, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtCoroEnqueue'

// `__gt_coro_dequeue(owner) -> gt | 0` — pop the head of `owner`'s strand queue, clearing the tail
// when the queue empties. `__gt_dequeue`'s body, off the owner struct. The caller holds the owner's
// record lock.
function buildGtCoroDequeue(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtCoroDequeueName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var popBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var clearTail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cleartail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	emitGtFifoPop(module, entry: entry, popBlock: popBlock, clearTail: clearTail, doneBlock: doneBlock, ends: GtQueueEnds.ownerFields(0 as ValueId), ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtCoroDequeue'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_strand_next(owner, parked) -> gt | 0` — WHAT THIS STRAND RUNS NEXT, OR 0 HAVING GIVEN
// THE STRAND UP.** The strand runner's consume door: the resume slot (`GtOffStrandResume`) or else
// the pop, and the tombstone refusal `emitGtTombstoneHandback` shares with `__sched_find_runnable`.
//
// `parked` is the member that has just parked, or 0, which is also what a mailbox park passes once
// `__gt_run_strand` has committed it. Its `__gt_register_park` runs before the pop, and the wake
// debt the registration answers is paid with no lock held on every road out. A tombstone retry
// re-enters at `snpop` and registers nothing again.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **AN EMPTY QUEUE RELEASES THE STRAND AND THEN LOOKS AGAIN** — the other half of
// `buildGtReadyDoor`'s Dekker pair: a member pushed before the decrement is seen by the acquire
// re-read and the strand re-claimed, and one pushed after finds the holder word 0 and publishes a
// token. The strand hold's reference is dropped only once the strand is given up
// (`emitGtStrandUnref`), which may reclaim `owner` — so the caller touches `owner` no more once
// this answers 0.
function buildGtStrandNext(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtStrandNextName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var first = module.addBlock(func, label: b"snfirst", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var registerPark = module.addBlock(func, label: b"snregister", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"snpop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var take = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sntake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var resume = module.addBlock(func, label: b"snresume", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var peek = module.addBlock(func, label: b"snpeek", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var dequeue = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sndequeue", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var dequeued = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sndequeued", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"snrelease", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var recheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"snrecheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var reclaim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"snreclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var gaveUp = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sngaveup", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var gotOne = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sngot", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var candidate = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sncandidate", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let owner = 0 as ValueId
	let parked = 1
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(first.id))

	let found = ids.mint()
	candidate.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(found, argType: StdType.i64))

	let owed = ids.mint()
	take.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(owed, argType: StdType.i64))

	let nothingParked = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: first, value: parked, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, ids: ids)
	let noDebt = emitConst(module, block: first, value: WakeDebtNone, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(first, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingParked, thenBlockId: take.id, elseBlockId: registerPark.id))
	first.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(take.id, argIds: argsOf(noDebt)))

	let registered = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(registerPark, op: StdOp.call(registered, callee: GtRegisterParkName, args: argsOf(parked)))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: registerPark, to: take, value: registered)

	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: pop, to: take, value: emitConst(module, block: pop, value: WakeDebtNone, ids: ids))

	let resumed = emitLoadField(module, block: take, record: owner, offset: GtOffStrandResume, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: take, value: resumed, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: peek, whenFalse: resume, ids: ids)

	emitStoreField(module, block: resume, record: owner, offset: GtOffStrandResume, value: emitConst(module, block: resume, value: 0, ids: ids))
	var resumePaid = emitGtPayDebtAfterRelease(module, func: func, cur: resume, owed: owed, label: b"snresume", ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(resumePaid, op: StdOp.branch(candidate.id))
	resumePaid.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(resumed)))

	let queued = emitLoadAcquireField(module, block: peek, record: owner, offset: GtOffCoroHead, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: peek, value: queued, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: release, whenFalse: dequeue, ids: ids)

	emitGtRecordLockEnter(module, block: dequeue, record: owner, ids: ids)
	let popped = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(dequeue, op: StdOp.call(popped, callee: GtCoroDequeueName, args: argsOf(owner)))
	emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: dequeue, record: owner, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: dequeue, value: popped, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: dequeued, whenFalse: gotOne, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: dequeued, to: take, value: owed)

	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: release, record: owner, offset: GtOffStrandHolder, delta: emitConst(module, block: release, value: OneFewer, ids: ids), multiM: true, ids: ids)
	let requeued = emitLoadAcquireField(module, block: release, record: owner, offset: GtOffCoroHead, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: release, value: requeued, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: gaveUp, whenFalse: recheck, ids: ids)

	let reclaimLost = emitGtStrandClaimLost(module, block: recheck, owner: owner, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(recheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(reclaimLost, thenBlockId: gaveUp.id, elseBlockId: reclaim.id))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: reclaim, to: take, value: owed)

	var gaveUpPaid = emitGtPayDebtAfterRelease(module, func: func, cur: gaveUp, owed: owed, label: b"snrelease", ids: ids)
	var unreffed = emitGtStrandUnref(module, func: func, cur: gaveUpPaid, owner: owner, ids: ids)
	emitConstReturn(module, block: unreffed, value: 0, ids: ids)

	var gotPaid = emitGtPayDebtAfterRelease(module, func: func, cur: gotOne, owed: owed, label: b"sngot", ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(gotPaid, op: StdOp.branch(candidate.id))
	gotPaid.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(candidate.id, argIds: argsOf(popped)))

	emitGtTombstoneHandback(module, func: func, candidate: candidate, found: found, retry: pop, requeue: GtCoroEnqueueName, handOff: GtHandOffAnswer.giveUpTheStrand(owner), ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtStrandNext'

function emitGtPayDebtAfterRelease(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, owed ValueId, label ByteArray, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var payWake = module.addBlock(func, label: bytesWithSuffix(b"wake", suffix: label), heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var paid = module.addBlock(func, label: bytesWithSuffix(b"paid", suffix: label), heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSchedWakeIfOwed(module, cur: cur, owed: owed, wake: payWake, cont: paid, ids: ids)
	return paid
end 'emitGtPayDebtAfterRelease'

// ⭐⭐ **DROP ONE OF `owner`'s STRAND REFERENCES (`GtOffStrandRefs`), AND ARRIVE AS ITS RUNNER IF IT WAS THE
// LAST.** It terminates `cur` and answers the block to continue in, where `owner` may already be on a free
// list. Never under `__sched_lock`, for `GtRunnerDoneName`'s reason: the arrival may reclaim.
function emitGtStrandUnref(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, owner ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var arrive = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandlastref", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandunreffed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let prior = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: cur, record: owner, offset: GtOffStrandRefs, delta: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: OneFewer, ids: ids), multiM: true, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: prior, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 1, whenTrue: arrive, whenFalse: done, ids: ids)

	var arrived = emitGtArriveAndReclaimIfSecond(module, func: func, cur: arrive, gt: owner, ticket: GtTeardownRunnerTicket, otherTicket: GtTeardownConsumerTicket, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(arrived, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))
	return done
end 'emitGtStrandUnref'

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_coro_sweep_dropped(owner)` — RECLAIM THE TOMBSTONES AT THE FRONT OF THIS GREEN THREAD's
// STRAND QUEUE.** Its one caller is `__gt_promise_drop`, which is what puts one there; the walk itself is
// `emitGtFifoSweepDropped`. `sched-runqueue.a-spawn-drop-loop-stays-bounded` is the measurement that it is not
// optional.
function buildGtCoroSweepDropped(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtCoroSweepDroppedName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"csdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let owner = 0 as ValueId

	// ⚠ **NO LOCK HERE — the walk takes and releases its own, per iteration**, because it calls
	// `__gt_runner_done`, which must never run under `__sched_lock`.
	emitGtFifoSweepDropped(module, func: func, cur: entry, cont: doneBlock, ends: GtQueueEnds.ownerFields(owner), ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtCoroSweepDropped'

// `__gt_now_ns()`, into a fresh value.
export function emitGtNowNs(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let now = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(now, callee: GtNowNsName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	return now
end 'emitGtNowNs'

// `__gt_now_ns() -> i64` — the monotonic clock, nanoseconds since boot. `osReadClock` reads the QPC counter
// and QPF frequency into two frame slots; the ticks→ns scale is done HERE in builder-built Std, via
// the OVERFLOW-SAFE split identity `ns = (ticks/freq)*1e9 + ((ticks mod freq)*1e9)/freq`. Both terms stay
// inside i64 for any realistic QPF (Windows fixes it at 10 MHz; even a TSC-derived few-GHz value keeps
// `(freq-1)*1e9 < 2^63`) and centuries of uptime — so no 128-bit MUL/DIV, and no new Target op, is needed.
function buildGtNowNs(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtNowNsName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// The two out-params live in THIS CALL's frame, not in `.data` — see `GtClockTicksSlot`.
	func.stackSize = GtClockScratchBytes
	let freqAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.stackRecordAddr(freqAddr, slot: GtClockFreqSlot))
	let ticksAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.stackRecordAddr(ticksAddr, slot: GtClockTicksSlot))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osReadClock(ticksAddr, freqPtrId: freqAddr))
	let ticks = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(ticks, addrId: ticksAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let freq = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(freq, addrId: freqAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))

	let billion = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(billion, value: NanosPerSecond, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// wholeSecs = ticks / freq ; wholeNs = wholeSecs * 1e9.
	let wholeSecs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.div(wholeSecs, lhs: ticks, rhs: freq, signedness: IntegerSignedness.signed))
	let wholeNs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(wholeNs, lhs: wholeSecs, rhs: billion, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))

	// fracNs = ((ticks mod freq) * 1e9) / freq — the sub-second remainder, exact and overflow-free.
	let rem = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.mod(rem, lhs: ticks, rhs: freq, signedness: IntegerSignedness.signed))
	let remNs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(remNs, lhs: rem, rhs: billion, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let fracNs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.div(fracNs, lhs: remNs, rhs: freq, signedness: IntegerSignedness.signed))
	let ns = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(ns, lhs: wholeNs, rhs: fracNs, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(ns))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtNowNs'

// ⭐⭐ **THE (deadline, gt, seq) TRIPLE OF ONE TIMER ENTRY, IN REGISTERS — and the ONE place the entry's
// field set is written down.** Every heap operation below moves WHOLE entries: the two sift walks exchange a
// pair, a pop moves the last entry over the root, `__gt_promise_drop`'s remove moves it over an arbitrary
// hole. Each of those is `emitTimerEntryLoad` + `emitTimerEntryStore`, so adding a field to the entry is an
// edit to exactly those two functions and every mover learns about it at once.
//
// ⚠ It REPLACES `emitTimerSwapDown`, which enumerated the fields for a one-directional COPY. That was the
// same anti-duplication device for the one mover the flat store had; a heap has three, and one of them is an
// exchange rather than a copy, so a copy-shaped helper would have needed a second enumeration beside it.
type TimerEntry
	export let deadline as ValueId
	export let gt as ValueId
	export let seq as ValueId
	export let tag as ValueId

	static function create(deadline ValueId, gt ValueId, seq ValueId, tag ValueId) returns TimerEntry
		return Self{deadline: deadline, gt: gt, seq: seq, tag: tag}
	end 'create'
end 'TimerEntry'

// `&heap[index]` — the `base + index * TimerEntrySize` arithmetic every heap site needs. It was written out
// longhand at NINE sites while the store was flat, and the heap has TWELVE.
function emitTimerEntryAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, heapBase ValueId, index ValueId) returns ValueId
	let entrySize = emitConst(module, block: block, value: TimerEntrySize, ids: ids)
	let offset = emitBin(module, block: block, a: index, b: entrySize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: heapBase, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitTimerEntryAddr'

// Read the entry at `addr` into registers.
function emitTimerEntryLoad(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, addr ValueId) returns TimerEntry
	let deadline = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: addr, offset: TimerOffDeadline, ids: ids)
	let gt = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: addr, offset: TimerOffGt, ids: ids)
	let seq = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: addr, offset: TimerOffSeq, ids: ids)
	let tag = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: addr, offset: TimerOffTag, ids: ids)
	return TimerEntry.create(deadline, gt: gt, seq: seq, tag: tag)
end 'emitTimerEntryLoad'

// Write `entry` to the slot at `addr`.
function emitTimerEntryStore(module StdModule, block IrBlock, addr ValueId, entry TimerEntry)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: addr, offset: TimerOffDeadline, value: entry.deadline)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: addr, offset: TimerOffGt, value: entry.gt)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: addr, offset: TimerOffSeq, value: entry.seq)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: addr, offset: TimerOffTag, value: entry.tag)
end 'emitTimerEntryStore'

// ⭐⭐ **THE HEAP'S ORDER, STATED ONCE: `a` comes before `b` iff `(a.deadline, a.seq) < (b.deadline, b.seq)`.**
// The sequence half is what makes the order TOTAL — see the store's header — and it is what makes the answer
// to a tie "the one armed first" rather than "whichever the sifts left on top". Every comparison in this
// family asks it here, so no site can key on the deadline alone by accident.
//
// BRANCH-FREE on purpose. A comparison's value lives in the 0/1 domain — `StdToX64Conversion`'s
// `BoolFalseImm` states that contract for x64, and `cset` and `i64.lt_s` give the same domain on the other
// two lanes — so the lexicographic test is `lt | (eq & seqLt)`, five ops in the caller's own block against
// the four-block diamond a branching form would cost at each of the four sites that ask.
function emitTimerEntryBefore(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, a TimerEntry, b TimerEntry) returns ValueId
	let earlier = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: a.deadline, b: b.deadline, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	let sameDeadline = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: a.deadline, b: b.deadline, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let armedFirst = emitCmp(module, block: block, a: a.seq, b: b.seq, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, ids: ids)
	let wonTheTie = emitBin(module, block: block, a: sameDeadline, b: armedFirst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: earlier, b: wonTheTie, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitTimerEntryBefore'

// Restore the heap UPWARDS from `startIndex`, then branch to `cont`: while the entry there comes before its
// parent, exchange the two and follow it up. O(log n) exchanges.
//
// The caller has just placed an entry at `startIndex` that may be too EARLY for where it sits — the append
// in `__gt_timer_add`, or the moved-last entry in `emitGtTimerStoreRemove` — and everything else is already a
// heap, so walking the ancestor chain is the whole repair.
function emitTimerSiftUp(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, startBlock IrBlock, heapBase ValueId, startIndex ValueId, cont IrBlock)
	var hdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"siftuphdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"siftupbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var exchange = module.addBlock(func, label: b"siftupswap", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	module.setTerminator(startBlock, op: StdOp.branch(hdr.id))
	startBlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(hdr.id, argIds: argsOf(startIndex)))

	// hdr(i): the root has no parent, so it is where every walk stops.
	let i = ids.mint()
	hdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: hdr, value: i, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: cont, whenFalse: body, ids: ids)

	// body: parent = (i - 1) >> 1. `i >= 1` on this edge, so the numerator is non-negative and the logical
	// shift is the halving. Both entries are read here, which is also what the exchange needs.
	let oneConst = emitConst(module, block: body, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let iMinusOne = emitBin(module, block: body, a: i, b: oneConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let parent = emitBin(module, block: body, a: iMinusOne, b: oneConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let childAddr = emitTimerEntryAddr(module, block: body, ids: ids, heapBase: heapBase, index: i)
	let parentAddr = emitTimerEntryAddr(module, block: body, ids: ids, heapBase: heapBase, index: parent)
	let child = emitTimerEntryLoad(module, block: body, ids: ids, addr: childAddr)
	let parentEntry = emitTimerEntryLoad(module, block: body, ids: ids, addr: parentAddr)
	let beatsParent = emitTimerEntryBefore(module, block: body, ids: ids, a: child, b: parentEntry)
	module.setTerminator(body, op: StdOp.condBranch(beatsParent, thenBlockId: exchange.id, elseBlockId: cont.id))

	// exchange: both entries are already in registers, so the swap is four stores and no reload.
	emitTimerEntryStore(module, block: exchange, addr: childAddr, entry: parentEntry)
	emitTimerEntryStore(module, block: exchange, addr: parentAddr, entry: child)
	module.setTerminator(exchange, op: StdOp.branch(hdr.id))
	exchange.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(hdr.id, argIds: argsOf(parent)))
end 'emitTimerSiftUp'

// Restore the heap DOWNWARDS from `startIndex` over the first `count` entries, then branch to `cont`: while
// the earlier of the two children comes before the entry, exchange and follow it down. O(log n) exchanges.
//
// The caller has just placed an entry at `startIndex` that may be too LATE for where it sits — the
// last live entry moved over the hole a timer pop or remove opened, or a heap sort's build or
// extraction step (`__gt_any_lock`).
function emitHeapSiftDown(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, startBlock IrBlock, heap SiftHeap, count ValueId, startIndex ValueId, cont IrBlock)
	var hdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"siftdownhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveLeft = module.addBlock(func, label: b"siftdownhaveleft", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var chooseChild = module.addBlock(func, label: b"siftdownchoose", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var takeLeft = module.addBlock(func, label: b"siftdowntakeleft", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var takeRight = module.addBlock(func, label: b"siftdowntakeright", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var compare = module.addBlock(func, label: b"siftdowncompare", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var exchange = module.addBlock(func, label: b"siftdownswap", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	module.setTerminator(startBlock, op: StdOp.branch(hdr.id))
	startBlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(hdr.id, argIds: argsOf(startIndex)))

	// hdr(i): left = 2i + 1. No left child means `i` is a leaf, and a leaf is where every walk stops.
	let i = ids.mint()
	hdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	let oneConst = emitConst(module, block: hdr, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let twoI = emitBin(module, block: hdr, a: i, b: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let left = emitBin(module, block: hdr, a: twoI, b: oneConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: hdr, a: left, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: count, whenTrue: cont, whenFalse: haveLeft, ids: ids)

	// haveLeft: right = left + 1. Past the live region and the left child is the only candidate.
	let rightOne = emitConst(module, block: haveLeft, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let right = emitBin(module, block: haveLeft, a: left, b: rightOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: haveLeft, a: right, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: count, whenTrue: takeLeft, whenFalse: chooseChild, ids: ids)

	// chooseChild: both children exist, so the candidate is whichever comes first — and the tie rule decides
	// when their deadlines match, which is what keeps two same-deadline entries in arming order.
	let leftAddr = emitSiftEntryAddr(module, block: chooseChild, ids: ids, heap: heap, index: left)
	let rightAddr = emitSiftEntryAddr(module, block: chooseChild, ids: ids, heap: heap, index: right)
	let leftEntry = emitSiftEntryLoad(module, block: chooseChild, ids: ids, heap: heap, addr: leftAddr)
	let rightEntry = emitSiftEntryLoad(module, block: chooseChild, ids: ids, heap: heap, addr: rightAddr)
	let rightWins = emitSiftEntryBefore(module, block: chooseChild, ids: ids, heap: heap, a: rightEntry, b: leftEntry)
	module.setTerminator(chooseChild, op: StdOp.condBranch(rightWins, thenBlockId: takeRight.id, elseBlockId: takeLeft.id))

	// takeLeft / takeRight: two blocks rather than one two-armed edge, because a block carries ONE argument
	// list per successor and these two arms disagree about the index they pass.
	module.setTerminator(takeLeft, op: StdOp.branch(compare.id))
	takeLeft.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(compare.id, argIds: argsOf(left)))
	module.setTerminator(takeRight, op: StdOp.branch(compare.id))
	takeRight.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(compare.id, argIds: argsOf(right)))

	// compare(child): the heap property holds here as soon as the entry comes before its earliest child.
	let child = ids.mint()
	compare.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(child, argType: StdType.i64))
	let childAddr = emitSiftEntryAddr(module, block: compare, ids: ids, heap: heap, index: child)
	let parentAddr = emitSiftEntryAddr(module, block: compare, ids: ids, heap: heap, index: i)
	let childEntry = emitSiftEntryLoad(module, block: compare, ids: ids, heap: heap, addr: childAddr)
	let parentEntry = emitSiftEntryLoad(module, block: compare, ids: ids, heap: heap, addr: parentAddr)
	let childWins = emitSiftEntryBefore(module, block: compare, ids: ids, heap: heap, a: childEntry, b: parentEntry)
	module.setTerminator(compare, op: StdOp.condBranch(childWins, thenBlockId: exchange.id, elseBlockId: cont.id))

	// exchange: both entries are already in registers, so the swap is four stores and no reload.
	emitSiftEntryStore(module, block: exchange, addr: parentAddr, entry: childEntry)
	emitSiftEntryStore(module, block: exchange, addr: childAddr, entry: parentEntry)
	module.setTerminator(exchange, op: StdOp.branch(hdr.id))
	exchange.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(hdr.id, argIds: argsOf(child)))
end 'emitHeapSiftDown'

union SiftHeap
	timerEntries(heapBase ValueId)
	addressWords(base ValueId)
end 'SiftHeap'

union SiftEntry
	timer(entry TimerEntry)
	word(value ValueId)
end 'SiftEntry'

function emitSiftEntryAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, heap SiftHeap, index ValueId) returns ValueId
	return match heap 'heap'
		timerEntries(heapBase) gives emitTimerEntryAddr(module, block: block, ids: ids, heapBase: heapBase, index: index)
		addressWords(base) gives emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: base, index: index, ids: ids)
	end 'heap'
end 'emitSiftEntryAddr'

function emitSiftEntryLoad(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, heap SiftHeap, addr ValueId) returns SiftEntry
	return match heap 'heap'
		timerEntries gives SiftEntry.timer(emitTimerEntryLoad(module, block: block, ids: ids, addr: addr))
		addressWords gives SiftEntry.word(emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: addr, offset: 0, ids: ids))
	end 'heap'
end 'emitSiftEntryLoad'

function emitSiftEntryStore(module StdModule, block IrBlock, addr ValueId, entry SiftEntry)
	match entry 'entry'
		timer(held) then emitTimerEntryStore(module, block: block, addr: addr, entry: held)
		word(held) then emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: addr, offset: 0, value: held)
	end 'entry'
end 'emitSiftEntryStore'

function emitSiftEntryBefore(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, heap SiftHeap, a SiftEntry, b SiftEntry) returns ValueId
	return match heap 'heap'
		timerEntries gives emitTimerEntryBefore(module, block: block, ids: ids, a: siftTimerOf(a), b: siftTimerOf(b))
		addressWords gives emitCmp(module, block: block, a: siftWordOf(a), b: siftWordOf(b), pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	end 'heap'
end 'emitSiftEntryBefore'

function siftTimerOf(entry SiftEntry) returns TimerEntry
	match entry 'entry'
		timer(held) then return held
		word then panic("siftTimerOf: a timer heap was handed a word entry")
	end 'entry'
end 'siftTimerOf'

function siftWordOf(entry SiftEntry) returns ValueId
	match entry 'entry'
		word(held) then return held
		timer then panic("siftWordOf: an address-word heap was handed a timer entry")
	end 'entry'
end 'siftWordOf'

// `__gt_timer_add(gt, deadline)` — insert a parked GT's entry into the timer MIN-HEAP: stamp it with the arm
// sequence (`__gt_timer_seq`, the tiebreaker the store's header explains), append it at `heap[count]`,
// count++, sift it up to its place and publish the root's deadline. O(log n) in the number of parked threads.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A FULL STORE GROWS RATHER THAN FAILING.** `TimerHeapCapacity` is a number this file picked rather
// than a bound anything outside it imposes, so refusing at it would be inventing a limit rather than reporting
// one — and `sleep` has nowhere to report one anyway (`stdlib/Sleep.maxon` declares it with no `throws`
// clause). ⇒ **The store DOUBLES**: a fresh
// `__slab_alloc`, the live entries copied across, the base and capacity republished, and the old region handed
// back. The free is AFTER the copy, and nothing else holds a pointer into the store: every walk re-reads
// `__gt_timer_heap_base` under the lock, which is what makes the base a `.data` word rather than a value
// anybody caches.
function buildGtTimerAdd(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtTimerAddName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var grow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"grow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doAdd = module.addBlock(func, label: b"doadd", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let gt = 0
	let deadline = 1
	let tag = 2

	// ⛔⛔ **THE WHOLE INSERT RUNS UNDER THE RUN-QUEUE LOCK, AND THE GROW IS WHY IT MUST BE THE WHOLE OF
	// IT RATHER THAN THE COUNT ALONE.** The store is five `.data` words plus a heap region, and a full store
	// REPUBLISHES the base and frees the old region — so a second M scanning it (`__gt_timer_check`,
	// `__gt_promise_drop`'s unlink) would be walking freed memory, and two Ms inserting
	// would both write slot `count` and lose one entry. An atomic per word cannot express any of that: what is
	// indivisible here is the transaction, not a word. `__sched_lock` is the scheduler's one lock and the store
	// is the scheduler's state, so there is no second lock and no order to remember. The SIFT is inside it for
	// the same reason and one more: mid-sift the array is not a heap, so no reader may see it.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: entry, ids: ids)

	let countAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(countAddr, label: GtTimerCountLabel))
	let count = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(count, addrId: countAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// count >= capacity → `heap[count]` would be PAST the store. Double it before writing, never through it.
	let capAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.globalAddr(capAddr, label: GtTimerCapacityLabel))
	let capacity = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(capacity, addrId: capAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isFull = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(isFull, lhs: count, rhs: capacity, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isFull, thenBlockId: grow.id, elseBlockId: doAdd.id))

	// grow: newCapacity = capacity * 2; copy the `count` live entries into a fresh region; republish both. The
	// copy is a byte-exact image (`count * TimerEntrySize`) and a heap is an ARRAY, so the heap property rides
	// across untouched — which is why the doubling needed no change when the discipline arrived.
	let twoConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.const(twoConst, value: 2, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let newCapacity = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.binOp(newCapacity, lhs: capacity, rhs: twoConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let growEntrySize = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.const(growEntrySize, value: TimerEntrySize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let newBytes = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.binOp(newBytes, lhs: newCapacity, rhs: growEntrySize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let newBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.call(newBase, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(newBytes)))
	let growBaseAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.globalAddr(growBaseAddr, label: GtTimerHeapBaseLabel))
	let oldBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(oldBase, addrId: growBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let liveBytes = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(grow, op: StdOp.binOp(liveBytes, lhs: count, rhs: growEntrySize, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	var copied = emitForwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: grow, dest: newBase, src: oldBase, byteCount: liveBytes, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(copied, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(growBaseAddr, offset: 0, valueId: newBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(copied, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(capAddr, offset: 0, valueId: newCapacity, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// The republished base is what every reader consults, so the old region is unreachable the instant the
	// store above lands — and on the FIRST grow `oldBase` is a real `__gt_init` region, never null, because the
	// store is allocated eagerly there rather than lazily here.
	emitSlabFree(module, block: copied, ptr: oldBase, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(copied, op: StdOp.branch(doAdd.id))

	let heapBase = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: doAdd, label: GtTimerHeapBaseLabel, ids: ids)

	// Stamp the arm sequence and bump the dispenser. Under the lock like every other word of the store, so two
	// Ms arming at once cannot take the same number — which is exactly what the tie rule needs of it.
	let seq = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: doAdd, label: GtTimerSeqLabel, ids: ids)
	let oneConst = emitConst(module, block: doAdd, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextSeq = emitBin(module, block: doAdd, a: seq, b: oneConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: doAdd, label: GtTimerSeqLabel, value: nextSeq, ids: ids)

	// heap[count] = {deadline, gt, seq, tag}; count++.
	let entryAddr = emitTimerEntryAddr(module, block: doAdd, ids: ids, heapBase: heapBase, index: count)
	emitTimerEntryStore(module, block: doAdd, addr: entryAddr, entry: TimerEntry.create(deadline, gt: gt, seq: seq, tag: tag))
	let newCount = emitBin(module, block: doAdd, a: count, b: oneConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doAdd, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(countAddr, offset: 0, valueId: newCount, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// The appended entry is the only one out of place, so sifting it up restores the whole heap.
	emitTimerSiftUp(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: doAdd, heapBase: heapBase, startIndex: count, cont: doneBlock)

	var published = emitGtPublishTimerWhen(module, func: func, cur: doneBlock, heapBase: heapBase, count: newCount, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: published, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(published, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtTimerAdd'

// ⭐⭐ **PUBLISH THE ROOT'S DEADLINE TO `__gt_timer_when` — the one writer of that word.** `__sched_lock` held,
// with `count` the store's live entry count after the caller's change. It terminates `cur` and answers the
// block to continue in. The region always exists (`__gt_init` allocates it eagerly), so reading its first
// entry is safe even when it is not live; the count decides which value is published.
function emitGtPublishTimerWhen(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, heapBase ValueId, count ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var hasRoot = module.addBlock(func, label: b"timerwhenroot", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var noRoot = module.addBlock(func, label: b"timerwhenempty", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var published = module.addBlock(func, label: b"timerwhenpublished", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: count, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: noRoot, whenFalse: hasRoot, ids: ids)

	emitStoreReleaseGlobalWord(module, block: hasRoot, label: GtTimerWhenLabel, value: emitLoadField(module, block: hasRoot, record: heapBase, offset: TimerOffDeadline, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(hasRoot, op: StdOp.branch(published.id))

	emitStoreReleaseGlobalWord(module, block: noRoot, label: GtTimerWhenLabel, value: emitConst(module, block: noRoot, value: TimerWhenEmpty, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(noRoot, op: StdOp.branch(published.id))
	return published
end 'emitGtPublishTimerWhen'

// `__gt_timer_when`, read UNLOCKED with acquire — see `GtTimerWhenLabel` for why that is sound. EXPORTED for
// the scheduler's poller deadline and its owed-poller test (`SchedRuntime`).
export function emitGtLoadTimerWhen(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: GtTimerWhenLabel, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtLoadTimerWhen'

// ⭐⭐ **IS A TIMER DUE? — the one due test, and it takes no lock.** It terminates `cur`: to `due` when the
// earliest deadline (`__gt_timer_when`) is at or before the clock, else to `notDue`, handing `notDueArgs` on
// both edges into it. The clock is read only when a timer is pending, and the answer is that reading: it is
// minted in a block that dominates `due`, for the fire that follows. EXPORTED for the scheduler loop's top
// (`SchedRuntime.buildSchedLoop`).
export function emitGtBranchOnTimerDue(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, due IrBlock, notDue IrBlock, notDueArgs ValueIdArray, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	var ask = module.addBlock(func, label: b"timerdueask", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let when = emitGtLoadTimerWhen(module, block: cur, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: when, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: TimerWhenEmpty, whenTrue: notDue, whenFalse: ask, ids: ids)

	let now = emitGtNowNs(module, block: ask, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: ask, a: when, pred: StdCmpPred.le, b: now, whenTrue: due, whenFalse: notDue, ids: ids)

	if notDueArgs.count() > 0 'handTheArgsOn'
		cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(notDue.id, argIds: notDueArgs))
		ask.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(notDue.id, argIds: notDueArgs))
	end 'handTheArgsOn'

	return now
end 'emitGtBranchOnTimerDue'

// `__gt_timer_check(now) -> wakeDebt` — fire every parked GT whose deadline has passed, IN DEADLINE ORDER:
// while the heap's root is due, pop it and ready it, sifting the moved last entry back down after each pop.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE ORDER IS THIS FUNCTION'S ANSWER, NOT ITS CALLER's.** A caller may hand the walk several due
// entries in one pass, and a pop-min loop readies them in `(deadline, seq)` order whatever it is handed; the
// store's header carries the measurement and the spec case that pins it.
//
// ⭐ **IT ANSWERS THE WAKE DEBT RATHER THAN PAYING IT.** Every caller is a machine about to look for work
// itself — the scheduler loop's top, its idle fire, the poller back from its wait, the yield placement — so the
// token a fire published is normally taken by the machine that fired it; the caller decides whether anyone
// else needs waking.
function buildGtTimerCheck(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtTimerCheckName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var loopHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"loophdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var checkDue = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkdue", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var popRoot = module.addBlock(func, label: b"poproot", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var moveLast = module.addBlock(func, label: b"movelast", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fire = module.addBlock(func, label: b"fire", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fireSleep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"firesleep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fireNotSleep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"firenotsleep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fireSend = module.addBlock(func, label: b"firesend", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fireSendWon = module.addBlock(func, label: b"firesendwon", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fireSendLost = module.addBlock(func, label: b"firesendlost", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fireNetpoll = module.addBlock(func, label: b"firenetpoll", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fireJoin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"firejoin", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let now = 0

	// The walk MUTATES the store (each pop removes an entry and sifts what is left), so it runs under the
	// run-queue lock for `__gt_timer_add`'s stated reason, and every ready rides that critical section.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: entry, ids: ids)

	let count0 = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: GtTimerCountLabel, ids: ids)
	let heapBase = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: GtTimerHeapBaseLabel, ids: ids)
	let zeroConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))

	// loopHdr(count, owesWake): an empty heap ends the walk; otherwise ask the root. `owesWake` is loop-carried
	// because a ready inside the lock may not wake anybody.
	let count = ids.mint()
	let owesWake = ids.mint()
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(count, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(owesWake, argType: StdType.i64))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(count0, second: zeroConst)))
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: loopHdr, a: count, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: zeroConst, whenTrue: doneBlock, whenFalse: checkDue, ids: ids)

	// checkDue: the root is the earliest `(deadline, seq)` in the store, so a root that is not due ends the
	// walk — nothing below it can be due either. Index 0 sits at offset 0, so the base IS its address.
	let rootDeadline = emitLoadField(module, block: checkDue, record: heapBase, offset: TimerOffDeadline, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: checkDue, a: rootDeadline, pred: StdCmpPred.le, b: now, whenTrue: popRoot, whenFalse: doneBlock, ids: ids)

	// popRoot: take the root's entry and shrink the live region by one. When it WAS the last entry there is
	// nothing to move over the hole and nothing to sift.
	let root = emitTimerEntryLoad(module, block: popRoot, ids: ids, addr: heapBase)
	let oneConst = emitConst(module, block: popRoot, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let newCount = emitBin(module, block: popRoot, a: count, b: oneConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: popRoot, a: newCount, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: zeroConst, whenTrue: fire, whenFalse: moveLast, ids: ids)

	// moveLast: the last live entry fills the root's hole and sifts down to its place — the standard pop. The
	// sift chain runs to `fire`, so the GT is readied only once the store is a heap again.
	let lastAddr = emitTimerEntryAddr(module, block: moveLast, ids: ids, heapBase: heapBase, index: newCount)
	let last = emitTimerEntryLoad(module, block: moveLast, ids: ids, addr: lastAddr)
	emitTimerEntryStore(module, block: moveLast, addr: heapBase, entry: last)
	emitHeapSiftDown(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: moveLast, heap: SiftHeap.timerEntries(heapBase), count: newCount, startIndex: zeroConst, cont: fire)

	// fire: end the popped wait. The order these happen in IS the order the threads resume in, which is what
	// makes the pop-min above observable at all.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE TAG DECIDES WHOSE DEADLINE THIS IS.** A sleep or an I/O yield is readied straight
	// away; a waiting send only if the timer wins its claim (`MailboxRuntime.emitMboxClaimSender`),
	// the send then unlinking itself under its mailbox lock; a SOCKET
	// deadline goes through the poll-descriptor band, which must take the thread off the direction word it is
	// published on first — and which answers no debt at all when a readiness event got there first.
	let isSleepTag = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: fire, value: root.tag, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: TimerTagSleep, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fire, op: StdOp.condBranch(isSleepTag, thenBlockId: fireSleep.id, elseBlockId: fireNotSleep.id))

	let sleepDebt = emitGtReady(module, block: fireSleep, gt: root.gt, gate: GtReadyGate.fromWaiting, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fireSleep, op: StdOp.branch(fireJoin.id))
	fireSleep.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(fireJoin.id, argIds: argsOf(sleepDebt)))

	let isSendTag = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: fireNotSleep, value: root.tag, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: TimerTagSend, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fireNotSleep, op: StdOp.condBranch(isSendTag, thenBlockId: fireSend.id, elseBlockId: fireNetpoll.id))

	emitMboxClaimSender(module, block: fireSend, sender: root.gt, outcome: MboxSendWake.timedOut, lost: fireSendLost, won: fireSendWon, ids: ids)

	let sendDebt = emitGtReady(module, block: fireSendWon, gt: root.gt, gate: GtReadyGate.fromWaiting, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fireSendWon, op: StdOp.branch(fireJoin.id))
	fireSendWon.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(fireJoin.id, argIds: argsOf(sendDebt)))

	let sendLostDebt = emitConst(module, block: fireSendLost, value: WakeDebtNone, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fireSendLost, op: StdOp.branch(fireJoin.id))
	fireSendLost.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(fireJoin.id, argIds: argsOf(sendLostDebt)))

	let expireDebt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fireNetpoll, op: StdOp.call(expireDebt, callee: NetpollPdExpireName, args: argsOf(root.gt)))
	module.setTerminator(fireNetpoll, op: StdOp.branch(fireJoin.id))
	fireNetpoll.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(fireJoin.id, argIds: argsOf(expireDebt)))

	let firedDebt = ids.mint()
	fireJoin.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(firedDebt, argType: StdType.i64))
	let fireNowOwes = emitBin(module, block: fireJoin, a: owesWake, b: firedDebt, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fireJoin, op: StdOp.branch(loopHdr.id))
	fireJoin.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(loopHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(newCount, second: fireNowOwes)))

	// done(count, owesWake): publish the surviving entry count, release, and answer the debt. Its two
	// predecessors are the two ways the walk ends: an emptied heap and a root that is not due yet.
	let doneCount = ids.mint()
	let doneOwesWake = ids.mint()
	doneBlock.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(doneCount, argType: StdType.i64))
	doneBlock.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(doneOwesWake, argType: StdType.i64))
	loopHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(doneBlock.id, argIds: twoArgs(count, second: owesWake)))
	checkDue.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(doneBlock.id, argIds: twoArgs(count, second: owesWake)))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: doneBlock, label: GtTimerCountLabel, value: doneCount, ids: ids)
	var published = emitGtPublishTimerWhen(module, func: func, cur: doneBlock, heapBase: heapBase, count: doneCount, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: published, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(published, op: StdOp.ret(doneOwesWake))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtTimerCheck'

// Emit `ceil((earliest - now) / 1e6)` into `block` and return the deltaMs id — the millisecond delta the
// poller waits for. Every caller has established `earliest > now`, and rounding UP then guarantees ≥1 ms, so a
// sub-millisecond remainder cannot busy-spin. EXPORTED for the scheduler loop's idle tail
// (`SchedRuntime.buildSchedLoop`), beside the nanosecond constants it divides by.
export function emitCeilMs(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, earliest ValueId, now ValueId) returns ValueId
	let delta = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.binOp(delta, lhs: earliest, rhs: now, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let milliMinusOne = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(milliMinusOne, value: NanosPerMilli - 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let deltaRounded = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.binOp(deltaRounded, lhs: delta, rhs: milliMinusOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let milliConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(milliConst, value: NanosPerMilli, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let deltaMs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.div(deltaMs, lhs: deltaRounded, rhs: milliConst, signedness: IntegerSignedness.signed))
	return deltaMs
end 'emitCeilMs'

// ⭐⭐ **THE ANSWER `__gt_any_completed` GIVES WHEN NOTHING HAS FINISHED.** It cannot be the `0` every other
// "or nothing" answerer in this runtime uses (`__gt_strand_next`, `__sched_find_runnable`), because 0 is a
// perfectly good ARRAY INDEX — and a select over an array whose slot 0
// has completed is the commonest shape there is. A negative index is unrepresentable, so it is the one value
// the answer cannot collide with.
let NoCompletedPromise = -1

// ⭐ **THE WIDTH OF ONE `Promise with …` SLOT, WHICH IS WHY THE SCAN BELOW NEEDS NO ELEMENT-SIZE LOAD.**
// `PromiseType.maxon`'s first fact: a promise handle owns no `__mm_alloc` box — the value IS the green-thread
// pointer — so a `Promise` instance is a one-word slot whatever its type arguments are, and the
// parser's argument rule for `__Builtins.awaitAny` is what guarantees the element type is one.
// An `Array with Promise with Integer` is created with `elementSize: 8`; its `elementDestroy` is the promise drop
// (`Parser.arrayElementDestroyValue`), so a container dropped with un-awaited elements reclaims their green threads.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT IS THE SAME WIDTH `ProgramSignatures.arrayElementSize` STAMPS INTO THE RECORD, AND THE TWO ARE
// COMPARED RATHER THAN TRUSTED.** One compiled scan serves every array in every program, so the stride here
// HAS to be a constant while the stamp is per-instance — two statements of one fact, whose divergence would
// address every slot past the first wrongly and answer a wrong INDEX, never a diagnostic. `Parser.emitAwaitAny`
// compares `arrayElementSize` of the argument instance against THIS constant and panics if the two have come
// apart; that assertion is what keeps this constant honest.
export let PromiseSlotBytes = PointerFieldBytes

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_any_completed(record) -> index | NoCompletedPromise` — THE `awaitAny` SCAN.** It
// walks `[0, length)` of the caller's `__ManagedMemory` record and answers the FIRST slot whose GT
// has reached `completed`. `__gt_await_any` asks it before it parks and after every wake, and the
// registration asks it under every slot's record lock (`__gt_any_lock`), which is what closes the
// window between the first scan and the registration.
//
// ⚠ **THE STATUS READ IS AN ACQUIRE** (`emitGtLoadPublishedStatus`): both writers publish `completed` with a
// release after the two answer words, so a scanner on another M that sees `completed` also sees the result.
//
// ⚠ **A `0` SLOT IS SKIPPED RATHER THAN DEREFERENCED.** `Array.resize` publishes length over slots nobody
// filled and the gap past the old length is always ZERO, so a zero inside `[0, length)` is a slot with no
// promise in it.
//
// ⚠ **IT CANNOT TELL A CONSUMED HANDLE FROM A LIVE ONE: `__gt_await` recycles the GT struct and writes
// nothing back to the slot.** What keeps the scan honest is the consume at the READ door, which empties the
// slot the promise was moved out of (`__managed_vacate`), leaving the zero this walk already skips. A handle
// that reaches a slot by a road recording no such read stays visible here after it has been spent, which is
// the slot-level linearity gap that makes `awaitAny` a function answering an INDEX rather than a `select`
// statement consuming a slot.
function buildGtAnyCompleted(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtAnyCompletedName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var scanHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scanhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var scanBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scanbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var checkStatus = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkstatus", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var advance = module.addBlock(func, label: b"advance", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"found", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notFound = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notfound", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let record = 0

	// The buffer and the live length are read ONCE per scan, in a block that dominates the walk: nothing this
	// function calls runs a green thread, so the array cannot change during it.
	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let zeroConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zeroConst)))

	// scanHdr(i): past the last live slot → nothing has completed; else inspect slot i.
	let i = ids.mint()
	scanHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: scanHdr, a: i, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: length, whenTrue: notFound, whenFalse: scanBody, ids: ids)

	// scanBody: gt = buffer[i]. An empty slot holds 0 and is skipped — see this function's header.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE ADDRESS IS `emitSlotAddr`'S AND NOT A SECOND `mul`/`add`.** That function is the runtime's one
	// home of *"where does element `index` start"* — sixteen call sites across the managed-memory runtime and
	// the inline element primitives read a buffer through it — and a private copy here would be that
	// arithmetic written down in the file least likely to hear about a change to it.
	let slotBytes = emitConst(module, block: scanBody, value: PromiseSlotBytes, ids: ids)
	let slotAddr = emitSlotAddr(module, block: scanBody, buffer: buffer, index: i, elementSize: slotBytes, ids: ids)
	let gt = emitLoadField(module, block: scanBody, record: slotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: scanBody, a: gt, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: zeroConst, whenTrue: advance, whenFalse: checkStatus, ids: ids)

	// checkStatus: the one word this whole primitive turns on. ACQUIRE — the thread that completed it may
	// be on another M (`emitGtLoadPublishedStatus`).
	let status = emitGtLoadPublishedStatus(module, block: checkStatus, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	let completed = emitGtStatusEquals(module, block: checkStatus, status: status, want: GtStatus.completed, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(checkStatus, op: StdOp.condBranch(completed, thenBlockId: found.id, elseBlockId: advance.id))

	// advance: i + 1 → scanHdr.
	let one = emitConst(module, block: advance, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let next = emitBin(module, block: advance, a: i, b: one, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(advance, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	advance.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(next)))

	// `i` is scanHdr's block argument and scanHdr dominates this block, so the index needs no second carrier.
	module.setTerminator(found, op: StdOp.ret(i))
	emitConstReturn(module, block: notFound, value: NoCompletedPromise, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtAnyCompleted'

function emitIndexStep(module StdModule, block IrBlock, index ValueId, delta FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: index, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: delta, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitIndexStep'

// `__gt_any_lock(record) -> scratch` — take the record lock of every promise in `record`, in
// ascending address order, as Go's `sellock`: two awaitAny calls over overlapping promises take
// their common locks in one order and cannot deadlock. The addresses are heap-sorted in a slab
// scratch of `length + 1` words whose word 0 is the count; `__gt_any_unlock(scratch)` releases them
// in reverse and frees it.
function buildGtAnyLock(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtAnyLockName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var gatherHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylockgatherhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var gatherBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylockgatherbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var gatherKeep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylockgatherkeep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var gatherSkip = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylockgatherskip", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var gathered = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylockgathered", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var buildHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylockbuildhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var buildBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylockbuildbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var buildStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylockbuildstep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sortHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylocksorthdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sortBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylocksortbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sortStep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylocksortstep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sorted = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylocksorted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var lockHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylocklockhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var lockBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylocklockbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var locked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anylocklocked", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let record = 0

	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let scratchBytes = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: emitIndexStep(module, block: entry, index: length, delta: OneMore, ids: ids), b: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: PointerFieldBytes, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let scratch = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(scratch, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(scratchBytes)))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(gatherHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(gatherHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(zero, second: zero)))

	let i = ids.mint()
	let n = ids.mint()
	gatherHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	gatherHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(n, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: gatherHdr, a: i, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: length, whenTrue: gathered, whenFalse: gatherBody, ids: ids)

	let slotAddr = emitSlotAddr(module, block: gatherBody, buffer: buffer, index: i, elementSize: emitConst(module, block: gatherBody, value: PromiseSlotBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	let promise = emitLoadField(module, block: gatherBody, record: slotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: gatherBody, value: promise, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: gatherSkip, whenFalse: gatherKeep, ids: ids)

	let kept = emitIndexStep(module, block: gatherKeep, index: n, delta: OneMore, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: gatherKeep, record: emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: gatherKeep, base: scratch, index: kept, ids: ids), offset: 0, value: promise)
	module.setTerminator(gatherKeep, op: StdOp.branch(gatherHdr.id))
	gatherKeep.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(gatherHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(emitIndexStep(module, block: gatherKeep, index: i, delta: OneMore, ids: ids), second: kept)))

	module.setTerminator(gatherSkip, op: StdOp.branch(gatherHdr.id))
	gatherSkip.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(gatherHdr.id, argIds: twoArgs(emitIndexStep(module, block: gatherSkip, index: i, delta: OneMore, ids: ids), second: n)))

	emitStoreField(module, block: gathered, record: scratch, offset: 0, value: n)
	let words = emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: gathered, base: scratch, index: emitConst(module, block: gathered, value: 1, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	let half = emitBin(module, block: gathered, a: n, b: emitConst(module, block: gathered, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(gathered, op: StdOp.branch(buildHdr.id))
	gathered.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(buildHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(half)))

	let pending = ids.mint()
	buildHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pending, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: buildHdr, value: pending, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: sortHdr, whenFalse: buildBody, ids: ids)
	buildHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(sortHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(n)))

	let root = emitIndexStep(module, block: buildBody, index: pending, delta: OneFewer, ids: ids)
	emitHeapSiftDown(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: buildBody, heap: SiftHeap.addressWords(words), count: n, startIndex: root, cont: buildStep)
	module.setTerminator(buildStep, op: StdOp.branch(buildHdr.id))
	buildStep.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(buildHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(root)))

	let unsorted = ids.mint()
	sortHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(unsorted, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: sortHdr, value: unsorted, pred: StdCmpPred.le, against: 1, whenTrue: sorted, whenFalse: sortBody, ids: ids)

	let last = emitIndexStep(module, block: sortBody, index: unsorted, delta: OneFewer, ids: ids)
	let rootAddr = emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: sortBody, base: words, index: zero, ids: ids)
	let lastAddr = emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: sortBody, base: words, index: last, ids: ids)
	let largest = emitLoadField(module, block: sortBody, record: rootAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let displaced = emitLoadField(module, block: sortBody, record: lastAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: sortBody, record: rootAddr, offset: 0, value: displaced)
	emitStoreField(module, block: sortBody, record: lastAddr, offset: 0, value: largest)
	emitHeapSiftDown(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: sortBody, heap: SiftHeap.addressWords(words), count: last, startIndex: zero, cont: sortStep)
	module.setTerminator(sortStep, op: StdOp.branch(sortHdr.id))
	sortStep.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(sortHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(last)))

	module.setTerminator(sorted, op: StdOp.branch(lockHdr.id))
	sorted.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(lockHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	let index = ids.mint()
	lockHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(index, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: lockHdr, a: index, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: n, whenTrue: locked, whenFalse: lockBody, ids: ids)

	let ordered = emitWordSlotLoad(module, block: lockBody, base: words, index: index, ids: ids)
	emitGtRecordLockEnter(module, block: lockBody, record: ordered, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(lockBody, op: StdOp.branch(lockHdr.id))
	lockBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(lockHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(emitIndexStep(module, block: lockBody, index: index, delta: OneMore, ids: ids))))

	module.setTerminator(locked, op: StdOp.ret(scratch))
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtAnyLock'

function buildGtAnyUnlock(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtAnyUnlockName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unlockHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anyunlockhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unlockBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anyunlockbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unlocked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anyunlocked", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let scratch = 0

	let n = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: scratch, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let words = emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: entry, base: scratch, index: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(unlockHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(unlockHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(n)))

	let remaining = ids.mint()
	unlockHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(remaining, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: unlockHdr, value: remaining, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: unlocked, whenFalse: unlockBody, ids: ids)

	let index = emitIndexStep(module, block: unlockBody, index: remaining, delta: OneFewer, ids: ids)
	let ordered = emitWordSlotLoad(module, block: unlockBody, base: words, index: index, ids: ids)
	emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: unlockBody, record: ordered, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(unlockBody, op: StdOp.branch(unlockHdr.id))
	unlockBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(unlockHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(index)))

	emitSlabFree(module, block: unlocked, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(unlocked, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtAnyUnlock'

// Ask the scan which slot has completed, into a fresh value.
function emitAnyCompletedIndex(module StdModule, block IrBlock, record ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let index = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(index, callee: GtAnyCompletedName, args: argsOf(record)))
	return index
end 'emitAnyCompletedIndex'

// What an awaitAny walk does at each promise slot: take the awaiter word, or give it back.
enum AwaitAnySlotAct
	// Claim the slot's awaiter word for `g` with `AwaitAnyTag` set (`emitGtClaimAwaiter`).
	register

	// Clear the slot's awaiter if it is still `g`'s registration; a completer may already have taken it.
	withdraw
end 'AwaitAnySlotAct'

// ⭐⭐ **WALK EVERY PROMISE IN `record`, TAKING OR GIVING BACK ITS AWAITER WORD FOR `g`.** Every
// slot's record lock held (`__gt_any_lock`). It terminates `cur` and continues at `cont`. A `0`
// slot holds no promise and is skipped, for `__gt_any_completed`'s reason.
function emitGtAwaitAnySlotWalk(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, record ValueId, g ValueId, act AwaitAnySlotAct, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var hdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anywalkhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var body = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anywalkbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var slotAct = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anywalkslot", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var next = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anywalknext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let buffer = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let length = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: record, offset: ManagedRecordLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let tagged = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: g, b: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: AwaitAnyTag, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(hdr.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(hdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	let i = ids.mint()
	hdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: hdr, a: i, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: length, whenTrue: cont, whenFalse: body, ids: ids)

	let slotAddr = emitSlotAddr(module, block: body, buffer: buffer, index: i, elementSize: emitConst(module, block: body, value: PromiseSlotBytes, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	let slotGt = emitLoadField(module, block: body, record: slotAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: body, a: slotGt, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: zero, whenTrue: next, whenFalse: slotAct, ids: ids)

	match act 'slotAction'
		register then emitGtClaimAwaiter(module, func: func, cur: slotAct, promise: slotGt, awaiter: tagged, cont: next, ids: ids)
		withdraw then emitGtAwaitAnyWithdrawSlot(module, func: func, cur: slotAct, slotGt: slotGt, tagged: tagged, cont: next, ids: ids)
	end 'slotAction'

	let step = emitBin(module, block: next, a: i, b: emitConst(module, block: next, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(next, op: StdOp.branch(hdr.id))
	next.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(hdr.id, argIds: argsOf(step)))
end 'emitGtAwaitAnySlotWalk'

// ⭐⭐ **MAKE `awaiter` THE ONE GT `promise` READIES ON COMPLETION — both registrations' claim.**
// `promise`'s record lock held. A promise holds one awaiter and moves whole, so a word already
// taken is a second waiter on one promise, which the front end cannot produce: the abort names it
// rather than overwrite the first, whose wake would then be lost. Terminates `cur` into `cont`.
function emitGtClaimAwaiter(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, promise ValueId, awaiter ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var take = module.addBlock(func, label: b"claimawaitertake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var twice = module.addBlock(func, label: b"claimawaitertwice", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let prior = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: promise, offset: GtOffAwaiter, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: prior, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: twice, whenFalse: take, ids: ids)
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: twice, abort: RuntimeAbort.schedulerPromiseAwaitedTwice, ids: ids)

	emitStoreField(module, block: take, record: promise, offset: GtOffAwaiter, value: awaiter)
	module.setTerminator(take, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitGtClaimAwaiter'

// One slot's withdrawal: clear the awaiter word only if it still names this registration. Terminates `cur`
// into `cont`.
function emitGtAwaitAnyWithdrawSlot(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, slotGt ValueId, tagged ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var clear = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anywithdrawclear", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let awaiter = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: slotGt, offset: GtOffAwaiter, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: cur, a: awaiter, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: tagged, whenTrue: clear, whenFalse: cont, ids: ids)

	emitStoreField(module, block: clear, record: slotGt, offset: GtOffAwaiter, value: emitConst(module, block: clear, value: 0, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(clear, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitGtAwaitAnyWithdrawSlot'

// `__gt_await_any(record) -> index` — see `GtAwaitAnyName`. Scan; with nothing complete, park (the registration
// is `__gt_register_park`'s awaitAny arm); on every wake withdraw from each slot and scan again.
//
// ⚠ **AN EMPTY ARRAY PARKS ON NOTHING**: awaiting any of zero promises can never complete, and
// `__sched_checkdead` answers it with `schedulerDeadlock` exactly as Go's `select {}` answers its detector.
function buildGtAwaitAny(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtAwaitAnyName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var scan = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anyscan", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anyfound", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var park = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anypark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var withdraw = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anywithdraw", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var withdrawn = module.addBlock(func, label: b"anywithdrawn", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let record = 0
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(scan.id))

	let index = emitAnyCompletedIndex(module, block: scan, record: record, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: scan, value: index, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: NoCompletedPromise, whenTrue: found, whenFalse: park, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(found, op: StdOp.ret(index))

	emitGtPark(module, ids: ids, cur: park, kind: GtParkKind.awaitAny.rawValue, arg: record, cont: withdraw)

	// Withdrawn under the slot locks every registration and every completer's hand-over take, so no
	// slot is left naming this thread once the scan below can answer.
	let selecting = emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: withdraw, ids: ids)
	let lockedSlots = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(withdraw, op: StdOp.call(lockedSlots, callee: GtAnyLockName, args: argsOf(record)))
	emitGtAwaitAnySlotWalk(module, func: func, cur: withdraw, record: record, g: selecting, act: AwaitAnySlotAct.withdraw, cont: withdrawn, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(withdrawn, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtAnyUnlockName, args: argsOf(lockedSlots)))
	module.setTerminator(withdrawn, op: StdOp.branch(scan.id))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtAwaitAny'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_drain_at_exit(code) -> code` — THE SCHEDULER'S TEARDOWN, ON THE EXIT PATH.** See
// `GtDrainAtExitName` for where it is called from and why the join is not optional. It runs on the main
// machine once `main` has completed, in three steps:
//
//   1. **RUN THE SCHEDULER LOOP UNTIL NOTHING IS LEFT TO RUN** — nothing runnable, no store pending and no
//      other machine running, or every machine idle (`SchedRuntime.emitSchedMainLoopUntilMet`). What still
//      owns a green thread then is the leak gate's to report.
//   2. **RELEASE THE REGISTRY SLOTS AND THE MANAGED GLOBALS** (`__maxon_registry_release`,
//      `__maxon_global_cleanup`). Both stay live through step 1, because a handler still running
//      there may look a key up or read a module `let`. The release closes the mailboxes only parked
//      services held, so the loop runs once more after it.
//   3. **STOP AND JOIN THE WORKER Ms** (`__sched_stop_workers`), which is where the counter this
//      exit path is about to read stops moving.
//
// ⚠ **`__mm_leak_check` IS UNTOUCHED BY ANY OF IT.** A count still non-zero after the drain is a real leak —
// a green thread neither awaited nor dropped — and still reports 75.
function buildGtDrainAtExit(module StdModule, releasesRegistry bool, releasesGlobals bool)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtDrainAtExitName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let code = 0 as ValueId

	emitSchedMainLoop(module, block: entry, until: SchedMainLoopUntil.drained, ids: ids)
	var drained = emitReleaseOwnersAfterDrain(module, func: func, cur: entry, releasesRegistry: releasesRegistry, releasesGlobals: releasesGlobals, code: code, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(drained, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedStopWorkersName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(drained, op: StdOp.ret(code))
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtDrainAtExit'

// `__gt_quiesce()`: park the caller until `__sched_checkdead` finds every machine idle with nothing
// pending, and answer the live sum it stored at that moment. It tears nothing down. A renounced
// caller answers without that wait, for the reason the renounce mark in `buildGtPromiseDrop` gives;
// its sum is taken unlocked, so steps in flight on other machines can leave it transiently negative,
// and it is clamped to 0.
function buildGtQuiesce(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtQuiesceName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wait = module.addBlock(func, label: b"quiescepark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var resumed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"quiesceresumed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var answered = module.addBlock(func, label: b"quiesceanswered", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var renounced = module.addBlock(func, label: b"quiescerenounced", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var negative = module.addBlock(func, label: b"quiescenegative", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var counted = module.addBlock(func, label: b"quiescecounted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let renouncedBeforeParking = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: entry, ids: ids), offset: GtOffRenounced, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: renouncedBeforeParking, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: GtPromiseHeld, whenTrue: renounced, whenFalse: wait, ids: ids)

	emitGtPark(module, ids: ids, cur: wait, kind: GtParkKind.quiesce.rawValue, arg: emitConst(module, block: wait, value: GtParkArgUnused, ids: ids), cont: resumed)

	let quiescer = emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: resumed, ids: ids)
	let renouncedWhileParked = emitLoadField(module, block: resumed, record: quiescer, offset: GtOffRenounced, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: resumed, value: renouncedWhileParked, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: GtPromiseHeld, whenTrue: renounced, whenFalse: answered, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(answered, op: StdOp.ret(emitLoadField(module, block: answered, record: quiescer, offset: GtOffParkArg, ids: ids)))

	let outstanding = emitSchedGtLiveSum(module, block: renounced, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: renounced, value: outstanding, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: 0, whenTrue: negative, whenFalse: counted, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(negative, op: StdOp.ret(emitConst(module, block: negative, value: 0, ids: ids)))
	module.setTerminator(counted, op: StdOp.ret(outstanding))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtQuiesce'

function emitReleaseOwnersAfterDrain(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, releasesRegistry bool, releasesGlobals bool, code ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	if not (releasesRegistry or releasesGlobals) 'nothingOwnsAService'
		return cur
	end 'nothingOwnsAService'

	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"drainreleaseowners", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(release.id))

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: release, ids: ids)
	emitSchedEnterPhaseLocked(module, block: release, phase: SchedPhase.draining, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: release, ids: ids)

	emitOwnerReleaseCalls(module, block: release, releasesRegistry: releasesRegistry, releasesGlobals: releasesGlobals, code: code, ids: ids)
	emitSchedMainLoop(module, block: release, until: SchedMainLoopUntil.drained, ids: ids)
	return release
end 'emitReleaseOwnersAfterDrain'

function emitOwnerReleaseCalls(module StdModule, block IrBlock, releasesRegistry bool, releasesGlobals bool, code ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	if releasesRegistry 'theRegistry'
		module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: RegistryReleaseName, args: argsOf(code)))
	end 'theRegistry'

	if releasesGlobals 'managedGlobals'
		module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GlobalCleanupName, args: argsOf(code)))
	end 'managedGlobals'
end 'emitOwnerReleaseCalls'

// `__gt_run_main(entry) -> ExitCode` — see `GtRunMainName`. In order: bring the scheduler up; create `main`'s
// green thread and ready it onto this machine's ring; start the system monitor; run the scheduler loop until
// `main` has completed; take its ExitCode; and arrive as its consumer.
//
// ⚠ **THE READY OWES A WAKE THAT IS NOT PAID**: the machine about to search is this one, and a wake would start
// a worker for a token this machine takes at once.
//
// ⚠ **THE RESULT IS READ BEFORE THE ARRIVAL.** The second of the two parties puts the struct on a free list,
// where the next spawn may zero it (`emitGtTeardownArrive`).
function buildGtRunMain(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtRunMainName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var running = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mainrunning", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var finished = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mainfinished", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unfinished = module.addBlock(func, label: b"mainunfinished", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let mainEntry = 0

	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtInitName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let mainGt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(mainGt, callee: GtSpawnMainName, args: argsOf(mainEntry)))
	_ = emitGtReady(module, block: entry, gt: mainGt, gate: GtReadyGate.alreadyRunnable, ids: ids)
	emitSchedSysmonStart(module, func: func, cur: entry, cont: running, target: target, ids: ids)
	emitSchedMainLoop(module, block: running, until: SchedMainLoopUntil.mainCompleted, ids: ids)

	// ⛔ The loop leaves only once `main`'s completion has moved the phase on; anything else would hand the exit
	// path an ExitCode nobody wrote.
	let status = emitGtLoadPublishedStatus(module, block: running, gt: mainGt, ids: ids)
	let completed = emitGtStatusEquals(module, block: running, status: status, want: GtStatus.completed, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(running, op: StdOp.condBranch(completed, thenBlockId: finished.id, elseBlockId: unfinished.id))
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: unfinished, abort: RuntimeAbort.mainThreadSwitchedBackUnfinished, ids: ids)

	let code = emitLoadField(module, block: finished, record: mainGt, offset: GtOffResult, ids: ids)
	var doneBlock = emitGtArriveAndReclaimIfSecond(module, func: func, cur: finished, gt: mainGt, ticket: GtTeardownConsumerTicket, otherTicket: GtTeardownRunnerTicket, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.ret(code))
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtRunMain'

union AwaitTraceState
	untraced
	read(tags GtTraceTags, traceId ValueId, alreadyDone ValueId)
end 'AwaitTraceState'

// ⭐ **THE TAG FOLLOWS `throwing`**: `await` prints `await #` and `try await` prints `try_await #`
// (`specs/async-tcp.md`'s cases pin the second spelling against `async-await.md`'s first).
function emitAwaitTraceLines(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, tags GtTraceTags, throwing bool, traceId ValueId, alreadyDone ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	let awaitTag = tags.tryAwaitTag if throwing else tags.awaitTag
	var immediateBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awaitimm", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var yieldedBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awaityield", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var joinBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awaitjoin", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(alreadyDone, thenBlockId: immediateBlock.id, elseBlockId: yieldedBlock.id))
	emitGtTraceLine(module, block: immediateBlock, tag: awaitTag, gtId: traceId, suffix: GtTraceSuffix.tagged(tags.immediateSuffix), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(immediateBlock, op: StdOp.branch(joinBlock.id))
	emitGtTraceLine(module, block: yieldedBlock, tag: awaitTag, gtId: traceId, suffix: GtTraceSuffix.tagged(tags.yieldSuffix), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(yieldedBlock, op: StdOp.branch(joinBlock.id))
	return joinBlock
end 'emitAwaitTraceLines'

// `__gt_await(promise) -> result` (when `throwing` is false) / `__gt_try_await(promise) -> (result, threw)`
// (when true): park until the promise's GT is `completed`, then extract the awaited scalar. The two differ
// ONLY in the exit terminator (a plain `ret` vs the dual-register `errorReturn` for `try await`).
// `__gt_try_await_holding` is the throwing one leaving its arrival to `__gt_reply_witness` (`arrival`).
//
// ⭐ **THE WAIT IS A LOOP ON THE STATUS**, so a wake that finds the promise still pending parks
// again rather than reading a result nobody wrote. The registration (`__gt_register_park`'s await
// arm) re-reads the status under the promise's record lock and resumes at once if the promise
// completed after this read.
function buildGtAwait(module StdModule, name ByteArray, throwing bool, arrival GtAwaitArrival, tracing GtTracing)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var check = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awaitcheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wait = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awaitpark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var awaited = module.addBlock(func, label: b"awaitdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let promise = 0 as ValueId

	// ⭐ THE `[immediate]` / `[yield]` DISTINCTION IS DECIDED ON THE WAY IN, BECAUSE AFTERWARDS IT IS GONE. A
	// promise already `completed` when we arrived is `[immediate]`; anything else had to wait and is `[yield]`.
	// ⚠ MINTED INSIDE THE GUARD, THOUGH BOTH ARE READ BY THE SECOND `asyncTrace` BLOCK BELOW — hence the two
	// `var`s. Minted outside it, an UNTRACED build would burn two ids on values it never defines.
	var awaitTrace = AwaitTraceState.untraced

	if let tags = try tracing.tags() 'readAwaitTraceState'
		let awaitedTraceId = ids.mint()
		module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(awaitedTraceId, addrId: promise, offset: GtOffTraceId, valueType: StdType.i64))
		let statusOnEntry = emitGtLoadPublishedStatus(module, block: entry, gt: promise, ids: ids)
		let alreadyDone = emitGtStatusEquals(module, block: entry, status: statusOnEntry, want: GtStatus.completed, ids: ids)
		awaitTrace = AwaitTraceState.read(tags, traceId: awaitedTraceId, alreadyDone: alreadyDone)
	end 'readAwaitTraceState'

	entry = emitSchedEventOf(module, func: func, block: entry, eventType: DsEvSchedAwait, gt: promise, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(check.id))

	let status = emitGtLoadPublishedStatus(module, block: check, gt: promise, ids: ids)
	let isDone = emitGtStatusEquals(module, block: check, status: status, want: GtStatus.completed, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(check, op: StdOp.condBranch(isDone, thenBlockId: awaited.id, elseBlockId: wait.id))

	emitGtPark(module, ids: ids, cur: wait, kind: GtParkKind.awaitPromise.rawValue, arg: promise, cont: check)

	// ⚠ **FROM HERE `cur` IS THE ONLY NAME FOR THE AWAITED BLOCK.** Both bindings are mutable and mutability is
	// what decides move-vs-alias, so this bind MOVES — naming `awaited` again below is use-after-move (E3102).
	var cur = awaited

	match awaitTrace 'awaitTrace'
		untraced then break
		read(tags, traceId, alreadyDone) then cur = emitAwaitTraceLines(module, func: func, cur: cur, tags: tags, throwing: throwing, traceId: traceId, alreadyDone: alreadyDone, ids: ids)
	end 'awaitTrace'

	// extract: result = promise.result. `__gt_await` never reads `threw` — a throwing promise is only ever
	// `try await`ed (E3057), so a non-throwing thunk's garbage R10 never reaches a reader.
	let result = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(result, addrId: promise, offset: GtOffResult, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// ⭐⭐ **RELEASE THE THREAD'S ARGUMENTS — WITH THE RESULT CONSUMED.** The `+1` on the awaited value has
	// just been loaded above and is handed to the awaiting frame, which enrols it and drops it once
	// (`Parser.emitAwaitOp`); releasing it here as well is a DOUBLE FREE, and `consumedByAwaiter` is what says
	// so. The ARGUMENTS the spawn took on the thread's behalf are still outstanding and are given back exactly
	// here. See `Runtime/AsyncReleaser.maxon` for the other call site.
	var exit = emitGtReleaserCall(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: cur, gt: promise, resultDisposition: AsyncResultDisposition.consumedByAwaiter)

	// ⭐⭐ **THE CONSUMER'S HALF OF THE RENDEZVOUS, NOT A FREE.** The runner that ran this thread may still be
	// between the completing switch and its own hand-off, reading the struct; `__gt_owner_done` frees only if
	// the runner had already arrived, and otherwise the runner frees.
	//
	// ⛔⛔ **EVERY READ OF THE STRUCT MUST PRECEDE THE ARRIVAL.** After it the struct may already be on a free
	// list; `awaitExitTerminator` performs the `threw` load and hands back the terminator, so the last read of
	// the struct is over before the arrival is emitted.
	let exitTerminator = awaitExitTerminator(module, block: exit, promise: promise, result: result, throwing: throwing, ids: ids)

	match arrival 'arrival'
		inTheAwait then module.appendOp(exit, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtOwnerDoneName, args: argsOf(promise)))
		inReplyWitness then break
	end 'arrival'

	module.setTerminator(exit, op: exitTerminator)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtAwait'

// `__gt_reply_witness(cell) -> witness` — see `GtTryAwaitHoldingName`. The load precedes the arrival, which
// may free the cell.
function buildGtReplyWitness(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtReplyWitnessName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let cell = 0

	let witness = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: cell, offset: GtOffReplyWitness, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtOwnerDoneName, args: argsOf(cell)))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(witness))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtReplyWitness'

// ⭐⭐ **THE AWAIT'S EXIT, BUILT BEFORE THE STRUCT IS RELEASED.** The two awaits differ in exactly
// one thing — a `try await` also carries the error flag the trampoline captured into `promise.threw`, which is
// what makes `__gt_try_await` a throwing function to the backend — and that difference is a LOAD out of a
// struct the caller is about to hand back for reuse. Emitting the load here and returning the
// terminator is what keeps the read and the release in that order at the one site that could get it wrong.
//
// `__gt_await` never reads `threw`: a throwing promise is only ever `try await`ed (E3057), so a non-throwing
// thunk's garbage R10 never reaches a reader, and the plain await emits no load at all.
function awaitExitTerminator(module StdModule, block IrBlock, promise ValueId, result ValueId, throwing bool, ids ValueMinter) returns StdOp
	if not throwing 'plainExit'
		return StdOp.ret(result)
	end 'plainExit'

	let threw = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(threw, addrId: promise, offset: GtOffThrew, valueType: StdType.i64))
	return StdOp.errorReturn(result, errorFlagId: threw)
end 'awaitExitTerminator'

// ⭐⭐ **CALL THIS GT'S PER-CALLEE RELEASER, IF IT HAS ONE, AND THEN CLEAR THE WORD** — the ONE emitter for
// both release sites (`__gt_await`/`__gt_try_await` and `__gt_promise_drop`), so the null guard, the
// argument order and the clear are stated once. Returns the block execution continues in.
//
// **A NULL RELEASER IS THE COMMON CASE AND MUST BE FREE.** A spawn of a callee with no managed
// argument, result or error leaves it 0 (`LowerMaxonToStd.emitAsyncSpawn`), and this SKIPS rather
// than indirect-calling through it — so a scalar-only async program pays one load, one compare and
// one branch, and emits nothing else.
//
// ⚠ **THE CLEAR IS A BACKSTOP AND NOT THE MECHANISM, AND THE ORDER OF THOSE TWO WORDS MATTERS.** What
// actually stops a GT being released twice is a COMPILE-TIME fact: `Parser.emitAwaitOp` consumes the
// promise's thread (`consumePromiseThread` — `movedFrom` on the owning binding, cleared from the pending
// temps), so an awaited promise emits no `__gt_promise_drop` for the drop site to reach. The store here is
// second in line: it makes "this GT no longer owes a release" a state the STRUCT carries, which is what
// composes with cancel — cancel reaches `__gt_promise_drop` by a road the parser's move analysis does not
// walk. Both sites clear it, because the invariant is about the GT and not about which door released it.
// The clear is also what leaves a recycled record's word 0 for its next tenant (`GtOffReleaser`).
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE CLAIM IS A CAS, AND IT IS THE ONE ITEM ON THE MULTI-M AUDIT WHOSE FAILURE IS A DOUBLE FREE
// RATHER THAN A LOST COUNT.** A load / call / clear is a read-modify-write with the entire release sitting
// inside it: two Ms can both observe the same non-null releaser, both call it, and both drop every managed
// argument the spawn owned — one free too many per slot, on memory the other M may already have handed back
// to the slab. The compile-time argument above does NOT close it, because it is an argument about which
// SITES the parser emits and not about how many Ms reach one of them (`cancel` specifically arrives by a road
// that analysis does not walk). `CAS(releaser, observed -> 0)` makes the caller that WINS the swap the only
// one that calls, which is
// `SchedRuntime.POffStatus`'s shape one struct over and for the identical reason — that note carries the
// heap corruption a read/test/store produced there, and names the emitter it cost.
//
// ⚠ **NO RETRY LOOP, AND THAT IS A PROPERTY OF THE WORD RATHER THAN AN OMISSION.** `gt.releaser` is written
// exactly twice in its life — once by the spawn, before the GT is published, and once to 0 here — so a
// failed swap can only mean another party got there first, and re-reading would find the 0 it just lost to.
// A CAS loop would spin to reach the conclusion the single failure already carries.
function emitGtReleaserCall(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, startBlock IrBlock, gt ValueId, resultDisposition AsyncResultDisposition) returns IrBlock
	var claim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"releaseclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"release", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cont = module.addBlock(func, label: b"releasedone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// The cheap test first: a spawn whose callee owns nothing leaves 0 and MUST pay nothing, which is
	// most async programs (see this function's header). Only a GT that really owes a release reaches
	// the CAS.
	let releaser = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(startBlock, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(releaser, addrId: gt, offset: GtOffReleaser, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: startBlock, value: releaser, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: claim, whenFalse: cont, ids: ids)

	let releaserAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: claim, record: gt, offset: GtOffReleaser, ids: ids)
	let cleared = emitConst(module, block: claim, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let won = emitCasAt(module, block: claim, addr: releaserAddr, expected: releaser, replacement: cleared, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: claim, result: won, lost: cont, won: release, ids: ids)

	let consumedFlag = emitConst(module, block: release, value: asyncResultConsumedFlag(resultDisposition), ids: ids)
	let released = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(release, op: StdOp.callIndirect(released, calleeReg: releaser, resultType: StdReturnType.void, argFloatMask: 0, args: twoArgs(gt, second: consumedFlag)))
	module.setTerminator(release, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))

	return cont
end 'emitGtReleaserCall'

// ⭐⭐ **ADD ONE HALF-TICKET TO THE TEARDOWN WORD AND ANSWER "AM I SECOND?" — the ONE atomic step of the
// rendezvous, and the ONE place its convention is spelled.** `atomicRmw` hands back the PRIOR value
// (`StdDialect` states that contract against `atomicCas`'s opposite one), so the party that finds the OTHER
// ticket already there is the second arrival and owes the teardown.
//
// ⛔ **IT IS ONE `lock xadd` AND THERE IS NO RETRY LOOP, BECAUSE THERE IS NOTHING TO RETRY.** A compare-and-
// swap would have to name the value it expects, and neither party can: each knows only its OWN half. An add
// commutes, so the two orders differ in nothing but which caller reads back the other's ticket — which is
// exactly the question being asked.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY READ OF THE STRUCT MUST PRECEDE THE CALLER'S OWN ARRIVAL.** After the add the other party may
// already have put the struct on a free list and a spawn may already have zeroed and reused it, so the
// runner loads `stackBase`/`stackSize` and the awaiter loads `result`/`threw` BEFORE stepping the word —
// never after. This is the ordering rule the whole mechanism rests on, and it is why the drop path never
// reads `gt.status` and then `gt.threw`/`gt.result` while a completion on another M moves `status` in
// between (`Runtime/AsyncReleaser.maxon`'s multi-M item 2): the drop reads neither word — the SECOND party
// does, after completion.
function emitGtTeardownArrive(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, gt ValueId, ticket FullRangeInt, otherTicket FullRangeInt) returns ValueId
	let wordAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffTeardown, ids: ids)
	let step = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ticket, ids: ids)
	let prior = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.atomicRmw(prior, addrId: wordAddr, operandId: step, opcode: StdAtomicRmwOpcode.add))
	return emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: prior, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: otherTicket, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtTeardownArrive'

// Arrive with `ticket` and, as the second party, reclaim — the whole of every party's arrival. Terminates `cur`
// and answers the block to continue in, where the struct may already belong to the next spawn.
function emitGtArriveAndReclaimIfSecond(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, gt ValueId, ticket FullRangeInt, otherTicket FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var reclaim = module.addBlock(func, label: b"teardownreclaim", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var arrived = module.addBlock(func, label: b"teardownarrived", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let second = emitGtTeardownArrive(module, block: cur, ids: ids, gt: gt, ticket: ticket, otherTicket: otherTicket)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(second, thenBlockId: reclaim.id, elseBlockId: arrived.id))

	module.appendOp(reclaim, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtReclaimName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	module.setTerminator(reclaim, op: StdOp.branch(arrived.id))
	return arrived
end 'emitGtArriveAndReclaimIfSecond'

// A record keeps a stack only of the size `kept` names: `__gt_free_get` hands a kept stack to a
// spawn as a seed of the size it asked for, so one of any other size would be recorded, framed and
// freed at the wrong size. The seed can move while a record waits, so `__gt_runner_done`,
// `__gt_reclaim` and `__gt_free_get` each check against the size current there.
export function emitGtDropStackUnlessSized(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, record ValueId, kept ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var checkSize = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stackkeepcheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var dropStack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stackkeepdrop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let base = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: record, offset: GtOffStackBase, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: base, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: checkSize, whenFalse: cont, ids: ids)

	let size = emitLoadField(module, block: checkSize, record: record, offset: GtOffStackSize, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: checkSize, a: size, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, b: kept, whenTrue: dropStack, whenFalse: cont, ids: ids)

	emitGtFreeRecordStack(module, block: dropStack, record: record, base: base, size: size, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dropStack, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitGtDropStackUnlessSized'

export function emitGtDropStack(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, record ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var dropStack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stackdrop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let base = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: record, offset: GtOffStackBase, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: base, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: dropStack, whenFalse: cont, ids: ids)

	let size = emitLoadField(module, block: dropStack, record: record, offset: GtOffStackSize, ids: ids)
	emitGtFreeRecordStack(module, block: dropStack, record: record, base: base, size: size, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dropStack, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitGtDropStack'

function emitGtFreeRecordStack(module StdModule, block IrBlock, record ValueId, base ValueId, size ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtStackFreeName, args: twoArgs(base, second: size)))
	let noStack = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: record, offset: GtOffStackBase, value: noStack)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: record, offset: GtOffStackSize, value: noStack)
end 'emitGtFreeRecordStack'

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_reclaim(gt)` — THE SECOND ARRIVAL's HALF: release what the promise still owned, then put the
// struct on a free list.** Reached from `__gt_owner_done` and `__gt_runner_done`, whichever of them was
// second, which is why it is a function rather than two copies of two calls.
//
// ⚠ **THE DISPOSITION IS `ownedByThisDrop` ON BOTH ROADS, AND THAT IS DERIVED RATHER THAN CHOSEN.** An
// AWAITED promise has already been released by its awaiter with `consumedByAwaiter` — releasing the result a
// second time would be the double free that union exists to prevent — and that release CLEARED `gt.releaser`
// by compare-and-swap, so this call finds 0 and emits nothing at all. A DROPPED promise's releaser is still
// armed, and `ownedByThisDrop` is the only correct answer for it (nobody else will ever release the
// result-or-error). One spelling serves both because the awaited road is a no-op, not because the two
// dispositions agree.
//
// ⚠ **`__gt_free_put` IS THE LAST USE OF `gt`.** It threads the struct onto a list through `GtOffNext`, and
// the next spawn may take it from there at once, so nothing below the put reads the struct.
function buildGtReclaim(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtReclaimName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0

	var released = emitGtReleaserCall(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: entry, gt: gt, resultDisposition: AsyncResultDisposition.ownedByThisDrop)

	var cellBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"reclaimcell", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var recordBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"reclaimrecord", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var putBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"reclaimput", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	module.setTerminator(released, op: StdOp.condBranch(emitGtIsReplyCell(module, block: released, gt: gt, ids: ids), thenBlockId: cellBlock.id, elseBlockId: recordBlock.id))

	module.appendOp(cellBlock, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtCellPutName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	module.setTerminator(cellBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	let seed = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: recordBlock, label: GtSeedBytesLabel, ids: ids)
	emitGtDropStackUnlessSized(module, func: func, cur: recordBlock, record: gt, kept: seed, cont: putBlock, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(putBlock, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtFreePutName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	module.setTerminator(putBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtReclaim'

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_owner_done(gt)` — THE CONSUMER's HALF: this promise is finished with.** Give back the thread's
// slot in `MOffGtLive`, then arrive at the rendezvous; if the runner was already there, reclaim.
//
// ⚠ **THE DEBIT IS THE CONSUMER'S AND IS UNCONDITIONAL, WHICH IS WHAT KEEPS THE EXIT GATE HONEST.**
// `MOffGtLive` counts threads whose OWNERSHIP is outstanding, and this call is exactly where a
// promise's ownership ends — whether the thread has finished, is running on another M, or is sitting in a
// ring that nothing will ever schedule. Moving it to the reclaim instead would make the exit gate report a
// leak for the honest program that spawns, drops and returns without ever driving the scheduler
// (`async-promise-drop.spawn-drop-loop-bounded` is that program); the MEMORY outliving the debit by a few
// schedules is the runner's business and `__gt_coro_sweep_dropped` is what bounds it.
function buildGtOwnerDone(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtOwnerDoneName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0

	emitSchedStepGtLive(module, block: entry, delta: OneFewer, ids: ids)

	var doneBlock = emitGtArriveAndReclaimIfSecond(module, func: func, cur: entry, gt: gt, ticket: GtTeardownConsumerTicket, otherTicket: GtTeardownRunnerTicket, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtOwnerDone'

// ⭐⭐ **IS THIS THREAD ONE THE SCHEDULER MUST RECLAIM RATHER THAN RUN? — the ONE spelling of the
// dropped-thread question.** `emitGtTombstoneHandback` asks it of the candidate `__gt_strand_next` or
// `__sched_find_runnable` is about to hand back, and `emitGtFifoSweepDropped` of each queue's front. Spelled
// out per site it would be two loads and two compares at each — and the day the protocol grows a second
// reclaimable state (a mark published in two steps, say) a copy would still be asking the old question and
// answering it wrongly, which is a thread RUN after its promise was dropped rather than a compile error.
//
// ⭐ **IT READS THE TEARDOWN WORD AND NOT THE STATUS, WHICH IS WHY IT IS NOW EXACT.** Exactly
// `GtTeardownConsumerTicket` means *"the promise is gone and no runner has taken responsibility"*. It
// replaced a `status == dropped` mark, which could not be exact for two reasons the layout forces: the
// trampoline overwrites the status word at completion, and a park overwrites it with `waiting`, so a mark
// left there was ERASED by either — while this word is written by nothing on the run/park/resume path.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AND IT ASKS A SECOND QUESTION — HAS THIS THREAD EVER RUN — BECAUSE RECLAIMING FROM A QUEUE IS
// FREEING WITHOUT RUNNING THE THREAD'S OWN CLEANUP.** A thread that has never been switched into holds
// nothing but its untouched seed stack and struct, so taking it off a queue and freeing both is complete. A
// thread that HAS run and is back in a queue may be holding a per-GT pipe handle, its read scratch, an
// operation's poll source or a child process HANDLE — and every one of those is released by the thread's OWN
// resumption path and by nothing else. Freeing such a thread here would strand every one of them.
//
// ⚠ **EVERY ASKER HAS CLAIMED THE THREAD FIRST**: the handback asks of a candidate already POPPED, and the
// sweep of a head it holds `__sched_lock` over. Asked of a thread nobody claimed, the answer could be read
// out of a struct already reclaimed and recycled — another thread's teardown word
// (`SchedRuntime.buildSchedSweepDropped`).
//
// ⇒ **A STARTED thread whose promise was renounced is therefore RUN TO COMPLETION, not abandoned in a
// queue**, and the strand runner's hand-off reclaims it: a drop cancels a thread that has not STARTED, and
// for one that has, it renounces the RESULT and lets the body finish. A started thread PARKED when its promise
// is dropped is no exception: the drop expires its wait, or cancels the operation the kernel still serves, and
// the thread is readied, runs to completion and is reclaimed by its runner (`buildGtPromiseDrop`).
// `async-promise-drop.never-ran-drop-not-run` pins that a never-run thread's body never runs.
function emitGtIsDropped(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let teardown = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffTeardown, ids: ids)
	let renounced = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: teardown, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtTeardownConsumerTicket, ids: ids)
	let waiter = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffWaiter, ids: ids)
	let neverRan = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: waiter, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, ids: ids)

	// Both operands are `cmp` results, so they are 0 or 1 and a bitwise AND is the logical one.
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: renounced, b: neverRan, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtIsDropped'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **IS THIS GT A GREEN THREAD, OR A COROUTINE? — `owner == gt`, and it is a PREDICATE rather than a
// KIND WORD ON PURPOSE.** A green thread owns itself and a coroutine is owned by the green thread that
// created it, so the relation `GtOffOwner` already carries answers the question exactly — where a second
// field saying *"I am a green thread"* would be the same fact written twice, free to disagree with the owner
// chain the whole invariant rests on, and wrong in exactly the program where it mattered.
//
// Its readers are the places the two kinds are handled differently: `__gt_ready_locked` (which end of the
// strand queue), `__gt_runner_done` (only a coroutine can be awaited) and `__gt_promise_drop` (which SWEEPS).
function emitGtIsGreenThread(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitCmp(module, block: block, a: emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids), b: gt, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtIsGreenThread'

// ⭐⭐ **DOES A GT'S ALREADY-LOADED STATUS WORD EQUAL `want`? — the ONE spelling of the comparison three
// entry points make, and the reason it is worth a name is that they read it for three different verdicts:**
// `__gt_promise_drop` asks `waiting?` to decide whether a store has to be deregistered before the thread can
// be given up, the awaits and the strand runner gate on `completed?` (where getting it wrong is a
// use-after-free rather than a leak), and `__gt_is_complete` answers it to a caller.
//
// ⚠ **IT TAKES THE LOADED WORD RATHER THAN THE GT**, so a caller asking two questions of one status loads it
// once, and it pairs with `emitLoadField(record: gt, offset: GtOffStatus)`, which is the load.
//
// ⚠ The scheduler's dropped-thread askers do NOT come through here: `emitGtIsDropped` reads the TEARDOWN
// word, which is a different question about a different field, and folding the two would put a status
// offset behind a name that no longer names one.
module function emitGtStatusEquals(module StdModule, block IrBlock, status ValueId, want GtStatus, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: status, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: want.rawValue, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtStatusEquals'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **PUBLISH `completed` — THE ONE STORE IN THIS RUNTIME A SECOND OS THREAD RACES, AND THE ONLY
// SPELLING OF IT.**
//
// A finishing green thread writes THREE words: `gt.result`, `gt.threw`, and then `gt.status = completed`.
// The first two are the DATA and the third is the FLAG that announces them, with no lock and no atomic
// anywhere between — so on a weakly-ordered ISA nothing stops the flag from becoming visible to another M
// before the two words it announces. The awaiter would then read `gt.result` before it was written: **a
// WRONG AWAITED VALUE, not a crash**.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A SECOND OS THREAD RUNS MAXON CODE.** `osThreadCreate` is a real `pthread_create` on
// arm64-macOS and a real `clone` on arm64-linux, and a `spawn` program runs several worker Ms at
// once. ⇒ the release half is HERE and the acquire half is `emitGtLoadPublishedStatus` below;
// **neither is a fix on its own** — a barrier on one side orders one thread's accesses against
// nothing.
//
// ⚠ **THE STORE ORDER IS STILL LOAD-BEARING AND THE RELEASE DOES NOT REPLACE IT.** A release orders the
// EARLIER accesses before itself; it says nothing about accesses that come after. So `result` and `threw`
// must still be written BEFORE this call, exactly as `GtCellCompleteName` states.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO HAND-ASSEMBLED TRAMPOLINES PUBLISH THE SAME WORD AND CANNOT COME THROUGH HERE**, being raw
// machine code rather than Std ops (`Arm64GtRuntime.buildArm64GtTrampolineChunk`,
// `X64GtRuntime.buildGtTrampolineChunk`). Each emits its own ordered store at its own site and says so;
// `assertCompletedPublicationIsOrdered` below can see neither, which is stated at that checker.
function emitGtPublishCompleted(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let completed = emitConst(module, block: block, value: GtStatus.completed.rawValue, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeRelease(gt, offset: GtOffStatus, valueId: completed, valueType: StdType.i64))
end 'emitGtPublishCompleted'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **OBSERVE A GREEN THREAD'S STATUS — the ACQUIRE half of the publication above, and the only
// spelling of a read that a `completed` answer can come out of.**
//
// Every reader of this word that can see another M's `completed` reads it through here: the awaits' test and
// their registration, the select's scan, the runner's hand-off gate and the `gtIsComplete` peek. The acquire
// is what makes `gt.result` / `gt.threw` — written before the release store — visible to the thread that saw
// the flag.
//
// ⚠ **THE ORDERING IS A PROPERTY OF THE READ, NOT OF WHAT THE READ IS THEN COMPARED AGAINST**, which is why
// every reader of the word comes through here whatever it compares with — a reader whose comparand is not
// the `completed` constant in the source is one `assertCompletedPublicationIsOrdered` structurally cannot
// see, so it is the one that would stay wrong.
//
// ⚠ **NOT `emitLoadField`.** That helper is the runtime's generic field read and must stay generic; the
// status word is not a generic field, and a caller who reaches for the generic door gets a plain load.
//
// ⚠ **NEITHER OF THE PAIR IS EXPORTED, AND THE SELF-COMPILE IS WHAT SAYS SO** (E3092): every
// Std-tier access to a green thread's status word is in THIS FILE. `MailboxRuntime` writes the word
// too, but only a mailbox park's `waiting`, made under the mailbox lock, which is a different
// question — see the `completed` note above for why the two are not one rule.
function emitGtLoadPublishedStatus(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLoadAcquireField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffStatus, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtLoadPublishedStatus'

let CompletedPublicationCheckName = "assertCompletedPublicationIsOrdered"

// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE CHECK — because "publish `completed` with a release" must not be a CONVENTION FOUR CALL SITES
// ARE TRUSTED TO HONOUR.** That is this project's signature failure written at the scheduler's most
// dangerous word — a fact stated in a comment, honoured at three sites and checked at none.
//
// It walks the functions `installGtRuntime` just built and REFUSES two spellings:
//
//   • a plain `storeIndirect` of the constant `completed` into `GtOffStatus` — a PUBLICATION with no
//     release, which is the defect itself;
//   • a plain `loadIndirect` from `GtOffStatus` whose result is compared FOR EQUALITY against the constant
//     `completed` — an OBSERVATION with no acquire, which is the same defect read from the other end.
//
// Both tests require the OFFSET **and** the constant together: `GtOffStatus` is a small offset other structs
// in this file also use, and `2` is an ordinary small integer, so only the pair names the publication.
//
// ⚠ **THE PAIR ALONE IS NOT ENOUGH, WHICH IS WHY THE OBSERVATION TEST ALSO READS THE PREDICATE** — see
// `requireOrderedObservation` for the record in another file that matched both halves and asked nothing about a
// green thread.
//
// ⚠ **THE TWO HAND-ASSEMBLED TRAMPOLINES ARE OUTSIDE ITS REACH**: they are machine-code bytes and never Std
// ops. Each publishes at ONE site and names its ordering in the instruction it emits. ⇒ the check is the
// backstop for the population that GROWS — a fifth reader, a second reply path — and the single-homed emitters
// are the mechanism for the rest.
//
// ⚠ It is a PANIC and not a diagnostic: no program can provoke it, because the ops it inspects are the
// compiler's own emission. A user-facing error code would name a rule a user cannot break.
function assertCompletedPublicationIsOrdered(module StdModule, firstFuncIndex ElementIndex)
	for index in firstFuncIndex upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		assertOneFuncPublishesInOrder(module, func: module.getFunc(index))
	end 'eachFunc'
end 'assertCompletedPublicationIsOrdered'

// One function's two passes: collect the ids that MEAN `completed` and the ids that came out of a plain
// status read, then look for either forbidden pairing. TWO passes and not one, because a `const` may be
// defined in a block the store or the compare does not follow in layout order — dominance is not what this
// question is about.
//
// ⚠ **THE OP LIST IS `module.liveOpIndices`, WHICH IS ALSO WHY TERMINATORS ARE COVERED.** A private block
// walk here would be a third copy of that walk and would have missed exactly what the private ones miss:
// `IrBlock` keeps its terminator outside `opRefs`, so a hand-rolled loop sees no `ret`, no `condBranch` and
// no `switch` at all.
function assertOneFuncPublishesInOrder(module StdModule, func IrFunction)
	let opIndices = module.liveOpIndices(func)
	var completedConsts = ValueIdArray.create()
	var plainStatusLoads = ValueIdArray.create()
	var access = StdAccessOperands.create()
	var arith = StdArithOperands.create()

	for opIdx in opIndices 'collect'
		classifyAccessOperands(liveRuntimeOpAt(module, opIdx: opIdx, reader: CompletedPublicationCheckName), access: access)
		collectCompletedWordDefs(access, completedConsts: completedConsts, plainStatusLoads: plainStatusLoads)
	end 'collect'

	// Nothing in this function names the constant, so neither pairing can exist in it — every `__gt_subp_`
	// entry point and most of the scheduler take this exit before the second walk.
	if completedConsts.count() == 0 'namesNoCompletedWord'
		return
	end 'namesNoCompletedWord'

	for opIdx in opIndices 'check'
		let op = liveRuntimeOpAt(module, opIdx: opIdx, reader: CompletedPublicationCheckName)

		classifyAccessOperands(op, access: access)
		classifyArithOperands(op, operands: arith)
		requireOrderedCompletedAccess(access, arith: arith, func: func, completedConsts: completedConsts, plainStatusLoads: plainStatusLoads)
	end 'check'
end 'assertOneFuncPublishesInOrder'

// The op at an index the live-op walk just handed out — so an out-of-range answer is a broken module rather
// than a bad argument, and every walk over an installed runtime says so once instead of each spelling it.
// `reader` names the checker, because the panic sends a reader to the walk that tripped it.
export function liveRuntimeOpAt(module StdModule, opIdx ElementIndex, reader String) returns StdOp
	return try module.ops.get(opIdx) otherwise panic("{reader}: opIdx {opIdx} from the live-op walk is not in module.ops")
end 'liveRuntimeOpAt'

function collectCompletedWordDefs(access StdAccessOperands, completedConsts ValueIdArray, plainStatusLoads ValueIdArray)
	match access.shape 'k'
		constant then pushIfCompletedConst(completedConsts, resultId: access.resultId, value: access.constValue)
		load then pushIfPlainStatusLoad(plainStatusLoads, access: access)
		neither or
		globalAddress or
		store or
		atomic then break
	end 'k'
end 'collectCompletedWordDefs'

function pushIfCompletedConst(completedConsts ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId, value FullRangeInt)
	if value == GtStatus.completed 'meansCompleted'
		completedConsts.push(resultId)
	end 'meansCompleted'
end 'pushIfCompletedConst'

function pushIfPlainStatusLoad(plainStatusLoads ValueIdArray, access StdAccessOperands)
	if access.ordering == StdAccessOrdering.ordered 'alreadyOrdered'
		return
	end 'alreadyOrdered'

	if access.offset == GtOffStatus 'readsTheStatusWord'
		plainStatusLoads.push(access.resultId)
	end 'readsTheStatusWord'
end 'pushIfPlainStatusLoad'

function requireOrderedCompletedAccess(access StdAccessOperands, arith StdArithOperands, func IrFunction, completedConsts ValueIdArray, plainStatusLoads ValueIdArray)
	if arith.isComparison() 'aCompare'
		requireOrderedObservation(func, lhs: arith.lhs, rhs: arith.rhs, pred: arith.pred, completedConsts: completedConsts, plainStatusLoads: plainStatusLoads)
	end 'aCompare'

	match access.shape 'k'
		store then requireOrderedCompletedStore(access, func: func, completedConsts: completedConsts)
		neither or
		constant or
		globalAddress or
		load or
		atomic then break
	end 'k'
end 'requireOrderedCompletedAccess'

function requireOrderedCompletedStore(access StdAccessOperands, func IrFunction, completedConsts ValueIdArray)
	if access.ordering == StdAccessOrdering.ordered 'alreadyOrdered'
		return
	end 'alreadyOrdered'

	requireOrderedPublication(func, offset: access.offset, valueId: access.valueId, completedConsts: completedConsts)
end 'requireOrderedCompletedStore'

function requireOrderedPublication(func IrFunction, offset ByteOffset, valueId ValueId, completedConsts ValueIdArray)
	if offset != GtOffStatus 'notTheStatusWord'
		return
	end 'notTheStatusWord'

	if not completedConsts.contains(valueId) 'notTheCompletedConstant'
		return
	end 'notTheCompletedConstant'

	panic("assertCompletedPublicationIsOrdered: '{String.from(func.name)}' publishes `completed` into a green thread's status word with a PLAIN store. That store announces `gt.result`/`gt.threw` to another OS thread, and a plain store on a weakly-ordered ISA may become visible before the words it announces — a wrong awaited value, not a crash. Emit it through `emitGtPublishCompleted`, which is the release store")
end 'requireOrderedPublication'

// ⚠ **`Array.contains` AND NOT A `Set`, NOR A THIRD PRIVATE LINEAR SCAN.** Each list holds a handful of
// ids — the `completed` constants a function names and the status words it reads — so the stdlib's own
// linear membership is both the cheapest structure and the one already written. (`SemanticCheck` and
// `HallCondition` each carry a private copy of that scan; neither is this pass's to consolidate.)
//
// ⚠ **BOTH OPERAND ORDERS ARE TESTED, and that is not defensive symmetry**: `emitGtStatusEquals` puts the
// loaded word on the LEFT, but `foldConstOperands` reorders around a constant operand, and a check that knew
// one spelling would go quiet the day a fold ran first.
function requireOrderedObservation(func IrFunction, lhs ValueId, rhs ValueId, pred StdCmpPred, completedConsts ValueIdArray, plainStatusLoads ValueIdArray)
	// ⛔⛔ **ONLY AN EQUALITY TEST ASKS THIS QUESTION, AND THE OFFSET-AND-CONSTANT PAIR IS NOT THE EXACT
	// DISCRIMINATOR THIS CHECK'S HEADER CLAIMS.** `completed` is 2, and a record in another file whose word
	// at `GtOffStatus`'s offset is ORDERED against 2 matches both halves while asking nothing about a
	// green thread. ⇒ the predicate is the third half of the pair: *"has this thread reached `completed`?"* is
	// `==` or `!=` and can be nothing else, so an ORDER comparison is not the observation this check exists for.
	//
	// ⚠ **IT LOSES NOTHING.** `emitGtStatusEquals` is the one speller of a status test and emits `eq`; a
	// `status >= completed` would be meaningless against an enum whose order carries no meaning.
	match pred 'asked'
		eq or
		ne then break

		lt or
		gt or
		le or
		ge then return
	end 'asked'

	let lhsIsPlainRead = plainStatusLoads.contains(lhs) and completedConsts.contains(rhs)
	let rhsIsPlainRead = plainStatusLoads.contains(rhs) and completedConsts.contains(lhs)

	if not (lhsIsPlainRead or rhsIsPlainRead) 'notTheCompletedTest'
		return
	end 'notTheCompletedTest'

	panic("assertCompletedPublicationIsOrdered: '{String.from(func.name)}' tests a green thread's status word against `completed` after reading it with a PLAIN load. Without the acquire, `gt.result`/`gt.threw` may still be unwritten on this M when the flag reads as set. Read it through `emitGtLoadPublishedStatus`, which is the acquire load")
end 'requireOrderedObservation'

function buildGtPromiseFieldRelease(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtPromiseFieldReleaseName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var dropIt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dropheld", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"released", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let box = 0
	let offset = 1

	let slot = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: box, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let held = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: slot, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: held, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: dropIt, whenFalse: done, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(dropIt, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtPromiseDropName, args: argsOf(held)))
	module.setTerminator(dropIt, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtPromiseFieldRelease'

// See `GtPromiseFieldVacateName` for the contract, and `emitPromiseSlotVacateTail` for the check it shares
// with `__managed_vacate`.
function buildGtPromiseFieldVacate(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtPromiseFieldVacateName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var consumedTwice = module.addBlock(func, label: b"vacated", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var empty = module.addBlock(func, label: b"empty", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let box = 0
	let offset = 1
	let read = 2

	let slot = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: box, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)

	emitPromiseSlotVacateTail(module, func: func, entry: entry, consumedTwice: consumedTwice, empty: empty, slot: slot, read: read, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtPromiseFieldVacate'

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_is_complete(gt) -> 1 when that green thread has reached `completed`, 0 otherwise** — the
// floor of `__Builtins.gtIsComplete(promise.inner)`. See `GtIsCompleteName` for what it is for, why it must
// have no side effect, and why it is target-neutral.
//
// **THE BODY IS THE PREDICATE `emitGtStatusEquals` NAMES**, which is the point: the same comparison
// the await and drop paths make, reachable from a program. It tests and dereferences the handle and nothing else — no drive, no queue walk, no status write — so it composes with
// `await`'s linearity for free (a peek is not an await, so E3100 counts none of them) and a caller may ask
// it in a scan loop as often as it likes.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS INSTALLED UNCONDITIONALLY WITH THE REST OF THE SCHEDULER, not on a usage bit of its own** — the
// opposite of what `__proc_pid` does one runtime over, and for a reason that does not carry across. The
// `__proc_` split was worth making because the cheap entry's neighbour drags in `usesHeap` AND
// `usesManagedMemory` transitively, i.e. two whole runtimes. Here the family is already installed as ONE
// closure (`installGtRuntime` builds `__gt_sleep`, `__gt_process_run`, `__gt_io_park` and the timer/proc
// stores for every async program, each far larger than this), and this entry is a few ops that reference
// nothing but the abort. A bit for it would be a mechanism whose whole yield is a few bytes.
//
// A handle of 0 is a read of a slot another read already emptied. A peek never reaches the vacate that
// aborts a consume of such a read, so it aborts with 118 here rather than loading through null.
function buildGtIsComplete(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtIsCompleteName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var vacated = module.addBlock(func, label: b"vacated", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var peek = module.addBlock(func, label: b"peek", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: gt, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: vacated, whenFalse: peek, ids: ids)

	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: vacated, abort: RuntimeAbort.promiseSlotConsumedTwice, ids: ids)

	let status = emitGtLoadPublishedStatus(module, block: peek, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(peek, op: StdOp.ret(emitGtStatusEquals(module, block: peek, status: status, want: GtStatus.completed, ids: ids)))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtIsComplete'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **IS THIS PROMISE A REPLY CELL RATHER THAN A THREAD ANYTHING WILL RUN? — `funcPtr == 0`.** See
// `GtCellAllocName` for why the discriminator is this word and not a kind field, and for the one population
// that shares the zero and cannot reach this predicate.
//
// ⚠ NOT exported: its readers are `__gt_promise_drop`, `__gt_runner_done` and `__gt_reclaim`, in this
// file. `__gt_reclaim`'s answer picks the pool, and a cell put on a GT-record list would be handed to a
// spawn as a whole record. A cell needs no such question
// anywhere else — `__gt_await`, `__gt_try_await` and `__gt_is_complete` treat it as the ordinary promise it
// is, which is the design's whole claim.
function emitGtIsReplyCell(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let funcPtr = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffFuncPtr, ids: ids)
	return emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: funcPtr, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtIsReplyCell'

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_cell_alloc(releaser) -> cell` — MINT A REPLY CELL.** See `GtCellAllocName` for what a
// cell is and why it is a `Promise`.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS NOT `__gt_spawn`, AND THE THREE DIFFERENCES ARE EACH LOAD-BEARING**: no STACK is
// committed (nothing runs on it), `funcPtr` is stored as zero (that zero IS the discriminator), and
// it is never PUBLISHED to a queue — a spawned GT is published by `__gt_ready`, and a cell nobody
// schedules must not be discoverable by a scheduler at all.
//
// ⭐ **THE STATUS IS `waiting` AND THE OWNER IS THE AWAITER'S GREEN THREAD.** `waiting` is what
// `__gt_is_complete` and the awaits read as *"not yet"*; the owner is copied off the
// CURRENT GT's owner, exactly as a coroutine's is, so `__gt_promise_drop`'s sweep on the way out reaches a
// real queue rather than dereferencing a null (`buildGtSpawn` records that a zero owner is not a benign
// default). `owner != cell`, so `emitGtIsGreenThread` answers false for a cell and the coroutine sweep is
// the one it takes — which is right: a cell is in NO queue, so the sweep finds nothing and costs two loads.
//
// ⚠ **THE LIVE-COUNT CREDIT IS THE SPAWN'S, AND THE DEBIT IS `__gt_owner_done`'s.** A cell is owned by
// exactly one promise, so the exit gate counts it exactly as it counts a coroutine — and a cell whose
// awaiter never arrives is therefore a `greenThreadLeak` (75) with the count in it, not a silent exit.
function buildGtCellAlloc(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtCellAllocName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let releaser = 0

	let cell = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(cell, callee: GtCellGetName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))

	let fieldsStart = emitFieldAddr(module, block: entry, record: cell, offset: GtOffStackBase, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.memFill(fieldsStart, byteCountId: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: GtCellBytes - GtOffStackBase, ids: ids), value: 0))

	emitGtMarkWaiting(module, block: entry, gt: cell, ids: ids)
	let owner = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: entry, ids: ids), offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: cell, offset: GtOffOwner, value: owner)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: cell, offset: GtOffReleaser as ByteOffset, value: releaser)

	emitSchedStepGtLive(module, block: entry, delta: OneMore, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(cell))
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtCellAlloc'

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_cell_complete(cell, result, threw)` — ANSWER A REPLY.** See `GtCellCompleteName` for the
// store order and for why nothing may read the cell after the arrival below.
function buildGtCellComplete(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtCellCompleteName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 3, paramTypes: threeI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(3)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 3)
	let cell = 0
	let result = 1
	let threw = 2

	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: cell, offset: GtOffResult, value: result)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: cell, offset: GtOffThrew, value: threw)

	// ⭐ The RELEASE that makes the two stores above visible to the awaiting M — see
	// `emitGtPublishCompleted`, which is also why the store ORDER here still matters.
	emitGtPublishCompleted(module, block: entry, gt: cell, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtRunnerDoneName, args: twoArgs(cell, second: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: GtNoHeldStrand, ids: ids))))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtCellComplete'

// ⭐ **`__gt_promise_drop(gt)` — RENOUNCE AN UN-AWAITED GREEN THREAD.** The ownership DUAL of
// the `__gt_await` reclaim: the front-end enrols an `async` spawn's Promise as an owned value and emits this
// call on every path to scope exit / a re-arm that neither `await`s nor re-arms it, and `p.cancel()` arrives
// by the same road.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **A REPLY CELL TAKES THE FIRST ARM, AND IT IS THE ONE POPULATION THIS FUNCTION MAY NEVER
// FREE.** A cell is a `Promise` whose status is `waiting` for its whole life without ever having
// parked, so the `waiting` road below, which withdraws a park and readies the thread, has nothing
// to act on in a struct the REPLIER is about to write three words into. ⇒ a cell adds **the
// consumer's ticket only, never a free**, and the pair still closes exactly once on every (state ×
// consumer) combination:
//
//   • **PENDING, dropped, then answered** — the drop adds the consumer ticket and returns; the replier's
//     `__gt_cell_complete` writes into a live struct and its `__gt_runner_done` finds the consumer ticket
//     already there, so the REPLIER reclaims. (`services.pending-reply-dropped-then-answered`.)
//   • **COMPLETED, then dropped** — the runner ticket is already there, so `__gt_owner_done` is second and
//     the DROPPER reclaims. Taking the `waiting` road for this one would have stranded the struct
//     INVISIBLY: `__gt_owner_done` has already debited the live count, so the exit gate reads clean.
//     (`services.completed-reply-dropped-is-reclaimed`.)
//   • **PENDING, then AWAITED** — no drop happens at all; `__gt_try_await` is the consumer and the replier
//     the runner, in whichever order they arrive. (`services.send-and-await-a-reply`.)
//   • **ANSWERED and never dropped nor awaited** — impossible: the promise is enrolled owned at the send
//     (`Parser.emitServiceSend`), so every path either awaits it or drops it, and the live count would
//     report a `greenThreadLeak` if one did not.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT HAS THREE ARMS, AND THE TWO QUESTIONS IT ASKS ARE `a reply cell?` AND `waiting?`.**
// Everything a drop owes a thread that is queued, RUNNING on another M, or already completed is the
// same thing — the consumer's half of the teardown rendezvous (`GtOffTeardown`) — so those three
// collapse into one arm that cannot be got wrong by misreading a state. **A PARKED thread is the
// one genuine exception**: it is registered in the timer store, on a poll descriptor, on the
// quiesce word or in an in-flight OVERLAPPED read, and its wait must be ended before a runner will
// come back for it.
//
//   • `waiting` on the timer, the poller or the quiesce word — the drop withdraws the registration
//     and readies the thread ONLY IF ITS OWN WITHDRAWAL FOUND IT: the timer removal found the
//     entry, `__np_pd_drop` answered found, or the CAS on `GtQuiesceWaiterLabel` won. A miss means
//     the fire, the readiness walk, or `__gt_owner_done`/`__sched_checkdead` has readied it
//     already, and a second ready of one wait is fatal. Either way the thread runs its body out and
//     its runner reclaims it.
//   • `waiting` with an IN-FLIGHT OVERLAPPED READ: the thread parked on a yielding `ReadFile` and the KERNEL
//     still holds its OVERLAPPED (`gt + GtOffOverlapped`). Freeing the GT here would be a use-after-free when the read
//     completes, so the park is left standing and the read is CANCELLED (CancelIoEx): its own packet readies
//     the thread, which unwinds itself — the await arm's answer, and this dropper never owns the struct.
//   • `waiting` in an await or an awaitAny — the thread will be readied by the completion it waits for, so it
//     takes the arm below.
//   • `waiting` in a send — `__mbox_send_abandon`, or the waker that claimed it first, readies it,
//     and it then takes the arm below.
//   • anything else (`ready`, or a `completed`/running thread whose status this call never inspects) — the
//     thread is in a strand queue, on a processor, or finished, and in every one of those a RUNNER will
//     arrive: the party that takes a NEVER-RUN tombstone out of a queue, or — for a thread that has already
//     started — the strand runner whose switch comes back from its completion, because a started thread is run
//     out rather than abandoned (`emitGtIsDropped` states why). So the drop performs the consumer's half and
//     nothing else, and whichever of the two arrives SECOND reclaims.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE STATUS AND THE PARK NOTE ARE READ WITHOUT A LOCK, WHICH IS SOUND ONLY BECAUSE THE
// EXECUTING MACHINE HOLDS THE TARGET's STRAND.** A dropper is a member of that strand, or the
// scheduler context of the machine running it, reclaiming a tombstone's argument promise; a strand
// runs on one machine at a time, so the target cannot run, re-park or rewrite its note between
// these reads and the arm's acts. The premise is checked against `SchedRuntime.MOffHeldStrand`, and
// a drop on any other machine is fatal. A waker on another machine can still move `waiting` to
// `ready` meanwhile, which the found-or-missed rule above answers.
function buildGtPromiseDrop(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtPromiseDropName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), needsGreenThreadStackGuard: true, pinsItsThread: true)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notAReplyCell = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notreplycell", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var strandHeld = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dropstrandheld", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var strandNotHeld = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dropstrandnotheld", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var mailboxParked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedmailboxpark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notMailbox = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppednotmailbox", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sendParked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedsendpark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sendWake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedsendwake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notSendParked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppednotsendpark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var waitingArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"waitingarm", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notAwaiting = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppednotawaiting", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pollerParked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedpollerpark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var readParked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedreadpark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var readCancel = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedreadcancel", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var readMovedOn = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedreadmovedon", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var netpollParked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppednetpoll", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var netpollOwned = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppednetpollowned", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notNetpollPark = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppednotnetpoll", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var timerParked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedtimer", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parkMissed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedparkmissed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notTimerPark = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppednottimer", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var quiesceParked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedquiesce", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var quiesceOwned = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedquiesceowned", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unknownPark = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedunknownpark", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var queuedArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"queuedarm", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sweepGreen = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sweepgreen", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sweepCoro = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sweepcoro", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var dropDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dropdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0

	// entry: a REPLY CELL → the consumer's half alone (see the ⭐⭐⭐ block above; it is `waiting` for its whole
	// life and must never be freed here). Otherwise `waiting` → deregister, anything else → the shared
	// consumer's half. The status word decides nothing about ownership — only whether a store has to be
	// emptied first.
	let isReplyCell = emitGtIsReplyCell(module, block: entry, gt: gt, ids: ids)

	// The renounce mark is stored ahead of every arm below, because each of them readies the
	// thread (`emitGtRenouncePark`): a mark written past that point races the coroutine's own
	// resumption, and what slips through the window is a far-end wait this one-shot call can no
	// longer end. `__gt_io_park`, `__mbox_send` and `__gt_quiesce` read it.
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: gt, offset: GtOffRenounced, value: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: GtPromiseGivenUp, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isReplyCell, thenBlockId: queuedArm.id, elseBlockId: notAReplyCell.id))

	let heldStrand = emitLoadField(module, block: notAReplyCell, record: emitLoadCurrentM(module, block: notAReplyCell, ids: ids), offset: MOffHeldStrand, ids: ids)
	let targetStrand = emitLoadField(module, block: notAReplyCell, record: gt, offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids)
	let holdsTargetStrand = emitCmp(module, block: notAReplyCell, a: heldStrand, b: targetStrand, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notAReplyCell, op: StdOp.condBranch(holdsTargetStrand, thenBlockId: strandHeld.id, elseBlockId: strandNotHeld.id))
	emitRuntimeFatal(module, func: func, block: strandNotHeld, fatal: RuntimeFatal.promiseDroppedOffItsStrand, ids: ids)

	let status = emitLoadField(module, block: strandHeld, record: gt, offset: GtOffStatus, ids: ids)
	let isWaiting = emitGtStatusEquals(module, block: strandHeld, status: status, want: GtStatus.waiting, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(strandHeld, op: StdOp.condBranch(isWaiting, thenBlockId: waitingArm.id, elseBlockId: queuedArm.id))

	// waiting arm: the park kind decides which store has to be emptied before the thread can be given up.
	//
	// ⛔⛔⛔ **THE MAILBOX PARK KIND IS ANSWERED HERE, AND THE ANSWER IS AN ABORT.**
	// A park is registered on the TIMER or the POLLER by `__gt_register_park`, or on a MAILBOX by
	// `__mbox_recv`, which stores the GT into `mbox.parkedGt`. The road below empties the timer and poller
	// stores, and NOTHING anywhere clears `mbox.parkedGt` but the waker's own store — so a mailbox-parked GT
	// given up by this call would be reclaimed while `mbox.parkedGt` still pointed at it. The next
	// `__mbox_send` / `__mbox_close` would then store into freed memory and publish a freed GT: a
	// cross-green-thread use-after-free.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT IS AN ABORT RATHER THAN A THIRD DEREGISTRATION BECAUSE NOTHING IN THE LANGUAGE CAN REACH IT.**
	// `__mbox_recv` — the ONE producer of a mailbox park — has exactly two callers, both inside the synthesized
	// `<T>.__loop`: a reply cell has `funcPtr == 0` and is never enqueued, so it can execute nothing and can
	// never call `__mbox_recv`; `<T>.__loop` is reached only by `__gt_spawn_green` from `__svc_spawn`, which
	// hands back a mailbox rather than a promise; and no `async` can spawn `<T>.__loop`, whose symbol
	// (`serviceLoopSymbol`) is `__`-prefixed and unspellable in source (E2051). ⇒ **the only GT that can be
	// mailbox-parked is a service's own green thread, and nothing in the language names one with a
	// `Promise`.**
	//
	// ⇒ **What is written here is the STOP, not a repair.** A third deregistration would be emitter code
	// nothing in this tree can execute, in a reclamation path, deciding a question the design has not
	// answered — what a service whose loop is freed mid-`recv` owes its mailbox's `refs` and its state. The
	// abort turns a silent use-after-free into a named exit at the instruction that caused it, and it is
	// SABOTAGE-PROVABLE where a deregistration would not be: point the compare at `GtParkKind.timer` and a
	// dropped sleeping promise exits 94.
	let parkKind = emitLoadField(module, block: waitingArm, record: gt, offset: GtOffParkKind, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: waitingArm, value: parkKind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.mailbox.rawValue, whenTrue: mailboxParked, whenFalse: notMailbox, ids: ids)
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: mailboxParked, abort: RuntimeAbort.schedulerPromiseParkedOnAMailbox, ids: ids)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: notMailbox, value: parkKind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.mailboxSend.rawValue, whenTrue: sendParked, whenFalse: notSendParked, ids: ids)

	let abandonDebt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(sendParked, op: StdOp.call(abandonDebt, callee: MboxSendAbandonName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	emitSchedWakeIfOwed(module, cur: sendParked, owed: abandonDebt, wake: sendWake, cont: queuedArm, ids: ids)

	// ⭐⭐ **A THREAD PARKED IN AN AWAIT OR AN awaitAny IS RENOUNCED, NOT DEREGISTERED.** Its frame holds the
	// promise it awaits, and only its own body can drop that; the completion it waits for readies it, it runs
	// its body out, and its runner reclaims it — the queued arm's contract.
	let inAwait = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: notSendParked, value: parkKind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.awaitPromise.rawValue, ids: ids)
	let inAwaitAny = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: notSendParked, value: parkKind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.awaitAny.rawValue, ids: ids)
	let awaiting = emitBin(module, block: notSendParked, a: inAwait, b: inAwaitAny, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(notSendParked, op: StdOp.condBranch(awaiting, thenBlockId: queuedArm.id, elseBlockId: notAwaiting.id))

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **A PARKED THREAD IS RENOUNCED, NOT ABANDONED — the AWAIT arm's answer, generalised.**
	// Nothing can unwind a SUSPENDED frame from outside: freeing the stack of a thread that will never
	// resume strands every heap value its locals own. So the wait is EXPIRED and the thread READIED:
	// it resumes, unwinds its own frame, and its runner reclaims it.
	emitGtBranchOnPollerPark(module, block: notAwaiting, kind: parkKind, onPoller: pollerParked, elsewhere: notNetpollPark, ids: ids)

	// ⭐⭐⭐ **AN OPERATION THE KERNEL IS STILL SERVING IS RENOUNCED IN PLACE, WITHOUT BEING TAKEN OFF THE POLLER —
	// the AWAIT arm's answer, for the one park whose wait cannot be ended by the runtime.** The thread's
	// OVERLAPPED is embedded in its own struct, so a thread taken off the poller here could unwind and be
	// reclaimed while the kernel still holds that memory. Its completion packet is the only proof the kernel is
	// done, so the drop CANCELS the operation — which makes the packet come at once — and leaves the park
	// standing. The thread is readied by its own completion, answers a failed operation, unwinds its frame and
	// its runner reclaims it: the queued arm's contract exactly.
	//
	// ⚠ **`io_source` IS THE GUARD AND IT MEANS "AN OVERLAPPED OPERATION OF ANY KIND IS IN FLIGHT".** It is
	// stamped by `__np_op_open` for a pipe read and by `__np_op_aim` for a socket transfer, and cleared by the
	// completion's own wake or by `__np_op_close`; `io_handle` beside it names whichever handle the request is
	// on. ⛔ **A SOCKET PARKED IN A `WSARecv`/`WSASend` THEREFORE READS NON-ZERO AND BELONGS ON THIS ARM**, not
	// on the deregistering one below: taking it off its record while the kernel holds its OVERLAPPED is the
	// use-after-free this arm exists to prevent. Only a park with NO request outstanding — a child, or a socket
	// waiting on a readiness — reads 0.
	let inFlightSource = emitLoadField(module, block: pollerParked, record: gt, offset: GtOffIoSource, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: pollerParked, value: inFlightSource, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: readParked, whenFalse: netpollParked, ids: ids)

	// ⛔⛔ **THE CANCEL MUST NAME THE OPERATION THIS THREAD IS PARKED ON, AND ONLY A RE-READ UNDER THE LOCK SAYS
	// SO.** The status this arm was chosen on was read without the lock: a packet landing since readies the
	// thread, which can resume, finish its operation, give its source back and ISSUE ANOTHER on the same
	// OVERLAPPED — and the cancel would then abort a request nobody dropped. Under the lock a `waiting` status
	// is exact, because the only party that ends a poller park is a readiness walk and it holds this lock; and a
	// thread that is `waiting` is not running, so its own `io_source` and `io_handle` cannot move either.
	//
	// ⛔ **AND THE CANCEL IS ISSUED UNDER THE SAME HOLD, BECAUSE THE PROOF EXPIRES THE INSTANT IT IS RELEASED.**
	// Dropping the lock between the re-read and `CancelIoEx` is the very race the re-read closes. It costs
	// nothing to hold it: the call is `SyscallClass.schedulerBound`, so no handoff happens inside it, and
	// `CancelIoEx` only MARKS the request — it never waits on the poller that is waiting on this lock.
	// ⚠ **`ManagedSocketRuntime.emitSocketOverlappedTransfer`'s cancel is outside the lock for a reason that does
	// not apply here**: there a thread cancels its OWN in-flight request, so no second party can retire it
	// underneath.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: readParked, ids: ids)
	let readStatus = emitLoadField(module, block: readParked, record: gt, offset: GtOffStatus, ids: ids)
	let stillReading = emitGtStatusEquals(module, block: readParked, status: readStatus, want: GtStatus.waiting, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(readParked, op: StdOp.condBranch(stillReading, thenBlockId: readCancel.id, elseBlockId: readMovedOn.id))

	let readHandle = emitLoadField(module, block: readCancel, record: gt, offset: GtOffIoHandle, ids: ids)
	let readOvl = emitBin(module, block: readCancel, a: gt, b: emitConst(module, block: readCancel, value: GtOffOverlapped as FullRangeInt, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(readCancel, op: StdOp.osCancelIo(readHandle, ovlId: readOvl))
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: readCancel, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(readCancel, op: StdOp.branch(queuedArm.id))

	// The completion got there first, so the thread is queued and owes this dropper nothing but the consumer's
	// half — the same fall-through every arm below makes.
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: readMovedOn, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(readMovedOn, op: StdOp.branch(queuedArm.id))

	// The poller's expiry takes the thread off its direction word and marks the wait, so its resumption answers
	// `timedOut` rather than re-issuing a syscall on a descriptor nobody wants.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: netpollParked, ids: ids)
	let netpollTook = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(netpollParked, op: StdOp.call(netpollTook, callee: NetpollPdDropName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: netpollParked, value: netpollTook, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: NetpollDropMissed, whenTrue: netpollOwned, whenFalse: parkMissed, ids: ids)
	emitGtRenouncePark(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: netpollOwned, gt: gt, cont: queuedArm, label: b"netpoll")

	// ⚠ **A `sleep` AND AN I/O YIELD ARE ONE KIND HERE**, because `__gt_io_park` IS a timer park with a
	// deadline of `now` (`GtIoParkName`): both resume from `emitGtParkOnDeadline` and both simply carry on, so
	// the expiry is the store removal and nothing else — a sleep that ends early is an answer, not an error.
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: notNetpollPark, value: parkKind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.timer.rawValue, whenTrue: timerParked, whenFalse: notTimerPark, ids: ids)

	var timerExpired = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droppedtimerexpired", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: timerParked, ids: ids)
	emitGtTimerStoreRemove(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: timerParked, gt: gt, tag: emitConst(module, block: timerParked, value: TimerTagSleep, ids: ids), found: timerExpired, missing: parkMissed)
	emitGtRenouncePark(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: timerExpired, gt: gt, cont: queuedArm, label: b"timer")

	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: parkMissed, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(parkMissed, op: StdOp.branch(queuedArm.id))

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: notTimerPark, value: parkKind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.quiesce.rawValue, whenTrue: quiesceParked, whenFalse: unknownPark, ids: ids)
	emitRuntimeFatal(module, func: func, block: unknownPark, fatal: RuntimeFatal.promiseDroppedInAnUnknownPark, ids: ids)

	let quiesceClaim = emitCasGlobalWord(module, block: quiesceParked, label: GtQuiesceWaiterLabel, expected: gt, replacement: emitConst(module, block: quiesceParked, value: 0, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: quiesceParked, result: quiesceClaim, lost: queuedArm, won: quiesceOwned, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: quiesceOwned, ids: ids)
	emitGtRenouncePark(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: quiesceOwned, gt: gt, cont: queuedArm, label: b"quiesce")

	// queuedArm: the consumer's half, and the ONE exit of this function. The sweep after it reclaims
	// the tombstones sitting at the FRONT of the dropped coroutine's OWN owner's queue, which in the
	// common shape (spawn, drop, spawn, drop) is the one just renounced — see
	// `buildGtCoroSweepDropped` for why the front alone is the right bound. Every arm reaches it, a
	// renounced park included, where it finds nothing and costs two loads: a sweep guarded by which
	// arm arrived would be a second copy of the arm choice, in the one place a wrong copy is a
	// coroutine run after its promise was dropped.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE OWNER IS READ OFF THE DROPPED COROUTINE AND NOT FROM TLS**, so the sweep rests on no
	// claim about which green thread is dropping. Per-await linearity (E3100) already makes the dropper the
	// spawner, and therefore the two owners equal — but a sweep that read the CURRENT green thread's queue
	// would be resting on that theorem, where reading the field rests on nothing.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **WHICH SWEEP IS THE DROPPED THREAD'S OWN QUESTION**, read off `owner == gt`: a COROUTINE's (and a
	// reply cell's) tombstone sits in its owner's strand queue; a GREEN THREAD is named by no promise, and its
	// arm is the global queue's walk, so a drop cannot sweep the wrong tier by assuming what it renounced.
	//
	// ⚠ **BOTH READS HAPPEN BEFORE `__gt_owner_done`, WHICH MAY FREE THE STRUCT.** That call is the consumer's
	// arrival at the rendezvous, and if the runner was already there it reclaims — so `gt` is not readable
	// after it returns.
	let dropIsGreen = emitGtIsGreenThread(module, block: queuedArm, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	let dropOwner = emitLoadField(module, block: queuedArm, record: gt, offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(queuedArm, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtOwnerDoneName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	module.setTerminator(queuedArm, op: StdOp.condBranch(dropIsGreen, thenBlockId: sweepGreen.id, elseBlockId: sweepCoro.id))

	module.appendOp(sweepGreen, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedSweepDroppedName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(sweepGreen, op: StdOp.branch(dropDone.id))

	module.appendOp(sweepCoro, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtCoroSweepDroppedName, args: argsOf(dropOwner)))
	module.setTerminator(sweepCoro, op: StdOp.branch(dropDone.id))

	module.setTerminator(dropDone, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtPromiseDrop'

// ⭐⭐ **THE HALF EVERY EXPIRED PARK SHARES: READY THE THREAD AND PAY THE WAKE.** The caller holds
// `__sched_lock` and its own withdrawal found the registration, so no other party will ready this
// wait (a miss must not come here: `__gt_ready_locked` is fatal on a second ready). This readies
// the thread, releases, and pays a wake debt outside the lock (a wake inside a critical section has
// no StoreLoad fence).
function emitGtRenouncePark(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, gt ValueId, cont IrBlock, label ByteArray)
	var payWake = module.addBlock(func, label: bytesWithSuffix(b"droppedwake", suffix: label), heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let debt = emitGtReady(module, block: cur, gt: gt, gate: GtReadyGate.fromWaiting, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: cur, ids: ids)
	emitSchedWakeIfOwed(module, cur: cur, owed: debt, wake: payWake, cont: cont, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtRenouncePark'

// A block label with a per-arm suffix, so the renounce arms' blocks are distinguishable in a dump.
function bytesWithSuffix(base ByteArray, suffix ByteArray) returns ByteArray
	var out = base.clone()
	out.append(suffix)
	return out
end 'bytesWithSuffix'

// ⭐⭐ **`__gt_runner_done(gt, heldStrand)` — THE RUNNER's HALF OF THE TEARDOWN RENDEZVOUS: this party is
// finished with the thread's EXECUTION.** Ready whoever awaits it, free a stack that is not the seed's size,
// arrive at the word, and reclaim if the consumer was already there. Its callers are every way a GT's
// execution can end:
//
//   • the strand runner, when the context switch comes back from a `completed` thread — the common road.
//   • the tombstone reclaims: `__gt_strand_next`, refusing to hand back a renounced coroutine, and
//     `__gt_coro_sweep_dropped`, taking the ones off the front of an owner's queue — and their green-thread
//     counterparts in `SchedRuntime`. In all of them the thread never ran and never will.
//   • `__gt_cell_complete`, the replier's half for a reply cell that never runs.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE AWAITER IS TAKEN UNDER THIS GT's RECORD LOCK, THE LOCK ITS REGISTRATION HOLDS.** The
// completer published `completed` before this call and the registration re-reads the status under
// the lock before it stores itself (`__gt_register_park`'s await arm), so either the registration
// sees `completed` and resumes at once, or this read sees the registration and readies it — never
// neither. An awaitAny registration carries `AwaitAnyTag`, and `GtOffSelectDone` makes K completing
// slots ready it once (Go's `selectDone`).
//
// ⛔⛔ **EVERY READ OF THE STRUCT PRECEDES THE ARRIVAL.** The stack's length comes out of `gt.stackSize`, and
// the owner, the kind and the main mark are read up front, because the instant the runner ticket lands the
// consumer may already be putting the struct on a free list for the next spawn to zero and reuse.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A GREEN THREAD's RUNNER TICKET WAITS FOR ITS STRAND** (`GtOffStrandRefs`): its completion drops the
// reference for its own execution, and the arrival is made by whichever reference goes last. A coroutine's
// completion drops the reference it holds on its owner once its own arrival is made.
//
// ⚠ **IT DOES NOT TOUCH `MOffGtLive`.** That count is stepped once per PROMISE, by `__gt_owner_done`.
function buildGtRunnerDone(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtRunnerDoneName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var handAwaiter = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerawaiter", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var noAwaiter = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnernoawaiter", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveAwaiter = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerhaveawaiter", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var plainAwaiter = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerplainawaiter", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var readyHeld = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerreadyheld", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var readyElsewhere = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerreadyelsewhere", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var selectAwaiter = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerselectawaiter", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var claimSelect = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerclaimselect", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var payWake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerpaywake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerwake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var stackCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerstackcheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var releaseStack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerreleasestack", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sampleKind = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnersamplekind", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sampleDepth = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnersampledepth", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sampled = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnersampled", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnersampleoffset", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnersamplemachine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var countSample = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnercountsample", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var closeSeedWindow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerseedwindow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var keepStack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerkeepstack", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var afterStack = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnerafterstack", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var greenDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnergreendone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var mainDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnermaindone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var greenUnref = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnergreenunref", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var coroDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnercorodone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var coroUnref = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnercorounref", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let gt = 0
	let heldStrand = 1

	let stackBase = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackBase, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let noStack = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: entry, value: stackBase, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, ids: ids)
	let owner = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: gt, offset: GtOffOwner, ids: ids)
	let isGreen = emitGtIsGreenThread(module, block: entry, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	let isCell = emitGtIsReplyCell(module, block: entry, gt: gt, ids: ids)

	// Only a coroutine or a reply cell is named by a promise, so only they can have an awaiter.
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(isGreen, thenBlockId: stackCheck.id, elseBlockId: handAwaiter.id))

	// The wake debt reaches `payWake` as its argument from every road that settles the awaiter, and none owes
	// one but a ready.
	let debt = ids.mint()
	payWake.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(debt, argType: StdType.i64))

	emitGtRecordLockEnter(module, block: handAwaiter, record: gt, ids: ids)
	let awaiter = emitLoadField(module, block: handAwaiter, record: gt, offset: GtOffAwaiter, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: handAwaiter, a: awaiter, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: zero, whenTrue: noAwaiter, whenFalse: haveAwaiter, ids: ids)

	emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: noAwaiter, record: gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(noAwaiter, op: StdOp.branch(stackCheck.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: haveAwaiter, record: gt, offset: GtOffAwaiter, value: zero)
	let tag = emitConst(module, block: haveAwaiter, value: AwaitAnyTag, ids: ids)
	let tagBit = emitBin(module, block: haveAwaiter, a: awaiter, b: tag, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: haveAwaiter, a: tagBit, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, b: zero, whenTrue: selectAwaiter, whenFalse: plainAwaiter, ids: ids)

	emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: plainAwaiter, record: gt, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: plainAwaiter, a: awaiter, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: heldStrand, whenTrue: readyHeld, whenFalse: readyElsewhere, ids: ids)

	// An awaiter that is the strand the caller holds (`reapMember`) is readied with no lock, because the
	// holder may write `GtOffStrandResume`.
	var heldReady = emitGtCasWaitingToReady(module, func: func, cur: readyHeld, gt: awaiter, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: heldReady, record: awaiter, offset: GtOffStrandResume, value: awaiter)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: heldReady, to: payWake, value: emitConst(module, block: heldReady, value: WakeDebtNone, ids: ids))

	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: readyElsewhere, to: payWake, value: emitGtReady(module, block: readyElsewhere, gt: awaiter, gate: GtReadyGate.fromWaiting, ids: ids))

	let selecting = emitBin(module, block: selectAwaiter, a: awaiter, b: tag, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let selectClaim = emitCasField(module, block: selectAwaiter, record: selecting, offset: GtOffSelectDone as ByteOffset, expected: emitConst(module, block: selectAwaiter, value: SelectPending, ids: ids), replacement: emitConst(module, block: selectAwaiter, value: SelectReadied, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: selectAwaiter, record: gt, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: selectAwaiter, result: selectClaim, lost: stackCheck, won: claimSelect, ids: ids)

	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: claimSelect, to: payWake, value: emitGtReady(module, block: claimSelect, gt: selecting, gate: GtReadyGate.fromWaiting, ids: ids))

	emitSchedWakeIfOwed(module, cur: payWake, owed: debt, wake: wake, cont: stackCheck, ids: ids)

	// ⚠ The null guard is not defensive: a reply cell never had a stack and carries `stackBase == 0`.
	module.setTerminator(stackCheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(noStack, thenBlockId: afterStack.id, elseBlockId: releaseStack.id))

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ADAPTIVE SEED's SAMPLE IS THE STACK THIS THREAD NEEDED, NOT THE ONE IT WAS GIVEN** — Go averages the
	// stack a goroutine is USING. A thread that grew needed its final size. One that never grew needed AT LEAST its
	// deepest observed park plus the guard height (`GtOffStackHighWater`), and that least is its sample. Completion
	// observes nothing, its stack being empty by then. Sampling the seed it was handed would let one burst of deep
	// threads hold every later spawn at that seed, since a short-lived thread never shrinks. Adding the prologue's
	// margin, as the shrink rule does, would count the park's own frames twice, since they ride in that margin, and
	// on a lane whose floor leaves little above the guard it would lift every thread that parks off the floor.
	//
	// A thread that neither parked nor grew was seen at no depth, so it samples nothing and advances no window, and
	// a stretch of such threads leaves the seed where it is. Sampling it at the guard height pulls the seed to the
	// floor, where the next threads grow and push it back up, and each flip frees and reallocates the kept stacks
	// of recycled records. After a deep burst, short threads that never park leave the seed high.
	let stackMarks = emitLoadField(module, block: releaseStack, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackMarks, ids: ids)
	let observed = emitGtHasStackMark(module, block: releaseStack, marks: stackMarks, mark: GtStackMarksOfAnObservation, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(releaseStack, op: StdOp.condBranch(observed, thenBlockId: sampleKind.id, elseBlockId: keepStack.id))

	let stackSize = emitLoadField(module, block: sampleKind, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackSize, ids: ids)
	let grew = emitGtHasStackMark(module, block: sampleKind, marks: stackMarks, mark: GtStackMarkGrew, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(sampleKind, op: StdOp.condBranch(grew, thenBlockId: sampled.id, elseBlockId: sampleDepth.id))
	sampleKind.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(sampled.id, argIds: argsOf(stackSize)))

	let highWater = emitLoadField(module, block: sampleDepth, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackHighWater, ids: ids)
	let depthNeeded = emitBin(module, block: sampleDepth, a: highWater, b: emitConst(module, block: sampleDepth, value: gtStackGuardHeight(target), ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(sampleDepth, op: StdOp.branch(sampled.id))
	sampleDepth.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(sampled.id, argIds: argsOf(depthNeeded)))

	// The finish that makes its processor's count a multiple of the window closes one (`GtSeedWindowShift`).
	// A finisher holding no processor records no sample.
	let sample = ids.mint()
	sampled.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(sample, argType: StdType.i64))
	let p = emitLoadCurrentPOrBail(module, cur: sampled, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, haveProc: countSample, noProc: keepStack, ids: ids)

	let oneStack = emitConst(module, block: countSample, value: OneMore, ids: ids)
	_ = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: countSample, record: p, offset: POffStackSampleSum, delta: sample, multiM: false, ids: ids)
	let finishedBefore = emitFieldAccumulate(module, block: countSample, record: p, offset: POffStackSampleCount, delta: oneStack, multiM: false, ids: ids)
	let finished = emitBin(module, block: countSample, a: finishedBefore, b: oneStack, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let intoWindow = emitBin(module, block: countSample, a: finished, b: emitConst(module, block: countSample, value: GtSeedWindowMask, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: countSample, a: intoWindow, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, b: zero, whenTrue: keepStack, whenFalse: closeSeedWindow, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(closeSeedWindow, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtSeedAdaptName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	emitGtFreeStacks(module, func: func, cur: closeSeedWindow, cont: keepStack, ids: ids)

	let seed = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: keepStack, label: GtSeedBytesLabel, ids: ids)
	emitGtDropStackUnlessSized(module, func: func, cur: keepStack, record: gt, kept: seed, cont: afterStack, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(afterStack, op: StdOp.condBranch(isGreen, thenBlockId: greenDone.id, elseBlockId: coroDone.id))

	let isMain = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: greenDone, value: emitLoadField(module, block: greenDone, record: gt, offset: GtOffIsMainThread, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(greenDone, op: StdOp.condBranch(isMain, thenBlockId: mainDone.id, elseBlockId: greenUnref.id))

	// ⭐⭐ **`main` HAS COMPLETED: THE PROCESS IS NOW DRAINING.** The phase moves under the lock
	// `__sched_checkdead` and the exit drain read it under, and the main machine — which may be parked, or
	// running some other strand — is signalled so its loop sees it (`SchedRuntime.SchedPhase`).
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: mainDone, ids: ids)
	emitSchedEnterPhaseLocked(module, block: mainDone, phase: SchedPhase.draining, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: mainDone, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(mainDone, op: StdOp.branch(greenUnref.id))

	var greenUnreffed = emitGtStrandUnref(module, func: func, cur: greenUnref, owner: gt, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(greenUnreffed, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	var coroArrived = emitGtArriveAndReclaimIfSecond(module, func: func, cur: coroDone, gt: gt, ticket: GtTeardownRunnerTicket, otherTicket: GtTeardownConsumerTicket, ids: ids)
	var doneAfterCell = module.addBlock(func, label: b"runnercelldone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	module.setTerminator(coroArrived, op: StdOp.condBranch(isCell, thenBlockId: doneAfterCell.id, elseBlockId: coroUnref.id))
	module.setTerminator(doneAfterCell, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	var coroUnreffed = emitGtStrandUnref(module, func: func, cur: coroUnref, owner: owner, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(coroUnreffed, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtRunnerDone'

// Remove the `(gt, tag)` entry from the timer MIN-HEAP, then branch to `found`. Used by
// `__gt_promise_drop`'s waiting arm and by the poller's readiness path, whose readiness ends a wait
// the deadline no longer has to. Keyed on the PAIR, so FINDING the entry is a linear scan: the heap
// is ordered by `(deadline, seq)` and says nothing about which GT sits where. A thread holds at
// most one wait of each tag, so the first match removes and stops; a `(gt, tag)` NOT on the timer
// scans to the end and branches to `missing` untouched.
//
// ⚠ **THE TAG IS HALF THE KEY AND NOT A FILTER** — see `TimerTagSleep`: a thread holding both a `sleep` and a
// socket read deadline has two entries, and a gt-keyed scan would take whichever it met first.
//
// EXPORTED for `NetpollRuntime`, whose readiness path disarms the deadline of the wait it is ending.
//
// ⭐ **REMOVING FROM THE MIDDLE OF A HEAP NEEDS BOTH DIRECTIONS, AND THIS PICKS THE ONE THAT APPLIES.** The
// last live entry fills the hole, and from there it may be too EARLY for its new parent or too LATE for its
// new children — never both, because the rest of the array is still a heap. Comparing it against its parent
// answers which, so exactly one sift walk runs. Removing the last entry itself leaves a heap already.
export function emitGtTimerStoreRemove(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, startBlock IrBlock, gt ValueId, tag ValueId, found IrBlock, missing IrBlock)
	var scanHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tmscanhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var scanBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tmscanbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var advance = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tmadvance", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var removeAt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tmremoveat", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fillHole = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tmfillhole", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parentCmp = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tmparentcmp", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var siftUpStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tmsiftup", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var siftDownStart = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tmsiftdown", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var republish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tmrepublish", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let countAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(startBlock, op: StdOp.globalAddr(countAddr, label: GtTimerCountLabel))
	let count = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(startBlock, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(count, addrId: countAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let heapBase = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: startBlock, label: GtTimerHeapBaseLabel, ids: ids)
	let zeroConst = emitConst(module, block: startBlock, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(startBlock, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	startBlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zeroConst)))

	// scanHdr(i): if i >= count → missing; else inspect entry i.
	let i = ids.mint()
	scanHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: scanHdr, a: i, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: count, whenTrue: missing, whenFalse: scanBody, ids: ids)

	// scanBody: both halves of the key are compared, and the AND is branch-free for
	// `emitTimerEntryBefore`'s reason — a comparison's value lives in the 0/1 domain on every lane.
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **THE STEP IS COUNTED HERE, PER ENTRY INSPECTED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE VALUE OF THE READING** — a
	// count of CALLS would read the same whether this walked one entry or a thousand, which is exactly the
	// difference `SchedTimerScanStepsLabel` exists to make visible.
	emitSchedStepLockedCount(module, block: scanBody, label: SchedTimerScanStepsLabel, delta: OneMore, ids: ids)
	let entryAddr = emitTimerEntryAddr(module, block: scanBody, ids: ids, heapBase: heapBase, index: i)
	let entryGt = emitLoadField(module, block: scanBody, record: entryAddr, offset: TimerOffGt, ids: ids)
	let entryTag = emitLoadField(module, block: scanBody, record: entryAddr, offset: TimerOffTag, ids: ids)
	let sameGt = emitCmp(module, block: scanBody, a: entryGt, b: gt, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let sameTag = emitCmp(module, block: scanBody, a: entryTag, b: tag, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	let isMatch = emitBin(module, block: scanBody, a: sameGt, b: sameTag, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scanBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(isMatch, thenBlockId: removeAt.id, elseBlockId: advance.id))

	// advance: i + 1 → scanHdr.
	let oneAdv = emitConst(module, block: advance, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let iAdv = emitBin(module, block: advance, a: i, b: oneAdv, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(advance, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	advance.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(iAdv)))

	// removeAt: the live region shrinks by one. If `i` WAS the last live slot the array is a heap already.
	let oneRem = emitConst(module, block: removeAt, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let newCount = emitBin(module, block: removeAt, a: count, b: oneRem, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(removeAt, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(countAddr, offset: 0, valueId: newCount, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: removeAt, a: i, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: newCount, whenTrue: republish, whenFalse: fillHole, ids: ids)

	// fillHole: the last live entry fills slot i. At the ROOT there is no parent to compare against, so down
	// is the only direction it can move.
	let lastAddr = emitTimerEntryAddr(module, block: fillHole, ids: ids, heapBase: heapBase, index: newCount)
	let moved = emitTimerEntryLoad(module, block: fillHole, ids: ids, addr: lastAddr)
	let holeAddr = emitTimerEntryAddr(module, block: fillHole, ids: ids, heapBase: heapBase, index: i)
	emitTimerEntryStore(module, block: fillHole, addr: holeAddr, entry: moved)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: fillHole, a: i, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: zeroConst, whenTrue: siftDownStart, whenFalse: parentCmp, ids: ids)

	// parentCmp: parent = (i - 1) >> 1 — `i >= 1` on this edge, so the numerator is non-negative and the
	// logical shift is the halving. The moved entry goes UP iff it beats that parent; otherwise its ancestors
	// are already in order and only its descendants can be wrong.
	let parentOne = emitConst(module, block: parentCmp, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let iMinusOne = emitBin(module, block: parentCmp, a: i, b: parentOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let parent = emitBin(module, block: parentCmp, a: iMinusOne, b: parentOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let parentAddr = emitTimerEntryAddr(module, block: parentCmp, ids: ids, heapBase: heapBase, index: parent)
	let parentEntry = emitTimerEntryLoad(module, block: parentCmp, ids: ids, addr: parentAddr)
	let goesUp = emitTimerEntryBefore(module, block: parentCmp, ids: ids, a: moved, b: parentEntry)
	module.setTerminator(parentCmp, op: StdOp.condBranch(goesUp, thenBlockId: siftUpStart.id, elseBlockId: siftDownStart.id))

	emitTimerSiftUp(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: siftUpStart, heapBase: heapBase, startIndex: i, cont: republish)
	emitHeapSiftDown(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: siftDownStart, heap: SiftHeap.timerEntries(heapBase), count: newCount, startIndex: i, cont: republish)

	// Every road that removed an entry may have moved the root.
	var published = emitGtPublishTimerWhen(module, func: func, cur: republish, heapBase: heapBase, count: newCount, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(published, op: StdOp.branch(found.id))
end 'emitGtTimerStoreRemove'

// The absolute deadline `ms` from now, saturating at `GtDeadlineMaxNs`. A `Milliseconds` above
// `i64.max` reads negative here, and a product past the headroom wraps, and either would be a deadline
// already past: the wait meant to be longest would end at once. It ends `cur` and answers
// `joined`'s block argument.
//
// A timeout above `GtDeadlineMaxMs` saturates at any clock reading, because the clock is never
// negative, so it is decided before the clock is read: a waiting send computes this inside its
// mailbox lock, and where reading the clock is a system call every `ForeverMs` send would hold
// the lock across one, serialising the mailbox's producers.
export function emitGtDeadlineAfterMs(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, ms ValueId, joined IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	var saturated = module.addBlock(func, label: b"deadlinesaturated", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var finite = module.addBlock(func, label: b"deadlinefinite", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let aboveSignedRange = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: cur, value: ms, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: 0, ids: ids)
	let aboveCeiling = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: cur, value: ms, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: GtDeadlineMaxMs, ids: ids)
	let saturatesAtAnyClock = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: aboveSignedRange, b: aboveCeiling, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(saturatesAtAnyClock, thenBlockId: saturated.id, elseBlockId: finite.id))

	let latestWhenSaturated = emitConst(module, block: saturated, value: GtDeadlineMaxNs, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(saturated, op: StdOp.branch(joined.id))
	saturated.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(joined.id, argIds: argsOf(latestWhenSaturated)))

	let now = emitGtNowNs(module, block: finite, ids: ids)
	let milliConst = emitConst(module, block: finite, value: NanosPerMilli, ids: ids)
	let latest = emitConst(module, block: finite, value: GtDeadlineMaxNs, ids: ids)
	let headroomNs = emitBin(module, block: finite, a: latest, b: now, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let headroomMs = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(finite, op: StdOp.div(headroomMs, lhs: headroomNs, rhs: milliConst, signedness: IntegerSignedness.signed))
	let aboveHeadroom = emitCmp(module, block: finite, a: ms, b: headroomMs, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, ids: ids)
	let msNs = emitBin(module, block: finite, a: ms, b: milliConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let sum = emitBin(module, block: finite, a: now, b: msNs, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let deadline = emitSelectWord(module, block: finite, cond: aboveHeadroom, whenSet: latest, whenClear: sum, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(finite, op: StdOp.branch(joined.id))
	finite.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(joined.id, argIds: argsOf(deadline)))

	let value = ids.mint()
	joined.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(value, argType: StdType.i64))

	return value
end 'emitGtDeadlineAfterMs'

// `__gt_sleep(ms)` — suspend the current GT for `ms` milliseconds: compute the absolute deadline and park on
// the timer store (`__gt_park`).
function buildGtSleep(module StdModule, tracing GtTracing)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtSleepName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var timed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sleeptimed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let ms = 0

	let deadline = emitGtDeadlineAfterMs(module, func: func, cur: entry, ms: ms, joined: timed, ids: ids)
	entry = timed

	// The trace pair BRACKETS the park, exactly as `__gt_io_park`'s does — same exit, different tag, and the
	// tag is the only thing that tells a reader which kind of wait this green thread is in.
	if let tags = try tracing.tags() 'traceTheYield'
		emitCurrentGtTraceLine(module, block: entry, tag: tags.sleepYield, ids: ids)
	end 'traceTheYield'

	entry = emitCurrentGtSchedEvent(module, func: func, block: entry, eventType: DsEvSchedYield, ids: ids)
	var resumed = emitGtParkOnDeadline(module, func: func, cur: entry, deadline: deadline, ids: ids)

	if let tags = try tracing.tags() 'traceTheResume'
		emitCurrentGtTraceLine(module, block: resumed, tag: tags.sleepResume, ids: ids)
	end 'traceTheResume'

	resumed = emitCurrentGtSchedEvent(module, func: func, block: resumed, eventType: DsEvSchedResume, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(resumed, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtSleep'

// The PARK-ON-DEADLINE core, shared by `__gt_sleep` and by `__gt_io_park` (the filesystem yield point). Its
// two callers differ only in the deadline they compute; the suspension itself is `emitGtPark`'s, which the
// process park and the yielding read take too.
//
// ⚠ **CAPACITY: `__gt_timer_add`'s grow arm assumes a SEEDED store, and nothing on this path seeds one.**
// Both callers (`__gt_sleep`, `__gt_io_park`) are entered only after `__gt_init` has run — `__gt_init`
// commits the seed capacity — so the grow arm always doubles a non-zero capacity. Reached from anywhere that
// has not run `__gt_init`, capacity 0 would land on `__slab_alloc(0)`.
//
// Returns the block control reaches once the GT is running again. The CALLER terminates that block.
function emitGtParkOnDeadline(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, deadline ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var resumedBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"resumed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitGtPark(module, ids: ids, cur: cur, kind: GtParkKind.timer.rawValue, arg: deadline, cont: resumedBlock)
	return resumedBlock
end 'emitGtParkOnDeadline'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **PARK THE RUNNING GT: `__gt_park(kind, arg)`, then continue at `cont` once it runs again.** It
// terminates `cur`; the CALLER terminates `cont`. EXPORTED because `MailboxRuntime.buildMboxRecv` parks too, and
// must come through this door rather than write its own — a second spelling would skip the note
// `__gt_run_strand` reads to commit the park after the switch.
export function emitGtPark(module StdModule, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, kind FullRangeInt, arg ValueId, cont IrBlock)
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtParkName, args: twoArgs(emitConst(module, block: cur, value: kind, ids: ids), second: arg)))
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitGtPark'

// `__gt_park(kind, arg)` — see `GtParkName`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A PARKING THREAD MAY NOT BE DISCOVERABLE WHILE IT IS STILL EXECUTING ON ITS OWN STACK.** Registered on
// the store that will wake it before `__gt_context_switch` has saved its registers, another M could fire its
// deadline and resume it onto the SP saved at its PREVIOUS suspension: two Ms on one stack. So this writes
// only the NOTE and switches; the strand runner registers the park once the switch has come back. A mailbox
// receiver is published earlier, behind a lock it holds across the switch (`GtParkKind.mailbox`).
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE STATUS STAYS `ready` ACROSS THE SWITCH, AND THAT IS WHAT MAKES THE WINDOW SAFE.** A `ready` GT on
// no queue and in no store can be moved by nobody but the machine that switched it in, whose strand runner is
// the next thing to run; the runner's completion gate gives up only a `completed` GT, and a
// `__gt_promise_drop` that lands in the window reads `ready` and takes its queued arm.
function buildGtPark(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtParkName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let kind = 0
	let arg = 1

	let selfGt = emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: selfGt, offset: GtOffParkKind, value: kind)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: selfGt, offset: GtOffParkArg, value: arg)
	let waiter = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: selfGt, offset: GtOffWaiter, ids: ids)
	emitGtSwitchAway(module, func: func, cur: entry, selfGt: selfGt, to: waiter, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtPark'

// Switch from the running green thread `selfGt` to the scheduler context `to`, from a park it will resume from
// — on whichever machine runs it next — and return. Where the program reads its thread CPU clock
// (`GtKeepsCpuClockLabel`) the switch keeps that clock continuous across the gap (`GtOffCpuOffset`), with the
// two reads bracketing it as tightly as they can; elsewhere the switch is all there is.
function emitGtSwitchAway(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, selfGt ValueId, to ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	var clockKept = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkclock", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var switchOnly = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parknoclock", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: cur, label: GtKeepsCpuClockLabel, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: GtCpuClockUnkept, whenTrue: clockKept, whenFalse: switchOnly, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(switchOnly, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtContextSwitchName, args: twoArgs(selfGt, second: to)))
	module.setTerminator(switchOnly, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	let before = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(clockKept, op: StdOp.call(before, callee: ThreadCpuTicksName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.appendOp(clockKept, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtContextSwitchName, args: twoArgs(selfGt, second: to)))
	let after = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(clockKept, op: StdOp.call(after, callee: ThreadCpuTicksName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	let gap = emitBin(module, block: clockKept, a: before, b: after, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let offset = emitLoadField(module, block: clockKept, record: selfGt, offset: GtOffCpuOffset, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: clockKept, record: selfGt, offset: GtOffCpuOffset, value: emitBin(module, block: clockKept, a: offset, b: gap, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(clockKept, op: StdOp.retVoid)
end 'emitGtSwitchAway'

// The running green thread's CPU-clock offset (`GtOffCpuOffset`). EXPORTED for its one reader,
// `ClockRuntime.__thread_cpu_ticks`, which adds it to the OS thread's counter in a green-thread program.
export function emitLoadCurrentGtCpuOffset(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: block, ids: ids), offset: GtOffCpuOffset, ids: ids)
end 'emitLoadCurrentGtCpuOffset'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_register_park(gt) -> wake debt` — PUT A PARKED THREAD ON THE STORE THAT WILL WAKE
// IT.** The note `__gt_park` left says which store and with what; this dispatches on it. See
// `GtRegisterParkName` for its one caller and when it runs.
//
// ⛔⛔ **EACH ARM'S LOCK DISCIPLINE IS ITS OWN AND THEY ARE NOT INTERCHANGEABLE.**
//
//   • **timer** — the `waiting` store and the `__gt_timer_add` MUST be inside ONE `__sched_lock`
//     hold. Split, another M's `__gt_timer_check` can fire the entry BETWEEN them, and its ready
//     finds a thread not yet `waiting`: fatal (`RuntimeFatal.badGreenThreadStatusInReady`).
//     `__gt_timer_add` takes the lock itself, recursively. A deadline earlier than every other now
//     pending owes the poller a look (`SchedRuntime.__sched_wake_netpoller`), inside the hold.
//   • **netpoll** — `NetpollRuntime.__np_pd_commit` under `__sched_lock`, which may UNDO the park:
//     readiness that landed between the mark and the commit was recorded by a party that found
//     nobody published, so the thread is readied rather than left waiting for a wake already
//     delivered. A socket deadline armed here owes the poller a look, exactly as the timer arm's
//     does.
//   • **await** — under the awaited GT's record lock, re-read its status: `completed` means it
//     finished after the await's own read, so the thread is readied at once (Go's `unlockf`
//     answering false); otherwise the thread becomes that GT's awaiter and `waiting`.
//     `__gt_runner_done` takes the awaiter under the same lock.
//   • **awaitAny** — the same over every slot: re-scan under every slot's record lock
//     (`__gt_any_lock`) and ready at once if any slot has completed, else register on every slot
//     with `GtOffSelectDone` cleared.
//   • **quiesce** — no lock: `__gt_quiesce_waiter` is claimed by CAS, here and by
//     `__gt_owner_done`, `__sched_checkdead` and `__gt_promise_drop`, so exactly one of them
//     readies the thread. `waiting` is stored before the registration, because a claimer may ready
//     the thread the moment it is registered.
//
// Every arm releases its lock before it answers the wake debt its ready owes, or none, and the
// caller pays it.
function buildGtRegisterPark(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtRegisterParkName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var timerArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parktimer", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notTimer = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notparktimer", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var netpollArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parknetpoll", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var netpollWake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parknetpollwake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var netpollPaid = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parknetpollpaid", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notNetpoll = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notparknetpoll", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var awaitArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkawait", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var awaitRegister = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkawaitregister", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var awaitTake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkawaittake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var awaitCompleted = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkawaitcompleted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var awaitAnyCompleted = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkawaitanycompleted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notAwait = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notparkawait", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var awaitAnyArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkawaitany", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var awaitAnyRegister = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkawaitanyregister", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var awaitAnyRegistered = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkawaitanyregistered", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notAwaitAny = module.addBlock(func, label: b"notparkawaitany", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var quiesceArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkquiesce", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var quiesceTaken = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkquiescetaken", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var quiesceRegister = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkquiesceregister", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var resumeRunnable = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkresumerunnable", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unknownArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkunknown", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parkdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0

	let debt = ids.mint()
	doneBlock.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(debt, argType: StdType.i64))

	let kind = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: gt, offset: GtOffParkKind, ids: ids)
	let arg = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: gt, offset: GtOffParkArg, ids: ids)
	let noDebt = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: WakeDebtNone, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.timer.rawValue, whenTrue: timerArm, whenFalse: notTimer, ids: ids)

	var timerPaid = emitGtArmTimerDeadline(module, func: func, cur: timerArm, gt: gt, deadline: arg, tag: TimerTagSleep, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: timerPaid, to: doneBlock, value: noDebt)

	emitGtBranchOnPollerPark(module, block: notTimer, kind: kind, onPoller: netpollArm, elsewhere: notNetpoll, ids: ids)

	// ⭐⭐ **THE NETPOLL ARM OWES TWO WAKES AND THEY ARE NOT THE SAME WAKE.** `__np_pd_commit` may READY the
	// thread instead of parking it — readiness that landed between the mark and this commit — which is an
	// ordinary wake debt; and a socket DEADLINE it arms may be earlier than every other now pending, which owes
	// the poller a look exactly as the timer arm's does. The root is read on both sides of the commit rather
	// than compared against the arm's own deadline, because the commit arms nothing when no deadline is set.
	//
	// ⚠ **ONE SOURCE AND SEVERAL TAKE THE SAME ARM**, because the difference is entirely in how the park note's
	// argument is read — which is `__np_pd_commit`'s own question, asked once where the note is decoded rather
	// than twice here and there.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: netpollArm, ids: ids)
	let rootBeforeCommit = emitGtLoadTimerWhen(module, block: netpollArm, ids: ids)
	let commitDebt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(netpollArm, op: StdOp.call(commitDebt, callee: NetpollPdCommitName, args: argsOf(gt)))
	let rootAfterCommit = emitGtLoadTimerWhen(module, block: netpollArm, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: netpollArm, a: rootAfterCommit, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, b: rootBeforeCommit, whenTrue: netpollWake, whenFalse: netpollPaid, ids: ids)
	emitGtWakeNetpoller(module, block: netpollWake, when: rootAfterCommit, cont: netpollPaid, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: netpollPaid, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: netpollPaid, to: doneBlock, value: commitDebt)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: notNetpoll, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.awaitPromise.rawValue, whenTrue: awaitArm, whenFalse: notAwait, ids: ids)

	// await: `arg` is the awaited GT. ACQUIRE — it may have completed on another M.
	emitGtRecordLockEnter(module, block: awaitArm, record: arg, ids: ids)
	let awaitedStatus = emitGtLoadPublishedStatus(module, block: awaitArm, gt: arg, ids: ids)
	let awaitedDone = emitGtStatusEquals(module, block: awaitArm, status: awaitedStatus, want: GtStatus.completed, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(awaitArm, op: StdOp.condBranch(awaitedDone, thenBlockId: awaitCompleted.id, elseBlockId: awaitRegister.id))

	emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: awaitCompleted, record: arg, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(awaitCompleted, op: StdOp.branch(resumeRunnable.id))

	emitGtClaimAwaiter(module, func: func, cur: awaitRegister, promise: arg, awaiter: gt, cont: awaitTake, ids: ids)
	emitGtMarkWaiting(module, block: awaitTake, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: awaitTake, record: arg, ids: ids)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: awaitTake, to: doneBlock, value: noDebt)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: notAwait, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.awaitAny.rawValue, whenTrue: awaitAnyArm, whenFalse: notAwaitAny, ids: ids)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: notAwaitAny, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.quiesce.rawValue, whenTrue: quiesceArm, whenFalse: unknownArm, ids: ids)

	emitGtMarkWaiting(module, block: quiesceArm, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	let registered = emitCasGlobalWord(module, block: quiesceArm, label: GtQuiesceWaiterLabel, expected: emitConst(module, block: quiesceArm, value: 0, ids: ids), replacement: gt, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: quiesceArm, result: registered, lost: quiesceTaken, won: quiesceRegister, ids: ids)
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: quiesceTaken, abort: RuntimeAbort.schedulerQuiesceWaiterTaken, ids: ids)

	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: quiesceRegister, to: doneBlock, value: noDebt)

	// awaitAny: `arg` is the promise array's record.
	let lockedSlots = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(awaitAnyArm, op: StdOp.call(lockedSlots, callee: GtAnyLockName, args: argsOf(arg)))
	let completedSlot = emitAnyCompletedIndex(module, block: awaitAnyArm, record: arg, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: awaitAnyArm, value: completedSlot, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: NoCompletedPromise, whenTrue: awaitAnyCompleted, whenFalse: awaitAnyRegister, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(awaitAnyCompleted, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtAnyUnlockName, args: argsOf(lockedSlots)))
	module.setTerminator(awaitAnyCompleted, op: StdOp.branch(resumeRunnable.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: awaitAnyRegister, record: gt, offset: GtOffSelectDone, value: emitConst(module, block: awaitAnyRegister, value: SelectPending, ids: ids))
	emitGtAwaitAnySlotWalk(module, func: func, cur: awaitAnyRegister, record: arg, g: gt, act: AwaitAnySlotAct.register, cont: awaitAnyRegistered, ids: ids)
	emitGtMarkWaiting(module, block: awaitAnyRegistered, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(awaitAnyRegistered, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtAnyUnlockName, args: argsOf(lockedSlots)))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: awaitAnyRegistered, to: doneBlock, value: noDebt)

	// resumeRunnable: what the thread waits for has already happened, so it is readied at once, as still
	// `ready`.
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: resumeRunnable, to: doneBlock, value: emitGtReady(module, block: resumeRunnable, gt: gt, gate: GtReadyGate.alreadyRunnable, ids: ids))

	// ⛔ **A KIND NOTHING WRITES — a compiler bug.** A yield never reaches here (the strand runner places it),
	// and falling through silently would leave a thread suspended on no store at all.
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: unknownArm, abort: RuntimeAbort.schedulerUnknownParkKind, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.ret(debt))
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtRegisterPark'

// The timer arm's discipline (`buildGtRegisterPark`), for any tag: `waiting` and the add in one
// `__sched_lock` hold.
//
// Go's `maybeAdd`: `when < wakeTime`, read against the root before the add, is what makes this the deadline
// the scheduler waits for next. An empty store publishes `TimerWhenEmpty`, which every deadline precedes.
export function emitGtArmTimerDeadline(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, gt ValueId, deadline ValueId, tag FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var wake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parktimerwake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var paid = module.addBlock(func, label: b"parktimerpaid", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: cur, ids: ids)
	emitGtMarkWaiting(module, block: cur, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	let rootBefore = emitGtLoadTimerWhen(module, block: cur, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtTimerAddName, args: threeArgs(gt, second: deadline, third: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: tag, ids: ids))))
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: cur, a: deadline, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, b: rootBefore, whenTrue: wake, whenFalse: paid, ids: ids)
	emitGtWakeNetpoller(module, block: wake, when: deadline, cont: paid, ids: ids)
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: paid, ids: ids)

	return paid
end 'emitGtArmTimerDeadline'

// Call `__sched_wake_netpoller(when)` in `block`, inside a park's `__sched_lock` hold, and continue
// at `cont`.
function emitGtWakeNetpoller(module StdModule, block IrBlock, when ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: SchedWakeNetpollerName, args: argsOf(when)))
	module.setTerminator(block, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitGtWakeNetpoller'

export function emitBranchCarrying(module StdModule, from IrBlock, to IrBlock, value ValueId)
	module.setTerminator(from, op: StdOp.branch(to.id))
	from.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(to.id, argIds: argsOf(value)))
end 'emitBranchCarrying'

// ⭐⭐ **SWITCH INTO `g` FROM THIS MACHINE'S SCHEDULER CONTEXT — Go's `execute`.** Name the context `g` switches
// back to, make `g` the machine's running GT, switch, and restore the context on the way back. ⚠ It takes the
// M and not the P: *"which green thread is running"* is a fact about the OS thread executing it
// (`SchedRuntime.MOffCurrentGt`), and the processor may change hands while `g` is in a kernel call.
//
// The guard word is restored first, as Go's `execute` does, so a preemption request that reached `g` after it
// had already stopped for another reason cannot make it yield as soon as it resumes.
function emitGtExecute(module StdModule, block IrBlock, g ValueId, schedulerContext ValueId, m ValueId, target Target, ids ValueMinter)
	emitGtRestoreStackGuard(module, block: block, gt: g, target: target, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: g, offset: GtOffWaiter, value: schedulerContext)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentGt, value: g)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtContextSwitchName, args: twoArgs(schedulerContext, second: g)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffCurrentGt, value: schedulerContext)
end 'emitGtExecute'

// 1 when the timer store holds anything or any thread is published on a poll descriptor, else 0.
// `__sched_lock` held. EXPORTED for `SchedRuntime`: the idle tail claims the poller on it, and
// `__sched_checkdead` counts a pending entry as a wakeup source.
//
// ⚠ The two words are read differently on purpose: `__np_waiters` is `MultiMSharing.publishedFromTheLock`
// and owes an acquire at every site, while every reader of `__gt_timer_count` holds the lock.
//
// ⭐ **A SOURCE WAIT IS PENDING WORK AND IS THE ONLY KIND WITH NO DEADLINE OF ITS OWN.** A process whose sole
// outstanding wait is a read — or a child — has no timer at all, so without that count the idle tail would
// never enter the poller and `__sched_checkdead` would declare a program that is merely waiting to be
// deadlocked.
export function emitGtStoresPending(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let anyStore = emitBin(module, block: block, a: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: GtTimerCountLabel, ids: ids), b: emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: NetpollWaitersLabel, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	return emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: anyStore, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtStoresPending'

// ⭐⭐ **FIRE THE TIMERS THAT ARE DUE, ONLY IF AN UNLOCKED PEEK SAYS THERE IS ANYTHING TO TAKE** — the due
// test (`emitGtBranchOnTimerDue`) — so a poll with a far deadline pending takes no lock. It terminates `cur`
// and continues at `cont`, whose one block argument receives the wake debt the walk owes. EXPORTED for the
// scheduler loop's idle poll and its P-less poll (`SchedRuntime.buildSchedLoop`); the strand runner's yield is
// the third caller.
//
// ⚠ **A PARKED CHILD IS NOT SWEPT HERE, BECAUSE NOTHING SWEEPS IT.** It is a source on the poller like a
// socket, so its exit arrives as an entry the poller's own walk turns into a ready.
export function emitGtPollStores(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var fire = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pollfire", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let noDebt = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: WakeDebtNone, ids: ids)
	let now = emitGtBranchOnTimerDue(module, func: func, cur: cur, due: fire, notDue: cont, notDueArgs: argsOf(noDebt), ids: ids)

	let timerDebt = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(fire, op: StdOp.call(timerDebt, callee: GtTimerCheckName, args: argsOf(now)))
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: fire, to: cont, value: timerDebt)
end 'emitGtPollStores'

// `__gt_run_strand(m, owner)` — see `GtRunStrandName`.
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **WHAT A SWITCH BACK MEANS IS READ OFF THE GT THAT CAME BACK**: `completed` is the trampoline's, and
// anything else left a park note (`__gt_park`) — a yield among them. The completion gate is the half that must
// not be wrong: only a `completed` GT may be given up, because a parked one will resume on its stack.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A YIELDER GOES BEHIND ITS STRAND, OR THE STRAND GOES BEHIND EVERYONE.** With other members runnable
// it joins the back of the strand queue, so a sibling that never ran gets its turn
// (`sched-runqueue.a-yield-hands-the-processor-to-a-never-run-sibling`); with none, the whole strand goes to
// the GLOBAL queue's tail — Go's `Gosched` — still held, so the machine that pops the token picks it up
// (`sched-runqueue.a-yield-goes-behind-the-global-queue`). Due timers and exited children are fired first
// whenever either store holds anything, so a spin-wait on either makes progress.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A PREEMPTION TAKES THE PROCESSOR, NEVER THE TURN.** The preempted member becomes the next member its
// strand runs, ahead of the owner and every sibling (`GtOffStrandResume`), and the strand goes behind everyone
// on the global queue, always — Go's `gopreempt_m`. Placed as a yield is, a strand with a runnable sibling would
// keep this processor, so one service's coroutines could hold it for ever. The machine returns to its scheduler
// loop, which fires what is due before it searches.
function buildGtRunStrand(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtRunStrandName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var next = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandnext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var run = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandrun", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var finished = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandcompleted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var dropPin = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stranddroppin", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var reapMember = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandreap", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var parked = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandparked", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var judged = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandjudged", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var shrink = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandshrink", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var dispatch = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stranddispatch", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var register = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandregister", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notYield = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandnotyield", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notPreempt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandnotpreempt", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var mailboxCommit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandmailboxcommit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var notMailbox = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandnotmailbox", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var mailboxSendCommit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandmailboxsendcommit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var yielded = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandyield", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var preempted = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandpreempted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pollStores = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandpoll", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pollWake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandpollwake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var place = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandplace", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var behindSiblings = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandbehindsiblings", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var behindEveryone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandbehindeveryone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var released = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandreleased", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sortMember = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandsort", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var handedOffProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"strandhandedoff", heat: BlockHeat.cold)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let m = 0
	let owner = 1 as ValueId
	let schedulerContext = emitFieldAddr(module, block: entry, record: m, offset: MOffSchedulerContext, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: entry, record: m, offset: MOffHeldStrand, value: owner)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(next.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(next.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))
	let nothingParked = ids.mint()
	next.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(nothingParked, argType: StdType.i64))

	// `owner` is not touched again once this answers 0 — the release may have reclaimed it.
	let member = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(next, op: StdOp.call(member, callee: GtStrandNextName, args: twoArgs(owner, second: nothingParked)))
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: next, a: member, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: zero, whenTrue: released, whenFalse: sortMember, ids: ids)
	emitStrandRunReturn(module, block: released, m: m, value: 0, ids: ids)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: sortMember, value: member, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtProcessorHandedOff, whenTrue: handedOffProc, whenFalse: run, ids: ids)
	emitStrandRunReturn(module, block: handedOffProc, m: m, value: GtProcessorHandedOff, ids: ids)

	emitGtExecute(module, block: run, g: member, schedulerContext: schedulerContext, m: m, target: target, ids: ids)

	// ⚠ ACQUIRE, THOUGH THIS READ IS SAME-THREAD: the trampoline wrote `completed` on THIS M just before the
	// switch that landed here. It comes through the ordered door anyway rather than be the carve-out a later
	// reader copies.
	let status = emitGtLoadPublishedStatus(module, block: run, gt: member, ids: ids)
	let completed = emitGtStatusEquals(module, block: run, status: status, want: GtStatus.completed, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(run, op: StdOp.condBranch(completed, thenBlockId: finished.id, elseBlockId: parked.id))

	let lockedHere = emitLoadField(module, block: finished, record: m, offset: MOffLockedGt, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: finished, a: lockedHere, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, b: member, whenTrue: dropPin, whenFalse: reapMember, ids: ids)

	emitGtReleasePin(module, block: dropPin, gt: member, m: m, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dropPin, op: StdOp.branch(reapMember.id))

	module.appendOp(reapMember, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtRunnerDoneName, args: twoArgs(member, second: owner)))
	module.setTerminator(reapMember, op: StdOp.branch(next.id))
	reapMember.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(next.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	// ⭐⭐ **THE ONE DOOR EVERY PARK PASSES WHILE THE THREAD IS STOPPED AND REACHABLE BY NOBODY ELSE** — its
	// registers are saved and it is on no store and no queue yet, or, as a mailbox receiver, on one no waker
	// can reach until the commit below leaves the mailbox lock — so it is where a stack's depth is noted
	// (`emitGtNoteStackDepth`) and where a stack may move (`__gt_stack_shrink`). A stack at the floor has nothing
	// to give back, and the depth note, a load and a compare is all a park of one pays.
	// ⛔⛔ **A THREAD THAT DID NOT ASK TO STOP KEEPS ITS STACK WHERE IT IS — Go's rule, and the reason is what
	// a MOVE can and cannot rebase.** `__gt_stack_relocate` rebases `gt.sp`, `gt.fp` and the saved
	// frame-pointer chain; a REGISTER holding an address inside the stack is rebased by nothing. At a
	// voluntary park the thread stopped at a call boundary it wrote itself; at a preemption it was stopped
	// at a prologue it had not reached, or — asynchronously — at an arbitrary instruction with its whole
	// register file spilled verbatim, and a promoted stack record's address in any of those registers would
	// come back naming freed pages. Go never shrinks at a preemption either (`gopreempt_m` goes through
	// `goschedImpl`, which does not call `shrinkstack`) and refuses outright at an async safe point.
	emitGtNoteStackDepth(module, func: func, cur: parked, gt: member, cont: judged, ids: ids)

	let size = emitLoadField(module, block: judged, record: member, offset: GtOffStackSize, ids: ids)
	let aboveFloor = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: judged, value: size, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: gtSeedStackBytes(target), ids: ids)
	let stopped = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: judged, value: emitLoadField(module, block: judged, record: member, offset: GtOffParkKind, ids: ids), pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: GtParkKind.preempt.rawValue, ids: ids)
	let mayShrink = emitBin(module, block: judged, a: aboveFloor, b: stopped, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(judged, op: StdOp.condBranch(mayShrink, thenBlockId: shrink.id, elseBlockId: dispatch.id))

	module.appendOp(shrink, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtStackShrinkName, args: argsOf(member)))
	module.setTerminator(shrink, op: StdOp.branch(dispatch.id))

	let kind = emitLoadField(module, block: dispatch, record: member, offset: GtOffParkKind, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: dispatch, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.yielded.rawValue, whenTrue: yielded, whenFalse: notYield, ids: ids)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: notYield, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.preempt.rawValue, whenTrue: preempted, whenFalse: notPreempt, ids: ids)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: notPreempt, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.mailbox.rawValue, whenTrue: mailboxCommit, whenFalse: notMailbox, ids: ids)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: notMailbox, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: GtParkKind.mailboxSend.rawValue, whenTrue: mailboxSendCommit, whenFalse: register, ids: ids)

	// After the shrink, never before: once either mailbox commit leaves the mailbox lock a waker can ready the
	// member on another M, and a shrink then would relocate a stack that is running. Each commit also runs
	// before `__gt_strand_next`, so this OS thread runs no other green thread while the park's hold is open.
	emitMboxParkCommit(module, block: mailboxCommit, gt: member, mbox: emitLoadField(module, block: mailboxCommit, record: member, offset: GtOffParkArg, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(mailboxCommit, op: StdOp.branch(next.id))
	mailboxCommit.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(next.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	var sendCommitted = emitMboxSendParkCommit(module, func: func, block: mailboxSendCommit, gt: member, mbox: emitLoadField(module, block: mailboxSendCommit, record: member, offset: GtOffParkArg, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(sendCommitted, op: StdOp.branch(next.id))
	sendCommitted.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(next.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	module.setTerminator(register, op: StdOp.branch(next.id))
	register.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(next.id, argIds: argsOf(member)))

	emitGtPollStores(module, func: func, cur: yielded, cont: pollStores, ids: ids)
	let pollDebt = ids.mint()
	pollStores.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(pollDebt, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitSchedWakeIfOwed(module, cur: pollStores, owed: pollDebt, wake: pollWake, cont: place, ids: ids)

	// The yielder stays `ready`: it is runnable, merely not running.
	emitGtRecordLockEnter(module, block: place, record: owner, ids: ids)
	let head = emitQueueLoadHead(module, block: place, ends: GtQueueEnds.ownerFields(owner), ids: ids)
	let nothingElse = emitCmp(module, block: place, a: head, b: zero, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(place, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtCoroEnqueueName, args: argsOf(member)))
	emitGtRecordLockLeave(module, block: place, record: owner, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(place, op: StdOp.condBranch(nothingElse, thenBlockId: behindEveryone.id, elseBlockId: behindSiblings.id))

	module.setTerminator(behindSiblings, op: StdOp.branch(next.id))
	behindSiblings.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(next.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	emitStoreField(module, block: preempted, record: owner, offset: GtOffStrandResume, value: member)
	module.setTerminator(preempted, op: StdOp.branch(behindEveryone.id))

	// A token put on the global queue owes a wake once the lock is released, as Go's `goschedImpl` wakes a machine
	// after its global put: this machine searches next, but another may be idle with nobody to tell it.
	emitSchedLockEnter(module, block: behindEveryone, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(behindEveryone, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtEnqueueName, args: argsOf(owner)))
	emitSchedLockLeave(module, block: behindEveryone, ids: ids)
	emitSchedWakeAnIdleM(module, block: behindEveryone, ids: ids)
	emitStrandRunReturn(module, block: behindEveryone, m: m, value: 0, ids: ids)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtRunStrand'

function emitStrandRunReturn(module StdModule, block IrBlock, m ValueId, value FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: m, offset: MOffHeldStrand, value: emitConst(module, block: block, value: GtNoHeldStrand, ids: ids))
	emitConstReturn(module, block: block, value: value, ids: ids)
end 'emitStrandRunReturn'

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_stack_shrink(gt)` — Go's `shrinkstack`: HALVE A PARKED THREAD's STACK WHEN LESS THAN A QUARTER OF IT
// IS IN USE.** Its one caller is the strand runner's park door, for a stack above the floor, on the machine's
// scheduler context: the thread is stopped, its registers are saved on its own stack, and it is on no store and
// no queue yet, so nothing but this machine can reach it until the park is registered.
//
// ⭐⭐ **GO's `isShrinkStackSafe` REFUSALS HAVE NO SUBJECT HERE.** A park never happens inside a kernel call — the
// syscall shim runs the call on the machine's system stack and comes back to the thread before anything can
// switch it out — so there is no `syscallsp`. Nothing outside a thread holds a pointer into its stack: every
// wake store names the GT record, and the IOCP OVERLAPPED and a read's buffer live in the record and the heap,
// so there is no `sudog` and no `parkingOnChan` window. Inside it, the only stack-to-stack pointers are the ones
// `__gt_stack_relocate` rebases — `X64GtRuntime`'s relocation header argues that set is complete.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A STACK WHOSE SIZE WAS CHOSEN THIS WINDOW IS KEPT.** A grow or a spawn only marks the thread
// (`GtStackMarkResized`); the first park after it stamps the time instead of shrinking, and a later park shrinks
// only once `GtStackResizeWindowNs` has passed since the stamp. Go gets the same damping from its GC cadence.
//
// ⭐ **THE RULE IS GO's.** Halve iff what is in use, plus the guard height and one margin for the frames that ride
// free between guard checks, is under a quarter of the stack — so the halved stack is at most half full, and
// since every size is a power of two above the floor, never below it.
//
// ⭐⭐ **THE TESTS RUN CHEAPEST FIRST, AND THE CLOCK IS READ ONLY WHEN ITS ANSWER DECIDES SOMETHING** — at the park
// that stamps a fresh size, and for a stack the quarter rule would halve. A park that does neither costs the
// door's depth note and compare, this call, five loads and two tests. `__gt_now_ns` is a kernel call on some lanes; a thread
// the quarter rule would halve pays it at each park only until the window lets the halving happen, and that ends
// at the floor.
//
// ⚠ **A REFUSED ALLOCATION KEEPS THE STACK THE THREAD HAS**, which is still whole: a shrink only gives memory back.
function buildGtStackShrink(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtStackShrinkName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var stamp = module.addBlock(func, label: b"shrinkstamp", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var measure = module.addBlock(func, label: b"shrinkmeasure", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var window = module.addBlock(func, label: b"shrinkwindow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var halve = module.addBlock(func, label: b"shrinkhalve", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var relocate = module.addBlock(func, label: b"shrinkrelocate", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var keep = module.addBlock(func, label: b"shrinkkeep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0

	let size = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackSize, ids: ids)
	let used = emitGtStackUsed(module, block: entry, gt: gt, ids: ids)

	let stackMarks = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackMarks, ids: ids)
	let resized = emitGtHasStackMark(module, block: entry, marks: stackMarks, mark: GtStackMarkResized, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(resized, thenBlockId: stamp.id, elseBlockId: measure.id))

	// `grew` and `parked` outlive the park: the finish reads them.
	emitStoreField(module, block: stamp, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackResizedAt, value: emitGtNowNs(module, block: stamp, ids: ids))
	emitStoreField(module, block: stamp, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackMarks, value: emitBin(module, block: stamp, a: stackMarks, b: emitConst(module, block: stamp, value: GtStackMarksOfAnObservation, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(stamp, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	let slack = emitConst(module, block: measure, value: gtStackGuardHeight(target) + gtStackGuardMargin(target), ids: ids)
	let needed = emitBin(module, block: measure, a: used, b: slack, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let quarter = emitBin(module, block: measure, a: size, b: emitConst(module, block: measure, value: GtStackQuarterShift, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: measure, a: needed, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: quarter, whenTrue: keep, whenFalse: window, ids: ids)

	let now = emitGtNowNs(module, block: window, ids: ids)
	let age = emitBin(module, block: window, a: now, b: emitLoadField(module, block: window, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackResizedAt, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: window, value: age, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: GtStackResizeWindowNs, whenTrue: keep, whenFalse: halve, ids: ids)

	let half = emitBin(module, block: halve, a: size, b: emitConst(module, block: halve, value: GtStackHalfShift, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
	let newBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(halve, op: StdOp.call(newBase, callee: GtStackAllocName, args: argsOf(half)))
	let refused = emitGtStackRefused(module, block: halve, stackBase: newBase, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(halve, op: StdOp.condBranch(refused, thenBlockId: keep.id, elseBlockId: relocate.id))

	module.appendOp(relocate, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtStackRelocateName, args: threeArgs(gt, second: newBase, third: half)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: relocate, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackResizedAt, value: now)
	module.setTerminator(relocate, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.setTerminator(keep, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtStackShrink'

let GtStackPoolLabel = b"__gt_stack_pool"
let GtStackPoolMinShift = 11 as FullRangeInt
let GtStackPoolMaxShift = 16 as FullRangeInt
let GtStackPoolClassCount = GtStackPoolMaxShift - GtStackPoolMinShift + 1
let GtStackPoolMaxBytes = 1 shl GtStackPoolMaxShift
let GtStackPoolChunkBytes = 0x100000 as FullRangeInt
let GtStackPoolWordBytes = 8 as FullRangeInt
let GtStackPoolCursorOffset = (GtStackPoolClassCount * GtStackPoolWordBytes) as ByteOffset
let GtStackPoolLimitOffset = (GtStackPoolClassCount * GtStackPoolWordBytes + GtStackPoolWordBytes) as ByteOffset
let GtStackPoolLockOffset = (GtStackPoolClassCount * GtStackPoolWordBytes + 2 * GtStackPoolWordBytes) as ByteOffset
let GtStackPoolHeaderBytes = 0x1000 as FullRangeInt

// ⭐ **THE PER-P STACK CACHE — Go's `stackcache`.** A class is cached only while a half-capacity refill
// holds at least one stack of it, which is 2 to 16 KiB as Go's orders are; a larger class, and a thread
// holding no processor, takes the pool's lock instead. `__gt_stack_alloc` refills an empty class to half
// capacity in one hold of that lock, and `__gt_stack_free` sheds a full one back to half. The
// refill appends, so the class pops its stacks in the order the pool handed them out, ascending as
// carved; a refill that pushed would hand them out descending, which costs coroutine-heavy programs
// cycles.
let GtStackCacheCapacityShift = 15 as FullRangeInt
let GtStackCacheCapacityBytes = 1 shl GtStackCacheCapacityShift
let GtStackCacheHalfBytes = GtStackCacheCapacityBytes shr 1
let GtStackCacheMaxShift = GtStackCacheCapacityShift - 1
let GtStackCacheClassCount = GtStackCacheMaxShift - GtStackPoolMinShift + 1
let GtStackCacheEntryBytes = 16 as FullRangeInt
let GtStackCacheOffHead = 0
let GtStackCacheOffBytes = 8
export let GtStackCacheBytes = GtStackCacheClassCount * GtStackCacheEntryBytes

function requireStackPoolGeometry()
	if (1 shl GtStackPoolMinShift) != GtMinStackBytes 'minDrifted'
		panic("requireStackPoolGeometry: the pool's smallest class is {1 shl GtStackPoolMinShift} bytes but `GtMinStackBytes` is {GtMinStackBytes} — a stack below the smallest class would be handed a larger one that the thread never records")
	end 'minDrifted'

	if GtStackPoolMaxBytes != GtSeedMaxBytes 'maxDrifted'
		panic("requireStackPoolGeometry: the pool's largest class is {GtStackPoolMaxBytes} bytes but the adaptive seed may reach {GtSeedMaxBytes} — a seed above the pool would take the unpooled road on every spawn")
	end 'maxDrifted'

	if (GtStackPoolLockOffset as FullRangeInt) + ExclusiveLockBytes > GtStackPoolHeaderBytes 'headerTooSmall'
		panic("requireStackPoolGeometry: the pool header needs {(GtStackPoolLockOffset as FullRangeInt) + ExclusiveLockBytes} bytes but is laid in {GtStackPoolHeaderBytes}")
	end 'headerTooSmall'

	if GtStackCacheOffHead != 0 'headNotALink'
		panic("requireStackPoolGeometry: the per-P stack cache's head is at offset {GtStackCacheOffHead} but a cached stack links its successor at offset 0 — the refill appends through the entry as though it were the last stack's link")
	end 'headNotALink'

	if GtStackCacheMaxShift > GtStackPoolMaxShift 'cacheAbovePool'
		panic("requireStackPoolGeometry: the per-P stack cache holds classes up to {1 shl GtStackCacheMaxShift} bytes but the pool's largest class is {GtStackPoolMaxBytes} — a cached class would have no pool class to refill from")
	end 'cacheAbovePool'

	if GtStackPoolChunkBytes < GtStackPoolMaxBytes 'chunkTooSmall'
		panic("requireStackPoolGeometry: a pool chunk of {GtStackPoolChunkBytes} bytes cannot hold one stack of the largest class ({GtStackPoolMaxBytes})")
	end 'chunkTooSmall'
end 'requireStackPoolGeometry'

type GtStackClass
	export let shift as ValueId
	export let bytes as ValueId

	static function create(shift ValueId, bytes ValueId) returns GtStackClass
		return Self{shift: shift, bytes: bytes}
	end 'create'
end 'GtStackClass'

function emitGtStackClassOf(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, found IrBlock, size ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns GtStackClass
	var header = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkclasshdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var step = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkclassstep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let firstShift = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: GtStackPoolMinShift, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	cur.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(firstShift)))

	let shift = ids.mint()
	header.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(shift, argType: StdType.i64))
	let bytes = emitBin(module, block: header, a: emitConst(module, block: header, value: 1, ids: ids), b: shift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: header, a: bytes, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: size, whenTrue: found, whenFalse: step, ids: ids)

	let nextShift = emitBin(module, block: step, a: shift, b: emitConst(module, block: step, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(step, op: StdOp.branch(header.id))
	step.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(header.id, argIds: argsOf(nextShift)))

	return GtStackClass.create(shift, bytes: bytes)
end 'emitGtStackClassOf'

function emitGtStackPoolLock(module StdModule, block IrBlock, pool ValueId, held bool, ids ValueMinter)
	let lock = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: pool, offset: GtStackPoolLockOffset, ids: ids)

	if held 'enter'
		emitLockEnterAt(module, block: block, lock: lock, kind: RuntimeLockKind.pool, ids: ids)
	end 'enter' else 'leave'
		emitLockLeaveAt(module, block: block, lock: lock, kind: RuntimeLockKind.pool, ids: ids)
	end 'leave'
end 'emitGtStackPoolLock'

function emitGtStackFreeListHead(module StdModule, block IrBlock, pool ValueId, shift ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let index = emitBin(module, block: block, a: shift, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: GtStackPoolMinShift, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	return emitWordSlotAddr(module, block: block, base: pool, index: index, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtStackFreeListHead'

function emitGtStackCacheEntry(module StdModule, block IrBlock, p ValueId, shift ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let index = emitBin(module, block: block, a: shift, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: GtStackPoolMinShift, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let offset = emitBin(module, block: block, a: index, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: GtStackCacheEntryBytes, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let cache = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: p, offset: POffStackCache, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: cache, b: offset, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtStackCacheEntry'

function emitGtStackCachePop(module StdModule, block IrBlock, entry ValueId, bytes ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: entry, offset: GtStackCacheOffHead, ids: ids)
	let next = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: head, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: entry, offset: GtStackCacheOffHead, value: next)

	let cached = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: entry, offset: GtStackCacheOffBytes, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: entry, offset: GtStackCacheOffBytes, value: emitBin(module, block: block, a: cached, b: bytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids))
	return head
end 'emitGtStackCachePop'

function emitGtStackCacheCountAdd(module StdModule, block IrBlock, entry ValueId, bytes ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let cached = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: entry, offset: GtStackCacheOffBytes, ids: ids)
	let counted = emitBin(module, block: block, a: cached, b: bytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: entry, offset: GtStackCacheOffBytes, value: counted)
	return counted
end 'emitGtStackCacheCountAdd'

function emitGtStackCachePush(module StdModule, block IrBlock, entry ValueId, stack ValueId, bytes ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: entry, offset: GtStackCacheOffHead, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: stack, offset: 0, value: head)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: entry, offset: GtStackCacheOffHead, value: stack)

	return emitGtStackCacheCountAdd(module, block: block, entry: entry, bytes: bytes, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtStackCachePush'

function emitGtStackCacheAppend(module StdModule, block IrBlock, tail ValueId, entry ValueId, stack ValueId, bytes ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: tail, offset: 0, value: stack)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: stack, offset: 0, value: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids))

	return emitGtStackCacheCountAdd(module, block: block, entry: entry, bytes: bytes, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtStackCacheAppend'

function emitGtStackPoolPush(module StdModule, block IrBlock, pool ValueId, shift ValueId, stack ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let headAddr = emitGtStackFreeListHead(module, block: block, pool: pool, shift: shift, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: stack, offset: 0, value: emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: headAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids))
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: headAddr, offset: 0, value: stack)
end 'emitGtStackPoolPush'

function emitGtStackPoolTake(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, pool ValueId, shift ValueId, bytes ValueId, got IrBlock, refused IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var pop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkpop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var carve = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkcarve", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var newChunk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stknewchunk", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var chunkMapped = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkchunkmapped", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var carved = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkcarved", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let headAddr = emitGtStackFreeListHead(module, block: cur, pool: pool, shift: shift, ids: ids)
	let head = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: headAddr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: head, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: carve, whenFalse: pop, ids: ids)

	let next = emitLoadField(module, block: pop, record: head, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: pop, record: headAddr, offset: 0, value: next)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: pop, to: got, value: head)

	let cursor = emitLoadField(module, block: carve, record: pool, offset: GtStackPoolCursorOffset, ids: ids)
	let limit = emitLoadField(module, block: carve, record: pool, offset: GtStackPoolLimitOffset, ids: ids)
	let pastCarve = emitBin(module, block: carve, a: cursor, b: bytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: carve, a: pastCarve, pred: StdCmpPred.le, b: limit, whenTrue: carved, whenFalse: newChunk, ids: ids)
	carve.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(carved.id, argIds: twoArgs(cursor, second: pastCarve)))

	let chunkBytes = emitConst(module, block: newChunk, value: GtStackPoolChunkBytes, ids: ids)
	let raw = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(newChunk, op: StdOp.osAllocPages(raw, sizeId: chunkBytes))
	module.setTerminator(newChunk, op: StdOp.condBranch(emitGtStackRefused(module, block: newChunk, stackBase: raw, ids: ids), thenBlockId: refused.id, elseBlockId: chunkMapped.id))
	newChunk.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(refused.id, argIds: argsOf(raw)))

	emitStoreField(module, block: chunkMapped, record: pool, offset: GtStackPoolLimitOffset, value: emitBin(module, block: chunkMapped, a: raw, b: chunkBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids))
	let pastFirst = emitBin(module, block: chunkMapped, a: raw, b: bytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(chunkMapped, op: StdOp.branch(carved.id))
	chunkMapped.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(carved.id, argIds: twoArgs(raw, second: pastFirst)))

	let address = ids.mint()
	let nextCursor = ids.mint()
	carved.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(address, argType: StdType.i64))
	carved.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(nextCursor, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitStoreField(module, block: carved, record: pool, offset: GtStackPoolCursorOffset, value: nextCursor)
	emitBranchCarrying(module, from: carved, to: got, value: address)
end 'emitGtStackPoolTake'

// ⚠ Pooled stacks sit back to back in a chunk with no guard page between them. A green thread's
// only overflow check is its prologue guard, so a frame that outgrew the guard margin would write
// into a neighbour's stack without a fault — which is why `UnguardedChains` proves every unguarded
// chain fits that margin (E5004).
function buildGtStackAlloc(module StdModule)
	requireStackPoolGeometry()

	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtStackAllocName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var direct = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkdirect", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pooled = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkpooled", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkclassfound", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkoffset", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkmachine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cacheable = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkcacheable", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkcached", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var poolOnly = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkpoolonly", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var poolOnlyGot = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkpoolonlygot", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var poolOnlyRefused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkpoolonlyrefused", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var refill = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkrefill", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var refillGot = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkrefillgot", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var refillRefused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkrefillrefused", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var refillEmpty = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkrefillempty", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var refillDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkrefilldone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var popCached = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkpopcached", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let size = 0 as ValueId

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: size, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: GtStackPoolMaxBytes, whenTrue: direct, whenFalse: pooled, ids: ids)

	let mapped = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(direct, op: StdOp.osAllocPages(mapped, sizeId: size))
	module.setTerminator(direct, op: StdOp.ret(mapped))

	let sized = emitGtStackClassOf(module, func: func, cur: pooled, found: found, size: size, ids: ids)
	let pool = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: found, label: GtStackPoolLabel, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: found, value: sized.shift, pred: StdCmpPred.le, against: GtStackCacheMaxShift, whenTrue: cacheable, whenFalse: poolOnly, ids: ids)

	let p = emitLoadCurrentPOrBail(module, cur: cacheable, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, haveProc: haveProc, noProc: poolOnly, ids: ids)

	let entryAddr = emitGtStackCacheEntry(module, block: haveProc, p: p, shift: sized.shift, ids: ids)
	let cachedHead = emitLoadField(module, block: haveProc, record: entryAddr, offset: GtStackCacheOffHead, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: haveProc, value: cachedHead, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: refill, whenFalse: popCached, ids: ids)

	let poppedStack = emitGtStackCachePop(module, block: popCached, entry: entryAddr, bytes: sized.bytes, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(popCached, op: StdOp.ret(poppedStack))

	emitGtStackPoolLock(module, block: refill, pool: pool, held: true, ids: ids)
	let refillStack = ids.mint()
	refillGot.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(refillStack, argType: StdType.i64))
	let refillRefusal = ids.mint()
	refillRefused.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(refillRefusal, argType: StdType.i64))
	var refillTop = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkrefilltop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let refillTail = ids.mint()
	refillTop.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(refillTail, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(refill, op: StdOp.branch(refillTop.id))
	refill.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(refillTop.id, argIds: argsOf(entryAddr)))
	emitGtStackPoolTake(module, func: func, cur: refillTop, pool: pool, shift: sized.shift, bytes: sized.bytes, got: refillGot, refused: refillRefused, ids: ids)

	let filled = emitGtStackCacheAppend(module, block: refillGot, tail: refillTail, entry: entryAddr, stack: refillStack, bytes: sized.bytes, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: refillGot, value: filled, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: GtStackCacheHalfBytes, whenTrue: refillTop, whenFalse: refillDone, ids: ids)
	refillGot.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(refillTop.id, argIds: argsOf(refillStack)))

	let headAfterRefusal = emitLoadField(module, block: refillRefused, record: entryAddr, offset: GtStackCacheOffHead, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: refillRefused, value: headAfterRefusal, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: refillEmpty, whenFalse: refillDone, ids: ids)

	emitGtStackPoolLock(module, block: refillEmpty, pool: pool, held: false, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(refillEmpty, op: StdOp.ret(refillRefusal))

	emitGtStackPoolLock(module, block: refillDone, pool: pool, held: false, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(refillDone, op: StdOp.branch(popCached.id))

	emitGtStackPoolLock(module, block: poolOnly, pool: pool, held: true, ids: ids)
	let poolOnlyStack = ids.mint()
	poolOnlyGot.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(poolOnlyStack, argType: StdType.i64))
	let poolOnlyRefusal = ids.mint()
	poolOnlyRefused.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(poolOnlyRefusal, argType: StdType.i64))
	var poolOnlyTake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkpoolonlytake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	module.setTerminator(poolOnly, op: StdOp.branch(poolOnlyTake.id))
	emitGtStackPoolTake(module, func: func, cur: poolOnlyTake, pool: pool, shift: sized.shift, bytes: sized.bytes, got: poolOnlyGot, refused: poolOnlyRefused, ids: ids)

	emitGtStackPoolLock(module, block: poolOnlyGot, pool: pool, held: false, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(poolOnlyGot, op: StdOp.ret(poolOnlyStack))

	emitGtStackPoolLock(module, block: poolOnlyRefused, pool: pool, held: false, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(poolOnlyRefused, op: StdOp.ret(poolOnlyRefusal))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtStackAlloc'

function buildGtStackFree(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtStackFreeName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var direct = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkfreedirect", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pooled = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkfreepooled", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkclassfound", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveOffset = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkoffset", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveMachine = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkmachine", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cacheable = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkcacheable", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveProc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkcached", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var poolOnly = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkpoolonly", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var release = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkrelease", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var releaseHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkreleasehdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var releaseBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkreleasebody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var releaseDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkreleasedone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var kept = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkkept", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let base = 0 as ValueId
	let size = 1 as ValueId

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: size, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: GtStackPoolMaxBytes, whenTrue: direct, whenFalse: pooled, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(direct, op: StdOp.osFreePages(base, sizeId: size))
	module.setTerminator(direct, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	let sized = emitGtStackClassOf(module, func: func, cur: pooled, found: found, size: size, ids: ids)
	let pool = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: found, label: GtStackPoolLabel, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: found, value: sized.shift, pred: StdCmpPred.le, against: GtStackCacheMaxShift, whenTrue: cacheable, whenFalse: poolOnly, ids: ids)

	let p = emitLoadCurrentPOrBail(module, cur: cacheable, haveOffset: haveOffset, haveMachine: haveMachine, haveProc: haveProc, noProc: poolOnly, ids: ids)

	let entryAddr = emitGtStackCacheEntry(module, block: haveProc, p: p, shift: sized.shift, ids: ids)
	let cached = emitGtStackCachePush(module, block: haveProc, entry: entryAddr, stack: base, bytes: sized.bytes, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: haveProc, value: cached, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, against: GtStackCacheCapacityBytes, whenTrue: release, whenFalse: kept, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(kept, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	emitGtStackPoolLock(module, block: release, pool: pool, held: true, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(release, op: StdOp.branch(releaseHdr.id))

	let remaining = emitLoadField(module, block: releaseHdr, record: entryAddr, offset: GtStackCacheOffBytes, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: releaseHdr, value: remaining, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: GtStackCacheHalfBytes, whenTrue: releaseBody, whenFalse: releaseDone, ids: ids)

	let released = emitGtStackCachePop(module, block: releaseBody, entry: entryAddr, bytes: sized.bytes, ids: ids)
	emitGtStackPoolPush(module, block: releaseBody, pool: pool, shift: sized.shift, stack: released, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(releaseBody, op: StdOp.branch(releaseHdr.id))

	emitGtStackPoolLock(module, block: releaseDone, pool: pool, held: false, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(releaseDone, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	emitGtStackPoolLock(module, block: poolOnly, pool: pool, held: true, ids: ids)
	emitGtStackPoolPush(module, block: poolOnly, pool: pool, shift: sized.shift, stack: base, ids: ids)
	emitGtStackPoolLock(module, block: poolOnly, pool: pool, held: false, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(poolOnly, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtStackFree'

function buildGtSystemStackAlloc(module StdModule, target Target)
	requireProbeStrideFitsTheSystemStackGuard(target)
	requireWindowsDispatchFitsItsReserve(target)

	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtSystemStackAllocName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let size = 0

	match systemStackGuardOf(target) 'guard'
		pageGuardWithHeadroom then buildPageGuardedSystemStackBody(module, func: func, entry: entry, size: size, target: target, ids: ids)
		noAccessPage then buildNoAccessSystemStackBody(module, func: func, entry: entry, size: size, target: target, ids: ids)
	end 'guard'

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtSystemStackAlloc'

function requireProbeStrideFitsTheSystemStackGuard(target Target)
	let faulting = gtSystemStackFaultingBytes(target)

	if (StackProbeStrideBytes as FullRangeInt) > faulting 'strideSkipsTheGuard'
		panic("requireProbeStrideFitsTheSystemStackGuard: the stack probe touches one byte per {StackProbeStrideBytes} but the part of {targetToString(target)}'s system-stack guard region that faults is {faulting} bytes — a frame could step over it between two touches and write below it without a fault")
	end 'strideSkipsTheGuard'
end 'requireProbeStrideFitsTheSystemStackGuard'

function buildNoAccessSystemStackBody(module StdModule, func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, size ValueId, target Target, ids ValueMinter)
	var reserveRefused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkreserverefused", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var commit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkcommit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var commitRefused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkcommitrefused", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var committed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkcommitted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let guardBytes = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: gtSystemStackGuardRegionBytes(target), ids: ids)
	let reservedBytes = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: size, b: guardBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let reservation = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osReservePages(reservation, sizeId: reservedBytes))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(emitGtStackRefused(module, block: entry, stackBase: reservation, ids: ids), thenBlockId: reserveRefused.id, elseBlockId: commit.id))

	module.setTerminator(reserveRefused, op: StdOp.ret(reservation))

	let usableBase = emitBin(module, block: commit, a: reservation, b: guardBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let mapped = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(commit, op: StdOp.osCommitPages(mapped, addrId: usableBase, sizeId: size))
	module.setTerminator(commit, op: StdOp.condBranch(emitGtStackRefused(module, block: commit, stackBase: mapped, ids: ids), thenBlockId: commitRefused.id, elseBlockId: committed.id))

	module.appendOp(commitRefused, op: StdOp.osFreePages(reservation, sizeId: reservedBytes))
	module.setTerminator(commitRefused, op: StdOp.ret(mapped))

	module.setTerminator(committed, op: StdOp.ret(usableBase))
end 'buildNoAccessSystemStackBody'

function buildPageGuardedSystemStackBody(module StdModule, func IrFunction, entry IrBlock, size ValueId, target Target, ids ValueMinter)
	var arm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkarm", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var allocRefused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkallocrefused", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var armRefused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkarmrefused", heat: BlockHeat.cold)
	var armed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stkarmed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let regionBytes = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: gtSystemStackGuardRegionBytes(target), ids: ids)
	let totalBytes = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: size, b: regionBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let whole = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osAllocPages(whole, sizeId: totalBytes))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(emitGtStackRefused(module, block: entry, stackBase: whole, ids: ids), thenBlockId: allocRefused.id, elseBlockId: arm.id))

	module.setTerminator(allocRefused, op: StdOp.ret(whole))

	let guardAt = emitBin(module, block: arm, a: whole, b: emitConst(module, block: arm, value: WindowsSystemStackHeadroomBytes, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let guardResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(arm, op: StdOp.osGuardPages(guardResult, addrId: guardAt, sizeId: emitConst(module, block: arm, value: WindowsSystemStackGuardPageBytes, ids: ids)))
	module.setTerminator(arm, op: StdOp.condBranch(emitGtStackRefused(module, block: arm, stackBase: guardResult, ids: ids), thenBlockId: armRefused.id, elseBlockId: armed.id))

	module.appendOp(armRefused, op: StdOp.osFreePages(whole, sizeId: totalBytes))
	module.setTerminator(armRefused, op: StdOp.ret(guardResult))

	module.setTerminator(armed, op: StdOp.ret(emitBin(module, block: armed, a: whole, b: regionBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)))
end 'buildPageGuardedSystemStackBody'

// 1 when `marks`, a `GtOffStackMarks` word, carries `mark`.
function emitGtHasStackMark(module StdModule, block IrBlock, marks ValueId, mark FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let bit = emitBin(module, block: block, a: marks, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: mark, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	return emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: bit, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtHasStackMark'

function emitGtStackUsed(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let top = emitBin(module, block: block, a: emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackBase, ids: ids), b: emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackSize, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: top, b: emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffSp, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtStackUsed'

// Called at the park door for every park kind, preemption included: a thread at the floor never reaches
// `__gt_stack_shrink`, so this is the only place its depth is seen, and without it its finish samples nothing.
function emitGtNoteStackDepth(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, gt ValueId, cont IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	var deeper = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stacknotedeeper", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var mark = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stacknotemark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let used = emitGtStackUsed(module, block: cur, gt: gt, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: cur, a: used, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, b: emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackHighWater, ids: ids), whenTrue: deeper, whenFalse: mark, ids: ids)

	emitStoreField(module, block: deeper, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackHighWater, value: used)
	module.setTerminator(deeper, op: StdOp.branch(mark.id))

	let stackMarks = emitLoadField(module, block: mark, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackMarks, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: mark, record: gt, offset: GtOffStackMarks, value: emitBin(module, block: mark, a: stackMarks, b: emitConst(module, block: mark, value: GtStackMarkParked, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(mark, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
end 'emitGtNoteStackDepth'

// `size shr 2` and `size shr 1` — a quarter and a half of a power-of-two stack.
let GtStackQuarterShift = 2
let GtStackHalfShift = 1

// ⭐⭐⭐ **`__gt_seed_adapt()` — Go's `gcComputeStartingStackSize`: ONCE A WINDOW, THE SEED BECOMES
// THE AVERAGE STACK THAT THE THREADS WHICH FINISHED IN IT NEEDED.** Its one caller is
// `__gt_runner_done`, at the finish that closes a window on its processor (`GtSeedWindowShift`). The
// body runs under `__gt_seed_guard`, claimed by CAS; a closer that finds it taken does nothing, and its
// window's samples fall to the next adapt.
//
// The counters are per processor and only grow (`POffStackSampleSum`). The adapt sums every processor's
// and takes the window as the difference from the sums the last close saw. Go resets its counters at a
// collection; Maxon has no stop-the-world, and a reset would race a finisher's plain add.
//
// The seed is the smallest power of two in [`gtSeedStackBytes`, `GtSeedMaxBytes`] whose product with the
// window's count covers the window's sum — `round2(sum / count)`, clamped, with no division.
//
// ⚠ **THE COUNTERS ARE READ UNLOCKED WHILE THEIR PROCESSORS RUN**, so a window can carry a sample's size
// and the next its count. A window of fewer than `GtSeedWindowSamples` samples keeps the seed and leaves
// its samples to the next close.
function buildGtSeedAdapt(module StdModule, target Target)
	if GtSeedMaxBytes < gtSeedStackBytes(target) 'ceilingBelowFloor'
		panic("buildGtSeedAdapt: GtSeedMaxBytes ({GtSeedMaxBytes}) is below this target's seed floor ({gtSeedStackBytes(target)}) — the adaptive seed would have no size to choose")
	end 'ceilingBelowFloor'

	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtSeedAdaptName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var claimed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"seedclaimed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sumHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"seedsumhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sumBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"seedsumbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var summed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"seedsummed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var window = module.addBlock(func, label: b"seedwindow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var grow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"seedgrow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fits = module.addBlock(func, label: b"seedfits", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var publish = module.addBlock(func, label: b"seedpublish", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var finished = module.addBlock(func, label: b"seedfinished", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"seeddone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let guardClaimed = emitCasGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: GtSeedGuardLabel, expected: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids), replacement: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 1, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: entry, result: guardClaimed, lost: doneBlock, won: claimed, ids: ids)

	let numProcs = emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: claimed, label: SchedNumProcsLabel, ids: ids)
	let noneYet = emitConst(module, block: claimed, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let procIndex = ids.mint()
	let runningSum = ids.mint()
	let runningCount = ids.mint()
	sumHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(procIndex, argType: StdType.i64))
	sumHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(runningSum, argType: StdType.i64))
	sumHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(runningCount, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(claimed, op: StdOp.branch(sumHdr.id))
	claimed.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(sumHdr.id, argIds: threeArgs(noneYet, second: noneYet, third: noneYet)))

	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: sumHdr, a: procIndex, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: numProcs, whenTrue: summed, whenFalse: sumBody, ids: ids)
	sumHdr.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(summed.id, argIds: twoArgs(runningSum, second: runningCount)))

	let proc = emitLoadProc(module, block: sumBody, index: procIndex, ids: ids)
	let nextSum = emitBin(module, block: sumBody, a: runningSum, b: emitLoadField(module, block: sumBody, record: proc, offset: POffStackSampleSum, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let nextCount = emitBin(module, block: sumBody, a: runningCount, b: emitLoadField(module, block: sumBody, record: proc, offset: POffStackSampleCount, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let nextIndex = emitBin(module, block: sumBody, a: procIndex, b: emitConst(module, block: sumBody, value: OneMore, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(sumBody, op: StdOp.branch(sumHdr.id))
	sumBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(sumHdr.id, argIds: threeArgs(nextIndex, second: nextSum, third: nextCount)))

	let sum = ids.mint()
	let count = ids.mint()
	summed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(sum, argType: StdType.i64))
	summed.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(count, argType: StdType.i64))
	let windowSum = emitBin(module, block: summed, a: sum, b: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: summed, label: GtSeedSumSeenLabel, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let windowCount = emitBin(module, block: summed, a: count, b: emitLoadGlobalWord(module, block: summed, label: GtSeedCountSeenLabel, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: summed, value: windowCount, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: GtSeedWindowSamples, whenTrue: finished, whenFalse: window, ids: ids)

	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: window, label: GtSeedSumSeenLabel, value: sum, ids: ids)
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: window, label: GtSeedCountSeenLabel, value: count, ids: ids)
	let floorSeed = emitConst(module, block: window, value: gtSeedStackBytes(target), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(window, op: StdOp.branch(grow.id))
	window.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(grow.id, argIds: argsOf(floorSeed)))

	// grow(seed): stop at the ceiling, or at the first size that holds the window's average.
	let seed = ids.mint()
	grow.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(seed, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: grow, value: seed, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, against: GtSeedMaxBytes, whenTrue: publish, whenFalse: fits, ids: ids)
	grow.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(publish.id, argIds: argsOf(seed)))

	let covered = emitBin(module, block: fits, a: seed, b: windowCount, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let holdsAverage = emitCmp(module, block: fits, a: covered, b: windowSum, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)
	let doubled = emitBin(module, block: fits, a: seed, b: seed, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(fits, op: StdOp.condBranch(holdsAverage, thenBlockId: publish.id, elseBlockId: grow.id))
	fits.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(publish.id, argIds: argsOf(seed)))
	fits.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(grow.id, argIds: argsOf(doubled)))

	let chosen = ids.mint()
	publish.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(chosen, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitStoreGlobalWord(module, block: publish, label: GtSeedBytesLabel, value: chosen, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(publish, op: StdOp.branch(finished.id))

	_ = emitGlobalAccumulate(module, block: finished, label: GtSeedGuardLabel, delta: emitConst(module, block: finished, value: OneFewer, ids: ids), multiM: true, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(finished, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtSeedAdapt'

// `__gt_trace_emit(tagRecord, value, suffixRecord)` — print one async-trace line to stderr:
// `<tag><value><suffix?>\n`. `tagRecord`/`suffixRecord` are immortal `.rdata` String records (see
// `GtTraceTags`); a suffix of 0 means "no suffix". See `GtTraceEmitName` for the compile-time gating.
//
// ⭐ **IT ALLOCATES NOTHING AND RELEASES NOTHING, AND THAT IS WHY IT MAY SIT INSIDE THE SCHEDULER.** Turning
// the id into digits reuses `__int_to_string`, which is correct for every i64 including `i64.min` and fills a
// CALLER-OWNED buffer, returning the length it wrote — so this writes into 24 bytes of its OWN FRAME
// (`GtTraceDigitsSlot`, which is per-call storage rather than shared `.data`) and hands back no String for
// anybody to release. Writing a second digit loop here would be a second
// implementation of a solved problem, in the runtime, where a wrong answer is hardest to see. An untraced
// build never reaches this function at all.
function buildGtTraceEmit(module StdModule, tags GtTraceTags)
	var paramTypes = StdTypeArray.create()

	for _ in 0 upto GtTraceEmitArity 'eachParam'
		paramTypes.push(StdType.i64)
	end 'eachParam'

	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtTraceEmitName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: GtTraceEmitArity as ParamCount, paramTypes: paramTypes)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(GtTraceEmitArity as ParamCount)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var withSuffix = module.addBlock(func, label: b"suffix", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: GtTraceEmitArity as ParamCount)
	let tagPtr = 0 as ValueId
	let tagLen = 1 as ValueId
	let value = 2
	let suffixPtr = 3 as ValueId
	let suffixLen = 4 as ValueId

	let tagWritten = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osWriteStderr(tagWritten, bufId: tagPtr, lenId: tagLen))

	// The id's digits, into THIS CALL's frame (`GtTraceDigitsSlot`): `__int_to_string(value, dest)` returns the
	// LENGTH it wrote, so the write is one `osWriteStderr` of exactly those bytes and nothing is allocated or
	// released.
	func.stackSize = GtTraceDigitsBytes
	let digitsBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.stackRecordAddr(digitsBuf, slot: GtTraceDigitsSlot))
	let digitCount = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(digitCount, callee: IntToStringName, args: twoArgs(value, second: digitsBuf)))
	let digitsWritten = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.osWriteStderr(digitsWritten, bufId: digitsBuf, lenId: digitCount))

	// A zero-LENGTH suffix means there is none — the one signal, so no caller passes a second flag.
	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let noSuffix = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.cmp(noSuffix, lhs: suffixLen, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(noSuffix, thenBlockId: doneBlock.id, elseBlockId: withSuffix.id))

	let suffixWritten = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(withSuffix, op: StdOp.osWriteStderr(suffixWritten, bufId: suffixPtr, lenId: suffixLen))
	module.setTerminator(withSuffix, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))

	// The NEWLINE is part of the line, not of the caller: every trace site ends one, so ending it here is what
	// keeps the six sites from each remembering to.
	let newlineAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doneBlock, op: StdOp.rdataAddr(newlineAddr, label: tags.newline.label))
	let newlineLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doneBlock, op: StdOp.const(newlineLen, value: tags.newline.length, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let newlineWritten = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doneBlock, op: StdOp.osWriteStderr(newlineWritten, bufId: newlineAddr, lenId: newlineLen))
	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtTraceEmit'

// Emit one trace line at a call site: `<tag><gtId><suffix?>`. An ABSENT suffix (zero length) means no suffix.
// A no-op when the build is untraced, so every call site reads as an unconditional statement and the gating
// lives in exactly one place.
function emitGtTraceLine(module StdModule, block IrBlock, tag GtTraceTag, gtId ValueId, suffix GtTraceSuffix, ids ValueMinter)
	let suffixAddr = ids.mint()
	let suffixLen = ids.mint()

	match suffix 'suffix'
		none then module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(suffixAddr, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
		tagged(suffixTag) then module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.rdataAddr(suffixAddr, label: suffixTag.label))
	end 'suffix'

	let suffixLength = match suffix 'suffixLength'
		none gives 0 as FullRangeInt
		tagged(suffixTag) gives suffixTag.length
	end 'suffixLength'

	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(suffixLen, value: suffixLength, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitGtTraceLineOfSuffix(module, block: block, tag: tag, gtId: gtId, suffixAddr: suffixAddr, suffixLen: suffixLen, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtTraceLine'

// ⭐ The same line with the suffix arriving as a `(ptr, len)` PAIR OF VALUES rather than as a compile-time
// tag — which is what `__gt_io_park` has: its suffix is its own two PARAMETERS, chosen by whichever site
// called it (see `GtIoOperation`). It carries no `traced` guard, because a runtime value can only exist in a
// build that emitted the parameter holding it; the constant-suffix door above keeps the guard for the five
// sites whose suffix is decided here.
function emitGtTraceLineOfSuffix(module StdModule, block IrBlock, tag GtTraceTag, gtId ValueId, suffixAddr ValueId, suffixLen ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let tagAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.rdataAddr(tagAddr, label: tag.label))
	let tagLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(tagLen, value: tag.length, valueType: StdType.i64))
	var callArgs = ValueIdArray.create()
	callArgs.push(tagAddr)
	callArgs.push(tagLen)
	callArgs.push(gtId)
	callArgs.push(suffixAddr)
	callArgs.push(suffixLen)
	let emitted = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(emitted, callee: GtTraceEmitName, args: callArgs))
end 'emitGtTraceLineOfSuffix'

// ⭐ **THE CURRENT GREEN THREAD'S TRACE ID — the `#N` every trace line prints.** Read out of the GT STRUCT at
// the point of the line rather than carried across in a value, because a park's two lines sit either side of a
// CONTEXT SWITCH and nothing in a register survives one — which is also why the id lives on the struct at all
// (see `GtOffTraceId`). Both of `__gt_io_park`'s reads and both of `__gt_sleep`'s are this sequence; one
// spelling, so a fifth reader cannot name the offset differently.
function emitCurrentGtTraceId(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let selfGt = emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	let traceId = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(traceId, addrId: selfGt, offset: GtOffTraceId, valueType: StdType.i64))
	return traceId
end 'emitCurrentGtTraceId'

// One `<tag><gtId>` line about the CURRENT green thread with NO operation suffix — the shape both of
// `__gt_sleep`'s lines take.
//
// ⚠ **IT ASKS THE `traced` QUESTION BEFORE READING THE STRUCT, WHICH `buildGtIoPark` DOES NOT**, and the
// difference is deliberate rather than an inconsistency: the park hoists its two reads above its guard because
// they were there before the tags were, so an untraced park's image is what it always was. A sleep had no such
// reads, so emitting them unconditionally would put three new ops into every untraced program that sleeps.
// The `traced` it hands on to `emitGtTraceLine` is that same value travelling on, not a second answer to the
// question — the two guards gate different things (a STRUCT READ here, a LINE there) off one fact.
function emitCurrentGtTraceLine(module StdModule, block IrBlock, tag GtTraceTag, ids ValueMinter)
	let gtId = emitCurrentGtTraceId(module, block: block, ids: ids)
	emitGtTraceLine(module, block: block, tag: tag, gtId: gtId, suffix: GtTraceSuffix.none, ids: ids)
end 'emitCurrentGtTraceLine'

// ⭐ **ONE DebugStream sched event about the green thread `gt`, carrying its trace id.** The sites are the
// async trace's own — a spawn, an await, the two brackets of a sleep and of an I/O park — plus
// `Scheduler.yield()`, which the async trace does not report. A no-op unless this build carries the producer,
// asked BEFORE the trace-id read so an untraced build's image is unchanged (see `emitMmEvent` for why the
// gate cannot sit behind the argument). Returns the block to continue in, because the event splits it.
//
// ⚠ **`__gt_preempt` EMITS NONE**: its call tree rides the guard margin (`buildGtPreempt`), and a ring
// write is a lock and a call deeper.
function emitSchedEventOf(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, eventType FullRangeInt, gt ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	if not module.debugStream 'untracedBuild'
		return block
	end 'untracedBuild'

	let traceId = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffTraceId, ids: ids)
	return emitSchedEvent(module, func: func, block: block, eventType: eventType, traceId: traceId, ids: ids)
end 'emitSchedEventOf'

// The same event about the CURRENT green thread, read at the point of the event for `emitCurrentGtTraceId`'s
// reason: a park's two events sit either side of a context switch.
function emitCurrentGtSchedEvent(module StdModule, func IrFunction, block IrBlock, eventType FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	if not module.debugStream 'untracedBuild'
		return block
	end 'untracedBuild'

	return emitSchedEventOf(module, func: func, block: block, eventType: eventType, gt: emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: block, ids: ids), ids: ids)
end 'emitCurrentGtSchedEvent'

// `__gt_io_park(suffixPtr, suffixLen)` — the I/O yield point. See `GtIoParkName` for what it does and does not
// promise, for why it parks rather than taking `__gt_resched`'s run-queue handoff, and for why the SUFFIX is a
// pair of parameters in a traced build and no parameter at all in an untraced one.
function buildGtIoPark(module StdModule)
	// ⭐ The tags are asked of the MODULE rather than taken as a parameter, because the park's call sites, in
	// other files, have no way to be handed them and must ask the same question there (see `emitIoPark` and
	// `GtTracing`). One reader, one answer.
	view tracing = module.asyncTracing
	let traced = tracing.isTraced()

	// The arity IS the gating: a traced park is handed its operation's suffix by the site that called it, an
	// untraced one takes nothing and is byte-identical to the park built before the suffix existed.
	var paramCount = 0 as ParamCount
	var paramTypes = StdTypeArray.create()

	if traced 'suffixParams'
		paramCount = IoParkSuffixArity
		paramTypes = twoI64Params()
	end 'suffixParams'

	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtIoParkName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: paramCount, paramTypes: paramTypes)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(paramCount)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var yielding = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gtioyield", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gtiodone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	if traced 'readSuffixParams'
		emitParams(module, block: entry, count: paramCount)
	end 'readSuffixParams'

	let suffixAddr = 0
	let suffixLen = 1

	// ⛔⛔ **A YIELD NEEDS A GREEN THREAD TO YIELD *FROM*, AND THIS ENTRY IS REACHED WITHOUT ONE.** A dropped
	// promise's managed ARGUMENTS are released by `__gt_release_<callee>` inside `__gt_reclaim`, which the
	// strand runner reaches on the machine's own scheduler context — and a released argument may be a box
	// whose destructor closes an OS handle, so `__ms_close`/`__mf_close`/`__md_close` all arrive here with no
	// green thread current. `__gt_park` would then write its note onto the M's inline scheduler GT and switch
	// to its `waiter`, which is 0. **MEASURED as a nil-pointer panic in `__gt_context_switch`** on a program
	// that drops a promise whose coroutine co-owns a socket (`specs/netpoll-socket.md`'s two `drop-a-parked-*`
	// cases), with the stack `__gt_context_switch ← __gt_io_park ← __ms_close ← __ms_destruct ←
	// __destruct_TcpListener ← __gt_release_… ← __gt_reclaim ← __gt_runner_done`.
	//
	// ⭐ **SKIPPING THE PARK IS THE HONEST TRANSLATION AND NOT A SUPPRESSION.** The park exists to hand the
	// processor to another green thread while this one waits; on a machine's scheduler context there is no
	// green thread to hand it back to and no processor being held on one's behalf, which is exactly the
	// answer `module.parksOnIo` gives at compile time for a lane with no scheduler at all. The kernel call
	// the caller is about to make still happens.
	//
	// ⚠ **`stackBase == 0` IS THE SAME DISCRIMINATOR THE SYSCALL SHIM ALREADY USES** for *"am I on a green
	// stack?"* (`X64GtRuntime.emitGtGatedIatCall`), asked here rather than answered a second way.
	// ⚠ **AND THE GUARD IS THIS ENTRY'S ALONE.** `__gt_sleep` and `__mbox_recv` reach `__gt_park` from a
	// program's own code, where no green thread current is a defect worth faulting on; this is the one park
	// the runtime reaches from its OWN teardown path.
	let stackBase = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: entry, ids: ids), offset: GtOffStackBase, ids: ids)
	let onGreenStack = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: entry, value: stackBase, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, ids: ids)
	let noPromiseToGiveUp = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: GtPromiseHeld, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.condBranch(onGreenStack, thenBlockId: yielding.id, elseBlockId: doneBlock.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(doneBlock.id, argIds: argsOf(noPromiseToGiveUp)))

	// The trace pair BRACKETS the park: the yield line is emitted on the way down, the resume line after the
	// scheduler has switched back into us. Both read the id afresh — see `emitCurrentGtTraceId`.
	let traceIdBefore = emitCurrentGtTraceId(module, block: yielding, ids: ids)

	if let tags = try tracing.tags() 'yieldLine'
		emitGtTraceLineOfSuffix(module, block: yielding, tag: tags.ioYield, gtId: traceIdBefore, suffixAddr: suffixAddr, suffixLen: suffixLen, ids: ids)
	end 'yieldLine'

	yielding = emitCurrentGtSchedEvent(module, func: func, block: yielding, eventType: DsEvIoYield, ids: ids)

	var resumed = emitGtParkOnDeadline(module, func: func, cur: yielding, deadline: emitGtNowNs(module, block: yielding, ids: ids), ids: ids)

	let traceIdAfter = emitCurrentGtTraceId(module, block: resumed, ids: ids)

	if let tags = try tracing.tags() 'resumeLine'
		emitGtTraceLineOfSuffix(module, block: resumed, tag: tags.ioResume, gtId: traceIdAfter, suffixAddr: suffixAddr, suffixLen: suffixLen, ids: ids)
	end 'resumeLine'

	resumed = emitCurrentGtSchedEvent(module, func: func, block: resumed, eventType: DsEvIoResume, ids: ids)

	// Read after the park and never before it: the park is a full trip through `__sched_lock`,
	// which is what publishes a mark stored on another machine. A load ahead of the yield is
	// unsynchronised and reads stale as soon as more than one processor runs, so every refusal
	// built on it silently stops happening.
	let renounced = emitLoadField(module, block: resumed, record: emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: resumed, ids: ids), offset: GtOffRenounced, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(resumed, op: StdOp.branch(doneBlock.id))
	resumed.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(doneBlock.id, argIds: argsOf(renounced)))

	let answer = ids.mint()
	doneBlock.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(answer, argType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.ret(answer))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtIoPark'

// ⭐ **"THE KERNEL CALL THIS OPERATION EXISTS TO MAKE — YIELD FIRST" (see `GtIoParkName`), and this is the one
// place that sentence is written.** One helper rather than the same two lines at each site — it is the ROSTER,
// expressed as the single gesture each member makes, and `SemanticCheck.ioYieldingRuntimeCallee` covers
// exactly the functions that call it (by prefix, so it cannot rot).
//
// ⛔⛔ **ONE PARK PER BLOCKING OPERATION — NOT PER ENTRY POINT, AND NOT PER KERNEL CALL.** The park is what the
// async trace reports as `io_yield`/`io_resume`, so the pair must count the things that make a green thread
// WAIT:
//
//   • **A kernel call that cannot wait earns no park.** `WSAStartup` runs once per process
//     (`ManagedSocketRuntime.emitWinsockStartup`) and `socket()` allocates a descriptor, so neither is one.
//   • **A host that needs no resolver earns no resolve park.** A dotted-quad literal carries its own answer,
//     so `ManagedSocketRuntime.emitResolveHostIp` parks only on the name road.
//   • **A function that waits twice parks twice.** A `TcpClient.connect` to a hostname is a DNS round trip AND a
//     TCP handshake, either of which can be the slow one, and one park for both would leave the trace unable to
//     say which a hung connect is hung in.
//
// ⭐ So every filesystem site and `send`/`recv`/`close` parks exactly once; `__ms_tcp_connect` parks twice
// for a name and once for a literal; `__ml_bind` parks once for a name and not at all for a literal.
//
// ⚠ **THE COUNT IS TWENTY CALL SITES AND TWENTY-THREE EMITTED ENTRY POINTS, AND THOSE ARE DIFFERENT
// NUMBERS.** Eight sites in `ManagedFileRuntime`, six in `ManagedDirectoryRuntime`, five in
// `ManagedSocketRuntime` (the name road's resolve, the dial, and one each in send/recv/close — `__ms_destruct`
// parks through `__ms_close` and has none of its own) and one in `ManagedListenerRuntime` (the accept — its
// close and its drop are the socket band's). The resolve site serves two entry points, `__ms_tcp_connect` and
// `__ml_bind`. Two of the filesystem builders are parameterised (`buildManagedFileOpen` is invoked for
// read/write/writeExecutable, `buildManagedFilePathOperation` for delete/rename), so the emitted
// `__mf_*`/`__md_*` band is three larger than its fourteen sites. Say which noun is being counted wherever
// either number is quoted.
export function emitIoPark(module StdModule, block IrBlock, operation GtIoOperation, ids ValueMinter)
	// ⭐ **A TARGET WITH NO SCHEDULER EMITS NO PARK.** The park exists to let OTHER green threads run while
	// this one waits; a lane `targetProvidesFacility` denies `greenThreads` has exactly one thread of
	// execution and nothing to yield to, so the call would be a `bl` into machinery nothing installed —
	// `__gt_init`, the P/M tier, `__gt_now_ns`, the timer store. `RuntimeUsage.parksOnIo` reads the same
	// answer and keeps that machinery out of the program; see `IrModule.parksOnIo` for why the question is
	// asked of the module rather than threaded to every builder.
	if not module.parksOnIo 'noScheduler'
		return
	end 'noScheduler'

	_ = emitIoParkCall(module, block: block, operation: operation, ids: ids)
end 'emitIoPark'

// The yield is where a renounced coroutine is refused, because of where it sits: ahead of the
// kernel call, and ahead of `__np_pd_op_begin` at every send, recv and accept. A refusal here
// issues nothing the kernel must later complete and publishes no waiter, so `__np_waiters` never
// rises and the exit's drain still meets. Refusing any later leaves that count standing at one
// and the process in the poller for good — `__sched_checkdead` short-circuits on the same term,
// so nothing reports it.
export function emitIoParkBranchOnRenounced(module StdModule, cur IrBlock, operation GtIoOperation, proceed IrBlock, renounced IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	if not (module.parksOnIo and ioParkRefusesARenouncedCoroutine(operation)) 'carriesOn'
		emitIoPark(module, block: cur, operation: operation, ids: ids)
		module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(proceed.id))
		return
	end 'carriesOn'

	let answer = emitIoParkCall(module, block: cur, operation: operation, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: answer, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: GtPromiseHeld, whenTrue: renounced, whenFalse: proceed, ids: ids)
end 'emitIoParkBranchOnRenounced'

// A coroutine whose promise has been given up may not begin an operation that waits on a far end.
// The two `false` answers are a safety rule and not an exemption: a renounced frame's unwind runs
// `__ms_close` and `__mf_close`, and every file and directory site shares the `fileExists` tag,
// so refusing either would abandon the descriptor and the heap it owns — and a leaked descriptor
// is invisible to the exit-101 gate, because a socket is not memory.
//
// `netListen` is refusable for a reason only some lanes show. `emitResolveHostIp` is one park
// site serving both the dial and the bind, and where `socketResolveShape` answers
// `builtinResolver` the name road opens a socket of its own on the poller; the `getaddrinfo`
// shapes make a bounded syscall instead, so a reading taken from one of those alone says this
// could be `false`. Only a name parks at all — a dotted quad short-circuits above the yield, so a
// bind to one is untouched.
function ioParkRefusesARenouncedCoroutine(operation GtIoOperation) returns bool
	return match operation 'operation'
		fileExists or
		netClose gives false
		netConnect or
		netListen or
		netAccept or
		netSend or
		netRecv gives true
	end 'operation'
end 'ioParkRefusesARenouncedCoroutine'

function emitIoParkCall(module StdModule, block IrBlock, operation GtIoOperation, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	// The suffix travels as `(rdata address, byte length)`, and an untraced build's park has no suffix
	// parameters to fill, so the call carries no arguments at all.
	//
	// ⛔ **THE ARITY IS DECIDED BY `module.asyncTracing` AT BOTH ENDS — THE SAME QUESTION, NOT MERELY THE
	// SAME FIELD.** A different question here than the one `buildGtIoPark` asks could put an argument-less
	// call into a two-parameter park — a wrong FRAME, not a wrong trace.
	var args = ValueIdArray.create()

	if let tags = try module.asyncTracing.tags() 'tracedBuild'
		let tag = tags.ioTag(operation)
		let suffixAddr = ids.mint()
		module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.rdataAddr(suffixAddr, label: tag.label))
		let suffixLen = ids.mint()
		module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(suffixLen, value: tag.length, valueType: StdType.i64))
		args.push(suffixAddr)
		args.push(suffixLen)
	end 'tracedBuild'

	let parked = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(parked, callee: GtIoParkName, args: args))
	return parked
end 'emitIoParkCall'

// `__gt_resched()` — the cooperative yield: a park the strand runner places rather than registers. See
// `GtReschedName`.
function buildGtResched(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtReschedName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 0, paramTypes: StdTypeArray.create())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(0)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doneBlock = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	entry = emitCurrentGtSchedEvent(module, func: func, block: entry, eventType: DsEvSchedYield, ids: ids)
	emitGtPark(module, ids: ids, cur: entry, kind: GtParkKind.yielded.rawValue, arg: emitConst(module, block: entry, value: GtParkArgUnused, ids: ids), cont: doneBlock)
	doneBlock = emitCurrentGtSchedEvent(module, func: func, block: doneBlock, eventType: DsEvSchedResume, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(doneBlock, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtResched'

// `__gt_preempt(gt)` — see `GtPreemptName`. `gt` is the running thread, stopped at a prologue guard that found
// `GtStackGuardPreempt` in its guard word.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT RUNS ON THE THREAD's OWN STACK, BELOW A FRAME THAT DOES NOT EXIST YET**, so its whole
// call tree — `__gt_guard_tripped`'s preempt arm, this function and the park's switch — rides the
// guard margin the way every un-guarded runtime frame does. Each native backend adds that tree up
// from the frames it laid out and refuses the build if it outgrows the margin
// (`UnguardedChains.requireUnguardedChainsFitTheGuardMargin`, E5004).
//
// ⭐ **ONLY A FUNCTION-ENTRY GUARD REACHES IT** (`GtGuardSite.functionEntry`), and it still asks
// Go's `newstack` question of `m.locks`: a thread holding a runtime lock returns without parking,
// because a park there might never end.
//
// The guard word is restored before anything else, so the request is answered once, parked or
// declined; the count is stepped on the processor this thread is leaving.
function buildGtPreempt(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtPreemptName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var park = module.addBlock(func, label: b"preemptpark", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var declined = module.addBlock(func, label: b"preemptdeclined", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var resumed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"preemptresumed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let gt = 0

	emitGtRestoreStackGuard(module, block: entry, gt: gt, target: target, ids: ids)
	let locksHeld = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: emitSchedLockDepthAddr(module, block: entry, ids: ids), offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: locksHeld, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: park, whenFalse: declined, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(declined, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	emitSchedCountPreemption(module, block: park, ids: ids)
	emitGtPark(module, ids: ids, cur: park, kind: GtParkKind.preempt.rawValue, arg: emitConst(module, block: park, value: GtParkArgUnused, ids: ids), cont: resumed)
	module.setTerminator(resumed, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtPreempt'

// `__gt_process_run(strPtr) -> code` — spawn the Windows child named by the command-line String, wait for it to
// exit (yielding the current GT while it runs), and return its scalar exit code. THE subprocess mid-body yield.
// The command line is BORROWED: `strPtr` is the 56-byte String record, whose `buffer`/`length` this reads exactly
// as `__print_string` does; ownership stays with the caller.
//
// ⭐ **A THROWING FUNCTION** — its `throws RunProcessError` clause makes the x64/wasm backends give it
// the dual-register `errorReturn` ABI (exit code in R8, error flag in R10), the same shape a throwing array
// accessor uses. Every exit is an `errorReturn`: the reap path returns `(code, NoErrorFlag)`, a
// `CreateProcessW` failure returns `(0, RunProcessSpawnFailedFlag)`. The front end recognizes `__gt_process_run` as a
// throwing runtime callee (`isThrowingGtRuntimeCallee`), so a `runProcess` call must be a `tryCall`.
function buildGtProcessRun(module StdModule, commandShape SubprocessCommandShape)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(GtProcessRunName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), throwsTypeName: runProcessErrorThrows())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let strPtr = 0 as ValueId

	// cmdlinePtr = strPtr.buffer ; cmdlineLen = strPtr.length — borrowed, exactly as `__print_string` reads them.
	let cmdlinePtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(cmdlinePtr, addrId: strPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let cmdlineLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(cmdlineLen, addrId: strPtr, offset: StringLengthOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// ⭐ **THE PRE-SPAWN SCRATCH REGIONS, PER CALL.** STARTUPINFOW, PROCESS_INFORMATION, the MUTABLE
	// NUL-terminated command line (CreateProcessW may write to `lpCommandLine`, and the `.rdata` String blob is
	// neither writable nor NUL-terminated) and, under the vector shape, the command built from it. All are dead
	// by `afterCopy`, which is where they are released — so nothing here survives the park, and two concurrent
	// `__gt_process_run` calls share nothing.
	//
	// ⭐ **AND NO RE-ZEROING STORES, BECAUSE A FRESH SLOT ARRIVES ZEROED.** `startup.cb` is the only
	// field CreateProcessW reads that is not zero; `procInfo.hProcess` is the spawn-failure sentinel and must be
	// 0 going in; `cmBuf[cmdlineLen]` is the command line's terminator. A REUSED buffer arrives holding the
	// previous call's bytes and would need each of those written again — a fresh slot does
	// not.
	let startupSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(startupSizeConst, value: StartupInfoSize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let startupPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(startupPtr, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(startupSizeConst)))
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(startupPtr, offset: StartupInfoCbOffset, valueId: startupSizeConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let procInfoSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(procInfoSizeConst, value: ProcInfoSize, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let procInfoPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(procInfoPtr, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(procInfoSizeConst)))
	let oneConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(oneConst, value: TrailingNulBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let cmBufSize = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.binOp(cmBufSize, lhs: cmdlineLen, rhs: oneConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let cmBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(cmBuf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(cmBufSize)))

	// ⭐ **THE COPY IS `emitForwardCopy`, NOT A THIRD HAND-ROLLED BYTE LOOP.** This function and
	// `__gt_io_read` each carried their own header+body copy blocks, byte for byte the loop
	// `ManagedMemoryRuntime.emitForwardCopy` already exports and that `__gt_timer_add` two hundred lines up
	// already calls. Three spellings of one loop is three places a bound can be got wrong.
	var afterCopy = emitForwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: entry, dest: cmBuf, src: cmdlinePtr, byteCount: cmdlineLen, ids: ids)

	var spawnFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnfailed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spawnOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var waitChild = module.addBlock(func, label: b"waitchild", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var renounced = module.addBlock(func, label: b"renounced", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var reapExit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"reapexit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// afterCopy: spawn the child. On FAILURE (`hProcess == 0` — CreateProcessW leaves the PROCESS_INFORMATION
	// untouched, and this call's is a fresh, zeroed slot) → `spawnFailed` (throw, never park on a null handle).
	// On success → `spawnOk`.
	// `runProcess(cmd)` configures nothing: NULL `lpCurrentDirectory` inherits the parent's working directory
	// and 0 `dwCreationFlags` asks for no special creation. `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon`'s `Configuration` is where
	// a caller says anything else.
	let spawnDefaultsConst = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: 0, ids: ids)

	// `cmBuf` is a whole COMMAND LINE the caller wrote, so on a lane whose primitive takes a vector it
	// becomes `/bin/sh -c <line>` — see `SubprocessRuntime.emitSubpCommandFromLine`, which under the
	// command-line shape only widens it for a wide host.
	let command = emitSubpCommandFromLine(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids, shape: commandShape, linePtr: cmBuf, encoding: module.hostText)
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.osProcessSpawn(command, cwdPtrId: spawnDefaultsConst, creationFlagsId: spawnDefaultsConst, startupPtrId: startupPtr, procInfoPtrId: procInfoPtr, envPtrId: spawnDefaultsConst))
	let hProcess = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(hProcess, addrId: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoHProcessOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let hThread = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(hThread, addrId: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoHThreadOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// ⚠ **BOTH HANDLES COME OUT BEFORE THE RECORD GOES BACK, AND THAT IS WHY THE RELEASE IS HERE RATHER THAN
	// ON EACH ARM.** CreateProcessW has read the STARTUPINFOW and the command and written the
	// PROCESS_INFORMATION, so every region is dead at this point on EVERY path below — the throw and the
	// park alike. Freeing once, before the branch, is what keeps both arms from needing their own copy of it,
	// and `hThread` is read here for the same reason (on the failure path it is the slot's zero, which that arm
	// never looks at).
	let runTreeObject = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(runTreeObject, addrId: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoTreeObjectOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitSubpReleaseCommandFromLine(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids, shape: commandShape, command: command, encoding: module.hostText)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: afterCopy, ptr: cmBuf, ids: ids)
	emitSubpFreeLaunchRecords(module, block: afterCopy, startupPtr: startupPtr, procInfoPtr: procInfoPtr, ids: ids)
	afterCopy = emitSubpCloseTreeObject(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterCopy, treeObject: runTreeObject)
	let spawnZeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.const(spawnZeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let spawnFailedCmp = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.cmp(spawnFailedCmp, lhs: hProcess, rhs: spawnZeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.condBranch(spawnFailedCmp, thenBlockId: spawnFailed.id, elseBlockId: spawnOk.id))

	// spawnFailed: the command names no runnable executable — THROW `RunProcessError.spawnFailed` rather
	// than park on a null handle. The primary register carries a `0` sentinel the catcher ignores (the flag is
	// non-zero), and the flag is `RunProcessSpawnFailedFlag`.
	let spawnFailSentinel = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnFailed, op: StdOp.const(spawnFailSentinel, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let spawnFailFlag = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnFailed, op: StdOp.const(spawnFailFlag, value: RunProcessSpawnFailedFlag, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(spawnFailed, op: StdOp.errorReturn(spawnFailSentinel, errorFlagId: spawnFailFlag))

	// spawnOk: close the (unneeded) thread handle, then make the child a SOURCE ON THE POLLER and park on it.
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(hThread))
	let child = emitNetpollAcquireChildSource(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: spawnOk, hProcess: hProcess, cont: waitChild, gone: reapExit)

	// waitChild: suspend on the source's READ direction and resume when the child's exit lands there. The
	// registration is given back on every road out, because the source is a descriptor (or, on a completion
	// port, a key) this call owns for exactly as long as the wait — which is why the close is appended AFTER
	// the branch is set and still runs BEFORE it: a block's body ops all run ahead of its terminator, so one
	// copy serves both roads out.
	let childGen = emitNetpollPdOpBegin(module, block: waitChild, source: child.source, ids: ids)
	emitGtWaitReadable(module, cur: waitChild, source: child.source, gen: childGen, ready: reapExit, renounced: renounced, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(waitChild, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollChildCloseName, args: twoArgs(child.source, second: child.token)))

	// ⭐⭐ **A WAIT THAT ENDED ANY OTHER WAY WAS RENOUNCED, AND THE CHILD IS ABANDONED RATHER THAN KILLED.**
	// Only `__np_pd_drop` can end this wait without readiness — no deadline is ever armed on a child — so the
	// promise this coroutine belongs to has been dropped and nothing can observe the answer. The child runs to
	// completion on its own: reading its code would mean blocking the machine on a process nobody is waiting
	// for.
	let renouncedCode = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(renounced, op: StdOp.const(renouncedCode, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(renounced, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(hProcess))
	let renouncedNoError = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(renounced, op: StdOp.const(renouncedNoError, value: NoErrorFlag, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(renounced, op: StdOp.errorReturn(renouncedCode, errorFlagId: renouncedNoError))

	// reapExit: the child has exited. Read its code and close the handle.
	// ⭐ **THE EXIT-CODE SLOT IS TAKEN HERE, AFTER THE PARK, AND GIVEN BACK BEFORE THE RETURN** — so no
	// scratch of this call's is alive while the green thread is suspended, and the slot cannot be shared with
	// another call's reap. THE ZEROING IS WHY THE i64 READ IS CLEAN: GetExitCodeProcess writes only the low
	// DWORD (4 bytes) of the 8-byte slot, so the high DWORD must already be zero for a full-word load to yield
	// exactly this child's code (a full-word load is the only wide memory form — a u32 read would need an `as`
	// cast the runtime builder cannot spell). A fresh slab slot supplies that.
	let codeSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reapExit, op: StdOp.const(codeSizeConst, value: ExitCodeSlotBytes, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let codePtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reapExit, op: StdOp.call(codePtr, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(codeSizeConst)))
	module.appendOp(reapExit, op: StdOp.osProcessExitCode(hProcess, codePtrId: codePtr))
	let code = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reapExit, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(code, addrId: codePtr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitSlabFree(module, block: reapExit, ptr: codePtr, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(reapExit, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(hProcess))

	// A THROWING function's successful exit still writes BOTH registers — the child's exit code in the
	// primary, `NoErrorFlag` in the flag — so the `try` caller reads flag 0 and takes the success path.
	let reapNoError = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(reapExit, op: StdOp.const(reapNoError, value: NoErrorFlag, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(reapExit, op: StdOp.errorReturn(code, errorFlagId: reapNoError))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtProcessRun'

// Store the LOW byte of wide char `wcharIndex` in `bufPtr` to `asciiCode` (the high byte stays the slab-zero, so a
// wide char with an ASCII low byte results). The pipe-name buffer is freshly slab-zeroed, so only the non-zero low
// bytes are written and the wide NUL after the last digit is automatic.
function emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, bufPtr ValueId, wcharIndex PipeWcharIndex, asciiCode FullRangeInt)
	let offConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(offConst, value: (wcharIndex * WideCharBytes) as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let slotAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.binOp(slotAddr, lhs: bufPtr, rhs: offConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let chConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.const(chConst, value: asciiCode, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(slotAddr, offset: 0, valueId: chConst, valueType: StdType.u8))
end 'emitStoreWideCharLowByte'

// Fill the freshly slab-zeroed wide `bufPtr` with `\\.\pipe\mx<uniquifier-hex>` (a valid Win32 named-pipe name),
// then branch to `cont`. `uniquifier = (pid << 32) | (seq & 0xFFFFFFFF)`, where `seq` is a monotonic per-process
// counter (`__io_pipe_seq`, stepped here through the atomic `emitGlobalAccumulate`) and `pid` is
// GetCurrentProcessId — so two CONCURRENT reads (different seq) and two SEQUENTIAL reads (same recycled GT struct,
// different seq) and two separate PROCESSES (different pid) all get distinct names. The 16 hex digits are written
// MSB-first with a small loop.
function buildPipeName(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, startBlock IrBlock, bufPtr ValueId, cont IrBlock)
	var hexHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pnhexhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var hexBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pnhexbody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var digitArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pndigit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var letterArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pnletter", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var storeArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pnstore", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// seq = (*__io_pipe_seq)++ ; uniquifier = (pid << 32) | (seq & 0xFFFFFFFF).
	let seqCredit = emitConst(module, block: startBlock, value: OneMore, ids: ids)
	let seq = emitGlobalAccumulate(module, block: startBlock, label: IoPipeSeqLabel, delta: seqCredit, multiM: true, ids: ids)
	let pid = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(startBlock, op: StdOp.osGetPid(pid))
	let pidShiftConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(startBlock, op: StdOp.const(pidShiftConst, value: PidShiftBits as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let pidHi = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(startBlock, op: StdOp.binOp(pidHi, lhs: pid, rhs: pidShiftConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let seqMaskConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(startBlock, op: StdOp.const(seqMaskConst, value: PipeSeqMask, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let seqLo = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(startBlock, op: StdOp.binOp(seqLo, lhs: seq, rhs: seqMaskConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let uniquifier = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(startBlock, op: StdOp.binOp(uniquifier, lhs: pidHi, rhs: seqLo, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, operandType: StdType.i64))

	// The prefix `\\.\pipe\mx` — 11 wide chars, low bytes only (the buffer is zeroed).
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 0, asciiCode: 92)
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 1, asciiCode: 92)
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 2, asciiCode: 46)
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 3, asciiCode: 92)
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 4, asciiCode: 112)
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 5, asciiCode: 105)
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 6, asciiCode: 112)
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 7, asciiCode: 101)
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 8, asciiCode: 92)
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 9, asciiCode: 109)
	emitStoreWideCharLowByte(module, block: startBlock, ids: ids, bufPtr: bufPtr, wcharIndex: 10, asciiCode: 120)
	let zeroIdx = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(startBlock, op: StdOp.const(zeroIdx, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(startBlock, op: StdOp.branch(hexHdr.id))
	startBlock.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(hexHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zeroIdx)))

	// hexHdr(i): if i >= 16 → cont, else write hex digit i.
	let i = ids.mint()
	hexHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	let hexCountConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexHdr, op: StdOp.const(hexCountConst, value: PipeNameHexDigits as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let atEnd = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexHdr, op: StdOp.cmp(atEnd, lhs: i, rhs: hexCountConst, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(hexHdr, op: StdOp.condBranch(atEnd, thenBlockId: cont.id, elseBlockId: hexBody.id))

	// hexBody(i): nibble = (uniquifier >>logical (60 - i*4)) & 0xF ; digit vs letter?
	let nibbleBitsConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexBody, op: StdOp.const(nibbleBitsConst, value: NibbleBits as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iShift = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexBody, op: StdOp.binOp(iShift, lhs: i, rhs: nibbleBitsConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let topShiftConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexBody, op: StdOp.const(topShiftConst, value: TopNibbleShift as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let shift = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexBody, op: StdOp.binOp(shift, lhs: topShiftConst, rhs: iShift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let shifted = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexBody, op: StdOp.binOp(shifted, lhs: uniquifier, rhs: shift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let maskConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexBody, op: StdOp.const(maskConst, value: NibbleMask as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let nibble = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexBody, op: StdOp.binOp(nibble, lhs: shifted, rhs: maskConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let alphaThreshold = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexBody, op: StdOp.const(alphaThreshold, value: HexAlphaThreshold, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isDigit = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(hexBody, op: StdOp.cmp(isDigit, lhs: nibble, rhs: alphaThreshold, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, operandType: StdType.i64))

	// `i` (hexHdr's loop-carried block arg) DOMINATES hexBody/digitArm/letterArm/storeArm, so it is read directly
	// there — only `ch` (defined in one of the two arms) is a merge that needs a storeArm block arg.
	module.setTerminator(hexBody, op: StdOp.condBranch(isDigit, thenBlockId: digitArm.id, elseBlockId: letterArm.id))
	hexBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(digitArm.id, argIds: argsOf(nibble)))
	hexBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(letterArm.id, argIds: argsOf(nibble)))

	// digitArm(nibble): ch = nibble + '0' → storeArm(ch).
	let dNibble = ids.mint()
	digitArm.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(dNibble, argType: StdType.i64))
	let digitZero = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(digitArm, op: StdOp.const(digitZero, value: HexDigitZero, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let digitCh = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(digitArm, op: StdOp.binOp(digitCh, lhs: dNibble, rhs: digitZero, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(digitArm, op: StdOp.branch(storeArm.id))
	digitArm.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(storeArm.id, argIds: argsOf(digitCh)))

	// letterArm(nibble): ch = nibble + ('a' - 10) → storeArm(ch).
	let lNibble = ids.mint()
	letterArm.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(lNibble, argType: StdType.i64))
	let letterBase = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(letterArm, op: StdOp.const(letterBase, value: HexDigitLetterBase, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let letterCh = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(letterArm, op: StdOp.binOp(letterCh, lhs: lNibble, rhs: letterBase, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(letterArm, op: StdOp.branch(storeArm.id))
	letterArm.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(storeArm.id, argIds: argsOf(letterCh)))

	// storeArm(ch): buf[(PipeNamePrefixWchars + i)*2] low byte = ch ; loop to hexHdr(i+1). `ch` merges the two arms
	// (a block arg); `i` is read directly from hexHdr's block arg (it dominates here).
	let ch = ids.mint()
	storeArm.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(ch, argType: StdType.i64))
	let prefixWcharsConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(storeArm, op: StdOp.const(prefixWcharsConst, value: PipeNamePrefixWchars as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let wcharIdx = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(storeArm, op: StdOp.binOp(wcharIdx, lhs: prefixWcharsConst, rhs: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let wideBytesConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(storeArm, op: StdOp.const(wideBytesConst, value: WideCharBytes as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let byteOff = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(storeArm, op: StdOp.binOp(byteOff, lhs: wcharIdx, rhs: wideBytesConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let slotAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(storeArm, op: StdOp.binOp(slotAddr, lhs: bufPtr, rhs: byteOff, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(storeArm, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(slotAddr, offset: 0, valueId: ch, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let oneStep = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(storeArm, op: StdOp.const(oneStep, value: 1, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let iNext = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(storeArm, op: StdOp.binOp(iNext, lhs: i, rhs: oneStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(storeArm, op: StdOp.branch(hexHdr.id))
	storeArm.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(hexHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(iNext)))
end 'buildPipeName'

// ⭐ `__gt_io_read(strPtr) -> bytesRead` — THE YIELDING OVERLAPPED PIPE READ (the `spawnReadLine`
// probe). Spawns the Windows child named by the command String with its stdout redirected to a fresh overlapped pipe,
// issues an overlapped ReadFile that YIELDS the green thread onto the poller when the read is pending,
// and returns the byte count. The command String is BORROWED (buffer/length read like `__print_string`); ownership
// stays with the caller. On any spawn/pipe failure the read returns 0 (an environment error the slice-1a probe treats
// as EOF).
//
// ⭐⭐ **EVERY REGION IT TAKES, IT GIVES BACK — ~4.2 KB PER CALL.** Per-call allocation is the only shape
// that works here, because a read YIELDS and two concurrent reads must not share scratch; without the
// release, N reads would hold N × ~4.2 KB for the process lifetime. The regions split cleanly in two:
//
//   • The rest are dead inside straight-line code — the pipe name, the SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES, the two-handle
//     out-param block, the command line (and the command a vector-shaped lane builds from it), the STARTUPINFOW
//     and the PROCESS_INFORMATION. Each is freed at the point its last reader finishes, BEFORE the branch that
//     could throw the call away, so no arm carries a copy of the release.
//   • ONE survives the park — the read scratch, which the kernel is writing into while the green thread is
//     suspended. It rides this frame and is freed after the yielding read returns, on the dropped road as
//     well: a dropped reader is renounced rather than abandoned, so it comes back here to free it.
function buildGtIoRead(module StdModule, commandShape SubprocessCommandShape)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(IoReadName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var afterName = module.addBlock(func, label: b"aftername", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let strPtr = 0 as ValueId

	let zeroConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.const(zeroConst, value: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// The wide pipe-name buffer: (prefix + hex)*2 + NUL bytes, slab-zeroed.
	let namePtr = emitSubpAllocPipeName(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	buildPipeName(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: entry, bufPtr: namePtr, cont: afterName)

	// afterName: SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES (inheritable) for the child write end; the two out-handle slots; create the pipe.
	let saPtr = emitSubpSecurityAttributes(module, block: afterName, ids: ids)

	let slotsSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterName, op: StdOp.const(slotsSizeConst, value: SubpPipeScratchBytes as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let scratch = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterName, op: StdOp.call(scratch, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(slotsSizeConst)))
	var pipeFail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pipefail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	afterName = emitSubpCreateReadPipe(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterName, namePtr: namePtr, saPtr: saPtr, scratch: scratch, whenFailed: pipeFail)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: pipeFail, ptr: saPtr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pipeFail, op: StdOp.ret(zeroConst))
	let readEnd = emitLoadField(module, block: afterName, record: scratch, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let writeEnd = emitLoadField(module, block: afterName, record: scratch, offset: SubpPipeScratchSecondWord as ByteOffset, ids: ids)

	// The pipe exists and both ends are in registers, so the three regions that described it are dead: the
	// name has been resolved to a kernel object, the SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES was read by the create, and the
	// out-param block has been emptied.
	emitSubpFreePipeScratch(module, block: afterName, name: namePtr, scratch: scratch, ids: ids)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: afterName, ptr: saPtr, ids: ids)

	// The mutable NUL-terminated cmdline copy (CreateProcessW may write to lpCommandLine; the .rdata String blob is
	// neither writable nor NUL-terminated).
	let cmdlinePtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterName, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(cmdlinePtr, addrId: strPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let cmdlineLen = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterName, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(cmdlineLen, addrId: strPtr, offset: StringLengthOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let cmBufNulConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterName, op: StdOp.const(cmBufNulConst, value: TrailingNulBytes as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let cmBufSize = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterName, op: StdOp.binOp(cmBufSize, lhs: cmdlineLen, rhs: cmBufNulConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let cmBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterName, op: StdOp.call(cmBuf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(cmBufSize)))

	// The shared byte-copy loop — see `__gt_process_run`, which reaches the same helper for the same reason.
	var afterCopy = emitForwardCopy(module, func: func, cur: afterName, dest: cmBuf, src: cmdlinePtr, byteCount: cmdlineLen, ids: ids)

	var spawnFail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnfail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spawnOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// afterCopy: build STARTUPINFOW (stdout/stderr → pipe write end) and spawn (inherit). The command line
	// needs no explicit NUL store: `cmBuf` is `cmdlineLen + TrailingNulBytes` of a freshly zeroed slot and the
	// copy loop writes exactly `cmdlineLen` bytes, so the terminator is the allocator's zero.
	let startupPtr = emitSubpAllocStartupInfo(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(startupPtr, offset: StartupInfoHStdOutputOffset, valueId: writeEnd, valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(startupPtr, offset: StartupInfoHStdErrorOffset, valueId: writeEnd, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let procInfoSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.const(procInfoSizeConst, value: ProcInfoSize as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let procInfoPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.call(procInfoPtr, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(procInfoSizeConst)))

	// The one-shot read probe configures neither cwd nor creation flags — see `__gt_process_run`'s twin note.
	let probeSpawnDefaults = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: 0, ids: ids)

	// The probe's input is a command LINE too — `__gt_process_run`'s twin note carries the reason.
	let probeCommand = emitSubpCommandFromLine(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids, shape: commandShape, linePtr: cmBuf, encoding: module.hostText)
	afterCopy = emitSubpSpawnInheriting(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterCopy, command: probeCommand, cwd: probeSpawnDefaults, creationFlags: probeSpawnDefaults, startupPtr: startupPtr, procInfoPtr: procInfoPtr, env: probeSpawnDefaults, handles: threeArgs(probeSpawnDefaults, second: writeEnd, third: writeEnd))
	let hProcess = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(hProcess, addrId: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoHProcessOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let hThread = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(hThread, addrId: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoHThreadOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))

	// CreateProcessW has read the command and the STARTUPINFOW and written the PROCESS_INFORMATION, so
	// every region is dead on BOTH arms below — released once, before the branch, exactly as
	// `__gt_process_run` does it. On the failure path `hThread` is the fresh slot's zero, which that arm never
	// reads.
	let probeTreeObject = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(probeTreeObject, addrId: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoTreeObjectOffset, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitSubpReleaseCommandFromLine(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids, shape: commandShape, command: probeCommand, encoding: module.hostText)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: afterCopy, ptr: cmBuf, ids: ids)
	afterCopy = emitSubpReleaseSpawnAttributeList(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterCopy, startupPtr: startupPtr)
	emitSubpFreeLaunchRecords(module, block: afterCopy, startupPtr: startupPtr, procInfoPtr: procInfoPtr, ids: ids)
	afterCopy = emitSubpCloseTreeObject(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterCopy, treeObject: probeTreeObject)
	let spawnFailed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.cmp(spawnFailed, lhs: hProcess, rhs: zeroConst, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, operandType: StdType.i64))
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.condBranch(spawnFailed, thenBlockId: spawnFail.id, elseBlockId: spawnOk.id))

	// spawnFail: the command names no runnable executable — close BOTH pipe ends and return 0.
	module.appendOp(spawnFail, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(readEnd))
	module.appendOp(spawnFail, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(writeEnd))
	module.setTerminator(spawnFail, op: StdOp.ret(zeroConst))

	// spawnOk: close the child thread handle, the process handle (we do not reap — we read its stdout), and the
	// PARENT's copy of the write end (so the pipe reaches EOF once the child's inherited copy closes). Then issue the
	// overlapped read against the OVERLAPPED embedded in the GT struct (zeroed first — a dirty hEvent low bit would
	// suppress the completion packet).
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(hThread))
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(hProcess))
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(writeEnd))

	// The ONE region that outlives this function's straight-line code: the read buffer the kernel writes into
	// while we are parked, with `ReadFile`'s byte-count out-slot in its first word. It rides this FRAME across
	// the park and is freed below on every road out, the renounced one included — a dropped reader resumes and
	// unwinds, so no second party ever has to reach it.
	let scratchSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.const(scratchSizeConst, value: IoReadScratchBytes as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let scratch = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.call(scratch, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(scratchSizeConst)))
	let readBufOffConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.const(readBufOffConst, value: IoReadBufferOffset as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let readBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.binOp(readBuf, lhs: scratch, rhs: readBufOffConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))
	let readBufSizeConst = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.const(readBufSizeConst, value: IoReadBufferBytes as FullRangeInt, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let bytesOutPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.binOp(bytesOutPtr, lhs: scratch, rhs: emitConst(module, block: spawnOk, value: IoReadBytesOutOffset as FullRangeInt, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, operandType: StdType.i64))

	// The yielding read — the shared `__gt_subp_yield_read`, which owns the park/resume road the streaming
	// reader also uses. It returns the byte count, 0 at a clean end of stream and `SubpReadFailedCount` on a
	// failed read. The read end joins the poller BEFORE the first attempt, so an `EAGAIN` has a record to park
	// on and a readiness that lands between the two is remembered rather than lost.
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollPipeOpenName, args: argsOf(readEnd)))
	let rawRead = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.call(rawRead, callee: SubpYieldReadName, args: fourArgs(readEnd, second: readBuf, third: readBufSizeConst, fourth: bytesOutPtr)))
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollPipeCloseName, args: argsOf(readEnd)))
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(readEnd))

	// The read is over — the pipe is closed and the kernel is done with the buffer, so it goes back.
	emitSlabFree(module, block: spawnOk, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)

	// ⭐ **THE PROBE ANSWERS A COUNT, SO A FAILED READ IS 0 HERE.** `spawnReadLine(cmd)`'s whole contract is
	// *"an environment error reads as EOF"*, and it has nowhere to carry the difference; the STREAMING API
	// does — an `__SubprocessStreamState` word per stream — which is why the shared reader distinguishes them
	// and this one site, which owns the contract, folds them back together.
	var readOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ioreadok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var readRefused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"ioreadrefused", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: spawnOk, value: rawRead, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: 0, whenTrue: readRefused, whenFalse: readOk, ids: ids)

	let noBytes = emitConst(module, block: readRefused, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(readRefused, op: StdOp.ret(noBytes))
	module.setTerminator(readOk, op: StdOp.ret(rawRead))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildGtIoRead'

// ============================================================================================
// ⭐ STREAMING SUBPROCESS runtime — a long-lived child with caller-driven stdio.
// ============================================================================================
//
// Generalizes the one-shot `__gt_io_read` probe into a handle-table API. A `__subp_table` (.data) slot points at a
// slab region of `SubpMaxHandles` fixed slots (0 until the first spawn lazily allocs it); a HANDLE packs a slot
// index with the slot's generation (`SubpHandleSlotSpan`).
// ⛔ **THE TABLE IS NOT MAIN-THREAD-ONLY.** A WORKER M runs green threads and a green thread is what
// spawns, so two things need a discipline and have one: the lazy CREATION of the region is
// published by `CAS(table, 0 -> region)`, whose loser frees its region and takes the winner's (a
// test-then-allocate-then-store is one two Ms would both win, losing a whole table and every handle
// given out of it), and a SLOT is claimed by `CAS(inUse, 0 -> claimed)` at the scan rather than by
// a test whose write landed after `CreateProcessW`. Everything INSIDE a claimed slot stays
// lock-free and GT-local, which is 1a's the rule and is what the claim makes true. A slot's OS
// handles are closed at release; its reusable buffers PERSIST across release+respawn (a spawn
// reuses a free slot's buffers), so a spawn/read/release loop is memory-bounded.
//
// ⚠⚠ **THAT PERSISTENCE IS NOT A WORKAROUND FOR A MISSING FREE; IT RESTS ON WHAT RELEASE DOES NOT DO.**
// **`__gt_subp_release` does NOT cancel or drain a parked overlapped read.** It closes the pipe handles and
// clears `inUse`, and the slot GENERATION is what makes a still-parked reader harmless when the slot is
// respawned. A reader parked at that moment has handed the KERNEL a pointer INTO `bufPtr`, so freeing the
// buffer at release would be a use-after-free rather than a reclamation. Freeing here needs the release to
// cancel the read and wait for its packet first, which is a change to what release means, not a change to
// where memory goes.
//
// Each stream (stdout/stderr) carries a growable line buffer holding unconsumed bytes; a read appends a chunk into
// it (the yielding overlapped read the streaming reader shares with the probe), the reader returns through the
// first '\n' INCLUDING it, and compacts the tail forward for the next call. EOF (a 0-byte read) is latched per
// stream. stderr is read post-exit only in the harness, so it never multiplexes against a live stdout read.
let SubpTableLabel = b"__subp_table"
let SubpMaxHandles = 64

let SubpSlotBytes = 224

// A live handle is `generation * SubpHandleSlotSpan + slotIndex`: the index in the low bits, the slot's
// `SubpSlotGeneration` above it. The generation is bumped before the handle is minted, so every live handle
// carries a generation of at least 1 and is therefore at least `SubpHandleSlotSpan`; `-1` stays the failure
// answer, and a handle kept past its release decodes to a generation the slot no longer holds. The span is a
// power of two so the decode is a mask and a shift.
let SubpHandleSlotSpan = SubpMaxHandles
let SubpHandleSlotIndexMask = SubpHandleSlotSpan - 1

// Slot fields (i64). The two stream sub-blocks are identical 5-word layouts (readEnd/bufPtr/bufCap/bufLen/eof), so
// the reader takes a stream-base offset and indexes within. The persistent buffers (bufPtr/bytesOut/writeBuf)
// survive release for reuse; the OS handles are cleared at release.
let SubpSlotInUse = 0

// `inUse` goes free -> claimed by the scan's CAS (`buildSubpSpawnCore`), claimed -> live once the
// spawn has set the slot up, and back to free at the release or a failed spawn. A claimed slot is
// one no other scan may take; only a live one passes `emitSubpRequireHandle`, so a stale handle
// cannot reach a slot a re-spawn is still setting up.
let SubpSlotFree = 0
let SubpSlotClaimed = 1
let SubpSlotLive = 2
export let SubpSlotHProcess = 8
export let SubpSlotStdinWrite = 16

// ⛔⛔ **EVERY READ OF THE WORD THIS POINTS AT IS AN `emitLoadDword`, NEVER AN `emitLoadField`.** It is one
// 8-byte scratch shared by `WriteFile`, `ReadFile`, `PeekNamedPipe` and `GetExitCodeProcess`, it is never
// zeroed between users, and each of those Win32 out-params writes only its LOW DWORD — so the upper half
// is whatever the previous user left. A full-word load answers a byte count or an exit status with
// garbage above bit 31, which no caller can detect and a narrowed `ExitInt` turns into an uncatchable
// range panic. The readers live in this file and in `SubprocessRuntime.maxon`; all of them obey this.
export let SubpSlotBytesOut = 24
export let SubpStdoutBase = 32
export let SubpStderrBase = 72
let SubpSlotWriteBuf = 112
let SubpSlotWriteCap = 120

// ⭐ **THE SLOT GENERATION — what tells a slot's current child from the one a parked reader began with.**
// `subpSpawn` bumps it when it CLAIMS a slot, so every (slot, generation) pair names one handle for all time. A
// streaming reader captures it at PARK time and re-reads it on resume: a difference means the slot was released
// and re-spawned into a DIFFERENT handle while the reader slept, so it answers its own EOF and writes nothing
// back into the new handle's buffers. Plain loads and stores under no lock: the spawn's bump and a reader's
// compare on another processor are not ordered against each other, so the compare detects a claim that has
// FINISHED and is no fence against one in flight — which is why a collect rides `SubpSlotCollector` instead.
let SubpSlotGeneration = 128

// ⭐ **THE FIVE FIELDS THE ATTACHED SPAWN ADDED.** The streaming half needed none of them: it never reports a
// pid, never queues a payload for the child's stdin and never caps what it collects, because its caller does
// all three by hand. `subprocessGetPid`, `StdinKind.bytes` and `OutputDestination.collect(limit)` are three
// promises of the fourteen-argument contract, and each is a fact about the HANDLE rather than about a call.
//
// `stdinPend` is a POINTER into the caller's payload and `SubpSlotStdinRemaining` the count of
// its bytes still to push; the drain loop advances the one and shrinks the other until the count
// is 0, so a NUL in the payload is pushed like any other byte. A pointer of 0 means "nothing
// queued", which is what every non-`bytes` stdin kind leaves it. The two limits are capture
// ceilings in bytes, 0 meaning uncapped.
export let SubpSlotPid = 136 as ByteOffset
export let SubpSlotStdinPend = 144 as ByteOffset
export let SubpSlotOutLimit = 152 as ByteOffset
export let SubpSlotErrLimit = 160 as ByteOffset

// The wall-clock reading (in milliseconds) taken when `waitCollect` begins, so the result struct can report
// how long the child ran. It is a SPAWN-to-WAIT span rather than a spawn-to-exit one because the corpus asks
// for `durationMs` off the WAIT and a handle may be spawned long before anyone waits on it.
export let SubpSlotWaitStartMs = 168 as ByteOffset

// ⭐⭐ **WHEN `delayed`'s PAYLOAD MAY BE PUSHED — a three-valued word.** `0` is "push now": the slab's zero,
// which every kind but `delayed` leaves, so `emitSubpPushStdin`'s guard is a word test rather than a kind
// test repeated a pass later (the shape `SubpSlotStdinPend` and `SubpSlotStdinWrite` have, for the same
// reason). `SubpStdinFeedUnarmed` is what the spawn stores for `delayed`: hold, with no clock running. A
// positive value is a tick-count deadline, and `emitSubpArmDelayedFeed` stores one the pass that collects
// the child's FIRST STDOUT BYTE — the feed is measured from the moment the child is observably running, so
// a child slow to reach its first print is fed no earlier for it. A child that never writes to stdout is
// never fed, which is why the stdlib refuses `delayed` without a collected stdout.
//
// The feed is a deadline rather than a sleep because it comes from the drain loop: `__gt_subp_wait_collect`
// pushes one chunk per pass between drains, and the loop's own idle wait — which never holds a machine — is
// what makes the waiting free.
//
// ⚠ Negative is the unarmed spelling because `osTickCountMs` is non-negative on every lane.
export let SubpSlotStdinFeedAt = 176 as ByteOffset
let SubpStdinFeedUnarmed = -1

// What a deadline's kill is aimed at, and it always names something terminate-able: the child's job object
// where the lane has one, the child's own process handle where it has not (a POSIX wait object, whose pid is
// also its process-group id). `osProcessTerminate` takes this word and nothing else.
export let SubpSlotProcessTree = 184 as ByteOffset

export let SubpSlotStdinRemaining = 200 as ByteOffset

// Who may touch the stdin write end, and every move is a CAS. `pushing` is the collect writing one
// chunk; `closeRequested` is a close that found a push in flight, which the pusher carries out on
// its way out; `closing` is the one closer, which stores `idle` once the end is closed and zeroed.
// So the end is closed exactly once, and never under a write.
let SubpSlotStdinState = 208
export let SubpStdinIdle = 0 as FullRangeInt
export let SubpStdinPushing = 1 as FullRangeInt
let SubpStdinCloseRequested = 2
let SubpStdinClosing = 3

// The tree object this slot OWNS and must close at release, which is zero exactly when
// `SubpSlotProcessTree` is the process handle the release already closes. Two words rather than
// one, so the release cannot double-close.
let SubpSlotOwnedTreeObject = 192

// ⛔⛔ **THE COLLECT INTERLOCK — the one word a collect and a release contend for, and the last word of the
// slot.** Nothing else serialises `__gt_subp_wait_collect` against `__gt_subp_release`: the release closes the
// pipes, zeroes the handles and clears `inUse` under no lock, so a collect on another processor can pass any
// test of those words and then read a zeroed handle into a wait, a kill or an exit read. The word
// is taken by CAS instead: a collect takes `free -> held` at entry and stores `free` at every exit, a release
// takes `free -> released` for good, and either party finding the word taken stops at
// `RuntimeAbort.subpCollectSlotReleased`. The spawn's claim stores `free`, so a reused slot is collectable
// again.
let SubpSlotCollector = 216
let SubpCollectorFree = 0
export let SubpCollectorHeld = 1 as FullRangeInt
let SubpCollectorReleased = 2
export let SubpStreamReadEnd = 0
let SubpStreamBufPtr = 8
let SubpStreamBufCap = 16
let SubpStreamBufLen = 24

// ⭐ **THE STREAM'S END-STATE, AS AN `__SubprocessStreamState` ORDINAL** — `open` while bytes may still
// arrive, and whichever of `atEof`/`readFailed` the read that stopped the stream latched. It is the fact a
// reader's BYTES cannot carry: an empty answer is a clean end of stream and a refusal alike, and
// `__gt_subp_out_state`/`__gt_subp_err_state` is what separates them for `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon`.
// `noSuchChild` is never stored here — the query answers that from its own handle guard.
//
// ⚠ The ordinals are the CORPUS declaration's, threaded in as an `EnumLayout` rather than transcribed; the
// readers stop on "not `open`" rather than on "nonzero" so that this file states no ordinal at all.
let SubpStreamStateWord = 32
export let SubpReadChunkBytes = 4096
let SubpInitialLineBufBytes = 4096
export let SubpMaxLineBytes = 1048576  // 1 MiB per-line cap — a newline-less child cannot grow the buffer without bound
let SubpBytesOutBytes = 8

// The out-param block one pipe creation fills, reused across the 3 pipes: the read end, the write end,
// and — for the inbound form alone — the host's answer to whether an inline read stays off the completion port.
let SubpPipeScratchBytes = 24

// The SECOND and THIRD of those words. Named because `scratch + 8` at a pipe creation says nothing about which
// end of the pipe is being read back out of it.
let SubpPipeScratchSecondWord = 8
let SubpPipeScratchSilencedWord = 16

// The 3 CHILD-side pipe ends are parked in a slab region across pipe creation so they do not occupy registers across
// the (register-hungry) pipe-name-formatting loops — the "keep values off the working set" discipline a hand-built
// runtime function must observe (its values are span-less, so a register-pressure E5001 has no user code to blame).
// ⚠ **SIX WORDS, NOT THREE, AND THE SECOND THREE ARE A CORRECTNESS OBLIGATION.** The first three are the
// handles the STARTUPINFO names; the second three are the handles this spawn OWNS and must close once
// `CreateProcessW` has duplicated them into the child. They differ in exactly one case — `inherit`, where the
// handle is the PARENT's own standard stream and closing it would close the parent's stdout — and
// `emitSubpNonPipeChildHandle` writes 0 into the owned half there. See its header.
// The seventh word, used only when the host is wide, holds a failed redirect open's UTF-16 path,
// which `streamFail` frees after it has recorded the open's error.
let SubpChildScratchBytes = 56
let SubpChildFailedPathOff = 48

// STARTUPINFOEXW is STARTUPINFOW plus `lpAttributeList` @0x68. The two cells after it are the
// spawn's own, not Win32's: the attribute block and the list's size, which
// `emitSubpReleaseSpawnAttributeList` reads back, a size of 0 meaning the list was never
// initialised.
let StartupInfoExSize = 0x70
let StartupInfoAttributeListOffset = 0x68
let SubpAttributeBlockCell = StartupInfoExSize
let SubpAttributeSizeCell = StartupInfoExSize + 8
let SubpAttributeCellsBytes = 16
let ExtendedStartupInfoPresent = 0x80000

// The attribute block is a count word and up to three handles, then the attribute list. The handle
// array stays in the block until the list is deleted: `UpdateProcThreadAttribute` keeps a pointer
// to it rather than a copy, and `CreateProcessW` reads it there.
let SubpAttributeBlockHeaderBytes = 32
let SubpAttributeHandleWord = 8
let SubpChildStdinReadOff = 0
let SubpChildStdoutWriteOff = 8
let SubpChildStderrWriteOff = 16
let SubpOwnedStdinOff = 24
let SubpOwnedStdoutOff = 32
let SubpOwnedStderrOff = 40
let SubpNewlineByte = 10
export let SubpWaitInfinite = 0xFFFFFFFF as FullRangeInt

export let SubpYieldReadName = b"__gt_subp_yield_read"
export let SubpSpawnName = b"__gt_subp_spawn"

// The ONE `CreateProcessW` path. Every spelling a program can spawn a child with fills a request record and
// calls this; see `SubpReqCmdline` for why there is only one.
export let SubpSpawnCoreName = b"__gt_subp_spawn_core"
export let SubpReadLineName = b"__gt_subp_read_line"
export let SubpReadErrLineName = b"__gt_subp_read_err_line"
export let SubpReadExactName = b"__gt_subp_read_exact"

// ⭐ **THE NON-BLOCKING TWINS OF THE TWO LINE READERS.** Same body-builder, same per-handle buffer, same
// consume — the ONE difference is that a refill is preceded by a peek and a pipe with nothing ready ends
// the call rather than parking on it. A parent that must stay responsive while its child runs has no other
// way to drain a line: the blocking reader parks until one arrives, and a child that is quiet has not ended.
export let SubpTryReadLineName = b"__gt_subp_try_read_line"
export let SubpTryReadErrLineName = b"__gt_subp_try_read_err_line"
export let SubpWriteLineName = b"__gt_subp_write_line"
export let SubpCloseStdinName = b"__gt_subp_close_stdin"
export let SubpWaitName = b"__gt_subp_wait"
export let SubpReleaseName = b"__gt_subp_release"
let SubpStrFromBytesName = b"__gt_subp_str_from_bytes"

// The prefix `recordCallUsage` credits with what a reached child-process body needs, and the one
// `TargetFacilities.calleeHostFacility` maps onto `HostFacility.subprocess`, so a target that cannot lower
// it refuses the call with E3104.
let SubpCalleePrefix = b"__gt_subp_"

// ⭐ **IS THIS CALLEE A SUBPROCESS RUNTIME ENTRY?** THE one test, read by `scanRuntimeUsage` and by
// `SemanticCheck`, written once for the reason `isProcessRuntimeCallee` is: a band's membership question
// belongs with the band's names, not restated at each site.
export function isSubprocessRuntimeCallee(callee ByteArray) returns bool
	return bytesStartWith(callee, prefix: SubpCalleePrefix)
end 'isSubprocessRuntimeCallee'

// The `.data` slots the streaming runtime owns, each laid out where a surviving body names it
// (`DataReach.walked`). Just the table pointer and the last error; the table itself and every buffer are
// `__slab_alloc`'d lazily.
export function subpRuntimeGlobals() returns DataSectionEntryArray
	var out = DataSectionEntryArray.create()

	// The table POINTER is published once, by a CAS from 0, read with an acquire, and never
	// republished or freed; the per-SLOT claim is the CAS in `buildSubpSpawnCore`.
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SubpTableLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.publishedAtomically, reach: DataReach.walked)

	// ⚠ **THE ONE SLOT WHOSE *USEFULNESS* DEGRADES UNDER MULTI-M WHERE ITS SAFETY DOES NOT, AND THAT
	// DISTINCTION IS THE WHOLE OF WHY IT IS CLASSIFIED RATHER THAN FIXED.** It is one aligned machine word
	// written whole and read whole, so nothing tears and every value a reader can observe is a real Win32
	// error code with the step it failed in; what stops being exact is which failure "the LAST error" names, because two Ms failing at
	// once make one of them the loser. Its contract is already process-wide rather than per-caller
	// (`__Builtins.subprocessLastErrorMessage()` names no operation), so no promise breaks — the message can
	// simply be the other failure's. Making it exact needs per-GREEN-THREAD storage, since a green thread may
	// migrate between the failure and the read, and that is a GT-layout change.
	pushRuntimeGlobal(out, label: SubpLastErrorLabel, sharing: MultiMSharing.lastWriterWins, reach: DataReach.walked)
	return out
end 'subpRuntimeGlobals'

// ⭐ **THE LAST WIN32 ERROR A SUBPROCESS OPERATION FAILED WITH**, which `__Builtins.subprocessLastErrorMessage()`
// renders. ONE word rather than a message buffer — see `SubprocessRuntime`'s `buildSubpLastError` for the
// argument.
export let SubpLastErrorLabel = b"__subp_last_error"

// Capture `GetLastError()` into that word. Called at every site that is about to answer a failure, and
// IMMEDIATELY after the failing OS call: nothing between the call and this reads or writes the thread's
// last-error word (the shim's TIB writes do not touch the TEB's LastError), which is the same adjacency
// `osLastError`'s own header states for the overlapped read.
export function emitSubpRecordLastError(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSubpRecordSpawnError(module, block: block, preparation: emitConst(module, block: block, value: SpawnPreparation.none.rawValue, ids: ids), ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpRecordLastError'

// The same capture, with the `SpawnPreparation` step it failed in above the OS code (`SpawnPreparationShift`).
function emitSubpRecordSpawnError(module StdModule, block IrBlock, preparation ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let err = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osLastError(err))
	let shift = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SpawnPreparationShift, ids: ids)
	let step = emitBin(module, block: block, a: preparation, b: shift, opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	let word = emitBin(module, block: block, a: err, b: step, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	emitSubpStoreLastErrorWord(module, block: block, word: word, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpRecordSpawnError'

// Publish a code the runtime chose itself, for a refusal no OS call made. Without it the caller would read
// whatever an earlier failure left in the word.
function emitSubpStoreLastError(module StdModule, block IrBlock, code FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSubpStoreLastErrorWord(module, block: block, word: emitConst(module, block: block, value: code, ids: ids), ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpStoreLastError'

function emitSubpStoreLastErrorWord(module StdModule, block IrBlock, word ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	let slotAddr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.globalAddr(slotAddr, label: SubpLastErrorLabel))
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: slotAddr, offset: 0, value: word)
end 'emitSubpStoreLastErrorWord'

// slot = __subp_table's region + slotIdx * SubpSlotBytes. `table` is the already-loaded region base; `slotIdx` is a
// decoded index, never a caller's handle.
function emitSubpSlotAddr(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, table ValueId, slotIdx ValueId) returns ValueId
	let strideConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SubpSlotBytes as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let off = emitBin(module, block: block, a: slotIdx, b: strideConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: table, b: off, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpSlotAddr'

// ⭐⭐ **THE HANDLE CHECK EVERY ENTRY THAT TAKES A CALLER'S HANDLE OWES, AND THE RULE THAT MAKES THE LIST OF
// THEM COMPLETE.** `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon` hands these builtins whatever integer it was given, a spec can
// hand them `-1` directly, and `emitSubpSlotAddr` is bare arithmetic: `table + index * SubpSlotBytes`. An
// unguarded entry given a bad handle reads a garbage `inUse` and, believing the slot live, closes garbage
// words as HANDLEs and zeroes memory it does not own — corruption, not a wrong answer.
//
// ⭐ **THE RULE IS STRUCTURAL RATHER THAN REMEMBERED, AND IT IS GREPPABLE:**
//
//   * `emitSubpSlotAddr` (above) is the RAW arithmetic and is file-private. Its only callers are the spawn
//     CORE, which passes a slot index it produced itself, and `emitSubpSlotOf`.
//   * **`emitSubpSlotOf` is the ONE door from a CALLER'S HANDLE to a slot address**, and every FUNCTION that
//     calls it either opens with this guard or is an interior step of `__gt_subp_wait_collect`'s loop,
//     dominated by that entry's guard. The roster is `grep -n 'emitSubpSlotOf('` over `Runtime/`, grouped by
//     enclosing function; anything in it that is neither is a bug.
//   * The guard is at the ENTRY POINT, never at a wrapper, so the bare-name streaming builtins and the
//     `__Builtins.subprocess*` intrinsics reach it through the same entries with nothing to remember.
//
// Three facts, all required: the table exists, the handle's slot is `SubpSlotLive` (an acquire load, so the
// spawn's set-up of the slot is visible with it), and the slot's `SubpSlotGeneration` is the one the handle
// packs. Anything else takes `whenBad`. The generation compare is what makes a handle
// kept past its release a dead handle forever: the release frees the slot, the next spawn on it bumps the
// generation, and the old handle never matches again. A negative handle is refused ahead of the decode, so
// the mask and shift only ever see a non-negative value; a generation of 0 (a literal such as `3`) can never
// match a live slot, whose generation is at least 1. The one window this cannot cover is a reader PARKED
// across a release, which `emitSubpTopUpStream` closes by re-reading the generation on resume.
//
// It terminates `cur` and returns the block where the handle is known good.
export function emitSubpRequireHandle(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, handle ValueId, whenBad IrBlock) returns IrBlock
	var liveCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subphlive", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var sameGeneration = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subphgen", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var good = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subphok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: handle, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: 0, whenTrue: whenBad, whenFalse: liveCheck, ids: ids)

	// The table itself may never have been allocated — a program whose first subprocess call is a `wait` on a
	// handle it invented. A null table is "no handle is live", not a null dereference.
	var tableLive = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subphtbl", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let table = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: liveCheck, label: SubpTableLabel, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: liveCheck, value: table, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: whenBad, whenFalse: tableLive, ids: ids)

	let slot = emitSubpSlotOf(module, block: tableLive, ids: ids, handle: handle)
	let inUseAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: tableLive, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotInUse as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
	let inUse = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(tableLive, op: StdOp.loadAcquire(inUse, addrId: inUseAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: tableLive, value: inUse, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: SubpSlotLive, whenTrue: whenBad, whenFalse: sameGeneration, ids: ids)

	let slotGeneration = emitLoadField(module, block: sameGeneration, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotGeneration, ids: ids)
	let handleGeneration = emitSubpHandleGeneration(module, block: sameGeneration, ids: ids, handle: handle)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: sameGeneration, a: slotGeneration, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, b: handleGeneration, whenTrue: whenBad, whenFalse: good, ids: ids)

	return good
end 'emitSubpRequireHandle'

// Take `slot`'s collect interlock (`SubpSlotCollector`) from `free` to `replacement`, or stop: a taken word is a
// collect still holding the slot or a release that already took it. EXPORTED for the collect, which takes
// `held`; the release takes `released`. Terminates `cur` and answers the block where the word is this caller's.
export function emitSubpRequireCollectorTaken(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, slot ValueId, replacement FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var taken = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subpcolltaken", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var contended = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subpcollheld", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let won = emitCasField(module, block: cur, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotCollector, expected: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: SubpCollectorFree, ids: ids), replacement: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: replacement, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: cur, result: won, lost: contended, won: taken, ids: ids)
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: contended, abort: RuntimeAbort.subpCollectSlotReleased, ids: ids)

	return taken
end 'emitSubpRequireCollectorTaken'

// Leave `slot`'s collect interlock free: the collect's every exit, and the spawn's claim. EXPORTED for the former.
export function emitSubpCollectorFree(module StdModule, block IrBlock, slot ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotCollector, value: emitConst(module, block: block, value: SubpCollectorFree, ids: ids))
end 'emitSubpCollectorFree'

export function emitSubpStdinTransition(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, slot ValueId, expected FullRangeInt, replacement FullRangeInt, whenLost IrBlock) returns IrBlock
	var won = module.addBlock(func, label: b"stdinwon", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let answer = emitCasField(module, block: cur, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotStdinState, expected: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: expected, ids: ids), replacement: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: replacement, ids: ids), ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: cur, result: answer, lost: whenLost, won: won, ids: ids)

	return won
end 'emitSubpStdinTransition'

// A release store: a guard or a scan that reads the new state also sees every store the slot was
// given before it, which a plain store does not promise on arm64.
function emitSubpPublishSlotState(module StdModule, block IrBlock, slot ValueId, value FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter)
	let inUseAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotInUse as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeRelease(inUseAddr, offset: 0, valueId: emitConst(module, block: block, value: value, ids: ids), valueType: StdType.i64))
end 'emitSubpPublishSlotState'

function emitSubpStoreStdinState(module StdModule, block IrBlock, slot ValueId, value FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter)
	let stateAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: block, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotStdinState, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeRelease(stateAddr, offset: 0, valueId: emitConst(module, block: block, value: value, ids: ids), valueType: StdType.i64))
end 'emitSubpStoreStdinState'

export function emitSubpStoreStdinQueue(module StdModule, block IrBlock, slot ValueId, cursor ValueId, remaining ValueId)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotStdinPend, value: cursor)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotStdinRemaining, value: remaining)
end 'emitSubpStoreStdinQueue'

// ⭐ **THE ONE DOOR FROM A CALLER'S HANDLE TO A SLOT ADDRESS** — see the roster rule above. The address is
// freshly recomputed in `block` rather than threaded, because a pointer threaded across a loop is a live
// register across that loop, which is the pressure discipline this whole runtime is built around.
export function emitSubpSlotOf(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, handle ValueId) returns ValueId
	let table = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: block, label: SubpTableLabel, ids: ids)
	return emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: block, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: emitSubpHandleSlotIndex(module, block: block, ids: ids, handle: handle))
end 'emitSubpSlotOf'

// The shift between a handle's slot index and its generation (`SubpHandleSlotSpan`), shared by the spawn's
// encode and the entries' decode so the two cannot disagree.
function emitSubpHandleGenerationShift(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitConst(module, block: block, value: log2OfPowerOfTwo(SubpHandleSlotSpan as MachineWord) as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpHandleGenerationShift'

// The handle a slot claimed at generation `generation` names: `generation * SubpHandleSlotSpan + slotIdx`.
function emitSubpPackHandle(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, slotIdx ValueId, generation ValueId) returns ValueId
	let generationPart = emitBin(module, block: block, a: generation, b: emitSubpHandleGenerationShift(module, block: block, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shl, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: generationPart, b: slotIdx, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpPackHandle'

// The slot index a handle packs. Always within `[0, SubpMaxHandles)`, so the address it yields is inside the
// table for ANY integer — which is why the entry guard must still refuse a negative handle by sign.
function emitSubpHandleSlotIndex(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, handle ValueId) returns ValueId
	let mask = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SubpHandleSlotIndexMask as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: handle, b: mask, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpHandleSlotIndex'

// The generation a handle packs; asked only of a handle already known non-negative.
function emitSubpHandleGeneration(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, handle ValueId) returns ValueId
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: handle, b: emitSubpHandleGenerationShift(module, block: block, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.shrLogical, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpHandleGeneration'

// If `record[fieldOffset]` is 0 (a per-slot buffer never allocated on a FRESH slot), `__slab_alloc(size)` it and
// store the pointer (and `size` into `capField`); else reuse the buffer persisted from a prior use. Returns the
// continuation block. This is the per-HANDLE reuse that bounds a spawn/read/release loop's memory.
function emitEnsureSlotBuffer(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, record ValueId, fieldOffset ByteOffset, size FullRangeInt, capField ByteOffset) returns IrBlock
	var doAlloc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subpalloc", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cont = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subpallocd", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let existing = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: record, offset: fieldOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: existing, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: doAlloc, whenFalse: cont, ids: ids)

	let sizeConst = emitConst(module, block: doAlloc, value: size, ids: ids)
	let buf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAlloc, op: StdOp.call(buf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(sizeConst)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: doAlloc, record: record, offset: fieldOffset, value: buf)
	emitStoreField(module, block: doAlloc, record: record, offset: capField, value: sizeConst)
	module.setTerminator(doAlloc, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
	return cont
end 'emitEnsureSlotBuffer'

// ⭐ `__gt_subp_yield_read(readEnd, buf, cap, bytesOutPtr) -> bytesRead` — THE YIELDING READ, factored out of
// the `__gt_io_read` probe so the park/resume road has exactly ONE copy (both the probe and the streaming line
// reader call it). It answers three things and only three: a byte count, 0 at a clean end of stream, and
// `SubpReadFailedCount` for a read that failed. It does NOT close `readEnd` — the caller owns the handle's
// lifetime (the probe closes its one-shot pipe after; the streaming reader keeps its table-owned pipe).
//
// ⭐⭐⭐ **THE TWO SHAPES PARK ON DIFFERENT THINGS AND BOTH PARK ON THE POLLER.** A completion port carries the
// READ: the overlapped `ReadFile` answers `ERROR_IO_PENDING`, the operation gets a poll source of its own
// (`NetpollRuntime.__np_op_open`), and the packet the kernel delivers is what ends the wait — so the count and
// the status come off the thread's own record rather than out of a second attempt. A POSIX lane has no
// in-flight read: the descriptor is non-blocking (`NetpollRuntime.__np_pipe_open`), `EAGAIN` arrives as
// `ERROR_IO_PENDING`, and the thread parks on the DESCRIPTOR's poll record and RE-ISSUES the read when it is
// readable again.
//
// ⛔⛔ **A DROPPED READER MUST NOT WALK AWAY FROM A READ THE KERNEL IS STILL SERVING.** The OVERLAPPED is
// embedded in the green thread (`GtOffOverlapped`), so a thread that unwound and was reclaimed with its read in
// flight would be written into after it was freed. The renounced arm therefore CANCELS the read and waits for
// its completion packet: the packet is the only proof the kernel has finished with that memory, and waiting for
// it costs the dropped thread microseconds. It is also what keeps the read BUFFER a single owner's — the reader
// frees its own, on this road and on the normal one alike.
//
// ⛔⛔ **AN `EAGAIN` IS CONSUMED HERE AND NEVER ANSWERED AS A COUNT.** Every caller reads a 0 as a permanent
// end of stream (`emitSubpTopUpStream`'s `setEof`, `emitSubpDrainStream`'s `markDone`), so answering 0 for a
// merely-empty pipe would latch a stream the child is still writing to — a wrong answer that looks green on a
// fast child.
function buildSubpYieldRead(module StdModule, target Target)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SubpYieldReadName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 4, paramTypes: fourI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(4)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var attempt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"attempt", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var syncDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"syncdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var checkErr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"checkerr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var classifyErr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"classifyerr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cleanEof = module.addBlock(func, label: b"cleaneof", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var readFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readfailed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pending = module.addBlock(func, label: b"pending", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var answer = module.addBlock(func, label: b"yieldreadanswer", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 4)
	let readEnd = 0 as ValueId
	let buf = 1 as ValueId
	let cap = 2 as ValueId
	let bytesOutPtr = 3 as ValueId

	let zeroConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let selfGt = emitLoadCurrentGt(module, block: entry, ids: ids)
	let ovlOffConst = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: GtOffOverlapped as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let ovlPtr = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: selfGt, b: ovlOffConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitGtZeroOverlapped(module, block: entry, overlapped: ovlPtr, ids: ids)

	// ⛔ **THE SOURCE IS OPENED BEFORE THE READ IS ISSUED**, because a packet can be delivered the instant it is
	// — see `NetpollRuntime.NetpollOpOpenName`. The handle goes on the thread FIRST, because the open is what
	// makes `io_source` non-zero and that word is the only thing telling a dropping thread the handle is
	// readable. On a lane whose reads complete inline the DESCRIPTOR is the source and there is nothing to open.
	var source = readEnd

	match ioCompletionShape(target) 'source'
		completionPort then source = emitSubpOpenReadSource(module, block: entry, gt: selfGt, readEnd: readEnd, ids: ids)

		inlineCompletion then break
	end 'source'

	// The read begins on the source's record here, ahead of the retry ring, so the life its wait carries is the
	// one the read was issued in: a pipe end closed and reopened under a parked read ends the read rather than
	// publishing it into the next owner's word.
	let gen = emitNetpollPdOpBegin(module, block: entry, source: source, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(attempt.id))

	let readResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(attempt, op: StdOp.osReadOverlapped(readResult, handleId: readEnd, bufId: buf, capId: cap, bytesOutPtrId: bytesOutPtr, ovlId: ovlPtr))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: attempt, value: readResult, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: syncDone, whenFalse: checkErr, ids: ids)

	// A read served out of the pipe's own buffer posts NO packet, so it must not wait for one — and it gives the
	// operation's source back on its way out, so a packet that DID come would land on the next operation's
	// stamp. `emitSubpCreateReadPipe` is what makes that impossible rather than unlikely: the pipe does not
	// exist unless the host granted `FILE_SKIP_COMPLETION_PORT_ON_SUCCESS` on this handle.
	let syncBytes = emitLoadDword(module, block: syncDone, record: bytesOutPtr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(syncDone, op: StdOp.branch(answer.id))
	syncDone.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(answer.id, argIds: argsOf(syncBytes)))

	let err = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(checkErr, op: StdOp.osLastError(err))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: checkErr, value: err, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: ErrorIoPending as FullRangeInt, whenTrue: pending, whenFalse: classifyErr, ids: ids)

	match ioCompletionShape(target) 'wait'
		completionPort then emitSubpAwaitCompletion(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: pending, source: source, gen: gen, selfGt: selfGt, answer: answer, cleanEof: cleanEof, readFailed: readFailed)

		// The descriptor is merely empty. Suspend on its poll record and read again once it is readable; a wait
		// that ended any other way was RENOUNCED by a dropped promise or found the record in another life, and
		// the caller's own unwinding is what follows, so it answers a failed read rather than re-issuing on a
		// descriptor nobody wants.
		inlineCompletion then emitGtWaitReadable(module, cur: pending, source: source, gen: gen, ready: attempt, renounced: readFailed, ids: ids)
	end 'wait'

	// ⭐ **A BROKEN PIPE IS THE END OF THE STREAM; ANYTHING ELSE IS A FAILED READ.** A child that exited and
	// closed its stdout is the ONE way a `ReadFile` on a live pipe fails in normal operation, so
	// `ERROR_BROKEN_PIPE` is 0 bytes. Answering an invalid handle or an aborted operation with that same 0
	// would make `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon` hand a caller a short answer as though the child had finished.
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: classifyErr, value: err, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: ErrorBrokenPipe as FullRangeInt, whenTrue: cleanEof, whenFalse: readFailed, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(cleanEof, op: StdOp.branch(answer.id))
	cleanEof.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(answer.id, argIds: argsOf(zeroConst)))

	let failureConst = emitConst(module, block: readFailed, value: SubpReadFailedCount, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(readFailed, op: StdOp.branch(answer.id))
	readFailed.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(answer.id, argIds: argsOf(failureConst)))

	// Every road out gives the operation's poll source back, because every road out is a read the kernel has
	// finished with — the source outlives the read and nothing else.
	let count = ids.mint()
	answer.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(count, argType: StdType.i64))

	match ioCompletionShape(target) 'give'
		completionPort then emitNetpollOpClose(module, block: answer, source: source, ids: ids)

		inlineCompletion then break
	end 'give'

	module.setTerminator(answer, op: StdOp.ret(count))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpYieldRead'

// ⭐⭐ **PUBLISH THE READ'S SOURCE, THE HANDLE IT IS ON AND ITS DIRECTION, in the order a dropping thread and
// the poller's decode read them.** `io_source` is the guard and the other two are what it guards, so they are
// stored first and the open — which writes the guard — is what publishes the set.
function emitSubpOpenReadSource(module StdModule, block IrBlock, gt ValueId, readEnd ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffIoHandle, value: readEnd)
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: gt, offset: GtOffIoMode, value: emitConst(module, block: block, value: NetpollModeRead, ids: ids))
	return emitNetpollOpOpen(module, block: block, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpOpenReadSource'

// ⭐⭐ **WAIT FOR THE PACKET THAT SAYS THE KERNEL IS DONE WITH THIS READ, and sort what it carried.** Readiness
// IS the completion. `cur` is terminated and each answer reaches the block that carries it.
//
// ⛔ **NOTHING ELSE CAN END THIS WAIT, AND A WAIT THAT ENDED ANOTHER WAY IS A NAMED STOP.** A pipe read arms no
// deadline, its source is this operation's alone and nobody else releases it, and a DROPPED promise leaves the
// park exactly where it is — it cancels the read so the packet comes at once (`__gt_promise_drop`). So
// `NetpollWaitTimedOut` and `NetpollWaitStale` are both impossible, and answering a count for either would hand
// the caller bytes out of a buffer the kernel may still be writing into.
function emitSubpAwaitCompletion(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, source ValueId, gen ValueId, selfGt ValueId, answer IrBlock, cleanEof IrBlock, readFailed IrBlock)
	var completed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"completed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var completedOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"completedok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var completedErr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"completederr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var renounced = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readrenounced", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitGtWaitReadable(module, cur: cur, source: source, gen: gen, ready: completed, renounced: renounced, ids: ids)
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: renounced, abort: RuntimeAbort.netpollOverlappedWaitNotACompletion, ids: ids)

	let status = emitLoadField(module, block: completed, record: selfGt, offset: GtOffIoStatus, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: completed, value: status, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: NtStatusSuccess, whenTrue: completedOk, whenFalse: completedErr, ids: ids)

	let bytes = emitLoadField(module, block: completedOk, record: selfGt, offset: GtOffIoResult, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(completedOk, op: StdOp.branch(answer.id))
	completedOk.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(answer.id, argIds: argsOf(bytes)))

	// ⭐ **THE SAME FOLD THE SYNCHRONOUS ROAD MAKES, IN THE KERNEL'S OWN VOCABULARY.** A completion reports an
	// NTSTATUS rather than a Win32 error, and the two a child's own exit produces — the write end gone, and the
	// last byte already delivered — are the end of the stream. Everything else, a cancelled read included, is a
	// failure.
	let pipeBroken = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: completedErr, value: status, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: NtStatusPipeBroken, ids: ids)
	let atEnd = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: completedErr, value: status, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: NtStatusEndOfFile, ids: ids)
	let finished = emitBin(module, block: completedErr, a: pipeBroken, b: atEnd, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(completedErr, op: StdOp.condBranch(finished, thenBlockId: cleanEof.id, elseBlockId: readFailed.id))
end 'emitSubpAwaitCompletion'

// ⭐ **SUSPEND ON A SOURCE'S READ DIRECTION AND SORT THE TWO WAYS THE WAIT CAN END.** Readiness reaches
// `ready`; anything else reaches `renounced` — a DROPPED PROMISE, neither a child nor a pipe end arming a
// deadline, or a record that left the life `gen` names (`__np_pd_op_begin`). The child wait and the pipe read
// are the same three ops for the same reason, so they are one.
function emitGtWaitReadable(module StdModule, cur IrBlock, source ValueId, gen ValueId, ready IrBlock, renounced IrBlock, ids ValueMinter)
	let verdict = emitNetpollPdWait(module, block: cur, source: source, mode: NetpollModeRead, gen: gen, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: verdict, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: NetpollWaitReady, whenTrue: ready, whenFalse: renounced, ids: ids)
end 'emitGtWaitReadable'

// `__gt_subp_str_from_bytes(srcBuf, srcLen) -> ownedString` — a fresh OWNED inline String (record + bytes in ONE
// `__mm_alloc`, `capacity@16 = InlineBufferCapacity`, `parent@32 = 0`), independently droppable through
// `__str_decref`. The raw-bytes twin of `__str_clone` (same fused-record encoding `emitStringInterp` publishes);
// `singleByteGraphemes@48` is left 0 (the slab zeroes it) — child output is arbitrary bytes, and false is always the safe
// classification. Used by the line reader to hand a caller-droppable String back across the builtin boundary.
function buildSubpStrFromBytes(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SubpStrFromBytesName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let srcBuf = 0
	let srcLen = 1

	// `singleByteGraphemes@48` is 0 — child output is arbitrary bytes, and `not-ascii` is always the safe classification.
	// The record layout is built by the ONE shared encoder `__str_clone` also uses, so the two can never diverge.
	let notSingleByte = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let newRec = emitBuildOwnedInlineString(module, block: entry, ids: ids, srcBuf: srcBuf, srcLen: srcLen, singleByteGraphemes: notSingleByte)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.ret(newRec))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpStrFromBytes'

// The cap-free twin of `emitEnsureSlotBuffer` — for the `bytesOut` out-param slot, a fixed 8 bytes with no grow, so
// there is no capacity slot to write. Alloc-if-zero then reuse across the handle's life.
function emitEnsureSlotPtr(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, record ValueId, fieldOffset ByteOffset, size FullRangeInt) returns IrBlock
	var doAlloc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subpptr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cont = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subpptrd", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	let existing = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: record, offset: fieldOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: existing, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: doAlloc, whenFalse: cont, ids: ids)

	let sizeConst = emitConst(module, block: doAlloc, value: size, ids: ids)
	let buf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doAlloc, op: StdOp.call(buf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(sizeConst)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: doAlloc, record: record, offset: fieldOffset, value: buf)
	module.setTerminator(doAlloc, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
	return cont
end 'emitEnsureSlotPtr'

// `__slab_alloc` a fresh (prefix + hex)*2 + NUL wide-char pipe-name buffer and return its pointer — the buffer
// `buildPipeName` formats a unique `\\.\pipe\<hex>` name into. Spelled once for the probe read and the three
// streaming pipes rather than repeating the size arithmetic at each site.
function emitSubpAllocPipeName(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let nameSize = emitConst(module, block: block, value: ((PipeNamePrefixWchars + PipeNameHexDigits) * WideCharBytes + PipeNameTrailingNulBytes) as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let nameBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(nameBuf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(nameSize)))
	return nameBuf
end 'emitSubpAllocPipeName'

// The two regions one pipe's creation reads, dead once both of its ends are out of the scratch.
function emitSubpFreePipeScratch(module StdModule, block IrBlock, name ValueId, scratch ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: block, ptr: name, ids: ids)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: block, ptr: scratch, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpFreePipeScratch'

// A failed creation leaves both words 0, having closed any end it made. Nothing is closed here:
// `CloseHandle(NULL)` sets error 6 itself, and the spawn reports the creation's own error.
function emitSubpRequirePipeEnds(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, name ValueId, scratch ValueId, whenFailed IrBlock) returns IrBlock
	var broken = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subppipebad", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var made = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subppipegood", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let first = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: scratch, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let second = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: scratch, offset: SubpPipeScratchSecondWord as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
	let firstMade = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: cur, value: first, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: 0, ids: ids)
	let secondMade = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: cur, value: second, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: 0, ids: ids)
	let bothMade = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: firstMade, b: secondMade, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(bothMade, thenBlockId: made.id, elseBlockId: broken.id))

	emitSubpFreePipeScratch(module, block: broken, name: name, scratch: scratch, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(broken, op: StdOp.branch(whenFailed.id))

	return made
end 'emitSubpRequirePipeEnds'

enum SubpPipeDirection
	towardParent
	towardChild
end 'SubpPipeDirection'

let SubpPipeServerInboundMode = 0x40080001 as FullRangeInt
let SubpPipeServerOutboundMode = 0x00080002 as FullRangeInt
let SubpPipeClientWriteAccess = 0x40000000 as FullRangeInt
let SubpPipeClientReadAccess = 0x80000000 as FullRangeInt

function subpPipesAreNamed(module StdModule) returns bool
	return hostTextIsWide(module)
end 'subpPipesAreNamed'

function subpPipeServerMode(direction SubpPipeDirection) returns FullRangeInt
	return match direction 'd'
		towardParent gives SubpPipeServerInboundMode
		towardChild gives SubpPipeServerOutboundMode
	end 'd'
end 'subpPipeServerMode'

function subpPipeClientAccess(direction SubpPipeDirection) returns FullRangeInt
	return match direction 'd'
		towardParent gives SubpPipeClientWriteAccess
		towardChild gives SubpPipeClientReadAccess
	end 'd'
end 'subpPipeClientAccess'

function subpPipeServerWord(direction SubpPipeDirection) returns ByteOffset
	return match direction 'd'
		towardParent gives 0 as ByteOffset
		towardChild gives SubpPipeScratchSecondWord as ByteOffset
	end 'd'
end 'subpPipeServerWord'

function subpPipeClientWord(direction SubpPipeDirection) returns ByteOffset
	return match direction 'd'
		towardParent gives SubpPipeScratchSecondWord as ByteOffset
		towardChild gives 0 as ByteOffset
	end 'd'
end 'subpPipeClientWord'

function subpStartupInfoBytes(module StdModule) returns FullRangeInt
	if subpPipesAreNamed(module) 'extended'
		return StartupInfoExSize
	end 'extended'

	return StartupInfoSize
end 'subpStartupInfoBytes'

function emitSubpAppendListedHandle(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, region ValueId, handle ValueId, firstSeen ValueId, secondSeen ValueId) returns IrBlock
	var store = module.addBlock(func, label: b"splstore", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var skip = module.addBlock(func, label: b"splskip", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let usable = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: cur, value: handle, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, against: 0, ids: ids)
	let newToFirst = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: handle, b: firstSeen, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let newToSecond = emitCmp(module, block: cur, a: handle, b: secondSeen, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, ids: ids)
	let wanted = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: usable, b: emitBin(module, block: cur, a: newToFirst, b: newToSecond, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitAnd, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.condBranch(wanted, thenBlockId: store.id, elseBlockId: skip.id))

	let count = emitLoadField(module, block: store, record: region, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let wordBytes = emitConst(module, block: store, value: SubpAttributeHandleWord, ids: ids)
	let entry = emitBin(module, block: store, a: region, b: emitBin(module, block: store, a: emitBin(module, block: store, a: count, b: wordBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids), b: wordBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: store, record: entry, offset: 0, value: handle)
	emitStoreField(module, block: store, record: region, offset: 0, value: emitBin(module, block: store, a: count, b: emitConst(module, block: store, value: 1, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(store, op: StdOp.branch(skip.id))

	return skip
end 'emitSubpAppendListedHandle'

function emitSubpAllocStartupInfo(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let startupBytes = subpStartupInfoBytes(module)
	var recordBytes = startupBytes

	if subpPipesAreNamed(module) 'withCells'
		recordBytes = startupBytes + SubpAttributeCellsBytes
	end 'withCells'

	let startupPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(startupPtr, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(emitConst(module, block: block, value: recordBytes, ids: ids))))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(startupPtr, offset: StartupInfoCbOffset, valueId: emitConst(module, block: block, value: startupBytes, ids: ids), valueType: StdType.i64))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(startupPtr, offset: StartupInfoDwFlagsOffset, valueId: emitConst(module, block: block, value: StartfUseStdHandles as FullRangeInt, ids: ids), valueType: StdType.i64))
	return startupPtr
end 'emitSubpAllocStartupInfo'

// On x64-windows the child inherits only the handles its STARTUPINFO names, through a
// PROC_THREAD_ATTRIBUTE_HANDLE_LIST. `bInheritHandles=TRUE` alone hands it every inheritable handle
// in the process, the pipe ends of spawns running concurrently included, and a sibling's pipe then
// never reaches EOF while this child lives. With nothing to list, the child inherits nothing.
function emitSubpSpawnInheriting(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, command ValueId, cwd ValueId, creationFlags ValueId, startupPtr ValueId, procInfoPtr ValueId, env ValueId, handles ValueIdArray) returns IrBlock
	if not subpPipesAreNamed(module) 'whole'
		module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.osProcessSpawnInherit(command, cwdPtrId: cwd, creationFlagsId: creationFlags, startupPtrId: startupPtr, procInfoPtrId: procInfoPtr, envPtrId: env))
		return cur
	end 'whole'

	var sized = module.addBlock(func, label: b"splsized", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var unsized = module.addBlock(func, label: b"splunsized", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var listed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spllisted", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var updating = module.addBlock(func, label: b"splupdate", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var inheritListed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"splinherit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var inheritNothing = module.addBlock(func, label: b"splsealed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spawned = module.addBlock(func, label: b"splspawned", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let sizeCell = emitFieldAddr(module, block: cur, record: startupPtr, offset: SubpAttributeSizeCell as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
	let none = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)

	// The sizing call answers FALSE by design; only the size it writes is read.
	_ = emitNetpoll(module, block: cur, verb: NetpollVerb.spawnAttributesInit, operands: twoArgs(none, second: sizeCell), ids: ids)
	let listBytes = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: startupPtr, offset: SubpAttributeSizeCell as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
	let totalBytes = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: listBytes, b: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: SubpAttributeBlockHeaderBytes, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let block = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(cur, op: StdOp.call(block, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(totalBytes)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: startupPtr, offset: SubpAttributeBlockCell as ByteOffset, value: block)
	let list = emitSubpAttributeListOf(module, block: cur, attributeBlock: block, ids: ids)
	let initialised = emitNetpoll(module, block: cur, verb: NetpollVerb.spawnAttributesInit, operands: twoArgs(list, second: sizeCell), ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: initialised, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: unsized, whenFalse: sized, ids: ids)

	emitStoreField(module, block: unsized, record: startupPtr, offset: SubpAttributeSizeCell as ByteOffset, value: emitConst(module, block: unsized, value: 0, ids: ids))
	module.setTerminator(unsized, op: StdOp.branch(spawned.id))

	emitStoreField(module, block: sized, record: startupPtr, offset: StartupInfoAttributeListOffset as ByteOffset, value: list)
	let extendedFlags = emitBin(module, block: sized, a: creationFlags, b: emitConst(module, block: sized, value: ExtendedStartupInfoPresent, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	let noPrior = emitConst(module, block: sized, value: 0, ids: ids)
	var afterIn = emitSubpAppendListedHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: sized, region: block, handle: netpollOperand(handles, index: 0), firstSeen: noPrior, secondSeen: noPrior)
	var afterOut = emitSubpAppendListedHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterIn, region: block, handle: netpollOperand(handles, index: 1), firstSeen: netpollOperand(handles, index: 0), secondSeen: noPrior)
	var afterErr = emitSubpAppendListedHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterOut, region: block, handle: netpollOperand(handles, index: 2), firstSeen: netpollOperand(handles, index: 0), secondSeen: netpollOperand(handles, index: 1))
	module.setTerminator(afterErr, op: StdOp.branch(listed.id))

	let count = emitLoadField(module, block: listed, record: block, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: listed, value: count, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: updating, whenFalse: inheritNothing, ids: ids)

	let countU = emitLoadField(module, block: updating, record: block, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let handleBytes = emitBin(module, block: updating, a: countU, b: emitConst(module, block: updating, value: SubpAttributeHandleWord, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let handleArray = emitBin(module, block: updating, a: block, b: emitConst(module, block: updating, value: SubpAttributeHandleWord, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let updated = emitNetpoll(module, block: updating, verb: NetpollVerb.spawnAttributesInheritOnly, operands: threeArgs(list, second: handleArray, third: handleBytes), ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: updating, value: updated, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: spawned, whenFalse: inheritListed, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(inheritListed, op: StdOp.osProcessSpawnInherit(command, cwdPtrId: cwd, creationFlagsId: extendedFlags, startupPtrId: startupPtr, procInfoPtrId: procInfoPtr, envPtrId: env))
	module.setTerminator(inheritListed, op: StdOp.branch(spawned.id))

	module.appendOp(inheritNothing, op: StdOp.osProcessSpawn(command, cwdPtrId: cwd, creationFlagsId: extendedFlags, startupPtrId: startupPtr, procInfoPtrId: procInfoPtr, envPtrId: env))
	module.setTerminator(inheritNothing, op: StdOp.branch(spawned.id))

	return spawned
end 'emitSubpSpawnInheriting'

function emitSubpAttributeListOf(module StdModule, block IrBlock, attributeBlock ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: attributeBlock, b: emitConst(module, block: block, value: SubpAttributeBlockHeaderBytes, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpAttributeListOf'

function emitSubpReleaseSpawnAttributeList(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, startupPtr ValueId) returns IrBlock
	if not subpPipesAreNamed(module) 'whole'
		return cur
	end 'whole'

	var initialised = module.addBlock(func, label: b"splinitialised", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var freed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"splreleased", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let block = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: startupPtr, offset: SubpAttributeBlockCell as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
	let listBytes = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: startupPtr, offset: SubpAttributeSizeCell as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: listBytes, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: initialised, whenFalse: freed, ids: ids)

	let list = emitSubpAttributeListOf(module, block: initialised, attributeBlock: block, ids: ids)
	_ = emitNetpoll(module, block: initialised, verb: NetpollVerb.spawnAttributesDelete, operands: argsOf(list), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(initialised, op: StdOp.branch(freed.id))

	emitSlabFree(module, block: freed, ptr: block, ids: ids)
	return freed
end 'emitSubpReleaseSpawnAttributeList'

// The server end is the parent's: made with no SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES, it is never inheritable.
function emitSubpOpenNamedPipePair(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, name ValueId, saPtr ValueId, scratch ValueId, direction SubpPipeDirection) returns IrBlock
	var serverMade = module.addBlock(func, label: b"npserverok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var clientMade = module.addBlock(func, label: b"npclientok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var clientFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"npclientbad", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"npfailed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var made = module.addBlock(func, label: b"npmade", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let server = emitNetpoll(module, block: cur, verb: NetpollVerb.openPipeServer, operands: twoArgs(name, second: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: subpPipeServerMode(direction), ids: ids)), ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: server, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: -1, whenTrue: failed, whenFalse: serverMade, ids: ids)

	let client = emitNetpoll(module, block: serverMade, verb: NetpollVerb.openPipeClient, operands: threeArgs(name, second: emitConst(module, block: serverMade, value: subpPipeClientAccess(direction), ids: ids), third: saPtr), ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: serverMade, value: client, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: -1, whenTrue: clientFailed, whenFalse: clientMade, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(clientFailed, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(server))
	module.setTerminator(clientFailed, op: StdOp.branch(failed.id))

	var stored = clientMade

	match direction 'joins'
		towardParent then stored = emitSubpJoinReadPipeToPort(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: stored, server: server, client: client, scratch: scratch, whenFailed: failed)
		towardChild then break
	end 'joins'

	emitStoreField(module, block: stored, record: scratch, offset: subpPipeServerWord(direction), value: server)
	emitStoreField(module, block: stored, record: scratch, offset: subpPipeClientWord(direction), value: client)
	module.setTerminator(stored, op: StdOp.branch(made.id))

	let nothing = emitConst(module, block: failed, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: failed, record: scratch, offset: 0, value: nothing)
	emitStoreField(module, block: failed, record: scratch, offset: SubpPipeScratchSecondWord as ByteOffset, value: nothing)
	module.setTerminator(failed, op: StdOp.branch(made.id))

	return made
end 'emitSubpOpenNamedPipePair'

function emitSubpJoinReadPipeToPort(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, server ValueId, client ValueId, scratch ValueId, whenFailed IrBlock) returns IrBlock
	var joined = module.addBlock(func, label: b"npjoined", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var joinFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"npjoinbad", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let poller = emitNetpollPollerHandle(module, block: cur, ids: ids)
	let port = emitNetpoll(module, block: cur, verb: NetpollVerb.armSource, operands: twoArgs(poller, second: server), ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: port, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: joinFailed, whenFalse: joined, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(joinFailed, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(server))
	module.appendOp(joinFailed, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(client))
	module.setTerminator(joinFailed, op: StdOp.branch(whenFailed.id))

	let silenced = emitNetpoll(module, block: joined, verb: NetpollVerb.skipCompletionOnSuccess, operands: argsOf(server), ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: joined, record: scratch, offset: SubpPipeScratchSilencedWord as ByteOffset, value: silenced)

	return joined
end 'emitSubpJoinReadPipeToPort'

// Create ONE read pipe (overlapped on x64-windows) into `scratch`'s three words — the read end @0,
// the child's write end @8 — and reach the returned block with both in place. Terminates `cur`.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE THIRD WORD IS A PROMISE THE HOST HAS TO KEEP, AND A HOST THAT WILL NOT ENDS THE PROCESS HERE.**
// `__gt_subp_yield_read` answers a read the pipe served out of its own buffer without waiting for a packet, and
// gives the operation's poll source back on its way out. That is sound only while an inline completion posts NO
// packet: one that did would arrive under the reading thread's OVERLAPPED after the source was released, and the
// NEXT operation's `__np_op_wake` would take it — clearing that operation's stamp and storing this read's byte
// count and status as its answer. Nothing downstream can notice, so the stop is at the creation. The word is
// SEEDED true and only a lane with a completion port can falsify it.
function emitSubpCreateReadPipe(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, namePtr ValueId, saPtr ValueId, scratch ValueId, whenFailed IrBlock) returns IrBlock
	var stillPosts = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subppipeloud", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var silencedOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subppipemade", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let writePtr = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: scratch, b: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: SubpPipeScratchSecondWord, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let silencedPtr = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: scratch, b: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: SubpPipeScratchSilencedWord, ids: ids), opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: silencedPtr, offset: 0, value: emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 1, ids: ids))
	var created = cur

	if subpPipesAreNamed(module) 'named'
		created = emitSubpOpenNamedPipePair(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: created, name: namePtr, saPtr: saPtr, scratch: scratch, direction: SubpPipeDirection.towardParent)
	end 'named' else 'anonymous'
		let poller = emitNetpollPollerHandle(module, block: created, ids: ids)
		module.appendOp(created, op: StdOp.osPipeCreate(namePtr, saPtrId: saPtr, iocpId: poller, readPtrId: scratch, writePtrId: writePtr, syncSilencedPtrId: silencedPtr))
	end 'anonymous'

	var made = emitSubpRequirePipeEnds(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: created, name: namePtr, scratch: scratch, whenFailed: whenFailed)

	let silenced = emitLoadField(module, block: made, record: scratch, offset: SubpPipeScratchSilencedWord as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: made, value: silenced, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: silencedOk, whenFalse: stillPosts, ids: ids)
	emitRuntimeAbort(module, func: func, block: stillPosts, abort: RuntimeAbort.overlappedPipeStillPostsOnSuccess, ids: ids)

	return silencedOk
end 'emitSubpCreateReadPipe'

// Fill a fresh SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES (nLength + bInheritHandle=TRUE) into a slab region and return its pointer — the
// inheritable SA every child-facing pipe end is created with, spelled once for every spawn that creates a pipe.
function emitSubpSecurityAttributes(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let saSizeConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SecurityAttributesSize as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let saPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(saPtr, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(saSizeConst)))
	let saLenConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SecurityAttributesSize as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(saPtr, offset: SecurityAttributesNLengthOffset, valueId: saLenConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let saInheritConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SecurityAttributesInheritable as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(saPtr, offset: SecurityAttributesBInheritOffset, valueId: saInheritConst, valueType: StdType.i64))
	return saPtr
end 'emitSubpSecurityAttributes'

// ⭐⭐ **THE SPAWN REQUEST RECORD — ONE `CreateProcessW` PATH, THREE CALLERS.** `__gt_subp_spawn_core` reads
// exactly this and nothing else, so the three spellings a program can spawn a child with — the bare-name
// `subpSpawn(cmd)` probe, `__Builtins.subprocessSpawnStreaming` and the fourteen-argument
// `__Builtins.subprocessSpawn`/`subprocessDetach` — differ ONLY in how they FILL it. The pipe-inheritance
// rules, the slot claim, the generation bump and the failure unwind are written once.
//
// It is a slab record built by the caller rather than a parameter list because the fourteen-argument
// contract would otherwise arrive as fourteen live values across a spawn that immediately spills them all.
// Its fields are i64 because every one of them is a machine word: a buffer address, a `cstring`, a Win32
// flag word or a kind discriminator.
export let SubpReqCmdline = 0 as ByteOffset  // NUL-terminated; a wide host spawns from `SubpReqWideCmdline`
export let SubpReqCwd = 8 as ByteOffset  // cstring, or 0 to inherit the parent's working directory
export let SubpReqFlags = 16 as ByteOffset  // Win32 dwCreationFlags, already translated from the surface bitfield
export let SubpReqStdinKind = 24 as ByteOffset

// A `bytes`/`delayed` payload here is queued only up to its first NUL;
// `__Builtins.subprocessQueueStdin` queues one whole, which is how `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon`
// delivers it.
export let SubpReqStdinData = 32 as ByteOffset  // cstring: a path for `file`, the payload for `bytes`
export let SubpReqOutKind = 40 as ByteOffset
export let SubpReqOutData = 48 as ByteOffset
export let SubpReqOutLimit = 56 as ByteOffset
export let SubpReqErrKind = 64 as ByteOffset
export let SubpReqErrData = 72 as ByteOffset
export let SubpReqErrLimit = 80 as ByteOffset
export let SubpReqDetach = 88 as ByteOffset  // 1 ⇒ answer the child's PID and let it go, rather than a handle

// The child's whole environment, as a NUL-separated UTF-8 `NAME=VALUE` block, or 0 to
// inherit this process's. ⚠ ZERO IS THE INHERIT ANSWER AND THE SLAB ZERO-FILLS, so the two entry points that
// have no environment to pass (`__gt_subp_stream_spawn` and the two `runProcess(cmd)` shapes) say nothing at
// all and get it — the same arrangement `SubpReqDetach` has.
export let SubpReqEnv = 96 as ByteOffset

// UTF-16 copies of the command, the working directory and the environment, used only when the host
// is wide. `__gt_subp_widen_request` fills them just before the spawn, and
// `__gt_subp_free_wide_request` releases them on both the launch-failure and the success exit, after
// the spawn's error is recorded.
let SubpReqWideCmdline = 104
let SubpReqWideCwd = 112
let SubpReqWideEnv = 120
let SubpReqBytes = 128

// ⭐ **THE STDIO KIND DISCRIMINATORS, WHICH ARE `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon`'s OWN ENUM VALUES.** `StdinKind`
// (`none`/`inherit`/`bytes`/`file`) and `OutputKind` (`discard`/`inherit`/`collect`/`file`) are declared there
// with exactly these raw values and travel across the builtin boundary unchanged, so a translation here would
// be a second copy of a number the corpus already fixes.
//
// ⭐ **THE TWO ENUMS AGREE ON 1 AND 3, AND THAT AGREEMENT IS LOAD-BEARING**: `inherit` and `file` mean the same
// thing on either side of a child, which is what lets `emitSubpNonPipeChildHandle` serve all three streams
// with one body. `0` differs only in name (`none` reads the NUL device, `discard` writes it — the same open),
// and `2` is the one genuinely per-direction value (`bytes` feeds a pipe, `collect` drains one).
let SubpStdioInherit = 1
let SubpStdioFile = 3
let SubpStdinBytes = 2
export let SubpOutCollect = 2 as FullRangeInt

// ⭐⭐ **`hold` — A STDIN PIPE THE PARENT OPENS AND NEVER WRITES TO, SO THE CHILD'S READ BLOCKS IN THE KERNEL
// INSTEAD OF SEEING EOF.** `StreamingSubprocess` wants exactly that (a pipe with no payload preloaded), and so
// does `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon`'s `InputSource.hold`.
//
// ⚠⚠ **IT WAS `SubpStdinPipe`, *"the compiler's own fifth stdin kind with no surface spelling"*, AND THAT SENTENCE
// EXPIRED WHEN THE SURFACE ARRIVED.** `StdinKind.hold` was deliberately given the value **4** — its own
// declaration says *"the number is 4 because that is the value the compiler's runtime already decodes"* — so the two
// were one fact under two names, and the name that survives is the one the language spells. Renaming it is
// what stops the next reader from concluding that `hold` is unimplemented here because no constant says so.
export let SubpStdinHold = 4 as FullRangeInt

// ⭐⭐ **`delayed` — `hold`'s PIPE, THEN `bytes`'s PAYLOAD, fed `SubpStdinDelayedFeedMs` after the child's first
// stdout byte.** A kind that falls through to the non-pipe arm below opens the NULL DEVICE, so the child reads
// an instant EOF where it must block and then get the data — which silently turns
// `specs/sched-syscall-handoff.md`'s ordering cases into programs with nothing to block on.
let SubpStdinDelayed = 5

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH STDIN KINDS WANT A PARENT↔CHILD PIPE — STATED ONCE.** A chain of equality tests per kind
// ending in *"everything else gets the null device"* gives a kind added to the stdlib and not to the chain
// instant EOF, silently. This is the set instead.
//
// ⚠ **IT IS AN IR EMITTER AND NOT A COMPILE-TIME PREDICATE, BECAUSE THE KIND IS A RUNTIME VALUE.** It arrives
// in the request struct from `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon`'s `StdioRuntimeTriple`, so the set is a `bitOr` of
// comparisons rather than an `or` of constants — which is the same set stated once either way.
function emitSubpStdinKindWantsPipe(module StdModule, block IrBlock, kind ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let isBytes = emitSubpKindIs(module, block: block, kind: kind, value: SubpStdinBytes, ids: ids)
	let isHold = emitSubpKindIs(module, block: block, kind: kind, value: SubpStdinHold, ids: ids)
	let isDelayed = emitSubpKindIs(module, block: block, kind: kind, value: SubpStdinDelayed, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: emitBin(module, block: block, a: isBytes, b: isHold, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids), b: isDelayed, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpStdinKindWantsPipe'

// ⭐ **AND WHICH OF THOSE CARRY A PAYLOAD FOR THE DRAIN LOOP TO PUSH.** `hold` is the one that does not, and
// the distinction is not cosmetic: `SubpReqStdinData` is a valid pointer to an EMPTY cstring for every kind
// with no payload, so queueing it would make `emitSubpPushStdin`'s spent-test fire on its first pass and close
// the very pipe `hold` exists to keep open.
function emitSubpStdinKindQueuesPayload(module StdModule, block IrBlock, kind ValueId, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	let isBytes = emitSubpKindIs(module, block: block, kind: kind, value: SubpStdinBytes, ids: ids)
	let isDelayed = emitSubpKindIs(module, block: block, kind: kind, value: SubpStdinDelayed, ids: ids)
	return emitBin(module, block: block, a: isBytes, b: isDelayed, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpStdinKindQueuesPayload'

// `kind == value` as a 0/1 word — the comparison the two sets above are each an OR of, spelled once so the
// two of them state their MEMBERSHIP and nothing else.
function emitSubpKindIs(module StdModule, block IrBlock, kind ValueId, value FullRangeInt, ids ValueMinter) returns ValueId
	return emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: block, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: value, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpKindIs'

// The NUL device, as the bytes an `A` entry point reads. `none` (stdin) and `discard` (stdout/stderr) both open
// it — a child whose stream is wired to NUL reads instant EOF and writes into nothing, which is exactly what
// those two kinds promise, and it costs no pipe and no drain.
// ⭐⭐ **THE NULL DEVICE IS NAMED DIFFERENTLY ON THE TWO PLATFORMS, AND GETTING IT WRONG IS A SPAWN FAILURE.**
// `InputSource.none` and `OutputDestination.discard` open this path for the child; `"NUL"` is a Windows
// device name and `open("NUL")` on POSIX is an ordinary relative path that does not exist — so an
// `InputSource.none` spawn (which is `Subprocess.run`'s DEFAULT) fails with `ENOENT` before it reaches
// `posix_spawnp` at all. It rides `SubprocessCommandShape` because it is the same platform split asked
// about a different thing, and forking on it twice would be two answers to one question.
let SubpWindowsNullDeviceName = b"NUL"
let SubpPosixNullDeviceName = b"/dev/null"

// ⭐ `__gt_subp_spawn_core(reqPtr) -> handle` — spawn a Windows child with its three standard streams wired as
// the request asks, allocate a handle-table slot, and return its index (or -1 on failure; or the child's PID
// when the request asks to DETACH).
//
// ⭐ REGISTER-PRESSURE DISCIPLINE. A hand-built runtime function's values are SPAN-LESS, so a register-pressure
// E5001 has no user code to blame and PANICS. This function is therefore structured to keep only a handful of
// non-constant values live across each loop (constants are rematerialized, so they are free): every OS handle
// is stored STRAIGHT TO MEMORY (parent ends into the slot, child ends into a slab scratch) the instant it
// exists, so the register-hungry pipe-name-formatting loops carry only the pointers to those regions.
// ⚠ It is also why the command line is a field of the REQUEST rather than a String this function copies: the
// copy loop would carry four values across itself here, so each caller builds the buffer before any handle
// exists.
function buildSubpSpawnCore(module StdModule, commandShape SubprocessCommandShape, streamState EnumLayout)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SubpSpawnCoreName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), needsGreenThreadStackGuard: true, pinsItsThread: true)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var allocTbl = module.addBlock(func, label: b"alloctbl", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var dropTbl = module.addBlock(func, label: b"droptbl", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var haveTbl = module.addBlock(func, label: b"havetbl", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var slotHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"slothdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var slotCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"slotcheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var slotNext = module.addBlock(func, label: b"slotnext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var noSlot = module.addBlock(func, label: b"noslot", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var gotSlot = module.addBlock(func, label: b"gotslot", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let req = 0
	let negOne = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: -1, ids: ids)

	let tableInit = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: entry, label: SubpTableLabel, ids: ids)
	let zeroE = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: tableInit, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: allocTbl, whenFalse: haveTbl, ids: ids)

	// The slab zeroes the region, which is every slot's free state: `inUse` 0 and null buffers.
	let tblSize = emitConst(module, block: allocTbl, value: (SubpMaxHandles * SubpSlotBytes) as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let newTable = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(allocTbl, op: StdOp.call(newTable, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(tblSize)))
	let tablePublished = emitCasGlobalWord(module, block: allocTbl, label: SubpTableLabel, expected: zeroE, replacement: newTable, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: allocTbl, result: tablePublished, lost: dropTbl, won: haveTbl, ids: ids)

	emitSlabFree(module, block: dropTbl, ptr: newTable, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(dropTbl, op: StdOp.branch(haveTbl.id))

	let table = emitLoadAcquireGlobalWord(module, block: haveTbl, label: SubpTableLabel, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(haveTbl, op: StdOp.branch(slotHdr.id))
	haveTbl.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(slotHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zeroE)))

	// slotHdr(i): out of slots → -1; else check slot i.
	let slotIdx = ids.mint()
	slotHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(slotIdx, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: slotHdr, value: slotIdx, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, against: SubpMaxHandles as FullRangeInt, whenTrue: noSlot, whenFalse: slotCheck, ids: ids)

	// ⛔⛔ **THE CLAIM IS A CAS, NOT A TEST, AND THE WINDOW IT CLOSES IS THE WHOLE SPAWN.** Writing `inUse` at
	// the END of this function leaves a slot readable as FREE across `CreateProcessW`, so two Ms scanning at
	// once take the same index and then wire two different children's pipe ends into one slot.
	// `CAS(inUse, 0 -> claimed)` makes the SCAN the claim, so a loser simply walks on to the next index
	// (`SchedRuntime.POffStatus`'s shape, one table over), and the one path out of here that produces no handle
	// gives the claim back.
	let probeSlot = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: slotCheck, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	let claimedValue = emitConst(module, block: slotCheck, value: SubpSlotClaimed, ids: ids)
	let wonSlot = emitCasField(module, block: slotCheck, record: probeSlot, offset: SubpSlotInUse as ByteOffset, expected: emitConst(module, block: slotCheck, value: SubpSlotFree, ids: ids), replacement: claimedValue, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCasLost(module, block: slotCheck, result: wonSlot, lost: slotNext, won: gotSlot, ids: ids)

	let oneS = emitConst(module, block: slotNext, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextIdx = emitBin(module, block: slotNext, a: slotIdx, b: oneS, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(slotNext, op: StdOp.branch(slotHdr.id))
	slotNext.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(slotHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(nextIdx)))

	emitSubpStoreLastError(module, block: noSlot, code: ErrorTooManyOpenFiles, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(noSlot, op: StdOp.ret(negOne))

	// gotSlot: the slot is this spawn's, so its collect interlock starts free and its generation moves
	// on, which makes every handle to the previous occupant stale before any set-up begins; then the
	// inheritable SECURITY_ATTRIBUTES every child-facing pipe end is created with, the scratch the six
	// child-side handles are parked in, and the NUL device name the `none`/`discard` kinds open.
	emitSubpCollectorFree(module, block: gotSlot, slot: probeSlot, ids: ids)
	let claimedGeneration = emitLoadField(module, block: gotSlot, record: probeSlot, offset: SubpSlotGeneration, ids: ids)
	let generationStep = emitConst(module, block: gotSlot, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let nextGeneration = emitBin(module, block: gotSlot, a: claimedGeneration, b: generationStep, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: gotSlot, record: probeSlot, offset: SubpSlotGeneration, value: nextGeneration)
	let saPtr = emitSubpSecurityAttributes(module, block: gotSlot, ids: ids)
	let childScratchSizeConst = emitConst(module, block: gotSlot, value: SubpChildScratchBytes as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let childScratch = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(gotSlot, op: StdOp.call(childScratch, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(childScratchSizeConst)))
	let nulName = emitLiteralCString(module, block: gotSlot, ids: ids, text: subpNullDeviceName(commandShape))

	// ⚠ **THE FAILURE EXITS ARE CREATED BEFORE THE STDIO SETUP BECAUSE THE SETUP CAN REACH THEM.** A `file` or
	// `discard` stream opens a real file, and `CreateFileW` can fail; see `emitSubpNonPipeChildHandle`.
	// A pipe stream's pipe can fail to be made too (`emitSubpRequirePipeEnds`). Their exit records the
	// failure as the standard streams' so a missing redirect file is never read as a missing
	// executable; both exits then share the cleanup.
	var streamFail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"streamfail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var launchFail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"launchfail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spawnFail = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnfail", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var spawnOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spawnok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	var afterStdin = emitSubpStdinSetup(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: gotSlot, req: req, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx, saPtr: saPtr, childScratch: childScratch, nulName: nulName, whenFailed: streamFail)
	var afterStdout = emitSubpOutputSetup(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterStdin, req: req, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx, saPtr: saPtr, childScratch: childScratch, nulName: nulName, kindOffset: SubpReqOutKind, dataOffset: SubpReqOutData, limitOffset: SubpReqOutLimit, streamBase: SubpStdoutBase, slotLimitOffset: SubpSlotOutLimit, childOffset: SubpChildStdoutWriteOff, ownedOffset: SubpOwnedStdoutOff, stdSelector: StdOutputHandleSelector, whenFailed: streamFail)
	var afterStderr = emitSubpOutputSetup(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterStdout, req: req, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx, saPtr: saPtr, childScratch: childScratch, nulName: nulName, kindOffset: SubpReqErrKind, dataOffset: SubpReqErrData, limitOffset: SubpReqErrLimit, streamBase: SubpStderrBase, slotLimitOffset: SubpSlotErrLimit, childOffset: SubpChildStderrWriteOff, ownedOffset: SubpOwnedStderrOff, stdSelector: StdErrorHandleSelector, whenFailed: streamFail)

	let streamStep = emitConst(module, block: streamFail, value: SpawnPreparation.standardStream.rawValue, ids: ids)
	emitSubpRecordSpawnError(module, block: streamFail, preparation: streamStep, ids: ids)

	if hostTextIsWide(module) 'wideRedirect'
		var dropPath = module.addBlock(func, label: b"streamdrop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
		let failedPath = emitLoadField(module, block: streamFail, record: childScratch, offset: SubpChildFailedPathOff, ids: ids)
		emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: streamFail, value: failedPath, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: dropPath, whenFalse: spawnFail, ids: ids)
		emitSlabFree(module, block: dropPath, ptr: failedPath, ids: ids)
		module.setTerminator(dropPath, op: StdOp.branch(spawnFail.id))
	end 'wideRedirect' else 'narrowRedirect'
		module.setTerminator(streamFail, op: StdOp.branch(spawnFail.id))
	end 'narrowRedirect'

	var detached = module.addBlock(func, label: b"detached", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var attached = module.addBlock(func, label: b"attached", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// afterStderr: STARTUPINFO (child ends reloaded from the scratch) and the spawn — no loop from here on, so
	// the straight-line spill is free of the E5001 hazard.
	let startupPtr = emitSubpAllocStartupInfo(module, block: afterStderr, ids: ids)
	let hStdIn = emitLoadField(module, block: afterStderr, record: childScratch, offset: SubpChildStdinReadOff, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterStderr, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(startupPtr, offset: StartupInfoHStdInputOffset, valueId: hStdIn, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let hStdOut = emitLoadField(module, block: afterStderr, record: childScratch, offset: SubpChildStdoutWriteOff, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterStderr, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(startupPtr, offset: StartupInfoHStdOutputOffset, valueId: hStdOut, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let hStdErr = emitLoadField(module, block: afterStderr, record: childScratch, offset: SubpChildStderrWriteOff, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterStderr, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(startupPtr, offset: StartupInfoHStdErrorOffset, valueId: hStdErr, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let procInfoSizeConst = emitConst(module, block: afterStderr, value: ProcInfoSize as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let procInfoPtr = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterStderr, op: StdOp.call(procInfoPtr, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(procInfoSizeConst)))
	emitSubpWideRequestStep(module, block: afterStderr, req: req, step: SubpWideRequestStep.widen, ids: ids)
	let cmdlinePtr = emitLoadField(module, block: afterStderr, record: req, offset: subpSpawnOperandOffset(module, utf8: SubpReqCmdline, wide: SubpReqWideCmdline), ids: ids)
	let cwdPtr = emitLoadField(module, block: afterStderr, record: req, offset: subpSpawnOperandOffset(module, utf8: SubpReqCwd, wide: SubpReqWideCwd), ids: ids)
	let creationFlags = emitLoadField(module, block: afterStderr, record: req, offset: SubpReqFlags, ids: ids)
	let envPtr = emitLoadField(module, block: afterStderr, record: req, offset: subpSpawnOperandOffset(module, utf8: SubpReqEnv, wide: SubpReqWideEnv), ids: ids)
	afterStderr = emitSubpSpawnInheriting(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterStderr, command: cmdlinePtr, cwd: cwdPtr, creationFlags: creationFlags, startupPtr: startupPtr, procInfoPtr: procInfoPtr, env: envPtr, handles: threeArgs(hStdIn, second: hStdOut, third: hStdErr))
	let hProcess = emitLoadField(module, block: afterStderr, record: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoHProcessOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: afterStderr, value: hProcess, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: launchFail, whenFalse: spawnOk, ids: ids)

	// launchFail: publish the OS's reason with the preparation step the spawn chunk reported, which is `none`
	// unless a POSIX chunk failed before launching (`SpawnFailedPreparationOffset`). A missing working directory
	// needs no step on Windows: `CreateProcessW` answers `ERROR_DIRECTORY` for it, a code no missing executable
	// shares.
	let launchStep = emitLoadField(module, block: launchFail, record: procInfoPtr, offset: SpawnFailedPreparationOffset, ids: ids)
	emitSubpRecordSpawnError(module, block: launchFail, preparation: launchStep, ids: ids)

	launchFail = emitSubpReleaseSpawnAttributeList(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: launchFail, startupPtr: startupPtr)
	let launchTree = emitLoadField(module, block: launchFail, record: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoTreeObjectOffset, ids: ids)
	var afterLaunchTree = emitSubpCloseTreeObject(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: launchFail, treeObject: launchTree)
	emitSubpWideRequestStep(module, block: afterLaunchTree, req: req, step: SubpWideRequestStep.release, ids: ids)
	emitSubpFreeLaunchRecords(module, block: afterLaunchTree, startupPtr: startupPtr, procInfoPtr: procInfoPtr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterLaunchTree, op: StdOp.branch(spawnFail.id))

	// spawnFail: close every handle this attempt opened (reloaded from the slot + the owned-child scratch) — the
	// slot stays free — give back the setup's scratch, and answer -1. Both roads here have already published the
	// last error, and nothing below writes it.
	let slotF = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: spawnFail, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	emitSubpCloseSlotHandles(module, block: spawnFail, ids: ids, slot: slotF, childScratch: childScratch)
	emitSubpFreeSetupScratch(module, block: spawnFail, saPtr: saPtr, childScratch: childScratch, nulName: nulName, ids: ids)

	// ⭐ **AND GIVE THE CLAIM BACK.** The scan's CAS took this slot before any of the stdio setup ran, so this
	// — the only way out of this function that produced no handle — is what returns it. Without it the table
	// would lose one of its sixty-four slots per failed spawn.
	emitSubpPublishSlotState(module, block: spawnFail, slot: slotF, value: SubpSlotFree, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(spawnFail, op: StdOp.ret(negOne))

	// spawnOk: close the child thread handle and the PARENT's copies of the three OWNED child ends; store the
	// process handle and pid; reset per-stream buffered state; ensure the reusable buffers; mark the slot live.
	spawnOk = emitSubpReleaseSpawnAttributeList(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: spawnOk, startupPtr: startupPtr)
	let hThread = emitLoadField(module, block: spawnOk, record: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoHThreadOffset, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(spawnOk, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(hThread))
	emitSubpCloseOwnedChildEnds(module, block: spawnOk, ids: ids, childScratch: childScratch)

	// The mask discards `dwThreadId`, which sits in the high half of the word the load reads.
	let pid = emitLoadDword(module, block: spawnOk, record: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoDwProcessIdOffset, ids: ids)
	let treeObject = emitLoadField(module, block: spawnOk, record: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoTreeObjectOffset, ids: ids)
	let assigned = emitLoadField(module, block: spawnOk, record: procInfoPtr, offset: ProcInfoTreeAssignedOffset, ids: ids)
	emitSubpWideRequestStep(module, block: spawnOk, req: req, step: SubpWideRequestStep.release, ids: ids)
	emitSubpFreeLaunchRecords(module, block: spawnOk, startupPtr: startupPtr, procInfoPtr: procInfoPtr, ids: ids)
	emitSubpFreeSetupScratch(module, block: spawnOk, saPtr: saPtr, childScratch: childScratch, nulName: nulName, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: spawnOk, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnOk, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotHProcess, value: hProcess)
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnOk, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotPid, value: pid)
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnOk, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotOwnedTreeObject, value: treeObject)

	// A job the child was not assigned to cannot end it, so the tree a deadline kills falls back to
	// the child itself; the job is still owned and closed at release.
	let adoptingTree = emitBin(module, block: spawnOk, a: treeObject, b: assigned, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let noTreeObject = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: spawnOk, value: adoptingTree, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, ids: ids)
	let childItself = emitBin(module, block: spawnOk, a: noTreeObject, b: hProcess, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let processTree = emitBin(module, block: spawnOk, a: adoptingTree, b: childItself, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnOk, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotProcessTree, value: processTree)
	let zeroB = emitConst(module, block: spawnOk, value: 0, ids: ids)

	// Both streams start `open`. Written from the declaration rather than left to the slab's zero, because a
	// SLOT IS REUSED: the previous occupant's latched state would otherwise still be sitting here.
	let openState = emitConst(module, block: spawnOk, value: streamState.tagOfCase(SubprocessStreamOpenCaseName), ids: ids)
	let stdoutBaseConst = emitConst(module, block: spawnOk, value: SubpStdoutBase as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let stdoutStream = emitBin(module, block: spawnOk, a: slot, b: stdoutBaseConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnOk, record: stdoutStream, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, value: zeroB)
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnOk, record: stdoutStream, offset: SubpStreamStateWord, value: openState)
	let stderrBaseConst = emitConst(module, block: spawnOk, value: SubpStderrBase as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let stderrStream = emitBin(module, block: spawnOk, a: slot, b: stderrBaseConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnOk, record: stderrStream, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, value: zeroB)
	emitStoreField(module, block: spawnOk, record: stderrStream, offset: SubpStreamStateWord, value: openState)
	var afterStdoutBuf = emitEnsureSlotBuffer(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: spawnOk, record: stdoutStream, fieldOffset: SubpStreamBufPtr, size: SubpInitialLineBufBytes as FullRangeInt, capField: SubpStreamBufCap)
	var afterStderrBuf = emitEnsureSlotBuffer(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterStdoutBuf, record: stderrStream, fieldOffset: SubpStreamBufPtr, size: SubpInitialLineBufBytes as FullRangeInt, capField: SubpStreamBufCap)
	var afterWriteBuf = emitEnsureSlotBuffer(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterStderrBuf, record: slot, fieldOffset: SubpSlotWriteBuf, size: SubpInitialLineBufBytes as FullRangeInt, capField: SubpSlotWriteCap)
	var afterBytesOut = emitEnsureSlotPtr(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterWriteBuf, record: slot, fieldOffset: SubpSlotBytesOut, size: SubpBytesOutBytes as FullRangeInt)

	// ⭐ This is the point the slot becomes a live HANDLE: `SubpSlotLive` is published after every
	// set-up store above, so a guard that finds it sees them all. The handle packs the generation
	// taken at the claim, so a reader still parked on the PREVIOUS occupant sees a mismatch on resume
	// and writes nothing back (see `SubpSlotGeneration`). A failed spawn releases the claim at
	// `spawnfail`.
	let slotC = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: afterBytesOut, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	let claimedGenerationC = emitLoadField(module, block: afterBytesOut, record: slotC, offset: SubpSlotGeneration, ids: ids)
	emitSubpPublishSlotState(module, block: afterBytesOut, slot: slotC, value: SubpSlotLive, ids: ids)
	let handle = emitSubpPackHandle(module, block: afterBytesOut, ids: ids, slotIdx: slotIdx, generation: claimedGenerationC)

	// A DETACHED spawn answers the child's PID and hands the slot straight back. The slot is claimed first and
	// released here rather than skipped, because releasing is what CLOSES the process handle, the three pipe
	// ends and the child's job object — a detached child the parent still holds handles to is leaked kernel
	// objects per spawn, and the release path already knows every handle a slot owns. It is a guarded entry, so
	// it is given the
	// PACKED handle: a bare index decodes to generation 0, which the guard refuses, and the slot would stay live.
	let detachAsked = emitLoadField(module, block: afterBytesOut, record: req, offset: SubpReqDetach, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: afterBytesOut, value: detachAsked, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: attached, whenFalse: detached, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(attached, op: StdOp.ret(handle))

	// ⚠ **THE PID IS READ BEFORE THE RELEASE, AND THE ORDER IS THE WHOLE POINT.** Reading it AFTER would work
	// only while `buildSubpRelease` happens not to zero `SubpSlotPid` — a coupling neither end states, which
	// the next field the release path decides to clear breaks silently. Reading first costs nothing.
	let slotP = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: detached, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	let detachedPid = emitLoadField(module, block: detached, record: slotP, offset: SubpSlotPid, ids: ids)
	let releaseResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(detached, op: StdOp.call(releaseResult, callee: SubpReleaseName, args: argsOf(handle)))
	module.setTerminator(detached, op: StdOp.ret(detachedPid))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpSpawnCore'

// The two records the launch reads and writes, dead once `hProcess`, `hThread`, the pid and the failed step
// are out of them.
function emitSubpFreeLaunchRecords(module StdModule, block IrBlock, startupPtr ValueId, procInfoPtr ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: block, ptr: startupPtr, ids: ids)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: block, ptr: procInfoPtr, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpFreeLaunchRecords'

// The three regions the stdio setup reads, dead once the launch has been attempted — the child-handle
// scratch only after its owned ends are closed.
function emitSubpFreeSetupScratch(module StdModule, block IrBlock, saPtr ValueId, childScratch ValueId, nulName ValueId, ids ValueMinter)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: block, ptr: saPtr, ids: ids)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: block, ptr: childScratch, ids: ids)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: block, ptr: nulName, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpFreeSetupScratch'

// `"NUL"` in a fresh slab buffer, as a NUL-terminated UTF-8 path. Three stores and a
// slab-zeroed terminator — a four-byte `.rdata` blob would need a relocation and a symbol for what is three
// immediates.
function subpNullDeviceName(commandShape SubprocessCommandShape) returns ByteArray
	return match commandShape 'shape'
		commandLine gives SubpWindowsNullDeviceName
		argumentVector gives SubpPosixNullDeviceName
	end 'shape'
end 'subpNullDeviceName'

// ⭐ **THE TWO NON-PIPE STDIO ARMS, WHICH ALL THREE STREAMS SHARE EXACTLY.** `inherit` hands the child the
// PARENT's own standard handle for this stream; every other non-pipe kind opens a FILE — the caller's path for
// `file`, and the NUL device for `none`/`discard`, which is the same open with a different name.
//
// It terminates `cur` and branches to `join` carrying TWO values: the handle the STARTUPINFO names, and the
// handle this spawn OWNS and must close after `CreateProcessW` returns. They differ in exactly one case and
// that difference is a correctness obligation, not bookkeeping: an INHERITED handle belongs to the OS, and
// closing it would close the PARENT's own stdout. The inherit arm therefore passes 0 as the owned half, and
// `CloseHandle(0)` is the no-op that makes the close path uniform.
function emitSubpNonPipeChildHandle(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, kind ValueId, data ValueId, nulName ValueId, childScratch ValueId, stdSelector FullRangeInt, forWrite bool, join IrBlock, whenFailed IrBlock)
	var stdArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sstd", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pathPick = module.addBlock(func, label: b"spath", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var fileArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sfile", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var nulArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"snul", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var openArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sopen", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var openOk = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sopenok", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SubpStdioInherit, whenTrue: stdArm, whenFalse: pathPick, ids: ids)

	let selector = emitConst(module, block: stdArm, value: stdSelector, ids: ids)
	let stdHandle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(stdArm, op: StdOp.osStdHandle(stdHandle, whichId: selector))

	// A handle the attribute list names must be inheritable (`emitSubpSpawnInheriting`), and the
	// parent's own standard handle need not be.
	if subpPipesAreNamed(module) 'listed'
		module.appendOp(stdArm, op: StdOp.osHandleSetInherit(stdHandle))
	end 'listed'

	let notOurs = emitConst(module, block: stdArm, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(stdArm, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	stdArm.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: twoArgs(stdHandle, second: notOurs)))

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: pathPick, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SubpStdioFile, whenTrue: fileArm, whenFalse: nulArm, ids: ids)

	module.setTerminator(fileArm, op: StdOp.branch(openArm.id))
	fileArm.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(openArm.id, argIds: argsOf(data)))
	module.setTerminator(nulArm, op: StdOp.branch(openArm.id))
	nulArm.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(openArm.id, argIds: argsOf(nulName)))

	let path = ids.mint()
	openArm.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(path, argType: StdType.i64))
	let opened = ids.mint()
	let hostPath = emitHostCString(module, block: openArm, cstr: path, encoding: module.hostText, ids: ids)

	if forWrite 'writeEnd'
		module.appendOp(openArm, op: StdOp.osFileOpenWrite(opened, pathPtrId: hostPath))
	end 'writeEnd' else 'readEnd'
		module.appendOp(openArm, op: StdOp.osFileOpenRead(opened, pathPtrId: hostPath))
	end 'readEnd'

	// ⚠⚠ **THE OPEN IS CHECKED, BECAUSE AN UNCHECKED FAILED OPEN IS A SILENT SUCCESS.** `CreateFileW`
	// answers `INVALID_HANDLE_VALUE` (-1), and -1 in a STARTUPINFO under `bInheritHandles=TRUE` does not stop
	// `CreateProcessW`: `InputSource.file("Z:/no/such/dir/nope.txt")` would answer `spawn=0` with an EMPTY
	// `lastErrorMessage()` and launch a child whose stdin was a dead handle. `InputSource.file` and
	// `OutputDestination.file` are public corpus surface, so `Configuration` alone reaches it. Failing HERE
	// also means the reason a caller reads is `CreateFileW`'s — "the
	// file is not there" — rather than whatever `CreateProcessW` would have said about it afterwards.
	let openFailed = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: openArm, value: opened, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: -1, ids: ids)

	if not hostTextIsWide(module) 'narrowOpen'
		module.setTerminator(openArm, op: StdOp.condBranch(openFailed, thenBlockId: whenFailed.id, elseBlockId: openOk.id))
	end 'narrowOpen' else 'wideOpen'
		var keepPath = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swidekeep", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
		var dropPath = module.addBlock(func, label: b"swidedrop", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
		module.setTerminator(openArm, op: StdOp.condBranch(openFailed, thenBlockId: keepPath.id, elseBlockId: dropPath.id))

		emitStoreField(module, block: keepPath, record: childScratch, offset: SubpChildFailedPathOff, value: hostPath)
		module.setTerminator(keepPath, op: StdOp.branch(whenFailed.id))

		emitSlabFree(module, block: dropPath, ptr: hostPath, ids: ids)
		module.setTerminator(dropPath, op: StdOp.branch(openOk.id))
	end 'wideOpen'

	// ⚠ `CreateFileW` with a NULL `lpSecurityAttributes` yields a NON-inheritable handle, and a STARTUPINFO
	// handle a child cannot inherit leaves that stream CLOSED in the child with no error anywhere. See
	// `osHandleSetInherit`.
	module.appendOp(openOk, op: StdOp.osHandleSetInherit(opened))
	module.setTerminator(openOk, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	openOk.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: twoArgs(opened, second: opened)))
end 'emitSubpNonPipeChildHandle'

// Wire the child's STANDARD INPUT as the request asks, and return the block execution continues in.
//
// The three PIPE kinds — `bytes`, `hold` and `delayed` — all need the parent's WRITE end of a synchronous
// outbound pipe, and differ only in what the drain loop is then told to do with it: push a payload now
// (`bytes`), push nothing ever (`hold`, which is also what `StreamingSubprocess` asks for, writing by hand),
// or push a payload a fixed delay after the child's first stdout byte (`delayed`). Everything else is
// `emitSubpNonPipeChildHandle`'s.
//
// ⛔⛔ **THE MEMBERSHIP TEST IS A SET AND NOT A CHAIN OF EQUALITIES.** Per-kind `==` tests routing to the
// pipe and sending **everything else** down the non-pipe arm send a newly declared kind to `none`'s NUL open,
// which gives the child instant EOF. A kind the language can spell and this file cannot see is a WRONG
// ANSWER with no diagnostic anywhere; `emitSubpStdinKindWantsPipe` is that set stated once.
function emitSubpStdinSetup(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, req ValueId, table ValueId, slotIdx ValueId, saPtr ValueId, childScratch ValueId, nulName ValueId, whenFailed IrBlock) returns IrBlock
	var queueTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sinqtest", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var queueArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sinqueue", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var delayTest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sindtest", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var delayArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sindelay", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pipeArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sinpipe", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pipeMake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sinpmake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var nonPipe = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sinother", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var join = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sinjoin", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cont = module.addBlock(func, label: b"sindone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let kind = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: req, offset: SubpReqStdinKind, ids: ids)
	let data = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: req, offset: SubpReqStdinData, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: cur, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)

	// A non-pipe stdin leaves ALL THREE of these 0, which is what makes the drain loop's "is there a write
	// end?", "is there a payload?" and "may I push it yet?" plain word tests rather than kind tests repeated
	// one pass later.
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotStdinWrite, value: zero)
	emitSubpStoreStdinQueue(module, block: cur, slot: slot, cursor: zero, remaining: zero)
	emitSubpStoreStdinState(module, block: cur, slot: slot, value: SubpStdinIdle, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotStdinFeedAt, value: zero)
	let wantsPipe = emitSubpStdinKindWantsPipe(module, block: cur, kind: kind, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: wantsPipe, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: nonPipe, whenFalse: queueTest, ids: ids)

	let queuesPayload = emitSubpStdinKindQueuesPayload(module, block: queueTest, kind: kind, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: queueTest, value: queuesPayload, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: pipeArm, whenFalse: queueArm, ids: ids)

	let payloadLength = ids.mint()
	var measured = emitCStringLength(module, func: func, cur: queueArm, cstr: data, length: payloadLength, ids: ids)
	let slotB = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: measured, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	emitSubpStoreStdinQueue(module, block: measured, slot: slotB, cursor: data, remaining: payloadLength)
	module.setTerminator(measured, op: StdOp.branch(delayTest.id))

	// `delayed` is `bytes` plus a feed that is held UNARMED until the drain loop sees the child's first stdout
	// byte (`SubpSlotStdinFeedAt`); no clock runs at spawn, so nothing between here and the child's first
	// print can bring the feed forward.
	let isDelayed = emitSubpKindIs(module, block: delayTest, kind: kind, value: SubpStdinDelayed, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: delayTest, value: isDelayed, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: pipeArm, whenFalse: delayArm, ids: ids)

	let unarmed = emitConst(module, block: delayArm, value: SubpStdinFeedUnarmed, ids: ids)
	let slotD = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: delayArm, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	emitStoreField(module, block: delayArm, record: slotD, offset: SubpSlotStdinFeedAt, value: unarmed)
	module.setTerminator(delayArm, op: StdOp.branch(pipeArm.id))

	let name = emitSubpAllocPipeName(module, block: pipeArm, ids: ids)
	buildPipeName(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: pipeArm, bufPtr: name, cont: pipeMake)

	let scratchSizeConst = emitConst(module, block: pipeMake, value: SubpPipeScratchBytes as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let scratch = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(pipeMake, op: StdOp.call(scratch, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(scratchSizeConst)))
	let eight = emitConst(module, block: pipeMake, value: SubpPipeScratchSecondWord, ids: ids)
	let scratchB = emitBin(module, block: pipeMake, a: scratch, b: eight, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)

	if subpPipesAreNamed(module) 'named'
		pipeMake = emitSubpOpenNamedPipePair(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: pipeMake, name: name, saPtr: saPtr, scratch: scratch, direction: SubpPipeDirection.towardChild)
	end 'named' else 'anonymous'
		module.appendOp(pipeMake, op: StdOp.osPipeCreateOutbound(name, saPtrId: saPtr, childReadPtrId: scratch, parentWritePtrId: scratchB))
	end 'anonymous'

	pipeMake = emitSubpRequirePipeEnds(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: pipeMake, name: name, scratch: scratch, whenFailed: whenFailed)
	let parentWrite = emitLoadField(module, block: pipeMake, record: scratchB, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let slotP = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: pipeMake, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	emitStoreField(module, block: pipeMake, record: slotP, offset: SubpSlotStdinWrite, value: parentWrite)
	let childRead = emitLoadField(module, block: pipeMake, record: scratch, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitSubpFreePipeScratch(module, block: pipeMake, name: name, scratch: scratch, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pipeMake, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	pipeMake.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: twoArgs(childRead, second: childRead)))

	emitSubpNonPipeChildHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: nonPipe, kind: kind, data: data, nulName: nulName, childScratch: childScratch, stdSelector: StdInputHandleSelector, forWrite: false, join: join, whenFailed: whenFailed)

	let childHandle = ids.mint()
	let ownedHandle = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(join, values: twoArgs(childHandle, second: ownedHandle))
	emitStoreField(module, block: join, record: childScratch, offset: SubpChildStdinReadOff, value: childHandle)
	emitStoreField(module, block: join, record: childScratch, offset: SubpOwnedStdinOff, value: ownedHandle)
	module.setTerminator(join, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
	return cont
end 'emitSubpStdinSetup'

// Wire ONE of the child's two output streams as the request asks, and return the block execution continues in.
// Called twice — for stdout and for stderr — with the request field offsets, the slot's stream sub-block base
// and the parent's own standard-handle selector as its only differences, because those ARE the only differences
// (`GtRuntime`'s slot layout makes the two stream sub-blocks identical five-word records for this reason).
function emitSubpOutputSetup(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, req ValueId, table ValueId, slotIdx ValueId, saPtr ValueId, childScratch ValueId, nulName ValueId, kindOffset ByteOffset, dataOffset ByteOffset, limitOffset ByteOffset, streamBase ByteOffset, slotLimitOffset ByteOffset, childOffset ByteOffset, ownedOffset ByteOffset, stdSelector FullRangeInt, whenFailed IrBlock) returns IrBlock
	var pipeArm = module.addBlock(func, label: b"soutpipe", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var pipeMake = module.addBlock(func, label: b"soutpmake", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var nonPipe = module.addBlock(func, label: b"soutother", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var join = module.addBlock(func, label: b"soutjoin", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cont = module.addBlock(func, label: b"soutdone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let kind = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: req, offset: kindOffset, ids: ids)
	let data = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: req, offset: dataOffset, ids: ids)
	let limit = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: req, offset: limitOffset, ids: ids)
	let slot = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: cur, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: slot, offset: slotLimitOffset, value: limit)
	let streamBaseConst = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: streamBase as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let stream = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: slot, b: streamBaseConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: 0, ids: ids)

	// A stream with no pipe leaves its read end 0, which is what the drain loop reads as "nothing to collect".
	emitStoreField(module, block: cur, record: stream, offset: SubpStreamReadEnd, value: zero)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: kind, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SubpOutCollect, whenTrue: pipeArm, whenFalse: nonPipe, ids: ids)

	let name = emitSubpAllocPipeName(module, block: pipeArm, ids: ids)
	buildPipeName(module, func: func, ids: ids, startBlock: pipeArm, bufPtr: name, cont: pipeMake)

	let scratchSizeConst = emitConst(module, block: pipeMake, value: SubpPipeScratchBytes as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let scratch = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(pipeMake, op: StdOp.call(scratch, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(scratchSizeConst)))
	pipeMake = emitSubpCreateReadPipe(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: pipeMake, namePtr: name, saPtr: saPtr, scratch: scratch, whenFailed: whenFailed)
	let parentRead = emitLoadField(module, block: pipeMake, record: scratch, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	let slotP = emitSubpSlotAddr(module, block: pipeMake, ids: ids, table: table, slotIdx: slotIdx)
	let streamBaseConstP = emitConst(module, block: pipeMake, value: streamBase as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let streamP = emitBin(module, block: pipeMake, a: slotP, b: streamBaseConstP, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: pipeMake, record: streamP, offset: SubpStreamReadEnd, value: parentRead)
	module.appendOp(pipeMake, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollPipeOpenName, args: argsOf(parentRead)))
	let childWrite = emitLoadField(module, block: pipeMake, record: scratch, offset: SubpPipeScratchSecondWord as ByteOffset, ids: ids)
	emitSubpFreePipeScratch(module, block: pipeMake, name: name, scratch: scratch, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(pipeMake, op: StdOp.branch(join.id))
	pipeMake.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(join.id, argIds: twoArgs(childWrite, second: childWrite)))

	emitSubpNonPipeChildHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: nonPipe, kind: kind, data: data, nulName: nulName, childScratch: childScratch, stdSelector: stdSelector, forWrite: true, join: join, whenFailed: whenFailed)

	let childHandle = ids.mint()
	let ownedHandle = ids.mint()
	pushI64BlockArgs(join, values: twoArgs(childHandle, second: ownedHandle))
	emitStoreField(module, block: join, record: childScratch, offset: childOffset, value: childHandle)
	emitStoreField(module, block: join, record: childScratch, offset: ownedOffset, value: ownedHandle)
	module.setTerminator(join, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
	return cont
end 'emitSubpOutputSetup'

// ⭐ `__gt_subp_spawn(cmdStrPtr) -> handle` — the bare-name `subpSpawn(cmd)` probe's entry.
// Copies the BORROWED command String's bytes into a mutable NUL-terminated buffer (CreateProcessW writes to
// its `lpCommandLine`), fills a spawn request asking for three pipes and nothing else, and hands it to the
// core. It configures no working directory and no creation flags: NULL and 0.
//
// ⛔ **IT IS THE THIRD DOOR THAT TAKES A COMMAND *LINE*, AND IT MUST ASK THE SHAPE LIKE THE OTHER TWO.**
// `__gt_process_run` and `__gt_io_read` both route their line through `emitSubpCommandFromLine`. Storing the
// raw bytes into `SubpReqCmdline` instead hands them to a core that, under
// `SubprocessCommandShape.argumentVector`, reads that field as a `char *const argv[]` — a SIGSEGV at the
// spawn itself on arm64-macOS, because the first eight ASCII bytes of the command are dereferenced as
// `argv[0]`. The facility table says `subprocess gives true` there, so nothing refuses the program;
// `streaming-subprocess`'s POSIX cases are what hold this door shut.
function buildSubpSpawn(module StdModule, commandShape SubprocessCommandShape)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SubpSpawnName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var copyHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"copyhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var copyBody = module.addBlock(func, label: b"copybody", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var afterCopy = module.addBlock(func, label: b"aftercopy", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let cmdStrPtr = 0
	let cmdBuffer = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: cmdStrPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let cmdLen = emitLoadField(module, block: entry, record: cmdStrPtr, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let nulBytes = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: TrailingNulBytes as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let cmBufSize = emitBin(module, block: entry, a: cmdLen, b: nulBytes, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let cmBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(entry, op: StdOp.call(cmBuf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(cmBufSize)))
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: entry, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(entry, op: StdOp.branch(copyHdr.id))
	entry.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zero)))

	let ci = ids.mint()
	copyHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(ci, argType: StdType.i64))
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: copyHdr, a: ci, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: cmdLen, whenTrue: afterCopy, whenFalse: copyBody, ids: ids)

	let srcAddr = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: cmdBuffer, b: ci, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let srcByte = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(copyBody, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(srcByte, addrId: srcAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let dstAddr = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: cmBuf, b: ci, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(copyBody, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(dstAddr, offset: 0, valueId: srcByte, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let ciOne = emitConst(module, block: copyBody, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let ciNext = emitBin(module, block: copyBody, a: ci, b: ciOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(copyBody, op: StdOp.branch(copyHdr.id))
	copyBody.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(copyHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(ciNext)))

	// The slab zeroes the buffer, so the terminator is already there; the store is kept because the SIZE
	// arithmetic above is what reserves the byte, and a reader should see the two together.
	let zeroT = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let nulAddr = emitBin(module, block: afterCopy, a: cmBuf, b: cmdLen, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(nulAddr, offset: 0, valueId: zeroT, valueType: StdType.u8))

	// The line the caller wrote becomes `/bin/sh -c <line>` on a lane whose primitive takes a vector, and
	// stays exactly itself under the command-line shape — the same one call `__gt_process_run` makes.
	// It stays UTF-8 on a wide host too: the core widens the whole request (`SubpReqWideCmdline`).
	let command = emitSubpCommandFromLine(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids, shape: commandShape, linePtr: cmBuf, encoding: HostTextEncoding.utf8)
	let req = emitSubpNewRequest(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids, cmdline: command)
	let stdinPipe = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: SubpStdinHold, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: req, offset: SubpReqStdinKind, value: stdinPipe)
	let collect = emitConst(module, block: afterCopy, value: SubpOutCollect, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: req, offset: SubpReqOutKind, value: collect)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterCopy, record: req, offset: SubpReqErrKind, value: collect)
	let handle = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(afterCopy, op: StdOp.call(handle, callee: SubpSpawnCoreName, args: argsOf(req)))

	// The core has read the request and the command whichever way it answered.
	emitSlabFree(module, block: afterCopy, ptr: req, ids: ids)
	emitSubpReleaseCommandFromLine(module, block: afterCopy, ids: ids, shape: commandShape, command: command, encoding: HostTextEncoding.utf8)
	emitSlabFree(module, block: afterCopy, ptr: cmBuf, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterCopy, op: StdOp.ret(handle))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpSpawn'

let SubpWidenRequestName = b"__gt_subp_widen_request"
let SubpFreeWideRequestName = b"__gt_subp_free_wide_request"

function subpSpawnOperandOffset(module StdModule, utf8 ByteOffset, wide ByteOffset) returns ByteOffset
	if hostTextIsWide(module) 'wide'
		return wide
	end 'wide'

	return utf8
end 'subpSpawnOperandOffset'

enum SubpWideRequestStep
	widen
	release
end 'SubpWideRequestStep'

function subpWideRequestStepName(step SubpWideRequestStep) returns ByteArray
	return match step 'name'
		widen gives SubpWidenRequestName
		release gives SubpFreeWideRequestName
	end 'name'
end 'subpWideRequestStepName'

function emitSubpWideRequestStep(module StdModule, block IrBlock, req ValueId, step SubpWideRequestStep, ids ValueMinter)
	if hostTextIsWide(module) 'wide'
		module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: subpWideRequestStepName(step), args: argsOf(req)))
	end 'wide'
end 'emitSubpWideRequestStep'

function buildSubpWideRequestStep(module StdModule, step SubpWideRequestStep)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(subpWideRequestStepName(step), returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let req = 0

	var afterCommand = emitSubpWideOperandStep(module, func: func, cur: entry, req: req, source: SubpReqCmdline, wide: SubpReqWideCmdline, extent: Utf8Extent.cstring, step: step, ids: ids)
	var afterCwd = emitSubpWideOperandStep(module, func: func, cur: afterCommand, req: req, source: SubpReqCwd, wide: SubpReqWideCwd, extent: Utf8Extent.cstring, step: step, ids: ids)
	var afterEnv = emitSubpWideOperandStep(module, func: func, cur: afterCwd, req: req, source: SubpReqEnv, wide: SubpReqWideEnv, extent: Utf8Extent.block, step: step, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterEnv, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpWideRequestStep'

function emitSubpWideOperandStep(module StdModule, func IrFunction, cur IrBlock, req ValueId, source ByteOffset, wide ByteOffset, extent Utf8Extent, step SubpWideRequestStep, ids ValueMinter) returns IrBlock
	var present = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subpwideoperand", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var joined = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subpwideoperanddone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let operandOffset = match step 'operand'
		widen gives source
		release gives wide
	end 'operand'

	let operand = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: req, offset: operandOffset, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: operand, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: joined, whenFalse: present, ids: ids)

	match step 'act'
		widen then emitStoreField(module, block: present, record: req, offset: wide, value: emitUtf8ToWide(module, block: present, source: operand, extent: extent, ids: ids))
		release then emitSlabFree(module, block: present, ptr: operand, ids: ids)
	end 'act'

	module.setTerminator(present, op: StdOp.branch(joined.id))

	return joined
end 'emitSubpWideOperandStep'

// A zeroed spawn request carrying only the command line — every other field's zero IS its default (inherit the
// cwd, no creation flags, `StdinKind.none`, `OutputKind.discard`, no capture limit, do not detach), which the
// slab's zeroing contract supplies for free. Callers overwrite exactly the fields they mean to change.
export function emitSubpNewRequest(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, cmdline ValueId) returns ValueId
	let sizeConst = emitConst(module, block: block, value: SubpReqBytes, ids: ids)
	let req = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(req, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(sizeConst)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: req, offset: SubpReqCmdline, value: cmdline)
	return req
end 'emitSubpNewRequest'

// Close the three child-side handles this spawn OWNS — never the ones it merely inherited from the parent (see
// `emitSubpNonPipeChildHandle`, which stores 0 in the owned slot for those). Shared by the success path (where
// the child now holds its own copies) and by the failure unwind below.
function emitSubpCloseOwnedChildEnds(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, childScratch ValueId)
	let ownedStdin = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: childScratch, offset: SubpOwnedStdinOff, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(ownedStdin))
	let ownedStdout = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: childScratch, offset: SubpOwnedStdoutOff, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(ownedStdout))
	let ownedStderr = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: childScratch, offset: SubpOwnedStderrOff, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(ownedStderr))
end 'emitSubpCloseOwnedChildEnds'

function emitSubpCloseTreeObject(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, treeObject ValueId) returns IrBlock
	var doClose = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subptreec", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var cont = module.addBlock(func, label: b"subptreed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: cur, value: treeObject, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: cont, whenFalse: doClose, ids: ids)

	module.appendOp(doClose, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(treeObject))
	module.setTerminator(doClose, op: StdOp.branch(cont.id))
	return cont
end 'emitSubpCloseTreeObject'

// Close every OS handle a FAILED spawn attempt opened — the three parent ends stored in the slot (stdin write,
// stdout read, stderr read) plus the three OWNED child ends parked in `childScratch`. There is no process
// handle to close: this runs only where `CreateProcessW` left the slab-zeroed PROCESS_INFORMATION untouched.
// Each handle is reloaded from memory so no handle occupies a register here, and a slot field
// holding 0 — a non-pipe stream, or one the failure came before — reaches a close of 0, which is
// the no-op that keeps this path free of kind tests.
function emitSubpCloseSlotHandles(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, slot ValueId, childScratch ValueId)
	emitSubpCloseParentEnds(module, block: block, ids: ids, slot: slot)
	emitSubpCloseOwnedChildEnds(module, block: block, ids: ids, childScratch: childScratch)
end 'emitSubpCloseSlotHandles'

function emitSubpCloseParentEnds(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, slot ValueId)
	emitSubpCloseStdinWrite(module, block: block, ids: ids, slot: slot)
	emitSubpCloseReadEnds(module, block: block, ids: ids, slot: slot)
end 'emitSubpCloseParentEnds'

function emitSubpCloseReadEnds(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, slot ValueId)
	emitSubpCloseStreamReadEnd(module, block: block, ids: ids, slot: slot, streamBase: SubpStdoutBase)
	emitSubpCloseStreamReadEnd(module, block: block, ids: ids, slot: slot, streamBase: SubpStderrBase)
end 'emitSubpCloseReadEnds'

// A lost CAS is another closer part-way through, so the release yields until it has finished.
function emitSubpCloseStdinWriteOwned(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, slot ValueId) returns IrBlock
	var attempt = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rlsstdin", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var contended = module.addBlock(func, label: b"rlsstdinwait", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	module.setTerminator(cur, op: StdOp.branch(attempt.id))
	var closed = emitSubpCloseStdinIfIdle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: attempt, slot: slot, whenLost: contended)
	module.appendOp(contended, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: GtReschedName, args: ValueIdArray.create()))
	module.setTerminator(contended, op: StdOp.branch(attempt.id))

	return closed
end 'emitSubpCloseStdinWriteOwned'

function emitSubpCloseStdinIfIdle(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, slot ValueId, whenLost IrBlock) returns IrBlock
	var closing = emitSubpStdinTransition(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: cur, slot: slot, expected: SubpStdinIdle, replacement: SubpStdinClosing, whenLost: whenLost)
	emitSubpCloseStdinThenIdle(module, block: closing, ids: ids, slot: slot)
	return closing
end 'emitSubpCloseStdinIfIdle'

export function emitSubpCloseStdinThenIdle(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, slot ValueId)
	emitSubpCloseStdinWrite(module, block: block, ids: ids, slot: slot)
	emitSubpStoreStdinState(module, block: block, slot: slot, value: SubpStdinIdle, ids: ids)
end 'emitSubpCloseStdinThenIdle'

// The queue is dropped with the write end, so a slot whose write end is 0 never has bytes left to
// push; `emitSubpPushStdin` writes without testing for a pipe because of it.
export function emitSubpCloseStdinWrite(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, slot ValueId)
	emitSubpClosePipeEnd(module, block: block, ids: ids, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotStdinWrite as ByteOffset)
	let zero = emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitSubpStoreStdinQueue(module, block: block, slot: slot, cursor: zero, remaining: zero)
end 'emitSubpCloseStdinWrite'

// The end is zeroed as it is closed: the slot outlives this child, and a number left in it would be
// closed again by a later failed spawn on the slot, whose set-up can fail before it rewrites the
// word, when the number may name another file's descriptor.
function emitSubpClosePipeEnd(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, record ValueId, offset ByteOffset)
	let pipeEnd = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: record, offset: offset, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(ids.mint(), callee: NetpollPipeCloseName, args: argsOf(pipeEnd)))
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(pipeEnd))
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: record, offset: offset, value: emitConst(module, block: block, value: 0, ids: ids))
end 'emitSubpClosePipeEnd'

function emitSubpCloseStreamReadEnd(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, slot ValueId, streamBase ByteOffset)
	let base = emitConst(module, block: block, value: streamBase as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let stream = emitBin(module, block: block, a: slot, b: base, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitSubpClosePipeEnd(module, block: block, ids: ids, record: stream, offset: SubpStreamReadEnd as ByteOffset)
end 'emitSubpCloseStreamReadEnd'

// ⭐ **CONSUME `takeLen` BYTES OFF THE FRONT OF THE PER-HANDLE STREAM BUFFER.** Answers them as a fresh OWNED
// String (a COPY the caller drops) and compacts what is left forward, which is the whole of what a buffered
// reader does once it has decided WHERE its answer ends. WHERE is the caller's question and the two readers
// answer it differently — `buildSubpReadLine` ends at a `\n`, `buildSubpReadExact` at a count — but the consume
// is one fact, and the publish of the new length is the step that must not exist twice.
//
// `takeLen == bufLen` leaves the buffer empty through the same arithmetic, so an EOF remainder needs no
// separate reset; `takeLen == 0` copies nothing and is how a zero-byte request answers "" without a read.
function emitSubpTakeFromStream(module StdModule, block IrBlock, ids ValueMinter, streamPtr ValueId, takeLen ValueId) returns ValueId
	let bufPtr = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufPtr, ids: ids)
	let result = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(result, callee: SubpStrFromBytesName, args: twoArgs(bufPtr, second: takeLen)))
	let bufLen = emitLoadField(module, block: block, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, ids: ids)
	let remaining = emitBin(module, block: block, a: bufLen, b: takeLen, opcode: StdBinOpcode.sub, ids: ids)
	let tailSrc = emitBin(module, block: block, a: bufPtr, b: takeLen, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let compacted = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(block, op: StdOp.call(compacted, callee: StrCopyName, args: threeArgs(bufPtr, second: tailSrc, third: remaining)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: block, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, value: remaining)
	return result
end 'emitSubpTakeFromStream'

// ⭐⭐ **ONE CHUNK OFF THE PIPE INTO THE PER-HANDLE STREAM BUFFER — AND THE ONLY COPY OF THAT STEP.** Both
// readers over a streaming child need the identical four moves: grow the buffer if a chunk will not fit
// (PRESERVING what is buffered), park on the read, check the slot is still ours, and either latch WHY the
// stream stopped or publish the new length. Only the stopping RULE differs between them.
//
// ⭐ THE PARK is the `__gt_subp_yield_read` call below — the ONE point either reader sleeps, and therefore the
// one point `subpRelease(h)` + a re-`subpSpawn` can hand this slot to a DIFFERENT handle behind our back. So
// the slot's generation is captured HERE, before the park, and re-read immediately after: a mismatch means the
// slot we resumed into is no longer ours, and EVERY resume-side slot write — this function's eof and bufLen,
// and downstream of the caller's re-test, `emitSubpTakeFromStream`'s bufLen and this function's own next
// bufPtr/bufCap — must be skipped. Guarding at this single point covers all of them because every one is
// reachable only through here. ⚠ That is also why the guard cannot be duplicated per reader: a second copy is
// a second chance to get the ONE window in this substrate where a slot changes owner wrong.
//
// Terminates `cur` and answers the continuation block, which the caller terminates against its own re-test —
// the convention `emitSubpOwnedByteCopy` states, and for its reason: a builder answering both a block and a
// value would have to answer a pair, and a pair is a heap box.
function emitSubpTopUpStream(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, cur IrBlock, streamPtr ValueId, readEnd ValueId, bytesOutPtr ValueId, streamBase ByteOffset, streamState EnumLayout, whenStale IrBlock) returns IrBlock
	var doGrow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"topupgrow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doRead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"topupread", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var readDone = module.addBlock(func, label: b"topupclassify", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var stopped = module.addBlock(func, label: b"topupstopped", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var setEof = module.addBlock(func, label: b"topupeof", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var setFailed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"topupfailed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var didRead = module.addBlock(func, label: b"topupgot", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var resumed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"topupnext", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// cur: ensure room for one more chunk past the current length.
	let bufLenC = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, ids: ids)
	let bufCapC = emitLoadField(module, block: cur, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufCap, ids: ids)
	let chunkConst = emitConst(module, block: cur, value: SubpReadChunkBytes as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let needed = emitBin(module, block: cur, a: bufLenC, b: chunkConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: cur, a: needed, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, b: bufCapC, whenTrue: doGrow, whenFalse: doRead, ids: ids)

	// doGrow: newCap = needed*2 ; copy the buffered bytes into the fresh buffer (PRESERVE) ; update ptr + cap.
	let twoConst = emitConst(module, block: doGrow, value: 2, ids: ids)
	let newCap = emitBin(module, block: doGrow, a: needed, b: twoConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let newBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doGrow, op: StdOp.call(newBuf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(newCap)))
	let oldBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: doGrow, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufPtr, ids: ids)
	let grownCopy = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doGrow, op: StdOp.call(grownCopy, callee: StrCopyName, args: threeArgs(newBuf, second: oldBuf, third: bufLenC)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: doGrow, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufPtr, value: newBuf)
	emitStoreField(module, block: doGrow, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufCap, value: newCap)
	module.setTerminator(doGrow, op: StdOp.branch(doRead.id))

	// doRead: append one chunk at buf[bufLen]. A read that delivers nothing latches why.
	let genOffFromStream = (SubpSlotGeneration - streamBase) as ByteOffset
	let bufPtrD = emitLoadField(module, block: doRead, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufPtr, ids: ids)
	let bufLenD = emitLoadField(module, block: doRead, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, ids: ids)
	let readDst = emitBin(module, block: doRead, a: bufPtrD, b: bufLenD, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let chunkConst2 = emitConst(module, block: doRead, value: SubpReadChunkBytes as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)

	// The read pipe is TABLE-owned, so a promise dropped mid-read leaves it open for the next read on this
	// handle and `subpRelease` is its sole closer.
	let genAtPark = emitLoadField(module, block: doRead, record: streamPtr, offset: genOffFromStream, ids: ids)
	let nRead = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doRead, op: StdOp.call(nRead, callee: SubpYieldReadName, args: fourArgs(readEnd, second: readDst, third: chunkConst2, fourth: bytesOutPtr)))
	let genOnResume = emitLoadField(module, block: doRead, record: streamPtr, offset: genOffFromStream, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: doRead, a: genOnResume, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, b: genAtPark, whenTrue: whenStale, whenFalse: readDone, ids: ids)

	// readDone: the slot is still ours. A failed read must publish NOTHING — added to `bufLen` its negative
	// count would drive the length past the bytes the buffer holds, and every later reader of this stream
	// reads that length.
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: readDone, value: nRead, pred: StdCmpPred.le, against: 0, whenTrue: stopped, whenFalse: didRead, ids: ids)

	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: stopped, value: nRead, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: 0, whenTrue: setFailed, whenFalse: setEof, ids: ids)

	let atEofState = emitConst(module, block: setEof, value: streamState.tagOfCase(SubprocessStreamAtEofCaseName), ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: setEof, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamStateWord, value: atEofState)
	module.setTerminator(setEof, op: StdOp.branch(resumed.id))

	let readFailedState = emitConst(module, block: setFailed, value: streamState.tagOfCase(SubprocessStreamReadFailedCaseName), ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: setFailed, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamStateWord, value: readFailedState)
	module.setTerminator(setFailed, op: StdOp.branch(resumed.id))

	let newLen = emitBin(module, block: didRead, a: bufLenD, b: nRead, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: didRead, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, value: newLen)
	module.setTerminator(didRead, op: StdOp.branch(resumed.id))

	return resumed
end 'emitSubpTopUpStream'

// ⭐⭐ **MAY A LINE READER PARK ON THE PIPE?** The one fact that separates `__gt_subp_read_line` from
// `__gt_subp_try_read_line`, and it is a parameter rather than a second body-builder because everything else
// about them is identical: the newline scan, the per-handle buffer, the growth policy, the consume, the EOF
// remainder and the stale-slot guard. A second copy would be a second chance to get the generation guard
// wrong, which is the one window in this substrate where a slot changes owner.
enum LineReadWait
	// Refill and PARK until a line arrives or the stream ends.
	untilALineArrives

	// Refill only from bytes the pipe already holds, and answer "" rather than wait.
	onlyWhatIsBuffered
end 'LineReadWait'

// ⭐ **ASK THE PIPE BEFORE COMMITTING TO A REFILL** — the whole of what `onlyWhatIsBuffered` adds.
// `emitSubpTopUpStream` parks the calling green thread on the read, so a reader that must never wait has to
// establish first that bytes are already there; a peek that answers N then makes the overlapped read complete
// SYNCHRONOUSLY, which is the same structure the collect loop drains two pipes with.
//
// ⚠ **A FAILED PEEK ANSWERS "" AND LATCHES NOTHING.** A broken pipe is what a peek fails with once the child
// has closed its write end, and the stream's state word is `emitSubpTopUpStream`'s to write from a read that
// really happened — so a caller that wants the end-of-stream verdict asks `subprocessStdoutState` for it
// rather than inferring it from an empty answer.
//
// Answers the block `readMore` must branch to; `refill` is where a pipe with bytes ready continues.
function emitSubpPeekBeforeRefill(module StdModule, func IrFunction, ids ValueMinter, readEnd ValueId, bytesOutPtr ValueId, refill IrBlock) returns IrBlock
	var gate = module.addBlock(func, label: b"trypeek", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var ready = module.addBlock(func, label: b"trypeeked", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var nothing = module.addBlock(func, label: b"trynone", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	let peekOk = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(gate, op: StdOp.osPipePeek(peekOk, handleId: readEnd, availPtrId: bytesOutPtr))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: gate, value: peekOk, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: nothing, whenFalse: ready, ids: ids)

	let avail = emitLoadDword(module, block: ready, record: bytesOutPtr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: ready, value: avail, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: 0, whenTrue: nothing, whenFalse: refill, ids: ids)

	// An empty OWNED String rather than 0, for `buildSubpReadLine`'s stated reason: the result is dropped
	// through `__str_decref`, which dereferences the record header.
	let emptyLen = emitConst(module, block: nothing, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let emptyLine = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(nothing, op: StdOp.call(emptyLine, callee: SubpStrFromBytesName, args: twoArgs(emptyLen, second: emptyLen)))
	module.setTerminator(nothing, op: StdOp.ret(emptyLine))

	return gate
end 'emitSubpPeekBeforeRefill'

// ⭐ `<name>(handle) -> ownedString` — read one line (INCLUDING the trailing '\n') from the stream at `streamBase`
// (SubpStdoutBase or SubpStderrBase, baked in per instance so stdout and stderr share this ONE body-builder),
// yielding while a chunk read is in flight. Scans the handle's growable line buffer for '\n'; returns everything
// through the first one and compacts the tail forward. When none is buffered, reads a chunk into the buffer
// (growing it, PRESERVING the buffered bytes) and re-scans only the new bytes. A read that delivers nothing
// leaves the stream's state word past `open`, and the remaining bytes (or "") are returned. The result is a
// fresh OWNED String the caller drops (the parser marks it a pending owned temp). `i` (the scan resume index)
// is `scanHdr`'s loop arg; everything else flows by dominance.
function buildSubpReadLine(module StdModule, name ByteArray, streamBase ByteOffset, streamState EnumLayout, wait LineReadWait)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var scanHdr = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scanhdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var scanCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scancheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var scanInc = module.addBlock(func, label: b"scaninc", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var found = module.addBlock(func, label: b"found", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var noNewline = module.addBlock(func, label: b"nonewline", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var emitRemainder = module.addBlock(func, label: b"remainder", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var readMore = module.addBlock(func, label: b"readmore", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var growCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"growcheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var staleExit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"staleexit", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var badHandle = module.addBlock(func, label: b"badhandle", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	// ⭐ **THE PER-LINE CAP IS THE CALLER'S, NOT A CONSTANT.** `__Builtins.subprocessReadStdoutLine(h, maxBytes)`
	// promises that *"lines longer than maxBytes are truncated and the remainder is delivered on the next
	// call"*, and this reader's ceiling IS that promise — one truncation rather than a second,
	// differently-spelled one at the boundary. The bare-name `subpReadLine(h)` builtin passes
	// `SubpMaxLineBytes`.
	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let handle = 0
	let maxLineBytes = 1

	// ⭐ **THE HANDLE GUARD, AT THE ENTRY POINT.** See `emitSubpRequireHandle` for why it is here rather than
	// at a wrapper, and for the grep that keeps the roster of these openings complete.
	// A handle naming no live child reads as EOF — the SAME answer this reader gives a child that has closed
	// its stdout, and the one `stripTrailingCR` and `String.init` already handle. ⚠ It answers an empty OWNED
	// String rather than 0 for the reason `buildSubpResolvePath` states about its own miss: the result is
	// dropped through `__str_decref`, which dereferences the record header, so a null would move the fault
	// rather than remove it.
	let emptyLen = emitConst(module, block: badHandle, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let emptyLine = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(badHandle, op: StdOp.call(emptyLine, callee: SubpStrFromBytesName, args: twoArgs(emptyLen, second: emptyLen)))
	module.setTerminator(badHandle, op: StdOp.ret(emptyLine))

	var live = emitSubpRequireHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: entry, handle: handle, whenBad: badHandle)
	let slot = emitSubpSlotOf(module, block: live, ids: ids, handle: handle)
	let streamBaseConst = emitConst(module, block: live, value: streamBase as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let streamPtr = emitBin(module, block: live, a: slot, b: streamBaseConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let readEnd = emitLoadField(module, block: live, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamReadEnd, ids: ids)
	let bytesOutPtr = emitLoadField(module, block: live, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotBytesOut, ids: ids)
	let zeroStart = emitConst(module, block: live, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(live, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	live.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(zeroStart)))

	// scanHdr(i): i reached the buffered length with no '\n' → noNewline; else look at byte i.
	let i = ids.mint()
	scanHdr.blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(i, argType: StdType.i64))
	let bufLenH = emitLoadField(module, block: scanHdr, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: scanHdr, a: i, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: bufLenH, whenTrue: noNewline, whenFalse: scanCheck, ids: ids)

	let bufPtrC = emitLoadField(module, block: scanCheck, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufPtr, ids: ids)
	let byteAddr = emitBin(module, block: scanCheck, a: bufPtrC, b: i, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let scanByte = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(scanCheck, op: StdOp.loadIndirect(scanByte, addrId: byteAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.u8))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: scanCheck, value: scanByte, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SubpNewlineByte as FullRangeInt, whenTrue: found, whenFalse: scanInc, ids: ids)

	let scanOne = emitConst(module, block: scanInc, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let scanNext = emitBin(module, block: scanInc, a: i, b: scanOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(scanInc, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	scanInc.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(scanNext)))

	// found(i = the '\n' index): the line is buf[0 .. i] inclusive, so it consumes i+1 bytes.
	let foundOne = emitConst(module, block: found, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let lineLen = emitBin(module, block: found, a: i, b: foundOne, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let resultFound = emitSubpTakeFromStream(module, block: found, ids: ids, streamPtr: streamPtr, takeLen: lineLen)
	module.setTerminator(found, op: StdOp.ret(resultFound))

	// noNewline: no '\n' in the buffer. If the stream has STOPPED — at end of stream or on a failed read,
	// which are the same thing to a reader and different things to `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon` — return the
	// remaining bytes; else read more.
	let stateN = emitLoadField(module, block: noNewline, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamStateWord, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: noNewline, value: stateN, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: streamState.tagOfCase(SubprocessStreamOpenCaseName), whenTrue: emitRemainder, whenFalse: readMore, ids: ids)

	// emitRemainder: consume the WHOLE buffer (possibly "" = EOF), which is what leaves it empty.
	let bufLenR = emitLoadField(module, block: emitRemainder, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, ids: ids)
	let resultRem = emitSubpTakeFromStream(module, block: emitRemainder, ids: ids, streamPtr: streamPtr, takeLen: bufLenR)
	module.setTerminator(emitRemainder, op: StdOp.ret(resultRem))

	// readMore: coarse per-line cap (a newline-less stream cannot grow unbounded) → treat as a line; else grow-check.
	let bufLenM = emitLoadField(module, block: readMore, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, ids: ids)
	let overCap = emitCmp(module, block: readMore, a: bufLenM, b: maxLineBytes, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, ids: ids)

	// The BLOCK ID rather than the block: binding a block here would MOVE `growCheck`, which the top-up
	// below still needs.
	let refillEntry = match wait 'wait'
		untilALineArrives gives growCheck.id
		onlyWhatIsBuffered gives emitSubpPeekBeforeRefill(module, func: func, ids: ids, readEnd: readEnd, bytesOutPtr: bytesOutPtr, refill: growCheck).id
	end 'wait'

	module.setTerminator(readMore, op: StdOp.condBranch(overCap, thenBlockId: emitRemainder.id, elseBlockId: refillEntry))

	// The top-up — grow, park on the read, and either latch why the stream stopped or publish the new
	// length — is `emitSubpTopUpStream`'s, shared with `buildSubpReadExact`.
	var toppedUp = emitSubpTopUpStream(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: growCheck, streamPtr: streamPtr, readEnd: readEnd, bytesOutPtr: bytesOutPtr, streamBase: streamBase, streamState: streamState, whenStale: staleExit)

	// staleExit: our handle was released and its slot re-spawned into a different one while we slept. Return an
	// EMPTY line (correct for us — our handle is gone) and touch NO slot state, so the new occupant is untouched.
	// `streamPtr` is only a source address for a ZERO-length copy here, so it reads nothing.
	let staleLen = emitConst(module, block: staleExit, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let staleResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(staleExit, op: StdOp.call(staleResult, callee: SubpStrFromBytesName, args: twoArgs(streamPtr, second: staleLen)))
	module.setTerminator(staleExit, op: StdOp.ret(staleResult))

	// `i` (the scan resume index) is the buffered length the top-up started from — no '\n' was found in [0, i),
	// so re-entering `scanHdr(i)` scans only the bytes the read appended, keeping the whole read O(total bytes).
	module.setTerminator(toppedUp, op: StdOp.branch(scanHdr.id))
	toppedUp.branchEdges.push(BranchEdge.create(scanHdr.id, argIds: argsOf(i)))
	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpReadLine'

// ⭐⭐ `__gt_subp_read_exact(handle, want) -> ownedString` — EXACTLY `want` bytes off the child's stdout,
// blocking until it has them. It answers FEWER only at EOF, and then only what remained; a second call after
// that answers "" off the SAME latched flag the line reader uses, and `want == 0` answers "" without touching
// the pipe. The result is a fresh OWNED String the caller drops (the parser marks it a pending owned temp).
//
// ⭐⭐ **WHY A COUNT IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION FROM A LINE.** A framed protocol puts its body length in a
// header — `Content-Length: N\r\n\r\n<body>`, the LSP framing — and the body carries NO trailing newline. A
// line reader asked for it blocks for ever on a terminator the sender will never write, and
// `subpReadLineCapped`'s cap does not help: that cap is consulted only when a refill is needed and then
// answers the whole buffered remainder, so it is a CEILING, not a length.
//
// ⭐⭐ **IT READS THE SAME PER-HANDLE BUFFER `buildSubpReadLine` DOES, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.** Such a
// client alternates the two — headers by line, body by count — and the line reader pulls a whole 4 KiB chunk
// off the pipe to find its newline. A reader with a buffer of its own would be blind to every byte already
// pulled: it would block on a pipe holding nothing more, and those bytes would be silently lost. That is a
// WRONG ANSWER, not a limitation. The sharing is structural rather than remembered — both readers address the
// stream as `slot + streamBase` and neither owns the top-up (`emitSubpTopUpStream`) or the consume
// (`emitSubpTakeFromStream`).
//
// ⚠ **STDOUT ONLY.** The line readers are a stdout/stderr pair because both streams carry lines; nothing in
// the language asks stderr for a byte COUNT, and an uninstalled twin is a second body to keep right with no
// caller to say when it stopped being right.
function buildSubpReadExact(module StdModule, streamState EnumLayout)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SubpReadExactName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var handleCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"exacthandle", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wantCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"exacthdr", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var takeWant = module.addBlock(func, label: b"exactwant", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var eofCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"exacteof", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var takeRest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"exactrest", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var needMore = module.addBlock(func, label: b"exactmore", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var staleExit = module.addBlock(func, label: b"exactstale", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var refused = module.addBlock(func, label: b"exactrefused", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let handle = 0
	let want = 1

	// The ONE refusal, shared by both guards below: an empty OWNED String, no slot state touched. A handle
	// naming no live child reads as EOF, which is what `buildSubpReadLine` answers it, and an OWNED String
	// rather than 0 for the reason stated there — the result is dropped through `__str_decref`, which
	// dereferences the record header, so a null would move the fault rather than remove it.
	let emptyLen = emitConst(module, block: refused, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let emptyResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(refused, op: StdOp.call(emptyResult, callee: SubpStrFromBytesName, args: twoArgs(emptyLen, second: emptyLen)))
	module.setTerminator(refused, op: StdOp.ret(emptyResult))

	// ⚠ **A NEGATIVE COUNT MUST BE REFUSED BEFORE THE BUFFERED TEST, NOT INSIDE IT.** `bufLen >= want` is
	// VACUOUSLY TRUE for a negative want, so the request would fall straight into the take with a negative
	// length: a String record claiming a negative length, and — because the take publishes `bufLen - takeLen`
	// — a stream buffer whose length has been driven UP past what it holds, corrupting the NEXT reader too.
	// Unguarded, `readStdoutBytes(-1)` panics in `String.byteLength`.
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: entry, value: want, pred: StdCmpPred.lt, against: 0, whenTrue: refused, whenFalse: handleCheck, ids: ids)

	var live = emitSubpRequireHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: handleCheck, handle: handle, whenBad: refused)
	let slot = emitSubpSlotOf(module, block: live, ids: ids, handle: handle)
	let streamBaseConst = emitConst(module, block: live, value: SubpStdoutBase as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let streamPtr = emitBin(module, block: live, a: slot, b: streamBaseConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let readEnd = emitLoadField(module, block: live, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamReadEnd, ids: ids)
	let bytesOutPtr = emitLoadField(module, block: live, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotBytesOut, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(live, op: StdOp.branch(wantCheck.id))

	// wantCheck: enough buffered → hand back exactly `want`. `want == 0` satisfies this on the first pass,
	// which is what makes a zero-byte request cost no system call.
	let bufLenW = emitLoadField(module, block: wantCheck, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: wantCheck, a: bufLenW, pred: StdCmpPred.ge, b: want, whenTrue: takeWant, whenFalse: eofCheck, ids: ids)

	let resultWant = emitSubpTakeFromStream(module, block: takeWant, ids: ids, streamPtr: streamPtr, takeLen: want)
	module.setTerminator(takeWant, op: StdOp.ret(resultWant))

	// eofCheck: short of `want`. The stream has STOPPED → hand back what remains (possibly ""); else top up
	// and re-test. The loop terminates because a top-up either appends at least one byte or leaves the state
	// word past `open`.
	let stateE = emitLoadField(module, block: eofCheck, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamStateWord, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: eofCheck, value: stateE, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: streamState.tagOfCase(SubprocessStreamOpenCaseName), whenTrue: takeRest, whenFalse: needMore, ids: ids)

	let bufLenR = emitLoadField(module, block: takeRest, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamBufLen, ids: ids)
	let resultRest = emitSubpTakeFromStream(module, block: takeRest, ids: ids, streamPtr: streamPtr, takeLen: bufLenR)
	module.setTerminator(takeRest, op: StdOp.ret(resultRest))

	var toppedUp = emitSubpTopUpStream(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: needMore, streamPtr: streamPtr, readEnd: readEnd, bytesOutPtr: bytesOutPtr, streamBase: SubpStdoutBase, streamState: streamState, whenStale: staleExit)
	module.setTerminator(toppedUp, op: StdOp.branch(wantCheck.id))

	// staleExit: our handle was released and its slot re-spawned into a different one while we slept. Answer
	// EMPTY (correct for us — our handle is gone) and touch NO slot state, so the new occupant is untouched.
	// `streamPtr` is only a source address for a ZERO-length copy here, so it reads nothing.
	let staleLen = emitConst(module, block: staleExit, value: 0, ids: ids)
	let staleResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(staleExit, op: StdOp.call(staleResult, callee: SubpStrFromBytesName, args: twoArgs(streamPtr, second: staleLen)))
	module.setTerminator(staleExit, op: StdOp.ret(staleResult))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpReadExact'

// ⭐ `<name>(handle) -> __SubprocessStreamState ordinal` — WHAT A READER OF THIS STREAM WOULD ANSWER NEXT,
// which is the one fact a reader's BYTES cannot carry: `""` is a clean end of stream and every refusal
// alike, and `stdlib/Subprocess.maxon`'s three readers throw for one and return for the other on the
// strength of this. Never blocks, never parks. `streamBase` is baked in per instance, so stdout's and
// stderr's queries are this one body twice, as `buildSubpReadLine` is for the same pair of streams.
//
// `noSuchChild` comes from the guard rather than from any slot, which is what makes it answerable for a
// handle that names nothing at all: a released slot, an out-of-range index, or a table never allocated.
// It is the ONLY case never stored (`emitSubpTopUpStream` latches the other two, the spawn writes `open`).
function buildSubpStreamStateQuery(module StdModule, name ByteArray, streamBase ByteOffset, streamState EnumLayout)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(name, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var bad = module.addBlock(func, label: b"statenochild", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let handle = 0

	let noSuchChild = emitConst(module, block: bad, value: streamState.tagOfCase(SubprocessStreamNoSuchChildCaseName), ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(bad, op: StdOp.ret(noSuchChild))

	var good = emitSubpRequireHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: entry, handle: handle, whenBad: bad)
	let slot = emitSubpSlotOf(module, block: good, ids: ids, handle: handle)
	let baseConst = emitConst(module, block: good, value: streamBase as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	let streamPtr = emitBin(module, block: good, a: slot, b: baseConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let state = emitLoadField(module, block: good, record: streamPtr, offset: SubpStreamStateWord, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(good, op: StdOp.ret(state))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpStreamStateQuery'

// `__gt_subp_write_line(handle, strPtr) -> rc` — write `strPtr`'s bytes + '\n' to the child's stdin (a synchronous
// WriteFile on the sync outbound pipe). Uses the per-handle write buffer (grown on demand, reused across writes) so
// a line's bytes + the newline are one contiguous WriteFile. Returns 0 on success, 1 on a broken pipe.
function buildSubpWriteLine(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SubpWriteLineName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 2, paramTypes: twoI64Params())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(2)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var growCheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"growcheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doGrow = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dogrow", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doWrite = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dowrite", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var wrote = module.addBlock(func, label: b"wrote", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var failed = module.addBlock(func, label: b"failed", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var badHandle = module.addBlock(func, label: b"badhandle", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 2)
	let handle = 0
	let strPtr = 1

	// ⭐ **THE HANDLE GUARD, AT THE ENTRY POINT.** See `emitSubpRequireHandle` for why it is here rather than
	// at a wrapper, and for the grep that keeps the roster of these openings complete.
	// A handle naming no live child answers the same non-zero this entry answers for a broken pipe.
	let badRc = emitConst(module, block: badHandle, value: -1, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(badHandle, op: StdOp.ret(badRc))

	var live = emitSubpRequireHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: entry, handle: handle, whenBad: badHandle)
	let slot = emitSubpSlotOf(module, block: live, ids: ids, handle: handle)
	let stdinWrite = emitLoadField(module, block: live, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotStdinWrite, ids: ids)
	let strBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: live, record: strPtr, offset: StringBufferOffset, ids: ids)
	let strLen = emitLoadField(module, block: live, record: strPtr, offset: StringLengthOffset, ids: ids)
	let oneW = emitConst(module, block: live, value: 1, ids: ids)
	let needed = emitBin(module, block: live, a: strLen, b: oneW, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(live, op: StdOp.branch(growCheck.id))

	let writeCap = emitLoadField(module, block: growCheck, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotWriteCap, ids: ids)
	emitBranchOnCompareValues(module, block: growCheck, a: needed, pred: StdCmpPred.gt, b: writeCap, whenTrue: doGrow, whenFalse: doWrite, ids: ids)

	let twoConst = emitConst(module, block: doGrow, value: 2, ids: ids)
	let newCap = emitBin(module, block: doGrow, a: needed, b: twoConst, opcode: StdBinOpcode.mul, ids: ids)
	let newBuf = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doGrow, op: StdOp.call(newBuf, callee: SlabAllocName, args: argsOf(newCap)))
	emitStoreField(module, block: doGrow, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotWriteBuf, value: newBuf)
	emitStoreField(module, block: doGrow, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotWriteCap, value: newCap)
	module.setTerminator(doGrow, op: StdOp.branch(doWrite.id))

	// doWrite: copy the line into the write buffer, append '\n', WriteFile the whole thing.
	let writeBuf = emitLoadField(module, block: doWrite, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotWriteBuf, ids: ids)
	let wcopy = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doWrite, op: StdOp.call(wcopy, callee: StrCopyName, args: threeArgs(writeBuf, second: strBuf, third: strLen)))
	let nlAddr = emitBin(module, block: doWrite, a: writeBuf, b: strLen, opcode: StdBinOpcode.add, ids: ids)
	let nlByte = emitConst(module, block: doWrite, value: SubpNewlineByte as FullRangeInt, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(doWrite, op: StdOp.storeIndirect(nlAddr, offset: 0, valueId: nlByte, valueType: StdType.u8))
	let bytesOutW = emitLoadField(module, block: doWrite, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotBytesOut, ids: ids)
	let writeOk = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(doWrite, op: StdOp.osWriteFile(writeOk, handleId: stdinWrite, bufId: writeBuf, lenId: needed, bytesOutPtrId: bytesOutW))
	emitBranchOnCompare(module, block: doWrite, value: writeOk, pred: StdCmpPred.ne, against: 0, whenTrue: wrote, whenFalse: failed, ids: ids)

	let rcZero = emitConst(module, block: wrote, value: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(wrote, op: StdOp.ret(rcZero))
	let rcOne = emitConst(module, block: failed, value: 1, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(failed, op: StdOp.ret(rcOne))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpWriteLine'

// `__gt_subp_close_stdin(handle)` — close the parent's stdin write end so the child sees EOF. Idempotent: it
// zeroes the field after closing, so a second call (or release) does not double-close. A push in flight is
// asked to close instead, on its way out (`SubpSlotStdinState`).
function buildSubpCloseStdin(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SubpCloseStdinName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var tryClose = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tryclose", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var tryRequest = module.addBlock(func, label: b"tryrequest", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var recheck = module.addBlock(func, label: b"recheck", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let handle = 0

	// ⭐ **THE HANDLE GUARD, AT THE ENTRY POINT.** See `emitSubpRequireHandle` for why it is here rather than
	// at a wrapper, and for the grep that keeps the roster of these openings complete.
	// A handle naming no live child has no stdin to close, which is exactly what this entry's `done` arm
	// already means for a child whose stdin is closed already.
	var live = emitSubpRequireHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: entry, handle: handle, whenBad: done)
	let slot = emitSubpSlotOf(module, block: live, ids: ids, handle: handle)
	module.setTerminator(live, op: StdOp.branch(tryClose.id))

	var closing = emitSubpCloseStdinIfIdle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: tryClose, slot: slot, whenLost: tryRequest)
	module.setTerminator(closing, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	var requested = emitSubpStdinTransition(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: tryRequest, slot: slot, expected: SubpStdinPushing, replacement: SubpStdinCloseRequested, whenLost: recheck)
	module.setTerminator(requested, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	// Both CASes lost because the state moved between them. `idle` or `pushing` has no closer yet, so
	// the close is tried again; dropping out there would lose the request.
	let stateAddr = emitFieldAddr(module, block: recheck, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotStdinState, ids: ids)
	let observed = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(recheck, op: StdOp.loadAcquire(observed, addrId: stateAddr, offset: 0, valueType: StdType.i64))
	let isIdle = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: recheck, value: observed, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SubpStdinIdle, ids: ids)
	let isPushing = emitCompareWithConstant(module, block: recheck, value: observed, pred: StdCmpPred.eq, against: SubpStdinPushing, ids: ids)
	let unclaimed = emitBin(module, block: recheck, a: isIdle, b: isPushing, opcode: StdBinOpcode.bitOr, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(recheck, op: StdOp.condBranch(unclaimed, thenBlockId: tryClose.id, elseBlockId: done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpCloseStdin'

// `__gt_subp_wait(handle) -> exitCode` — block until the child exits and return its exit code. A blocking
// WaitForSingleObject (the harness waits after draining output, so the child is already exiting); GetExitCodeProcess
// writes the code into the handle's reusable bytesOut slot.
function buildSubpWait(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SubpWaitName, returnType: StdReturnType.value(StdType.i64), paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param())
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var badHandle = module.addBlock(func, label: b"badhandle", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let handle = 0

	// The handle guard is at the entry point; see `emitSubpRequireHandle` for why, and for the grep
	// that keeps the roster of these openings complete. A handle naming no live child answers -1,
	// which is `__gt_subp_wait_exit`'s failure answer for the identical reading.
	let badRc = emitConst(module, block: badHandle, value: -1, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(badHandle, op: StdOp.ret(badRc))

	var live = emitSubpRequireHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: entry, handle: handle, whenBad: badHandle)
	let slot = emitSubpSlotOf(module, block: live, ids: ids, handle: handle)
	let hProcess = emitLoadField(module, block: live, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotHProcess, ids: ids)
	let infinite = emitConst(module, block: live, value: SubpWaitInfinite, ids: ids)
	let waitResult = ids.mint()
	module.appendOp(live, op: StdOp.osWaitChild(waitResult, handleId: hProcess, timeoutMsId: infinite))
	let codePtr = emitLoadField(module, block: live, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotBytesOut, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(live, op: StdOp.osProcessExitCode(hProcess, codePtrId: codePtr))
	let exitCode = emitLoadDword(module, block: live, record: codePtr, offset: 0, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(live, op: StdOp.ret(exitCode))

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpWait'

// `__gt_subp_release(handle)` — close every OS handle and mark the slot free (its buffers persist for reuse).
// Idempotent via the `inUse` guard; every closed handle field is zeroed so a stale/duplicate close cannot hit a
// reused OS handle.
function buildSubpRelease(module StdModule)
	var func = IrFunction.createFromStd(SubpReleaseName, returnType: StdReturnType.void, paramCount: 1, paramTypes: oneI64Param(), needsGreenThreadStackGuard: true, pinsItsThread: true)
	var ids = ValueMinter.create(1)

	var entry = module.addBlock(func, label: b"entry", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var doRelease = module.addBlock(func, label: b"dorelease", heat: BlockHeat.normal)
	var done = module.addBlock(func, label: b"done", heat: BlockHeat.normal)

	emitParams(module, block: entry, count: 1)
	let handle = 0

	// ⭐ **THE HANDLE GUARD, AT THE ENTRY POINT.** See `emitSubpRequireHandle` for why it is here rather than
	// at a wrapper, and for the grep that keeps the roster of these openings complete.
	// ⭐ **THE GUARD SUBSUMES THIS ENTRY'S OWN `inUse` TEST**, which is what its documented IDEMPOTENCE is
	// made of, and it runs BEFORE the slot address is formed rather than after. A second release of a
	// released handle reaches `done` and does nothing; so does a handle that was never live, or one whose
	// slot has since gone to another child, instead of closing that child's HANDLEs.
	var live = emitSubpRequireHandle(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: entry, handle: handle, whenBad: done)
	let slot = emitSubpSlotOf(module, block: live, ids: ids, handle: handle)
	var owned = emitSubpRequireCollectorTaken(module, func: func, cur: live, slot: slot, replacement: SubpCollectorReleased, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(owned, op: StdOp.branch(doRelease.id))

	var afterStdin = emitSubpCloseStdinWriteOwned(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: doRelease, slot: slot)
	emitSubpCloseReadEnds(module, block: afterStdin, ids: ids, slot: slot)
	let hProcess = emitLoadField(module, block: afterStdin, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotHProcess, ids: ids)
	module.appendOp(afterStdin, op: StdOp.osCloseHandle(hProcess))
	let zeroA = emitConst(module, block: afterStdin, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterStdin, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotHProcess, value: zeroA)
	let ownedTree = emitLoadField(module, block: afterStdin, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotOwnedTreeObject, ids: ids)
	var afterTree = emitSubpCloseTreeObject(module, func: func, ids: ids, cur: afterStdin, treeObject: ownedTree)
	let zeroT = emitConst(module, block: afterTree, value: 0, ids: ids)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterTree, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotOwnedTreeObject, value: zeroT)
	emitStoreField(module, block: afterTree, record: slot, offset: SubpSlotProcessTree, value: zeroT)
	emitSubpPublishSlotState(module, block: afterTree, slot: slot, value: SubpSlotFree, ids: ids)
	module.setTerminator(afterTree, op: StdOp.branch(done.id))

	module.setTerminator(done, op: StdOp.retVoid)

	module.functions.push(func)
end 'buildSubpRelease'

// The streaming subprocess API's surface. `__gt_subp_yield_read`, the read it rides, is built beside
// `__gt_io_read` in `installGtRuntime`.
function installSubpRuntime(module StdModule, target Target, streamState EnumLayout)
	buildSubpStrFromBytes(module)

	if hostTextIsWide(module) 'wideRequest'
		buildSubpWideRequestStep(module, step: SubpWideRequestStep.widen)
		buildSubpWideRequestStep(module, step: SubpWideRequestStep.release)
	end 'wideRequest'

	buildSubpSpawnCore(module, commandShape: subprocessCommandShape(target), streamState: streamState)
	buildSubpSpawn(module, commandShape: subprocessCommandShape(target))
	buildSubpReadLine(module, name: SubpReadLineName, streamBase: SubpStdoutBase, streamState: streamState, wait: LineReadWait.untilALineArrives)
	buildSubpReadLine(module, name: SubpReadErrLineName, streamBase: SubpStderrBase, streamState: streamState, wait: LineReadWait.untilALineArrives)
	buildSubpReadLine(module, name: SubpTryReadLineName, streamBase: SubpStdoutBase, streamState: streamState, wait: LineReadWait.onlyWhatIsBuffered)
	buildSubpReadLine(module, name: SubpTryReadErrLineName, streamBase: SubpStderrBase, streamState: streamState, wait: LineReadWait.onlyWhatIsBuffered)
	buildSubpReadExact(module, streamState: streamState)

	// The two per-stream state queries, beside the readers whose empty answer they are the discriminator for.
	buildSubpStreamStateQuery(module, name: SubpOutStateName, streamBase: SubpStdoutBase, streamState: streamState)
	buildSubpStreamStateQuery(module, name: SubpErrStateName, streamBase: SubpStderrBase, streamState: streamState)
	buildSubpWriteLine(module)
	buildSubpCloseStdin(module)
	buildSubpWait(module)
	buildSubpRelease(module)

	// The ATTACHED half rides the same table, the same spawn core and the same release — see
	// `SubprocessRuntime`'s header for why it is one substrate and not two.
	installSubprocessRuntime(module, target: target)
end 'installSubpRuntime'
